/* SQE1 January 2027 — consolidated bank */
const QUESTIONS = [{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0001","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Shareholder resolutions","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Northbridge Design Ltd has adopted the Model Articles without amendment.\nIts board proposes a change that, under the Companies Act 2006, requires\nan ordinary resolution of the shareholders. At a properly convened\ngeneral meeting, 800 votes are validly cast on a poll: 405 in favour,\n375 against and 20 abstentions. A shareholder argues that the resolution\nhas failed because fewer than 51% of all 800 votes were cast in favour.\n\nWhich statement best describes the position?","options":{"A":"The resolution failed because an ordinary resolution requires at least 51% of all votes capable of being cast.","B":"The resolution failed because abstentions are treated as votes against.","C":"The resolution passed because it obtained a simple majority of the votes actually cast.","D":"The resolution passed only if every shareholder was present or represented.","E":"The resolution requires at least 75% of the votes cast."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"An ordinary resolution is passed by a simple majority. Abstentions are not votes cast for or against the resolution. Of the 780 votes cast for or against, 405 were in favour, so the resolution passed.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect: the statutory test is not 51% of all votes capable of being cast.","B":"Incorrect: an abstention is not automatically a vote against.","C":"Correct: 405 exceeds 375, so there is a simple majority of votes cast.","D":"Incorrect: unanimity or universal attendance is not the ordinary-resolution threshold.","E":"Incorrect: 75% is associated with a special resolution, not an ordinary resolution."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 282.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Companies Act 2006, s 282.","common_trap":"Including abstentions in the denominator or importing the 75% special-resolution threshold.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"eb9c33811693de63"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0002","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Distributions","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A profitable-looking private company has £900,000 cash in its bank\naccount after taking a large bank loan. Its latest relevant accounts\nshow accumulated realised losses and no profits available for\ndistribution. The directors want to declare a £250,000 dividend because\nthe company has ample cash and expects strong trading next year.\n\nWhich statement best describes whether the dividend may lawfully be paid\non those facts?","options":{"A":"Yes, because cash available to pay the dividend is the only statutory test.","B":"Yes, provided all shareholders consent to the dividend.","C":"Yes, because a private company may distribute borrowed money if it remains solvent.","D":"No, because a distribution may be made only out of profits available for the purpose.","E":"No, because companies may never pay dividends while they have outstanding bank debt."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Liquidity is not the same as distributable profits. The Companies Act restricts distributions to profits available for the purpose. Borrowing can increase cash without creating distributable profits.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect: cash availability alone is insufficient.","B":"Incorrect: shareholder consent does not convert capital or borrowed funds into distributable profits.","C":"Incorrect: solvency does not displace the statutory distribution rules.","D":"Correct.","E":"Incorrect: debt does not itself prohibit all dividends."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 830.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A company may make a distribution only out of profits available for the purpose.","common_trap":"Equating cash or solvency with distributable reserves.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a32909814af2a5b6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0003","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Summary judgment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A supplier sues a retailer for an unpaid contractual invoice. The\nretailer files a defence admitting delivery and the agreed price but\nalleging that payment can be withheld because its managing director is\npersonally unhappy with the supplier. The contract contains no term\nsupporting that position. Disclosure is unlikely to affect the point,\nand there is no identified factual dispute requiring oral evidence.\n\nThe supplier asks whether it can seek determination without waiting for\na full trial.\n\nWhich application is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"An application for default judgment because the defence has no merit.","B":"An application for summary judgment because the defence has no real prospect of success and there is no other compelling reason for trial.","C":"An application for an interim injunction restraining the retailer from defending the claim.","D":"A Part 36 offer asking the court to determine liability immediately.","E":"An application to strike out automatically because every weak defence must be struck out rather than determined summarily."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The facts engage CPR Part 24. A defence has been filed, so default judgment is not the appropriate route. On the stated facts the defence has no real prospect of success and there is no other compelling reason for trial.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect: default judgment is not the route once a defence has been filed in these circumstances.","B":"Correct.","C":"Incorrect: an injunction does not determine the merits of the debt claim.","D":"Incorrect: Part 36 governs settlement offers, not an application for adjudication.","E":"Incorrect: summary judgment and strike-out are distinct procedural mechanisms; a weak merits case is not automatically a mandatory strike-out."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 24.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"CPR 24.3.","common_trap":"Confusing default judgment, strike-out and summary judgment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2a17969d015c6dfa"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0004","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Part 36","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant in a damages action wishes to make a Part 36 offer. The\nclaimant proposes to send a signed letter stating that the claim will be\nsettled for £70,000. The letter says it is made under Part 36 and\nrelates to the whole claim, but gives the defendant only 10 days in\nwhich to accept while remaining liable for the claimant’s costs under\nthe ordinary Part 36 acceptance regime. There is no trial due within\nthat period.\n\nWhich is the best advice?","options":{"A":"The offer complies because any acceptance period chosen by the offeror is valid.","B":"The offer complies because Part 36 does not require an offer to be in writing.","C":"The offer does not comply with the ordinary Part 36 requirements because the relevant period specified must be at least 21 days.","D":"The offer is invalid because only defendants may make Part 36 offers.","E":"The offer is invalid because a Part 36 offer can relate only to part of a claim."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"CPR 36.5 requires a Part 36 offer to be in writing, identify itself as a Part 36 offer and, ordinarily, specify a period of not less than 21 days for the relevant costs consequences. The proposed 10-day period does not satisfy that requirement on the facts given.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect.","B":"Incorrect: writing is expressly required.","C":"Correct.","D":"Incorrect: either party may make a Part 36 offer.","E":"Incorrect: an offer may address the whole claim, part of it or an issue."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 36.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"CPR 36.5.","common_trap":"Treating Part 36 as an entirely informal settlement mechanism.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"36176f00ae643fdb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0005","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services / Ethics","subtopic":"Own-interest conflict","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor is asked to advise a client whether to sue an investment\ncompany for negligent advice. Before joining the firm, the solicitor\npersonally invested a substantial part of her savings in the same\ncompany. A successful group of claims against the company could\nmaterially reduce the value of her own investment. The client knows this\nand says in writing that he is happy for her to act.\n\nWhat is the best course under the SRA conflict rules?","options":{"A":"Act because written client consent cures every type of conflict.","B":"Act if another solicitor in the firm checks the final advice.","C":"Do not act where there is an own-interest conflict or a significant risk of one; the ordinary client-conflict exceptions do not cure an own-interest conflict.","D":"Act because a solicitor’s personal financial interests are irrelevant unless the client complains.","E":"Act provided the solicitor does not tell the client the amount she invested."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code prohibits acting where there is an own-interest conflict or a significant risk of one. The exceptions in paragraph 6.2 concern certain conflicts between clients; they do not create a consent-based exception to paragraph 6.1.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect: consent does not universally cure conflicts.","B":"Incorrect: supervision does not remove the solicitor’s own financial interest.","C":"Correct.","D":"Incorrect.","E":"Incorrect and inconsistent with transparent professional conduct."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, RELs, RFLs and RSLs, paras 6.1–6.2. ============================================================ FLK1 — SESSION 2 ============================================================","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 6.1.","common_trap":"Applying the informed-consent exceptions for client conflicts to an own-interest conflict.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d6dc245c016de760"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0006","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Third-party rights","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A manufacturer contracts with a retailer to install specialist equipment\nat the retailer’s premises. The written contract states: “The\nmanufacturer will maintain the equipment for five years for the benefit\nof Riverside Café Ltd,” which operates the premises under a concession.\nRiverside is expressly identified by name but is not a party to the\ncontract. The contract contains no term excluding the Contracts (Rights\nof Third Parties) Act 1999.\n\nThe manufacturer later refuses to perform the maintenance obligation.\nWhich statement best describes Riverside’s position under the 1999 Act?","options":{"A":"Riverside can never enforce the term because consideration did not move from it.","B":"Riverside may be able to enforce the term because it is expressly identified and the term purports to confer a benefit on it, subject to the contract showing no contrary intention.","C":"Riverside can enforce every term of the contract merely because it occupies the premises.","D":"Riverside can enforce only if it becomes a shareholder of the retailer.","E":"Riverside can enforce only if the manufacturer signs a second contract directly with it."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The 1999 Act can permit an identified third party to enforce a contractual term where the contract expressly provides for enforcement or the term purports to confer a benefit on that third party, subject to contrary intention and the statutory conditions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect: the Act is an exception to the traditional privity barrier.","B":"Correct.","C":"Incorrect: enforceability is term-specific and subject to the Act.","D":"Incorrect.","E":"Incorrect: a second contract is not necessarily required."},"primary_legal_authority":"Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999, s 1.","common_trap":"Treating privity as absolute despite the statutory exception.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a87c6309d7e45ed1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0007","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Misrepresentation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Before selling a small business, the seller tells the buyer that the\npremises have never suffered serious flooding. The seller genuinely\nbelieves this because the previous manager told her so, but she has made\nno further checks. The statement is false and materially induces the\nbuyer to contract. The buyer establishes actionable misrepresentation\nand seeks damages under section 2(1) of the Misrepresentation Act 1967.\n\nWhat is the seller’s most relevant statutory route to resisting\nliability under section 2(1)?","options":{"A":"Prove that she had reasonable grounds to believe, and did believe up to the time of the contract, that the represented facts were true.","B":"Prove only that she was subjectively honest, regardless of whether her belief had reasonable grounds.","C":"Prove that the statement was not written into the final contract.","D":"Prove that the buyer could have discovered the truth by making enquiries.","E":"Prove that the misrepresentation was not fraudulent."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Under section 2(1), once the statutory conditions are engaged, the representor avoids that damages liability by showing reasonable grounds for belief and actual belief in the truth of the representation up to the time the contract was made. Mere honesty is not enough.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct.","B":"Incorrect: section 2(1) requires more than subjective honesty.","C":"Incorrect: a representation need not become a contractual term.","D":"Incorrect: the buyer’s ability to investigate does not itself provide the stated statutory defence.","E":"Incorrect: section 2(1) specifically addresses non-fraudulent misrepresentation as well."},"primary_legal_authority":"Misrepresentation Act 1967, s 2(1).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Misrepresentation Act 1967, s 2(1).","common_trap":"Equating innocent subjective belief with reasonable grounds for belief.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5c7111531cf1c63c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0008","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Occupiers’ liability","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A garden centre invites customers onto its premises. A customer brings\nher six-year-old child. Beside the customer path is a shallow decorative\npond. The centre knows children frequently approach it, but the low\nbarrier around the pond has been broken for weeks. No temporary barrier\nor warning has been installed. The child leaves the path, reaches the\npond and is injured in a fall.\n\nIn considering the garden centre’s duty under the Occupiers’ Liability\nAct 1957, which statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"The centre owes no duty because only the paying adult, not the child, is a visitor.","B":"The centre must take reasonable care for visitors’ safety and must be prepared, where appropriate, for children to be less careful than adults.","C":"The centre is automatically liable whenever a child is injured on its premises.","D":"A warning always discharges the occupier’s duty regardless of whether it enables the visitor to be reasonably safe.","E":"The centre owes a duty only if it intended the child to be injured."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The common duty of care is to take reasonable care to see that visitors are reasonably safe for the permitted purpose. The Act expressly recognises that an occupier may need to be prepared for children to be less careful than adults. Liability is nevertheless not automatic.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect.","B":"Correct.","C":"Incorrect: breach and causation still require analysis.","D":"Incorrect: a warning is not automatically sufficient.","E":"Incorrect: negligence does not require an intention to injure."},"primary_legal_authority":"Occupiers’ Liability Act 1957, s 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Occupiers’ Liability Act 1957, s 2.","common_trap":"Turning the special consideration of children into strict liability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4827357cba758aa7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0009","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review time limit","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A public body makes a final administrative decision on 1 February. A\nperson directly affected obtains all relevant documents immediately but\ndecides to wait while attempting political lobbying. No special\nstatutory judicial-review time limit applies. On 25 April, the person\nasks a solicitor whether it is safe to wait until 30 April because that\nwould still be within three calendar months of the decision.\n\nWhich advice best reflects CPR Part 54?","options":{"A":"The claim may always be filed at any point within three months; promptness is irrelevant.","B":"The claim must be filed promptly and in any event no later than three months after the grounds first arose, so being within three months does not by itself establish timeliness.","C":"The claimant has six years because judicial review is a civil claim.","D":"The parties may agree between themselves to extend the judicial-review time limit.","E":"Time begins only when the claimant first consults a solicitor."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The general CPR rule contains two requirements: filing promptly and, in any event, within three months after the grounds arose. Parties cannot extend that period merely by agreement, and some categories have shorter limits.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect: it omits the promptness requirement.","B":"Correct.","C":"Incorrect.","D":"Incorrect.","E":"Incorrect."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 54, r 54.5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"CPR 54.5.","common_trap":"Treating three months as an entitlement to wait for the entire period.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"782b467d1e8f8668"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0010","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System / Professional Conduct","subtopic":"Misleading the court","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"During civil proceedings, a client tells her solicitor that a document\nalready disclosed to the opponent is authentic. Later, before a hearing,\nshe admits privately to the solicitor that she fabricated it and\ninstructs the solicitor to continue relying on it because “the other\nside will never prove it.” She refuses to permit the solicitor to\ncorrect the position.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the solicitor’s regulatory position?","options":{"A":"The solicitor may continue relying on the document because confidentiality always overrides duties concerning misleading the court.","B":"The solicitor may positively tell the court that the document is authentic because the client has instructed her to do so.","C":"The solicitor must not mislead or attempt to mislead the court and cannot be complicit in the client’s misleading conduct.","D":"The solicitor must publicly disclose every confidential communication from the client immediately, regardless of legal restrictions.","E":"The solicitor may rely on the document if doing so is likely to advance the client’s case."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code expressly prohibits misleading or attempting to mislead the court or others, including by being complicit in a client’s conduct. Confidentiality remains important, but it does not entitle a solicitor to make or support a statement known to be false. The precise steps concerning withdrawal/disclosure depend on the circumstances and applicable law; the core tested proposition is that the solicitor cannot continue misleading the court.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect.","B":"Incorrect.","C":"Correct.","D":"Incorrect: the duty not to mislead does not create an unlimited licence to disclose confidential information.","E":"Incorrect."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 1.4. ============================================================ FLK2 — SESSION 1 ============================================================","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A solicitor must not mislead the court or be complicit in misleading it.","common_trap":"Treating confidentiality as permission to advance facts known to be false.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"09ae9685456be8b1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0011","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Execution","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator signs a paper will alone in her study. The next morning she\nmeets two neighbours together, shows them the signature and clearly\nacknowledges that it is hers and that the document is her will. Both\nneighbours are present at the same time for that acknowledgment. Each\nthen signs the will as a witness in the testator’s presence.\n\nAssuming testamentary capacity and intention, which statement best\ndescribes the execution?","options":{"A":"Invalid because the testator had to make the original signature while both witnesses watched.","B":"Invalid because each witness had to sign in the presence of the other witness.","C":"Potentially valid because the testator may acknowledge her existing signature in the presence of two witnesses present at the same time, and each witness must sign or acknowledge their signature in the testator’s presence.","D":"Invalid because a will can be witnessed only by solicitors.","E":"Valid only if the witnesses read the entire will before signing."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 9 permits the testator either to sign or to acknowledge the signature in the presence of two or more witnesses present at the same time. Each witness must then attest and sign, or acknowledge their signature, in the testator’s presence; the witnesses need not necessarily sign in each other’s presence.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect.","B":"Incorrect.","C":"Correct.","D":"Incorrect.","E":"Incorrect."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 9.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Wills Act 1837, s 9.","common_trap":"Assuming both witnesses must observe the physical act of the testator signing.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8c3c12209c32600a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0012","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Investment criteria","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Trustees hold a £2 million investment fund for adult and minor\nbeneficiaries. The trust instrument contains no special investment\nrestriction. One trustee proposes putting the entire fund into shares in\na single early-stage technology company because he believes its product\nwill become highly profitable. The investment is within the trustees’\ngeneral investment power.\n\nWhat is the most relevant objection under the Trustee Act 2000?","options":{"A":"Trustees may invest only in government bonds.","B":"The general investment power removes any obligation to consider suitability or diversification.","C":"Trustees must consider the suitability of investments and the need for diversification so far as appropriate to the trust’s circumstances.","D":"Diversification is legally required in exactly the same proportions for every trust.","E":"Trustees may make any investment they personally prefer if they act honestly."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The broad general power of investment is constrained by the standard investment criteria. Trustees must consider suitability and the need for diversification, insofar as appropriate, and review investments from time to time.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect.","B":"Incorrect.","C":"Correct.","D":"Incorrect: the statutory criterion is context-sensitive.","E":"Incorrect."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, ss 3–4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trustee Act 2000, ss 3–4.","common_trap":"Reading the general investment power without the standard investment criteria.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"368d8d8f92ecf8bf"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0013","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Statutory duty of care","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two trustees are considering an investment decision to which the Trustee\nAct 2000 duty of care applies. One trustee is a retired teacher with no\ninvestment experience. The other is a partner in a professional\ninvestment-management business and was appointed partly because of that\nexpertise.\n\nHow does the statutory duty of care apply?","options":{"A":"Both trustees are judged solely by the standard of the least experienced trustee.","B":"Both trustees are automatically strictly liable for any fall in investment value.","C":"The required care and skill is reasonable in the circumstances, taking account of special knowledge or experience, including what is reasonably expected of a professional acting in that business.","D":"The professional trustee owes no duty because the beneficiaries chose to appoint an expert.","E":"Only paid trustees are subject to any duty of care."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 1 uses a contextual standard and expressly takes account of actual or held-out special knowledge or experience and, for a professional trustee, the knowledge or experience reasonably expected from that professional role.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect.","B":"Incorrect.","C":"Correct.","D":"Incorrect.","E":"Incorrect."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, s 1 and Schedule 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trustee Act 2000, s 1.","common_trap":"Treating the statutory duty as either strict liability or a single undifferentiated standard.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"21a7973f8b5843e2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0014","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts in a wills context","subtopic":"Client money","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm administering an estate receives £40,000 into its hands\nrepresenting estate money that it holds as personal representative in\nthe course of the regulated work. The firm has not yet become entitled\nto that money as its own fees.\n\nFor the purposes of the SRA Accounts Rules, which description is most\naccurate?","options":{"A":"It cannot be client money because the deceased, rather than the beneficiaries, originally owned it.","B":"It falls outside the Accounts Rules whenever a solicitor acts as personal representative.","C":"It is capable of being client money because the definition includes money held as trustee or as the holder of a specified office or appointment.","D":"It becomes office money immediately on receipt because the firm expects eventually to bill the estate.","E":"It may be treated as the firm’s money provided the partners unanimously agree."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Rule 2.1 includes, within client money, money held or received as trustee or as holder of specified offices or appointments, subject to the detailed Rules and exceptions. Future entitlement to fees does not itself make the entire fund office money.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect.","B":"Incorrect.","C":"Correct.","D":"Incorrect.","E":"Incorrect."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, rule 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 2.1.","common_trap":"Assuming estate/trust money is outside “client money” merely because the firm acts in a fiduciary capacity.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ec7facbb43cf34a2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0015","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Protecting a third-party interest","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"The registered proprietor of a house grants a neighbour an equitable\ninterest capable of protection by notice. The interest is valid but is\nnot entered in the register and does not have overriding status. The\nproprietor then sells the registered estate for full market value to a\nbuyer, who completes the registrable disposition by registration.\n\nWhat is the central priority consequence under the Land Registration Act\n2002?","options":{"A":"The earlier equitable interest necessarily has priority simply because it was created first.","B":"Registration of the buyer’s disposition for value can postpone an earlier interest whose priority was not protected, subject to the statutory exceptions.","C":"A notice would have guaranteed that the equitable interest itself was substantively valid.","D":"A restriction and a notice have exactly the same legal function.","E":"Registration is irrelevant to priority between interests in registered land."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Although the basic rule protects earlier interests, section 29 creates the important priority rule for registrable dispositions for value completed by registration. An unprotected interest can be postponed unless an exception applies. A notice protects priority but does not guarantee substantive validity.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect: it ignores the section 29 regime.","B":"Correct.","C":"Incorrect: a notice does not validate the underlying interest.","D":"Incorrect: notices and restrictions perform different functions.","E":"Incorrect."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, ss 28–29; HM Land Registry Practice Guide 19. ============================================================ FLK2 — SESSION 2 ============================================================","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Land Registration Act 2002, ss 28–29.","common_trap":"Applying “first in time” without considering a registered disposition for value.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8b6e4564730033e3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0016","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Priority after completion","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A conveyancer completes the purchase of a registered freehold for value\ntogether with a new registered charge. An official search with priority\nprotecting the purchase and charge is still current. A colleague\nsuggests delaying the registration application until the lender’s\nseparate discharge evidence for an old charge arrives, even though that\nmay take several weeks.\n\nWhich is the best conveyancing approach?","options":{"A":"Delay everything because a second official search will automatically extend the original priority period.","B":"Lodge the purchase/charge application promptly; a later fresh search does not extend the original priority period and an intervening application may gain priority.","C":"Priority is fixed permanently at contractual exchange, so timing of registration is irrelevant.","D":"Do nothing because registered charges never affect priority.","E":"Wait until the buyer next sells the property."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"HM Land Registry guidance warns that it is unsafe to delay the transfer/new-charge application merely while awaiting discharge material. A further search creates a fresh priority period; it does not extend the original one, so an intervening application can gain priority.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect.","B":"Correct.","C":"Incorrect.","D":"Incorrect.","E":"Incorrect."},"primary_legal_authority":"HM Land Registry Practice Guide 31.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Protect and use the completion-search priority period; do not assume a fresh search extends the original period.","common_trap":"Believing repeated priority searches simply roll forward the original protected period.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a9a7b7168eb64299"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0017","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts in a property context","subtopic":"Returning client money","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A conveyancing firm completes a purchase. After paying the purchase\nprice, SDLT, Land Registry fee and all properly billed costs, £1,800 of\nthe client’s money remains in client account. There is no outstanding\nliability, undertaking or other proper reason to retain it. The client\nhas provided verified bank details.\n\nWhat should the firm do under the SRA Accounts Rules?","options":{"A":"Keep the balance indefinitely in case the client instructs the firm again.","B":"Transfer it to office account as additional profit.","C":"Return it promptly because there is no longer any proper reason to hold the funds.","D":"Keep it for at least six years before attempting repayment.","E":"Donate it immediately to charity without contacting the client."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require client money to be returned promptly once there is no longer a proper reason to hold it.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect.","B":"Incorrect.","C":"Correct.","D":"Incorrect.","E":"Incorrect on these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, rule 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 2.5.","common_trap":"Treating residual client balances as harmless administrative float.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"82e81e6248e9c448"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0018","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Theft: appropriation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An employee is mistakenly handed a company tablet by a colleague who\nbelieves it has been permanently allocated to her. The employee\ninitially shares that belief and takes it home. The next day she\nreceives an email clearly explaining the mistake and requiring its\nreturn. She then decides to keep the tablet, deletes the company’s\ndevice-management software and advertises it for sale as her own.\n\nWhich statement best addresses the appropriation element of theft?","options":{"A":"There can be no appropriation because the employee originally obtained possession innocently.","B":"A later assumption of an owner’s rights, such as keeping or dealing with property as owner, can amount to appropriation even where the person originally came by it innocently.","C":"Appropriation requires physically taking property from the owner’s hands without consent.","D":"Appropriation exists only where the property is money.","E":"Once possession was initially innocent, later conduct is legally irrelevant to theft."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Theft Act expressly provides that appropriation includes a later assumption of a right to property where the person initially came by the property innocently or otherwise. The later decision to keep and sell the tablet is therefore capable of satisfying appropriation; the other elements of theft must also be established.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect.","B":"Correct.","C":"Incorrect.","D":"Incorrect.","E":"Incorrect."},"primary_legal_authority":"Theft Act 1968, ss 1 and 3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Theft Act 1968, s 3.","common_trap":"Assuming innocent initial receipt permanently prevents a later appropriation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8c05f56a2aa1e727"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0019","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Police-station legal advice","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An adult suspect is arrested for an indictable offence and taken to a\npolice station. He asks to speak privately to a solicitor before\ninterview. The investigating officer says that requesting a solicitor\nwill “only make this take longer” and proposes to begin the interview\nimmediately. No officer has authorised any exceptional delay in access\nto legal advice and none of the other exceptions permitting interview\nbefore advice applies.\n\nWhich statement best reflects PACE Code C?","options":{"A":"Police may routinely interview immediately because legal advice is available only after charge.","B":"A detainee may consult privately with a solicitor and free independent legal advice is available; where advice is requested, the ordinary position is that interview should not begin or continue until it has been received unless a specified exception applies.","C":"Legal advice is available only if the detainee can pay privately.","D":"Police may discourage legal advice whenever they believe the suspect is guilty.","E":"A detainee has a right to legal advice only for summary-only offences."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Code C provides a continuing right to private legal advice and free independent legal advice. It also restricts interviewing a detainee who has requested advice until advice has been received, unless one of the specified exceptional routes applies. Officers should not dissuade a person from obtaining legal advice.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect.","B":"Correct.","C":"Incorrect.","D":"Incorrect.","E":"Incorrect."},"primary_legal_authority":"PACE Code C 2023, paras 6.1, 6.4–6.6.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"PACE Code C, section 6.","common_trap":"Treating access to a solicitor as discretionary or as arising only after charge.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"33311ccb5d54d992"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0020","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Theft: belief in legal right","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"APPROVED_PILOT","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contractor and a customer genuinely dispute ownership of a specialist\nmeasuring device. The contractor takes the device from the customer’s\noffice openly because, after reading the purchase documents, he honestly\nbelieves that he has the legal right to take and keep it. His belief is\nunreasonable, but the prosecution accepts that it is genuinely held.\n\nFor theft, what is the significance of that accepted belief?","options":{"A":"It is irrelevant because any appropriation of another person’s property is automatically dishonest.","B":"It is relevant because an appropriation is not to be regarded as dishonest if the defendant believes he has in law the right to deprive the other person of the property.","C":"It is relevant only if a solicitor first confirmed that the belief was legally correct.","D":"It is irrelevant unless the customer also believed the contractor owned the device.","E":"It automatically proves robbery rather than theft."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 2(1)(a) of the Theft Act provides that appropriation is not to be regarded as dishonest where the person believes that he has in law the right to deprive the other of the property. The statutory wording focuses on the defendant’s belief, not on whether that belief ultimately proves legally correct.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect.","B":"Correct.","C":"Incorrect.","D":"Incorrect.","E":"Incorrect."},"primary_legal_authority":"Theft Act 1968, ss 1–2. ============================================================ PILOT AUDIT ============================================================ 1. SUBSTANTIVE UNIQUENESS 20 questions / 20 distinct tested propositions. No item is counted as unique merely because of a changed name, option order, number, or introductory phrase. Tested propositions: 01 ordinary-resolution voting threshold 02 distributable profits 03 summary judgment 04 formal requirements for Part 36 offer 05 own-interest conflict 06 third-party contractual enforcement 07 s 2(1) misrepresentation defence 08 occupier’s duty concerning children 09 judicial-review promptness/time limit 10 duty not to mislead court 11 will execution by acknowledgment 12 trustee suitability/diversification 13 trustee statutory duty of care 14 estate money/client money 15 registered-land priority under s 29 16 completion-search priority / prompt registration 17 residual client balance 18 later appropriation after innocent receipt 19 police-station right to legal advice 20 theft and belief in legal right 2. SESSION ALIGNMENT FLK1 Session 1: Q01–Q05 FLK1 Session 2: Q06–Q10 FLK2 Session 1: Q11–Q15 FLK2 Session 2: Q16–Q20 3. ANSWER-KEY DISTRIBUTION A: 1 B: 7 C: 9 D: 3 E: 0 AUDIT FLAG: This 20-item pilot has an undesirable answer-position imbalance. This does not affect substantive correctness, but it MUST be corrected in the production bank. The production generator should balance correct-answer positions across A–E without altering legal substance. 4. DUPLICATION CONTROL Exact duplicate: none. Same legal proposition with cosmetic factual variation: none. Two criminal-theft questions are intentionally in the same statutory topic but test different elements: - Q18: appropriation under s 3 after innocent receipt. - Q20: dishonesty exclusion based on belief in legal right under s 2(1)(a). They should therefore not be treated as duplicates. 5. DRAFTING CONTROL Every item: - presents a concrete factual scenario; - asks a single lead-in question; - contains five options; - is designed to have one best answer; - avoids “all/none of the above”; - explains each option; - identifies the rule and common trap. 6. LEGAL-SOURCE CONTROL Core sources used: - SRA SQE1 Assessment Specification applicable from September 2026. - SRA guidance on single-best-answer questions. - Companies Act 2006. - Civil Procedure Rules Parts 24, 36 and 54. - SRA Code of Conduct and SRA Accounts Rules. - Occupiers’ Liability Act 1957. - Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999. - Misrepresentation Act 1967. - Wills Act 1837. - Trustee Act 2000. - Land Registration Act 2002 / HM Land Registry Practice Guides 19 and 31. - Theft Act 1968. - PACE Code C 2023. 7. IMPORTANT LIMITATION BEFORE SCALING This is a methodological pilot, not yet a production bank. Before scaling, the full 1,020-item build should add: - a formal topic/subtopic blueprint against every line of the September 2026 FLK; - answer-position balancing; - lexical and semantic near-duplicate detection; - repeated-factual-pattern detection; - per-topic caps preventing hundreds of questions testing the same rule; - source validation and date control; - manual sampling after automated QA.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Theft Act 1968, s 2(1)(a).","common_trap":"Asking only whether the defendant actually had the legal right, rather than whether the statutory belief existed.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f930a24297aca43b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0021","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Separate legal personality","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A sole shareholder incorporates a private company to operate a catering business. The company signs a five-year equipment lease in its own name. Two years later the business fails. The lessor argues that the shareholder must automatically pay the company’s remaining lease liabilities because she owns all the shares and controls every board decision.\n\nWhich statement best describes the shareholder’s position?","options":{"A":"She is automatically liable because a company with one shareholder has no separate legal personality.","B":"The company is a separate legal person, so ownership and control alone do not make the shareholder personally liable for the company’s contractual debt.","C":"She is liable whenever the company becomes insolvent.","D":"She is liable because limited liability applies only to public companies.","E":"She is liable unless the company has at least two directors."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"An incorporated company has legal personality separate from its shareholders. A shareholder’s complete ownership and control do not, without some separate basis of liability, make the shareholder personally liable for a contractual debt incurred by the company.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Separate corporate personality means company liabilities are not automatically liabilities of its shareholders.","B":"Correct. An incorporated company has legal personality separate from its shareholders. A shareholder’s complete ownership and control do not, without some separate basis of liability, make the shareholder personally liable for a contractual debt incurred by the company.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Separate corporate personality means company liabilities are not automatically liabilities of its shareholders.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Separate corporate personality means company liabilities are not automatically liabilities of its shareholders.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'She is liable unless the company has at least two directors' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Separate corporate personality means company liabilities are not automatically liabilities of its shareholders."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006; Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Separate corporate personality means company liabilities are not automatically liabilities of its shareholders.","common_trap":"Treating a wholly owned company as legally identical to its shareholder.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"eda146ab589f501e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0022","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Directors' duties — conflicts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A director of a private company learns through his directorship that a valuable warehouse adjoining the company’s factory is about to be sold cheaply. The board has not yet considered acquiring it. Without telling the company, the director buys the warehouse personally and plans to resell it at a profit. The opportunity came to him solely because of his position as director.\n\nWhich is the best analysis of the director’s statutory duty?","options":{"A":"There can be no conflict because the company had not already resolved to buy the warehouse.","B":"The duty applies only to transactions between the director and the company itself.","C":"The director may take the opportunity if he pays market value.","D":"The situation is capable of engaging the duty to avoid a direct or indirect interest that conflicts, or possibly may conflict, with the company’s interests, particularly exploitation of corporate opportunities.","E":"No duty applies unless the director actually makes a profit."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 175 expressly addresses exploitation of property, information or opportunity and can apply even if the company itself could not take advantage of the opportunity. The absence of a prior board resolution does not eliminate the potential conflict.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'There can be no conflict because the company had not already resolved to buy the warehouse' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A director must avoid situations involving a direct or indirect conflict or possible conflict with the company’s interests, subject to statutory authorisation rules.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A director must avoid situations involving a direct or indirect conflict or possible conflict with the company’s interests, subject to statutory authorisation rules.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The director may take the opportunity if he pays market value' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A director must avoid situations involving a direct or indirect conflict or possible conflict with the company’s interests, subject to statutory authorisation rules.","D":"Correct. Section 175 expressly addresses exploitation of property, information or opportunity and can apply even if the company itself could not take advantage of the opportunity. The absence of a prior board resolution does not eliminate the potential conflict.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No duty applies unless the director actually makes a profit' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A director must avoid situations involving a direct or indirect conflict or possible conflict with the company’s interests, subject to statutory authorisation rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 175.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A director must avoid situations involving a direct or indirect conflict or possible conflict with the company’s interests, subject to statutory authorisation rules.","common_trap":"Assuming a corporate opportunity is free for personal exploitation until the company formally decides to pursue it.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4ff340653ae8096f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0023","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership authority","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A three-partner architectural partnership routinely buys computers for its design studio. One partner orders ordinary replacement computers from the partnership’s usual supplier in the partnership name. The partnership agreement privately states that purchases over £8,000 require unanimous consent, but the supplier does not know of that restriction. The order is for £10,000 and is otherwise a normal transaction for the business.\n\nWhich statement best describes the partnership’s liability to the supplier?","options":{"A":"The partnership can be bound because each partner is an agent of the firm for business carried on in the usual way, unless the third party knows of the lack of authority or does not know or believe the person to be a partner.","B":"The partnership cannot be bound because every restriction in a partnership agreement automatically binds outsiders.","C":"The partnership is bound only if all three partners later ratify the purchase.","D":"The partnership cannot be bound by contracts exceeding £8,000 under any circumstances.","E":"Only the ordering partner can ever be liable on a partnership contract."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A partner is an agent of the firm and the other partners for partnership business. An act done for carrying on in the usual way business of the kind carried on by the firm binds the firm unless the statutory qualification concerning authority and the third party applies. A private internal restriction does not by itself protect the firm against an unaware supplier.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A partner is an agent of the firm and the other partners for partnership business. An act done for carrying on in the usual way business of the kind carried on by the firm binds the firm unless the statutory qualification concerning authority and the third party applies. A private internal restriction does not by itself protect the firm against an unaware supplier.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Usual-course partnership acts may bind the firm despite undisclosed internal restrictions.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Usual-course partnership acts may bind the firm despite undisclosed internal restrictions.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The partnership cannot be bound by contracts exceeding £8,000 under any circumstances' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Usual-course partnership acts may bind the firm despite undisclosed internal restrictions.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Usual-course partnership acts may bind the firm despite undisclosed internal restrictions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, s 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Usual-course partnership acts may bind the firm despite undisclosed internal restrictions.","common_trap":"Confusing an internal limit on actual authority with the firm’s position toward an innocent third party.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"111a17f748eac4d8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0024","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Limitation — contract","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A consultant breaches a contractual obligation on 10 March 2021 by delivering a report that does not comply with the agreed specification. The client immediately suffers recoverable loss. There is no deed, no contractual modification of limitation, no acknowledgment or part payment, and no special statutory regime.\n\nUnder the ordinary limitation rule for a simple contract claim, when does the limitation period normally expire?","options":{"A":"Three years after the client discovers the breach.","B":"Six years after the client first obtains legal advice.","C":"Twelve years after breach because every written contract is a specialty.","D":"Six years after the client sends a letter before claim.","E":"Six years from accrual of the cause of action, so ordinarily on 10 March 2027 on these facts."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"An action founded on simple contract is ordinarily subject to a six-year limitation period from accrual of the cause of action. In a straightforward breach claim, the cause of action generally accrues when the breach occurs, not when legal advice is obtained or the breach is discovered.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Three years after the client discovers the breach' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Simple contract: ordinarily six years from accrual.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Six years after the client first obtains legal advice' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Simple contract: ordinarily six years from accrual.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Simple contract: ordinarily six years from accrual.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Six years after the client sends a letter before claim' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Simple contract: ordinarily six years from accrual.","E":"Correct. An action founded on simple contract is ordinarily subject to a six-year limitation period from accrual of the cause of action. In a straightforward breach claim, the cause of action generally accrues when the breach occurs, not when legal advice is obtained or the breach is discovered."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, s 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Simple contract: ordinarily six years from accrual.","common_trap":"Importing the personal-injury discovery regime or the 12-year specialty period into an ordinary simple contract.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ee3bbfae0a0e29d8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0025","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Without prejudice privilege","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two companies are already in a genuine dispute about an alleged breach of contract. Their solicitors begin correspondence expressly aimed at compromising the dispute. During those negotiations, one party offers to pay £100,000 without admitting liability. At trial, the opponent wants to rely on the offer simply as evidence that the offering party must have believed its defence was weak. No recognised exception to without-prejudice protection is suggested.\n\nWhat is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"The offer is admissible because every settlement offer is an admission of liability.","B":"The offer is admissible because privilege applies only to oral negotiations.","C":"The communication will ordinarily be protected by the without-prejudice rule because it forms part of a genuine attempt to settle an existing dispute.","D":"The communication is protected only if it was made under CPR Part 36.","E":"The communication is automatically admissible once proceedings have begun."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The without-prejudice rule generally protects genuine settlement negotiations concerning an existing dispute from being deployed as admissions on the merits. The protection is not confined to Part 36 or to oral communications.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Genuine settlement communications in an existing dispute are ordinarily inadmissible on the merits under the without-prejudice rule.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Genuine settlement communications in an existing dispute are ordinarily inadmissible on the merits under the without-prejudice rule.","C":"Correct. The without-prejudice rule generally protects genuine settlement negotiations concerning an existing dispute from being deployed as admissions on the merits. The protection is not confined to Part 36 or to oral communications.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Genuine settlement communications in an existing dispute are ordinarily inadmissible on the merits under the without-prejudice rule.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Genuine settlement communications in an existing dispute are ordinarily inadmissible on the merits under the without-prejudice rule."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law without-prejudice rule; CPR Part 36 is a separate regime.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Genuine settlement communications in an existing dispute are ordinarily inadmissible on the merits under the without-prejudice rule.","common_trap":"Equating without-prejudice protection with Part 36.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"91bcd40cbb622f04"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0026","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Money laundering — tipping off","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor working in a regulated-sector transaction develops a suspicion of money laundering and makes the appropriate internal report to the firm’s nominated officer. The client then asks why completion has been delayed. The solicitor considers telling the client that a suspicious activity report has been made and that the authorities may now investigate, even though doing so could prejudice an investigation.\n\nWhat is the principal legal concern with giving that explanation?","options":{"A":"There is no concern because solicitors must always tell clients about every regulatory report.","B":"The disclosure may engage the prohibition on tipping off where the statutory conditions are satisfied.","C":"Tipping off applies only to banks and never to legal professionals.","D":"The solicitor is required to provide a copy of any suspicious activity report to the client.","E":"Once an internal report is made, professional confidentiality ceases entirely."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"In the regulated sector, disclosures about a suspicious activity report or related investigation can amount to tipping off where the statutory requirements are met, including the relevant risk of prejudicing an investigation. Client communication must therefore be handled carefully.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A person in the regulated sector must consider the POCA tipping-off restrictions after relevant disclosures.","B":"Correct. In the regulated sector, disclosures about a suspicious activity report or related investigation can amount to tipping off where the statutory requirements are met, including the relevant risk of prejudicing an investigation. Client communication must therefore be handled carefully.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A person in the regulated sector must consider the POCA tipping-off restrictions after relevant disclosures.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The solicitor is required to provide a copy of any suspicious activity report to the client' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A person in the regulated sector must consider the POCA tipping-off restrictions after relevant disclosures.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Once an internal report is made, professional confidentiality ceases entirely' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A person in the regulated sector must consider the POCA tipping-off restrictions after relevant disclosures."},"primary_legal_authority":"Proceeds of Crime Act 2002, ss 333A–333D.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A person in the regulated sector must consider the POCA tipping-off restrictions after relevant disclosures.","common_trap":"Assuming ordinary duties to keep a client informed require revealing a SAR or investigation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e7a169f06c373148"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0027","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consideration — existing contractual duty","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A building contractor is contractually obliged to complete refurbishment by 30 June. Halfway through the project the contractor encounters cash-flow difficulties and says it may not finish on time. The client, anxious to open the premises, promises an additional £20,000 if the contractor performs exactly the work already promised by 30 June. The contractor completes on time. There is no duress. On the facts, timely completion gives the client a substantial practical commercial benefit and avoids disruption.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the consideration issue?","options":{"A":"Performance of an existing contractual duty can never amount to consideration for a promise of extra payment.","B":"The extra promise is enforceable only if the contractor supplies additional materials not required by the original contract.","C":"A practical benefit to the promisor may, subject to the relevant limitations, be capable of supporting the promise of additional payment for performance of an existing duty.","D":"Consideration is unnecessary whenever both parties are businesses.","E":"The extra payment is automatically a deed."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The modern rule recognises that performance of an existing contractual duty may supply consideration where the promisor obtains a practical benefit or avoids a disbenefit, provided the relevant requirements are satisfied and the promise is not procured by economic duress or fraud.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A practical benefit can in appropriate circumstances constitute consideration for a promise to pay more for performance of an existing contractual duty.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A practical benefit can in appropriate circumstances constitute consideration for a promise to pay more for performance of an existing contractual duty.","C":"Correct. The modern rule recognises that performance of an existing contractual duty may supply consideration where the promisor obtains a practical benefit or avoids a disbenefit, provided the relevant requirements are satisfied and the promise is not procured by economic duress or fraud.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A practical benefit can in appropriate circumstances constitute consideration for a promise to pay more for performance of an existing contractual duty.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A practical benefit can in appropriate circumstances constitute consideration for a promise to pay more for performance of an existing contractual duty."},"primary_legal_authority":"Williams v Roffey Bros & Nicholls (Contractors) Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A practical benefit can in appropriate circumstances constitute consideration for a promise to pay more for performance of an existing contractual duty.","common_trap":"Applying the older existing-duty proposition as an absolute rule.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c786be50200a945d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0028","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Frustration","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company hires a specific historic hall for a one-off awards ceremony. Before the event, and without fault by either party, the hall is destroyed by fire. The contract contains no term allocating this risk and performance at that hall has become impossible.\n\nWhich doctrine is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Frustration, because a supervening event without relevant fault has made the contractual performance impossible and the contract does not allocate the risk.","B":"Misrepresentation, because the hall existed when the contract was made.","C":"Undue influence, because neither party caused the fire.","D":"Penalty, because performance has become impossible.","E":"Promissory estoppel, because the venue can no longer perform."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Destruction of the specific subject matter necessary for performance is a classic type of supervening event capable of frustrating a contract, assuming the event is not self-induced and the contract has not allocated the risk.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Destruction of the specific subject matter necessary for performance is a classic type of supervening event capable of frustrating a contract, assuming the event is not self-induced and the contract has not allocated the risk.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Misrepresentation, because the hall existed when the contract was made' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Frustration may discharge a contract where a supervening event, without relevant fault, makes performance impossible or radically different.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Undue influence, because neither party caused the fire' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Frustration may discharge a contract where a supervening event, without relevant fault, makes performance impossible or radically different.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Penalty, because performance has become impossible' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Frustration may discharge a contract where a supervening event, without relevant fault, makes performance impossible or radically different.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Promissory estoppel, because the venue can no longer perform' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Frustration may discharge a contract where a supervening event, without relevant fault, makes performance impossible or radically different."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law doctrine of frustration; Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Frustration may discharge a contract where a supervening event, without relevant fault, makes performance impossible or radically different.","common_trap":"Treating every post-contract problem as breach or misrepresentation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d69a7dc05b18c3f7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0029","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Professional negligence — standard of care","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client alleges that a surveyor negligently failed to identify structural movement in a commercial building. The issue concerns a task ordinarily performed by reasonably competent surveyors. The surveyor argues that he should be judged by the standard of an ordinary member of the public because the client knew he was newly qualified.\n\nWhich standard is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"The subjective standard of whatever care this particular surveyor personally thought sufficient.","B":"The standard of the most eminent surveyor in England and Wales.","C":"Strict liability: any missed defect establishes breach.","D":"The standard of reasonable care and skill expected of an ordinarily competent professional carrying out that work.","E":"The standard of an ordinary member of the public because the surveyor was newly qualified."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A professional undertaking skilled work is ordinarily judged against the objective standard of the reasonably competent person exercising that skill. Inexperience does not generally reduce the standard owed to the client.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The subjective standard of whatever care this particular surveyor personally thought sufficient' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Professional negligence applies an objective standard appropriate to the skill undertaken.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The standard of the most eminent surveyor in England and Wales' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Professional negligence applies an objective standard appropriate to the skill undertaken.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Strict liability: any missed defect establishes breach' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Professional negligence applies an objective standard appropriate to the skill undertaken.","D":"Correct. A professional undertaking skilled work is ordinarily judged against the objective standard of the reasonably competent person exercising that skill. Inexperience does not generally reduce the standard owed to the client.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The standard of an ordinary member of the public because the surveyor was newly qualified' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Professional negligence applies an objective standard appropriate to the skill undertaken."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law negligence principles concerning professional standard of care.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Professional negligence applies an objective standard appropriate to the skill undertaken.","common_trap":"Lowering the legal standard because the individual professional is inexperienced.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"84ed3dbb0f672ae6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0030","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Contributory negligence","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A motorist negligently collides with a cyclist and is primarily responsible for the accident. The cyclist suffers greater injury because she was not wearing an available safety helmet. On the evidence, the failure to wear the helmet contributed to the extent of the injury, although it did not cause the collision.\n\nIf contributory negligence is established, what is its ordinary effect?","options":{"A":"It automatically eliminates the motorist’s liability entirely.","B":"It converts the claim from tort to contract.","C":"It requires criminal proceedings against the cyclist.","D":"It prevents any award for personal injury.","E":"The damages may be reduced to the extent the court considers just and equitable having regard to the claimant’s share in responsibility for the damage."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Contributory negligence is generally a partial defence. The Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945 permits reduction of damages to the extent the court considers just and equitable having regard to the claimant’s share in responsibility for the damage.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contributory negligence ordinarily reduces damages rather than extinguishing the claim.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It converts the claim from tort to contract' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Contributory negligence ordinarily reduces damages rather than extinguishing the claim.","C":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Contributory negligence ordinarily reduces damages rather than extinguishing the claim.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It prevents any award for personal injury' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Contributory negligence ordinarily reduces damages rather than extinguishing the claim.","E":"Correct. Contributory negligence is generally a partial defence. The Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945 permits reduction of damages to the extent the court considers just and equitable having regard to the claimant’s share in responsibility for the damage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contributory negligence ordinarily reduces damages rather than extinguishing the claim.","common_trap":"Treating contributory negligence as a complete defence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3648e8bce7b4020f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0031","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — public authorities","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant alleges that a local authority, in exercising a statutory public function, acted incompatibly with one of the claimant’s Convention rights. No primary legislation compelled the authority to act incompatibly in the particular way alleged.\n\nWhich provision of the Human Rights Act 1998 is most directly relevant to whether the authority acted unlawfully?","options":{"A":"Section 6, which addresses acts of public authorities that are incompatible with Convention rights.","B":"Section 4, because every alleged breach automatically requires a declaration of incompatibility.","C":"Section 3 only, because Convention rights regulate legislation but never executive action.","D":"Section 10 only, because a remedial order is the claimant’s sole remedy.","E":"None: local authorities fall outside the Human Rights Act."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 6 makes it unlawful for a public authority to act incompatibly with a Convention right, subject to the statutory qualifications. A declaration of incompatibility concerns incompatible primary legislation and is not automatically the route for an allegedly unlawful discretionary act.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 6 makes it unlawful for a public authority to act incompatibly with a Convention right, subject to the statutory qualifications. A declaration of incompatibility concerns incompatible primary legislation and is not automatically the route for an allegedly unlawful discretionary act.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. HRA 1998 s 6 directly regulates incompatible acts of public authorities, subject to its qualifications.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. HRA 1998 s 6 directly regulates incompatible acts of public authorities, subject to its qualifications.","D":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. HRA 1998 s 6 directly regulates incompatible acts of public authorities, subject to its qualifications.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'None: local authorities fall outside the Human Rights Act' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. HRA 1998 s 6 directly regulates incompatible acts of public authorities, subject to its qualifications."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 6.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"HRA 1998 s 6 directly regulates incompatible acts of public authorities, subject to its qualifications.","common_trap":"Using s 4 declaration of incompatibility for every public-authority rights claim.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"26afbb9d46308e76"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0032","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Revocation by marriage/civil partnership","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A woman validly executes a will leaving her estate to her sister. Two years later she marries. The will was not expressed to be made in contemplation of that particular marriage and no statutory exception applies. She dies without executing another will.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect of the marriage on the earlier will?","options":{"A":"The will remains valid because marriage never affects an existing will.","B":"The will is ordinarily revoked by the marriage in the absence of an applicable exception.","C":"The marriage changes only the identity of the executor.","D":"The will becomes a codicil.","E":"The will remains valid if the sister knew about the marriage."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Subject to statutory exceptions, including the rules concerning a will made in contemplation of a particular marriage or civil partnership, marriage ordinarily revokes an existing will.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Marriage/civil partnership can revoke an earlier will unless a statutory exception applies.","B":"Correct. Subject to statutory exceptions, including the rules concerning a will made in contemplation of a particular marriage or civil partnership, marriage ordinarily revokes an existing will.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Marriage/civil partnership can revoke an earlier will unless a statutory exception applies.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The will becomes a codicil' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Marriage/civil partnership can revoke an earlier will unless a statutory exception applies.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The will remains valid if the sister knew about the marriage' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Marriage/civil partnership can revoke an earlier will unless a statutory exception applies."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 18 (as applicable).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Marriage/civil partnership can revoke an earlier will unless a statutory exception applies.","common_trap":"Assuming only physical destruction or a later will can revoke a will.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"02efc0edfcd891af"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0033","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Ademption","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator’s valid will gives “my 500 ordinary shares in Delta plc to my nephew.” Before death the testator sells all 500 Delta shares and uses the proceeds for general living expenses. At death she owns no Delta shares. There is no relevant statutory saving provision and the wording is construed as a specific legacy.\n\nWhat is the ordinary consequence for the gift?","options":{"A":"The nephew automatically receives the cash value of 500 Delta shares from the residue.","B":"The executors must purchase 500 Delta shares for the nephew.","C":"The gift ordinarily fails by ademption because the specifically gifted property is no longer part of the estate at death.","D":"The gift becomes a pecuniary legacy of the original purchase price.","E":"The sale automatically revokes the entire will."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A specific legacy generally adeems where the specifically identified property is no longer in the testator’s estate at death. It does not ordinarily convert into a right to equivalent property or its value.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A specific gift ordinarily adeems if the identified property is absent from the estate at death.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A specific gift ordinarily adeems if the identified property is absent from the estate at death.","C":"Correct. A specific legacy generally adeems where the specifically identified property is no longer in the testator’s estate at death. It does not ordinarily convert into a right to equivalent property or its value.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The gift becomes a pecuniary legacy of the original purchase price' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A specific gift ordinarily adeems if the identified property is absent from the estate at death.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A specific gift ordinarily adeems if the identified property is absent from the estate at death."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law doctrine of ademption.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A specific gift ordinarily adeems if the identified property is absent from the estate at death.","common_trap":"Assuming a beneficiary of a specific legacy always receives an equivalent cash substitute.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7b1d81688ec2eb75"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0034","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Three certainties — objects","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor transfers investments to trustees “to hold on trust in equal shares for whichever of my three children are alive when I die.” The property and beneficial shares are otherwise clear, and the three children are individually identifiable.\n\nWhich statement best addresses certainty of objects?","options":{"A":"The trust necessarily fails because beneficiaries of an express trust can never be identified by a future condition.","B":"The trust necessarily fails because a trust must benefit the public.","C":"The trust necessarily fails because the settlor is alive when the trust is created.","D":"The description is capable of satisfying certainty of objects because the class is defined by an objective condition and the potential beneficiaries are identifiable.","E":"The trust is valid only if all three children are trustees."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The objects of the trust are defined by an objective criterion—being one of the settlor’s three children and surviving the settlor. On the facts, the potential beneficiaries are identifiable. A future condition does not itself destroy certainty.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An express private trust requires sufficiently certain objects; an objectively defined and identifiable class can satisfy that requirement.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An express private trust requires sufficiently certain objects; an objectively defined and identifiable class can satisfy that requirement.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The trust necessarily fails because the settlor is alive when the trust is created' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An express private trust requires sufficiently certain objects; an objectively defined and identifiable class can satisfy that requirement.","D":"Correct. The objects of the trust are defined by an objective criterion—being one of the settlor’s three children and surviving the settlor. On the facts, the potential beneficiaries are identifiable. A future condition does not itself destroy certainty.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An express private trust requires sufficiently certain objects; an objectively defined and identifiable class can satisfy that requirement."},"primary_legal_authority":"General equitable principles concerning the three certainties.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An express private trust requires sufficiently certain objects; an objectively defined and identifiable class can satisfy that requirement.","common_trap":"Confusing future contingency with uncertainty of beneficiaries.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0cff09c9fe4101bb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0035","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — severance","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two friends own a registered house as legal joint tenants and beneficial joint tenants. One friend wants to sever the beneficial joint tenancy so that her beneficial share will no longer pass automatically by survivorship. She serves on the other co-owner a written notice that demonstrates an immediate intention to sever her beneficial joint tenancy.\n\nWhat is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"A beneficial joint tenancy can never be severed during the co-owners’ lifetimes.","B":"Severance necessarily converts the legal title into a tenancy in common.","C":"A written notice showing an immediate intention to sever can effect severance of the beneficial joint tenancy, while the legal estate remains held jointly.","D":"Severance is possible only by physically partitioning the land.","E":"The notice is ineffective unless the property is first sold."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The beneficial joint tenancy may be severed, including by appropriate written notice under section 36(2) LPA 1925. The legal estate cannot be held as a tenancy in common, so severance concerns the beneficial ownership.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A beneficial joint tenancy may be severed by effective written notice; the legal title remains joint.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Severance necessarily converts the legal title into a tenancy in common' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A beneficial joint tenancy may be severed by effective written notice; the legal title remains joint.","C":"Correct. The beneficial joint tenancy may be severed, including by appropriate written notice under section 36(2) LPA 1925. The legal estate cannot be held as a tenancy in common, so severance concerns the beneficial ownership.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A beneficial joint tenancy may be severed by effective written notice; the legal title remains joint.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The notice is ineffective unless the property is first sold' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A beneficial joint tenancy may be severed by effective written notice; the legal title remains joint."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 36(2).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A beneficial joint tenancy may be severed by effective written notice; the legal title remains joint.","common_trap":"Assuming severance changes the form of the legal estate.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8a6d74a135aba3cc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0036","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Exchange of contracts","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer and seller of an English freehold have agreed all commercial terms and their solicitors hold signed counterparts. Before exchange, the buyer asks whether the parties are already contractually committed merely because both have signed their respective documents. No other binding preliminary agreement exists.\n\nWhat is the best advice in the ordinary conveyancing transaction?","options":{"A":"Yes. Signature alone necessarily completes the transfer of legal title.","B":"No. In the ordinary process, the parties become contractually committed on exchange rather than merely because counterparts have been signed and remain with their solicitors.","C":"Yes. Signing automatically amounts to registration at HM Land Registry.","D":"No, because contracts for the sale of land can never be binding before completion.","E":"Yes, but only the buyer is bound."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"In the ordinary conveyancing process, signed counterparts may be held pending exchange. Exchange is the point at which the sale contract becomes binding under the agreed exchange mechanism. Completion and registration are later stages.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Yes. Signature alone necessarily completes the transfer of legal title' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Signing and exchange are distinct conveyancing steps; ordinary contractual commitment occurs on exchange.","B":"Correct. In the ordinary conveyancing process, signed counterparts may be held pending exchange. Exchange is the point at which the sale contract becomes binding under the agreed exchange mechanism. Completion and registration are later stages.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Signing and exchange are distinct conveyancing steps; ordinary contractual commitment occurs on exchange.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Signing and exchange are distinct conveyancing steps; ordinary contractual commitment occurs on exchange.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Signing and exchange are distinct conveyancing steps; ordinary contractual commitment occurs on exchange."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1989, s 2; standard conveyancing practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Signing and exchange are distinct conveyancing steps; ordinary contractual commitment occurs on exchange.","common_trap":"Confusing signature, exchange, completion and registration.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0170336e67618fcc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0037","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Commercial leases — security of tenure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A business tenant occupies premises under a tenancy within Part II of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954. The statutory contracting-out procedure was not used before the tenancy was granted. The contractual term is about to expire and the landlord assumes the tenant must leave automatically on the final day.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the statutory position, assuming no other exclusion applies?","options":{"A":"The tenancy necessarily ends with no statutory protection because all business tenancies expire automatically.","B":"The tenant becomes owner of the freehold when the contractual term expires.","C":"The landlord can recover possession without following any statutory procedure.","D":"The 1954 Act is relevant only to residential tenancies.","E":"The protected business tenancy does not simply terminate by effluxion of time in the ordinary way; the statutory continuation and termination/renewal regime must be considered."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A business tenancy protected by Part II of the 1954 Act generally continues after the contractual term until terminated in accordance with the statutory regime, with associated renewal rights subject to the Act.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A protected business tenancy engages the LTA 1954 continuation and renewal/termination regime.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The tenant becomes owner of the freehold when the contractual term expires' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A protected business tenancy engages the LTA 1954 continuation and renewal/termination regime.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The landlord can recover possession without following any statutory procedure' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A protected business tenancy engages the LTA 1954 continuation and renewal/termination regime.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A protected business tenancy engages the LTA 1954 continuation and renewal/termination regime.","E":"Correct. A business tenancy protected by Part II of the 1954 Act generally continues after the contractual term until terminated in accordance with the statutory regime, with associated renewal rights subject to the Act."},"primary_legal_authority":"Landlord and Tenant Act 1954, Part II.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A protected business tenancy engages the LTA 1954 continuation and renewal/termination regime.","common_trap":"Treating the contractual expiry date as automatically ending a protected business tenancy.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e2ec54bda843fc19"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0038","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Attempts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person decides to steal cash from a particular office safe. At night he enters the office unlawfully, walks directly to the safe carrying tools, and begins forcing its lock. Police intervene before the safe opens. Assume the evidence establishes the necessary intent to commit the full offence.\n\nWhat is the central actus reus question for attempt liability?","options":{"A":"Whether his conduct had gone beyond merely preparatory acts toward commission of the offence.","B":"Whether the completed offence actually caused financial loss.","C":"Whether the safe contained more than £1,000.","D":"Whether the police waited until he left the building.","E":"Whether he had previously been convicted of theft."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Under the Criminal Attempts Act 1981, liability for attempt requires an act that is more than merely preparatory to the commission of the offence, together with the necessary mens rea. On the facts, beginning to force the targeted safe is assessed against that statutory threshold.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Under the Criminal Attempts Act 1981, liability for attempt requires an act that is more than merely preparatory to the commission of the offence, together with the necessary mens rea. On the facts, beginning to force the targeted safe is assessed against that statutory threshold.","B":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Attempt requires an act more than merely preparatory to the commission of the offence plus the required intent.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the safe contained more than £1,000' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Attempt requires an act more than merely preparatory to the commission of the offence plus the required intent.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the police waited until he left the building' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Attempt requires an act more than merely preparatory to the commission of the offence plus the required intent.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether he had previously been convicted of theft' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Attempt requires an act more than merely preparatory to the commission of the offence plus the required intent."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Attempts Act 1981, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Attempt requires an act more than merely preparatory to the commission of the offence plus the required intent.","common_trap":"Requiring completion of part of the full offence rather than applying the statutory attempt threshold.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8be1358ad075393d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0039","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Secondary participation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person knowingly encourages a friend to commit a burglary, intending to assist or encourage that offence. The friend commits the burglary as contemplated. The encourager never enters the building and never physically handles the stolen property.\n\nWhich statement best addresses the encourager’s potential liability?","options":{"A":"Physical presence at the scene is always required for secondary liability.","B":"Only a person who personally performs every element of the principal offence can incur criminal liability.","C":"A person who intentionally assists or encourages the commission of an offence can potentially be liable as a secondary party even without being physically present at the scene.","D":"Secondary liability exists only for homicide offences.","E":"Encouragement is legally irrelevant unless money changes hands."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Secondary liability does not require the accessory personally to perform the principal offence or to be present at the scene. Intentional assistance or encouragement, coupled with the required mental element, can found liability.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Intentional assistance or encouragement can create secondary liability without physical presence at the offence.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Intentional assistance or encouragement can create secondary liability without physical presence at the offence.","C":"Correct. Secondary liability does not require the accessory personally to perform the principal offence or to be present at the scene. Intentional assistance or encouragement, coupled with the required mental element, can found liability.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Intentional assistance or encouragement can create secondary liability without physical presence at the offence.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Encouragement is legally irrelevant unless money changes hands' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Intentional assistance or encouragement can create secondary liability without physical presence at the offence."},"primary_legal_authority":"Accessories and Abettors Act 1861, s 8; common-law principles of secondary liability.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Intentional assistance or encouragement can create secondary liability without physical presence at the offence.","common_trap":"Assuming only the person carrying out the actus reus can be criminally liable.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"12374a54971cc3bf"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0040","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Burden and standard of proof","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"At a criminal trial the prosecution bears the legal burden of proving the elements of the offence. No reverse legal burden or special defence alters the ordinary position. A juror asks what level of certainty the prosecution must achieve before the defendant may be convicted.\n\nWhich answer best states the ordinary criminal standard?","options":{"A":"The prosecution need prove its case only on the balance of probabilities.","B":"The defendant must prove innocence beyond reasonable doubt.","C":"The prosecution succeeds whenever its account is slightly more likely than the defence account.","D":"The prosecution must make the jury sure of the defendant’s guilt.","E":"The prosecution need establish only a reasonable suspicion."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The ordinary criminal standard requires the prosecution to prove guilt so that the tribunal of fact is sure. The civil balance-of-probabilities standard is not the ordinary standard for proving the prosecution case.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The prosecution ordinarily bears the legal burden and must prove guilt so the tribunal is sure.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The prosecution ordinarily bears the legal burden and must prove guilt so the tribunal is sure.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The prosecution ordinarily bears the legal burden and must prove guilt so the tribunal is sure.","D":"Correct. The ordinary criminal standard requires the prosecution to prove guilt so that the tribunal of fact is sure. The civil balance-of-probabilities standard is not the ordinary standard for proving the prosecution case.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The prosecution ordinarily bears the legal burden and must prove guilt so the tribunal is sure."},"primary_legal_authority":"General criminal evidence principles; standard jury direction.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The prosecution ordinarily bears the legal burden and must prove guilt so the tribunal is sure.","common_trap":"Using the civil balance-of-probabilities standard in a criminal trial.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e79757b0bab7c832"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0041","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — preferences","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company is unable to pay its debts. Four months before entering insolvent liquidation, it repays in full a substantial unsecured loan owed to one of its directors, while leaving unrelated unsecured trade creditors unpaid. The director had repeatedly pressed for payment. The liquidator is considering whether the transaction may be challenged as a preference.\n\nWhich additional matter is particularly important to establishing a preference under the Insolvency Act 1986?","options":{"A":"Whether the company desired to put the director into a better position on an insolvent liquidation than the director would otherwise have occupied.","B":"Whether every unrelated creditor consented to the repayment.","C":"Whether the loan agreement was executed as a deed.","D":"Whether the director used the repaid money to buy shares.","E":"Whether the company had ever paid a dividend."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A preference requires, among other matters, that the company was influenced by a desire to produce the preferential effect. For a connected person, the statutory presumptions must also be considered. Mere payment to one creditor before insolvency is not by itself enough.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A preference requires, among other matters, that the company was influenced by a desire to produce the preferential effect. For a connected person, the statutory presumptions must also be considered. Mere payment to one creditor before insolvency is not by itself enough.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A preference involves the statutory preferential effect and the requisite desire, subject to the connected-person rules and other statutory conditions.","C":"Incorrect. This makes consent or agreement determinative when the legal test requires the identified statutory or doctrinal conditions. A preference involves the statutory preferential effect and the requisite desire, subject to the connected-person rules and other statutory conditions.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the director used the repaid money to buy shares' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A preference involves the statutory preferential effect and the requisite desire, subject to the connected-person rules and other statutory conditions.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the company had ever paid a dividend' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A preference involves the statutory preferential effect and the requisite desire, subject to the connected-person rules and other statutory conditions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, s 239.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A preference involves the statutory preferential effect and the requisite desire, subject to the connected-person rules and other statutory conditions.","common_trap":"Assuming every payment to one creditor shortly before insolvency is automatically a preference.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"49414201dd7e5cf4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0042","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Share capital — private company buyback","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solvent private company wants to purchase some of its own ordinary shares from a departing shareholder. The directors assume that because the seller agrees, the company can simply transfer the purchase price and cancel the shares without considering the Companies Act procedures.\n\nWhich is the best advice?","options":{"A":"A company can never purchase its own shares.","B":"Shareholder consent alone removes all statutory restrictions on a buyback.","C":"A private company may purchase its own shares only through the statutory regime, including the applicable requirements concerning authority, funding and the purchase contract.","D":"The shares must always be purchased by another shareholder rather than the company.","E":"A buyback is lawful whenever the directors believe it commercially sensible."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Companies Act permits purchases of own shares but regulates them. The relevant statutory requirements depend on the type of buyback and source of funding. Agreement between company and seller does not displace those requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A company purchase of its own shares must comply with the Companies Act 2006 buyback regime.","B":"Incorrect. This makes consent or agreement determinative when the legal test requires the identified statutory or doctrinal conditions. A company purchase of its own shares must comply with the Companies Act 2006 buyback regime.","C":"Correct. The Companies Act permits purchases of own shares but regulates them. The relevant statutory requirements depend on the type of buyback and source of funding. Agreement between company and seller does not displace those requirements.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A company purchase of its own shares must comply with the Companies Act 2006 buyback regime.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A company purchase of its own shares must comply with the Companies Act 2006 buyback regime."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, Part 18.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A company purchase of its own shares must comply with the Companies Act 2006 buyback regime.","common_trap":"Treating a share buyback as an ordinary bilateral sale free from capital-maintenance rules.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f45fdb1584126e44"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0043","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Corporation tax — allowable expenditure","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trading company calculates its taxable trading profit. It has incurred several expenses. One item is the managing director’s purely private family holiday, which the company paid and recorded as a general business expense. Assume no separate employment-tax issue needs to be considered for this question.\n\nFor computing the company's trading profit, which principle is most directly relevant to whether that private holiday cost is deductible?","options":{"A":"Every expense paid from a company bank account is deductible.","B":"An expense is deductible merely because a director approved it.","C":"The expense must satisfy the applicable rules for deductions, including the requirement that trading expenditure be incurred wholly and exclusively for the purposes of the trade.","D":"Private expenditure is deductible whenever the company is profitable.","E":"Corporation tax does not distinguish business and private expenditure."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Trading-profit deductions are governed by statutory rules, including the wholly-and-exclusively test. Paying a director’s purely private family holiday does not become trading expenditure merely because the company paid it.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Trading expenses generally require a sufficient business purpose, including the wholly-and-exclusively requirement.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'An expense is deductible merely because a director approved it' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Trading expenses generally require a sufficient business purpose, including the wholly-and-exclusively requirement.","C":"Correct. Trading-profit deductions are governed by statutory rules, including the wholly-and-exclusively test. Paying a director’s purely private family holiday does not become trading expenditure merely because the company paid it.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Trading expenses generally require a sufficient business purpose, including the wholly-and-exclusively requirement.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Corporation tax does not distinguish business and private expenditure' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Trading expenses generally require a sufficient business purpose, including the wholly-and-exclusively requirement."},"primary_legal_authority":"Corporation Tax Act 2009, including s 54.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trading expenses generally require a sufficient business purpose, including the wholly-and-exclusively requirement.","common_trap":"Equating payment by the company with deductibility for corporation-tax purposes.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ecb7fea88cb5c32d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0044","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Service of claim form","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant issues a claim form in the courts of England and Wales against a defendant who is to be served within the jurisdiction. No special rule or court order applies. The claimant’s solicitor is planning the timetable and asks how long the claim form normally remains available for service.\n\nWhat is the ordinary CPR position?","options":{"A":"It must always be served on the day of issue.","B":"It must generally be served within four months after the date of issue when service is within the jurisdiction.","C":"It remains valid indefinitely unless the defendant objects.","D":"It must generally be served within 14 days of issue.","E":"It can be served only after particulars of claim have been filed at trial."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Under CPR 7.5, where the claim form is to be served within the jurisdiction, the claimant must complete the relevant step for the chosen method of service before midnight on the calendar day four months after issue, subject to applicable rules and any valid extension.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Ordinary claim-form service within the jurisdiction is subject to the CPR 7.5 four-month period.","B":"Correct. Under CPR 7.5, where the claim form is to be served within the jurisdiction, the claimant must complete the relevant step for the chosen method of service before midnight on the calendar day four months after issue, subject to applicable rules and any valid extension.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It remains valid indefinitely unless the defendant objects' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Ordinary claim-form service within the jurisdiction is subject to the CPR 7.5 four-month period.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Ordinary claim-form service within the jurisdiction is subject to the CPR 7.5 four-month period.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Ordinary claim-form service within the jurisdiction is subject to the CPR 7.5 four-month period."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 7.5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Ordinary claim-form service within the jurisdiction is subject to the CPR 7.5 four-month period.","common_trap":"Confusing the claim-form validity period with shorter deadlines applicable to other procedural documents.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c765f65a406a0f22"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0045","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Interim injunctions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A technology company alleges that a former distributor is about to disclose confidential information. Damages may be difficult to quantify, and the company seeks an interim injunction pending trial. There is a serious issue to be tried. The court turns to the conventional principles governing whether interim injunctive relief should be granted.\n\nWhich consideration forms part of the conventional approach?","options":{"A":"The claimant must prove the entire case finally at the interim hearing.","B":"The court ignores whether damages would be an adequate remedy.","C":"The court considers matters including adequacy of damages and the balance of convenience after identifying a serious issue to be tried.","D":"An interim injunction is available only after final judgment.","E":"The claimant automatically succeeds whenever confidentiality is alleged."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The American Cyanamid approach asks whether there is a serious issue to be tried and then considers, among other matters, adequacy of damages and the balance of convenience. The interim hearing is not ordinarily a full trial of the merits.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Interim injunction analysis ordinarily includes serious issue, adequacy of damages and balance of convenience.","B":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Interim injunction analysis ordinarily includes serious issue, adequacy of damages and balance of convenience.","C":"Correct. The American Cyanamid approach asks whether there is a serious issue to be tried and then considers, among other matters, adequacy of damages and the balance of convenience. The interim hearing is not ordinarily a full trial of the merits.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Interim injunction analysis ordinarily includes serious issue, adequacy of damages and balance of convenience.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Interim injunction analysis ordinarily includes serious issue, adequacy of damages and balance of convenience."},"primary_legal_authority":"American Cyanamid Co v Ethicon Ltd; CPR Part 25.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Interim injunction analysis ordinarily includes serious issue, adequacy of damages and balance of convenience.","common_trap":"Treating the interim application as requiring final proof of the claim.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ebca750e2a87f504"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0046","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Costs — general rule","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After a multi-track commercial trial, the claimant succeeds on the principal issue. There is no contractual costs clause that dictates the result and no special costs regime displaces the CPR. The parties ask what starting point the court will use when deciding costs.\n\nWhat is the general rule?","options":{"A":"Each party always bears its own costs.","B":"The unsuccessful party will generally be ordered to pay the costs of the successful party, although the court retains discretion and considers all the circumstances.","C":"The successful party must always pay the unsuccessful party’s costs.","D":"Costs can never be awarded in civil litigation.","E":"Costs follow whichever party issued proceedings first."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"CPR 44.2 states the general rule that the unsuccessful party will be ordered to pay the successful party’s costs, but the court may make a different order and must consider all the circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Costs generally follow the event, subject to the court's broad discretion.","B":"Correct. CPR 44.2 states the general rule that the unsuccessful party will be ordered to pay the successful party’s costs, but the court may make a different order and must consider all the circumstances.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Costs generally follow the event, subject to the court's broad discretion.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Costs generally follow the event, subject to the court's broad discretion.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Costs follow whichever party issued proceedings first' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Costs generally follow the event, subject to the court's broad discretion."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 44.2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Costs generally follow the event, subject to the court's broad discretion.","common_trap":"Turning the general rule into an inflexible entitlement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9ee4cd6a4907bae1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0047","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Client confidentiality and disclosure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor holds confidential information obtained from a former client. A new client would find that information commercially useful in negotiating against the former client. The information is not generally known and no legal or regulatory exception requiring disclosure applies. The former client has not consented.\n\nWhich is the best statement of the solicitor’s continuing duty?","options":{"A":"Confidentiality ends automatically when the retainer ends.","B":"The solicitor may disclose the information if the new client offers to pay for it.","C":"The solicitor may disclose anything that is commercially useful to a current client.","D":"The duty of confidentiality continues after the retainer, subject to consent or a legal or regulatory basis for disclosure.","E":"Former-client information is confidential only for one year."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The SRA Code requires solicitors to keep the affairs of current and former clients confidential unless disclosure is required or permitted by law or the client consents. Termination of the retainer does not itself end confidentiality.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client confidentiality generally survives the end of the retainer.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The solicitor may disclose the information if the new client offers to pay for it' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Client confidentiality generally survives the end of the retainer.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The solicitor may disclose anything that is commercially useful to a current client' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Client confidentiality generally survives the end of the retainer.","D":"Correct. The SRA Code requires solicitors to keep the affairs of current and former clients confidential unless disclosure is required or permitted by law or the client consents. Termination of the retainer does not itself end confidentiality.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client confidentiality generally survives the end of the retainer."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, RELs, RFLs and RSLs, para 6.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client confidentiality generally survives the end of the retainer.","common_trap":"Assuming duties to former clients disappear once the matter closes.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0d5442e93f9bd99d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0048","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Contract terms — conditions and warranties","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contract expressly classifies a particular obligation as a condition. The promisor commits a breach of that condition. The innocent party has not affirmed the contract and no statutory rule changes the contractual classification.\n\nWhat remedy is ordinarily available because of the classification as a condition?","options":{"A":"The innocent party may ordinarily elect to terminate for the breach and claim damages, subject to the applicable rules.","B":"The innocent party can obtain only nominal damages and can never terminate.","C":"The contract automatically becomes void from the beginning.","D":"The breach is legally irrelevant unless it causes personal injury.","E":"The innocent party automatically acquires ownership of the promisor’s assets."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Breach of a contractual condition ordinarily entitles the innocent party to terminate (more precisely, treat itself as discharged from future performance) and claim damages, subject to affirmation and other relevant doctrines.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Breach of a contractual condition ordinarily entitles the innocent party to terminate (more precisely, treat itself as discharged from future performance) and claim damages, subject to affirmation and other relevant doctrines.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Breach of condition ordinarily gives a right to terminate as well as claim damages.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Breach of condition ordinarily gives a right to terminate as well as claim damages.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The breach is legally irrelevant unless it causes personal injury' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Breach of condition ordinarily gives a right to terminate as well as claim damages.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Breach of condition ordinarily gives a right to terminate as well as claim damages."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law classification of contractual terms.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Breach of condition ordinarily gives a right to terminate as well as claim damages.","common_trap":"Confusing a condition with a warranty, breach of which ordinarily sounds only in damages.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"06da76cb4a1252b8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0049","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Remoteness of contractual damages","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A courier breaches a delivery contract. The ordinary consequence of late delivery was foreseeable, but the customer also suffers an exceptionally large loss because of a highly unusual onward transaction. The customer never communicated that special transaction or its risk to the courier when the contract was made.\n\nWhich principle is most directly relevant to whether the exceptional loss is recoverable?","options":{"A":"All losses factually caused by a breach are automatically recoverable.","B":"Contract damages are never available for financial loss.","C":"The remoteness rules ask whether the loss arose naturally in the usual course or was within the parties’ reasonable contemplation because relevant special circumstances were known when contracting.","D":"Only losses expressly quantified in the written contract are recoverable.","E":"Remoteness is relevant only in tort."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The contractual remoteness rule associated with Hadley v Baxendale distinguishes losses arising naturally from those dependent on special circumstances communicated or otherwise within the parties’ contemplation at formation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contractual damages are limited by the remoteness principles concerning ordinary and communicated special losses.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contractual damages are limited by the remoteness principles concerning ordinary and communicated special losses.","C":"Correct. The contractual remoteness rule associated with Hadley v Baxendale distinguishes losses arising naturally from those dependent on special circumstances communicated or otherwise within the parties’ contemplation at formation.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contractual damages are limited by the remoteness principles concerning ordinary and communicated special losses.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contractual damages are limited by the remoteness principles concerning ordinary and communicated special losses."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hadley v Baxendale; subsequent contractual-remoteness authorities.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contractual damages are limited by the remoteness principles concerning ordinary and communicated special losses.","common_trap":"Assuming factual causation alone establishes recoverability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"71f24b46e800e734"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0050","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Exclusion clauses — incorporation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A customer pays for parking at an automated barrier. Only after payment does a ticket emerge containing, for the first time, a wide exclusion clause. There was no earlier notice of the clause and no established course of dealing between the parties.\n\nWhat is the principal issue with relying on the clause?","options":{"A":"An exclusion clause can never form part of a contract.","B":"The clause may not have been incorporated because reasonable notice was not given before or at the time the contract was made.","C":"All terms printed on tickets are automatically incorporated.","D":"The clause is valid only if handwritten.","E":"Incorporation depends solely on whether the customer actually read the clause after contracting."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A party relying on an unsigned contractual term ordinarily needs to give reasonable notice of it before or at formation. Notice first supplied after the contract is concluded is generally too late to incorporate the term through notice.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Reasonable notice of an unsigned term ordinarily must be given before or at contract formation.","B":"Correct. A party relying on an unsigned contractual term ordinarily needs to give reasonable notice of it before or at formation. Notice first supplied after the contract is concluded is generally too late to incorporate the term through notice.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Reasonable notice of an unsigned term ordinarily must be given before or at contract formation.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Reasonable notice of an unsigned term ordinarily must be given before or at contract formation.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Incorporation depends solely on whether the customer actually read the clause after contracting' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Reasonable notice of an unsigned term ordinarily must be given before or at contract formation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law incorporation principles, including Olley v Marlborough Court and Thornton v Shoe Lane Parking.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Reasonable notice of an unsigned term ordinarily must be given before or at contract formation.","common_trap":"Assuming any term printed on a receipt or ticket is necessarily incorporated.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6b87ad32ba016cd0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0051","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — factual causation","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A patient alleges that a professional’s negligent omission caused a particular physical injury. Expert evidence establishes that the injury would have occurred at the same time and to the same extent even if the professional had exercised reasonable care.\n\nUnder the ordinary factual-causation test, what is the likely consequence?","options":{"A":"Factual causation is automatically established whenever breach is proved.","B":"The claimant ordinarily fails to establish that the breach caused the injury because the injury would have occurred in any event.","C":"The defendant is strictly liable because the claimant suffered injury.","D":"Causation is irrelevant in negligence.","E":"The burden automatically shifts permanently to the defendant in every negligence case."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The ordinary 'but for' inquiry asks whether the damage would have occurred but for the defendant’s breach. If it would have occurred in the same way regardless, ordinary factual causation is not established, subject to exceptional causation doctrines not engaged on these facts.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Ordinary negligence requires factual causation, commonly assessed using the but-for test.","B":"Correct. The ordinary 'but for' inquiry asks whether the damage would have occurred but for the defendant’s breach. If it would have occurred in the same way regardless, ordinary factual causation is not established, subject to exceptional causation doctrines not engaged on these facts.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The defendant is strictly liable because the claimant suffered injury' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Ordinary negligence requires factual causation, commonly assessed using the but-for test.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Causation is irrelevant in negligence' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Ordinary negligence requires factual causation, commonly assessed using the but-for test.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Ordinary negligence requires factual causation, commonly assessed using the but-for test."},"primary_legal_authority":"Barnett v Chelsea and Kensington Hospital Management Committee; general negligence principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Ordinary negligence requires factual causation, commonly assessed using the but-for test.","common_trap":"Assuming proof of breach plus damage automatically proves causation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c8ad439077a9ef79"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0052","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Vicarious liability — employee relationship","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A delivery company employs a driver under a contract of employment. During an assigned delivery route, while performing the task entrusted to him, the driver negligently injures a pedestrian. There is no suggestion that the driver had embarked on an independent personal venture.\n\nWhich doctrine is most directly relevant to the company’s potential liability for the driver’s tort?","options":{"A":"Contributory negligence.","B":"Rylands v Fletcher.","C":"Vicarious liability, including the requirement for the appropriate relationship and a sufficient connection between the employment and the tort.","D":"Private nuisance only.","E":"Frustration."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"An employer may be vicariously liable for torts committed by an employee where the required relationship exists and the tort is sufficiently connected with the employment. A negligent act during the assigned delivery task is a conventional context for the doctrine.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Contributory negligence' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Vicarious liability can make an employer liable for an employee’s tort sufficiently connected with employment.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Rylands v Fletcher' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Vicarious liability can make an employer liable for an employee’s tort sufficiently connected with employment.","C":"Correct. An employer may be vicariously liable for torts committed by an employee where the required relationship exists and the tort is sufficiently connected with the employment. A negligent act during the assigned delivery task is a conventional context for the doctrine.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Private nuisance only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Vicarious liability can make an employer liable for an employee’s tort sufficiently connected with employment.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Vicarious liability can make an employer liable for an employee’s tort sufficiently connected with employment."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law principles of vicarious liability.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Vicarious liability can make an employer liable for an employee’s tort sufficiently connected with employment.","common_trap":"Assuming an employer is liable for every act of an employee, regardless of connection to employment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e797502e323eba9b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0053","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Statutory interpretation — precedent","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor researching a statutory provision finds a Supreme Court decision that directly determines the same point of statutory interpretation. The material facts and legislation are materially unchanged. The solicitor is advising on proceedings in the High Court.\n\nHow should the High Court ordinarily treat the Supreme Court’s relevant ratio decidendi?","options":{"A":"As binding precedent, subject to any legally relevant distinction or subsequent change.","B":"As merely persuasive because all courts have equal authority.","C":"As irrelevant unless Parliament later approves it.","D":"As binding only if the parties in the new case are the same.","E":"As optional whenever the High Court judge disagrees with it."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Under the doctrine of precedent, the ratio of a relevant Supreme Court decision binds lower courts, including the High Court, absent a valid distinction, overruling development or relevant legislative change.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Under the doctrine of precedent, the ratio of a relevant Supreme Court decision binds lower courts, including the High Court, absent a valid distinction, overruling development or relevant legislative change.","B":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Lower courts are bound by applicable ratios of higher courts in the hierarchy.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'As irrelevant unless Parliament later approves it' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Lower courts are bound by applicable ratios of higher courts in the hierarchy.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Lower courts are bound by applicable ratios of higher courts in the hierarchy.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Lower courts are bound by applicable ratios of higher courts in the hierarchy."},"primary_legal_authority":"Doctrine of judicial precedent and the court hierarchy of England and Wales.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Lower courts are bound by applicable ratios of higher courts in the hierarchy.","common_trap":"Confusing binding precedent with persuasive authority.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f36f6ddefd5421d2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0054","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — standing","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An environmental association seeks judicial review of a public authority’s decision affecting a local river. The association has worked on that river for many years, represents affected local users and participated in the authority’s consultation. The defendant argues that only a person who owns riverside land can ever bring judicial review.\n\nWhich test governs whether the association has standing?","options":{"A":"Whether it owns freehold land affected by the decision.","B":"Whether it can prove a private-law cause of action in contract.","C":"Whether it has a sufficient interest in the matter to which the application relates.","D":"Whether every member suffered financial loss.","E":"Whether the public authority consents to the claim."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The statutory standing test for judicial review is whether the applicant has a sufficient interest in the matter. The assessment is contextual and is not confined to ownership of property or private-law rights.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether it owns freehold land affected by the decision' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Judicial-review standing turns on sufficient interest.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether it can prove a private-law cause of action in contract' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Judicial-review standing turns on sufficient interest.","C":"Correct. The statutory standing test for judicial review is whether the applicant has a sufficient interest in the matter. The assessment is contextual and is not confined to ownership of property or private-law rights.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Judicial-review standing turns on sufficient interest.","E":"Incorrect. This makes consent or agreement determinative when the legal test requires the identified statutory or doctrinal conditions. Judicial-review standing turns on sufficient interest."},"primary_legal_authority":"Senior Courts Act 1981, s 31(3).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Judicial-review standing turns on sufficient interest.","common_trap":"Importing private-law title requirements into public-law standing.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a6ea1a246ec650e3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0055","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Intestacy — spouse and issue","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person domiciled in England and Wales dies wholly intestate leaving a surviving spouse and two adult children. The estate exceeds the statutory legacy. Assume the monetary amount of the statutory legacy is supplied separately and no special complication applies.\n\nWhich description best captures the structure of the spouse’s entitlement under the intestacy rules?","options":{"A":"The spouse receives nothing because descendants survive.","B":"The spouse receives the entire estate in every intestacy.","C":"The spouse receives only personal chattels and no financial entitlement.","D":"The spouse ordinarily receives the personal chattels, the statutory legacy and one half of the remaining residuary estate absolutely, with the other half passing under the statutory trusts for issue.","E":"The spouse and children automatically take equal shares of every individual estate asset."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Where a spouse/civil partner and issue survive, the statutory scheme gives the spouse personal chattels, the fixed statutory legacy and half the residue absolutely, while the remaining half is held for issue under the statutory trusts. The relevant monetary threshold is supplied where required in SQE tax/threshold-style questions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The spouse receives nothing because descendants survive' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Intestacy with spouse/civil partner and issue divides the estate according to the statutory scheme rather than simple equal shares.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Intestacy with spouse/civil partner and issue divides the estate according to the statutory scheme rather than simple equal shares.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Intestacy with spouse/civil partner and issue divides the estate according to the statutory scheme rather than simple equal shares.","D":"Correct. Where a spouse/civil partner and issue survive, the statutory scheme gives the spouse personal chattels, the fixed statutory legacy and half the residue absolutely, while the remaining half is held for issue under the statutory trusts. The relevant monetary threshold is supplied where required in SQE tax/threshold-style questions.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Intestacy with spouse/civil partner and issue divides the estate according to the statutory scheme rather than simple equal shares."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925, s 46, as amended.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Intestacy with spouse/civil partner and issue divides the estate according to the statutory scheme rather than simple equal shares.","common_trap":"Assuming either that the spouse takes everything or that spouse and children divide all assets equally.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9f249692e2968ccc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0056","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Inheritance tax — potentially exempt transfers","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual makes an outright lifetime cash gift to an adult child. The gift is not to a trust, the donor retains no benefit in the money and no special exemption needs to be quantified for this question.\n\nFor inheritance-tax purposes, how is such a lifetime transfer ordinarily characterised?","options":{"A":"As a potentially exempt transfer, so its ultimate IHT treatment can depend on whether the donor survives the relevant period.","B":"As immediately chargeable in the same way as every transfer into a discretionary trust.","C":"As outside inheritance tax under all circumstances.","D":"As corporation-tax expenditure.","E":"As a transfer that is effective only if made by deed."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An outright lifetime transfer by an individual to another individual is ordinarily a potentially exempt transfer. If the donor survives the relevant period it becomes exempt; if not, it is brought into the death-transfer calculation subject to the applicable rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An outright lifetime transfer by an individual to another individual is ordinarily a potentially exempt transfer. If the donor survives the relevant period it becomes exempt; if not, it is brought into the death-transfer calculation subject to the applicable rules.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Many outright lifetime gifts from one individual to another are PETs rather than immediately chargeable lifetime transfers.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'As outside inheritance tax under all circumstances' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Many outright lifetime gifts from one individual to another are PETs rather than immediately chargeable lifetime transfers.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'As corporation-tax expenditure' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Many outright lifetime gifts from one individual to another are PETs rather than immediately chargeable lifetime transfers.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Many outright lifetime gifts from one individual to another are PETs rather than immediately chargeable lifetime transfers."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, including s 3A.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Many outright lifetime gifts from one individual to another are PETs rather than immediately chargeable lifetime transfers.","common_trap":"Treating all lifetime gifts alike for IHT purposes.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e13c9e917b49285c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0057","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Inheritance Act 1975 claims","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A deceased person’s adult child receives nothing under the will and is considering a claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975. The child assumes that being a child of the deceased automatically guarantees a fixed percentage of the estate.\n\nWhich is the best advice?","options":{"A":"Every adult child automatically receives one half of the estate.","B":"The Act is relevant only to surviving spouses.","C":"Eligibility to apply does not itself guarantee an award; the court considers whether the disposition of the estate fails to make the applicable reasonable financial provision and applies the statutory factors.","D":"An eligible claimant always defeats the will completely.","E":"The court cannot consider the claimant’s financial resources or needs."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Act identifies classes of eligible applicants, including children, but eligibility is only the gateway. The court then applies the statutory reasonable-financial-provision test and relevant factors. There is no automatic fixed share for an adult child.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Eligibility under the 1975 Act does not equal entitlement; reasonable financial provision and statutory factors must be assessed.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Eligibility under the 1975 Act does not equal entitlement; reasonable financial provision and statutory factors must be assessed.","C":"Correct. The Act identifies classes of eligible applicants, including children, but eligibility is only the gateway. The court then applies the statutory reasonable-financial-provision test and relevant factors. There is no automatic fixed share for an adult child.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Eligibility under the 1975 Act does not equal entitlement; reasonable financial provision and statutory factors must be assessed.","E":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Eligibility under the 1975 Act does not equal entitlement; reasonable financial provision and statutory factors must be assessed."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975, ss 1–3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Eligibility under the 1975 Act does not equal entitlement; reasonable financial provision and statutory factors must be assessed.","common_trap":"Treating family-provision legislation as a forced-heirship regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0c224cbd478bd86e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0058","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Constitution of trusts — Re Rose","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A donor intends to make an outright gift of registered shares. The donor executes the appropriate transfer form and delivers it, together with the share certificate, to the donee, having done everything within the donor’s own power required to transfer title. Registration in the company’s register remains outstanding.\n\nWhich equitable principle may be relevant to whether the gift is effective in equity before legal registration?","options":{"A":"The rule that equity will never recognise an incompletely transferred gift under any circumstances.","B":"The principle associated with Re Rose, under which equity may treat the transfer as effective where the donor has done everything necessary within the donor’s power to transfer the property.","C":"The rule against perpetuities only.","D":"The doctrine of frustration.","E":"The criminal law doctrine of attempt."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Re Rose principle is an exception to the general rule that equity will not perfect an imperfect gift. Where the donor has done everything necessary within their own power to effect the transfer, equity may recognise the beneficial transfer even though registration by a third party remains outstanding.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Re Rose may perfect an intended transfer in equity once the donor has done everything necessary within their own power.","B":"Correct. The Re Rose principle is an exception to the general rule that equity will not perfect an imperfect gift. Where the donor has done everything necessary within their own power to effect the transfer, equity may recognise the beneficial transfer even though registration by a third party remains outstanding.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The rule against perpetuities only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Re Rose may perfect an intended transfer in equity once the donor has done everything necessary within their own power.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The doctrine of frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Re Rose may perfect an intended transfer in equity once the donor has done everything necessary within their own power.","E":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Re Rose may perfect an intended transfer in equity once the donor has done everything necessary within their own power."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Rose [1952] Ch 499; 2026 FLK expressly includes the Re Rose exception.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Re Rose may perfect an intended transfer in equity once the donor has done everything necessary within their own power.","common_trap":"Applying the maxim that equity will not assist a volunteer as an exceptionless rule.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a56fe5cfda3f84a6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0059","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Dishonest assistance","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee deliberately misapplies trust money. An outsider, knowing facts that make the conduct dishonest by the applicable objective standard, actively helps the trustee route the money through a series of accounts. The outsider never receives any of the trust property beneficially.\n\nWhich equitable liability is most directly engaged in relation to the outsider?","options":{"A":"Knowing receipt, which always requires no receipt of trust property.","B":"Dishonest assistance, because the outsider has assisted a breach of trust with the requisite dishonesty.","C":"Resulting trust solely because the outsider opened a bank account.","D":"Proprietary estoppel.","E":"Ademption."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Dishonest assistance concerns assistance in a breach of trust or fiduciary duty accompanied by the requisite dishonesty. Unlike knowing receipt, it does not depend on the defendant beneficially receiving trust property.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Dishonest assistance requires assistance in the relevant breach plus dishonesty; beneficial receipt is not its defining requirement.","B":"Correct. Dishonest assistance concerns assistance in a breach of trust or fiduciary duty accompanied by the requisite dishonesty. Unlike knowing receipt, it does not depend on the defendant beneficially receiving trust property.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Resulting trust solely because the outsider opened a bank account' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Dishonest assistance requires assistance in the relevant breach plus dishonesty; beneficial receipt is not its defining requirement.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Proprietary estoppel' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Dishonest assistance requires assistance in the relevant breach plus dishonesty; beneficial receipt is not its defining requirement.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Ademption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Dishonest assistance requires assistance in the relevant breach plus dishonesty; beneficial receipt is not its defining requirement."},"primary_legal_authority":"Equitable principles of dishonest assistance; Royal Brunei Airlines v Tan; Ivey v Genting Casinos as relevant to dishonesty.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Dishonest assistance requires assistance in the relevant breach plus dishonesty; beneficial receipt is not its defining requirement.","common_trap":"Confusing dishonest assistance with knowing receipt.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"72b59a0f255b4756"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0060","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Easements — characteristics","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"The owner of House A claims an easement over neighbouring House B. The claimed right benefits the use of House A, the two properties have different owners, the right is sufficiently definite, and it is of a kind capable of forming the subject matter of a grant.\n\nWhich classic requirement for an easement is illustrated by the fact that the right benefits House A rather than merely conferring a personal advantage on its current owner?","options":{"A":"There must be dominant and servient tenements and the right must accommodate the dominant tenement.","B":"The dominant and servient land must always have the same owner.","C":"An easement must give exclusive possession of the servient land.","D":"Every easement must require payment of rent.","E":"An easement can exist only for rights of way."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The classic characteristics include identifiable dominant and servient tenements and a right that accommodates the dominant tenement, meaning it benefits the land rather than merely its owner personally. The tenements must also be in different ownership/occupation and the right must be capable of forming the subject matter of a grant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The classic characteristics include identifiable dominant and servient tenements and a right that accommodates the dominant tenement, meaning it benefits the land rather than merely its owner personally. The tenements must also be in different ownership/occupation and the right must be capable of forming the subject matter of a grant.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An easement must accommodate the dominant tenement rather than merely provide a personal benefit.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An easement must accommodate the dominant tenement rather than merely provide a personal benefit.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An easement must accommodate the dominant tenement rather than merely provide a personal benefit.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An easement must accommodate the dominant tenement rather than merely provide a personal benefit."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Ellenborough Park [1956] Ch 131.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An easement must accommodate the dominant tenement rather than merely provide a personal benefit.","common_trap":"Treating any useful personal privilege over another’s land as an easement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cf37f4219200f24a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0061","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Pre-contract searches — local authority search","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer is purchasing a registered freehold house with mortgage finance. The buyer’s solicitor is deciding which pre-contract investigations are relevant. The client asks whether registration of title makes local authority searches unnecessary.\n\nWhat is the best response?","options":{"A":"Yes. The register contains every matter that a local authority search could reveal.","B":"No. Title investigation and pre-contract searches perform different functions; a local authority search can reveal matters such as relevant local land charges and other local-authority information affecting the property.","C":"Yes, unless the seller is a company.","D":"No, but only because a local authority search proves the physical boundaries conclusively.","E":"Yes, because searches are required only for unregistered land."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Registered title investigation does not replace searches. Local authority searches and enquiries investigate matters arising from public records and local authority functions that are not simply reproduced as registered proprietary title entries.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Pre-contract searches supplement rather than duplicate investigation of registered title.","B":"Correct. Registered title investigation does not replace searches. Local authority searches and enquiries investigate matters arising from public records and local authority functions that are not simply reproduced as registered proprietary title entries.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Yes, unless the seller is a company' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Pre-contract searches supplement rather than duplicate investigation of registered title.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Pre-contract searches supplement rather than duplicate investigation of registered title.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Pre-contract searches supplement rather than duplicate investigation of registered title."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA FLK2 Property Law and Practice specification; standard conveyancing practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Pre-contract searches supplement rather than duplicate investigation of registered title.","common_trap":"Assuming registered title contains all planning, roads and local-authority information relevant to acquisition.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"74010d9799225b7e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0062","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Mortgages — priority","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer acquires registered land using a bank loan secured by a legal charge that is completed by registration. The buyer later grants another registrable legal charge to a second lender. Both transactions are properly registered in their intended sequence and no special priority agreement applies.\n\nWhat is the ordinary priority position between the registered charges?","options":{"A":"The later charge always takes priority because it is more recent.","B":"The charges automatically rank equally.","C":"Priority is ordinarily governed by the registered-land priority rules, so the earlier registered charge will generally rank ahead of the later registered charge.","D":"Priority depends solely on which lender charges the higher interest rate.","E":"A registered charge can never have priority over another interest."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Priority in registered land is governed by the Land Registration Act regime and the order/effect of registered dispositions. In the ordinary sequential mortgage scenario, the first registered legal charge ranks ahead of a later registered charge absent an agreement or special rule altering priority.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Registered charges ordinarily rank according to the registered-land priority regime, with an earlier charge generally ahead of a later one.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Registered charges ordinarily rank according to the registered-land priority regime, with an earlier charge generally ahead of a later one.","C":"Correct. Priority in registered land is governed by the Land Registration Act regime and the order/effect of registered dispositions. In the ordinary sequential mortgage scenario, the first registered legal charge ranks ahead of a later registered charge absent an agreement or special rule altering priority.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Priority depends solely on which lender charges the higher interest rate' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Registered charges ordinarily rank according to the registered-land priority regime, with an earlier charge generally ahead of a later one.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Registered charges ordinarily rank according to the registered-land priority regime, with an earlier charge generally ahead of a later one."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, including ss 28–29 and registration rules.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Registered charges ordinarily rank according to the registered-land priority regime, with an earlier charge generally ahead of a later one.","common_trap":"Assuming later lending displaces an existing registered mortgage.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ee347cb04e8d4c54"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0063","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Delayed completion — contractual remedies","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A residential sale contract incorporates the Standard Conditions of Sale. The contractual completion date arrives, but the buyer is not ready to complete. The seller is ready, willing and able to complete and wants to preserve the contract while requiring completion in accordance with the contractual machinery.\n\nWhich step is commonly relevant under the standard contractual regime?","options":{"A":"Serving a notice to complete in accordance with the contract, making time of the essence under the applicable standard conditions.","B":"Treating the buyer as the registered proprietor automatically.","C":"Applying for a criminal conviction against the buyer.","D":"Changing the purchase price unilaterally without contractual authority.","E":"Registering the seller as the buyer’s trustee without notice."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Under the Standard Conditions of Sale, where the relevant requirements are met after failure to complete on the contractual date, a party ready, able and willing to complete may serve a notice to complete. The contractual consequences include making time of the essence in accordance with the conditions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Under the Standard Conditions of Sale, where the relevant requirements are met after failure to complete on the contractual date, a party ready, able and willing to complete may serve a notice to complete. The contractual consequences include making time of the essence in accordance with the conditions.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Notice to complete is a central contractual mechanism following delayed completion under the standard conditions.","C":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Notice to complete is a central contractual mechanism following delayed completion under the standard conditions.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Changing the purchase price unilaterally without contractual authority' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Notice to complete is a central contractual mechanism following delayed completion under the standard conditions.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Registering the seller as the buyer’s trustee without notice' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Notice to complete is a central contractual mechanism following delayed completion under the standard conditions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Standard Conditions of Sale; SRA FLK2 completion/remedies syllabus.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Notice to complete is a central contractual mechanism following delayed completion under the standard conditions.","common_trap":"Assuming any delay automatically terminates the contract without use of the contractual machinery.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ec144ce8ff3be16e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0064","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"SDLT — chargeable consideration","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer acquires an English freehold for a cash price. The transaction is within the SDLT regime and no relief applies. The question supplies all monetary thresholds and rates needed for any calculation.\n\nWhat is the basic conceptual starting point for calculating SDLT on the acquisition?","options":{"A":"SDLT is based only on the seller’s capital gain.","B":"The buyer considers the chargeable consideration for the land transaction and applies the relevant SDLT rules, rates and thresholds supplied or applicable to the transaction.","C":"SDLT is a yearly tax on occupation after completion.","D":"SDLT is payable only by companies.","E":"SDLT and inheritance tax are the same tax."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"SDLT is a transaction tax on land transactions in England and Northern Ireland. The computation starts from chargeable consideration and applies the statutory regime, including rates, thresholds and any reliefs. Under the SQE specification, required monetary rates or thresholds are provided.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. SDLT liability is transaction-based and calculated by reference to chargeable consideration under the statutory regime.","B":"Correct. SDLT is a transaction tax on land transactions in England and Northern Ireland. The computation starts from chargeable consideration and applies the statutory regime, including rates, thresholds and any reliefs. Under the SQE specification, required monetary rates or thresholds are provided.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'SDLT is a yearly tax on occupation after completion' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. SDLT liability is transaction-based and calculated by reference to chargeable consideration under the statutory regime.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. SDLT liability is transaction-based and calculated by reference to chargeable consideration under the statutory regime.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'SDLT and inheritance tax are the same tax' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. SDLT liability is transaction-based and calculated by reference to chargeable consideration under the statutory regime."},"primary_legal_authority":"Finance Act 2003; SRA September 2026 taxation guidance.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"SDLT liability is transaction-based and calculated by reference to chargeable consideration under the statutory regime.","common_trap":"Confusing SDLT with CGT, IHT or a recurring property tax.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5a776ae469ad6357"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0065","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Self-defence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person is attacked in the street and honestly believes that immediate defensive force is necessary. He uses force against the attacker. At trial, the issue is whether the degree of force used was lawful.\n\nWhich is the central question when assessing the degree of force for self-defence?","options":{"A":"Whether the defendant used exactly the same type of force as the attacker.","B":"Whether the defendant had any opportunity to call a solicitor first.","C":"Whether the force used was reasonable in the circumstances as the defendant believed them to be, applying the statutory and common-law principles.","D":"Whether the attacker was later convicted.","E":"Whether the defendant suffered a financial loss."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Self-defence requires consideration of the defendant’s belief as to the circumstances and whether the force used was reasonable in those circumstances. The law does not require mechanical equivalence between the force threatened and force used.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the defendant used exactly the same type of force as the attacker' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Defensive force must be reasonable in the circumstances as the defendant believed them to be.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the defendant had any opportunity to call a solicitor first' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Defensive force must be reasonable in the circumstances as the defendant believed them to be.","C":"Correct. Self-defence requires consideration of the defendant’s belief as to the circumstances and whether the force used was reasonable in those circumstances. The law does not require mechanical equivalence between the force threatened and force used.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the attacker was later convicted' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Defensive force must be reasonable in the circumstances as the defendant believed them to be.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the defendant suffered a financial loss' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Defensive force must be reasonable in the circumstances as the defendant believed them to be."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Justice and Immigration Act 2008, s 76; common-law self-defence.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Defensive force must be reasonable in the circumstances as the defendant believed them to be.","common_trap":"Assuming lawful defensive force must precisely match the attacker’s force.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d96ee90609758c7f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0066","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Intoxication","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant voluntarily drinks a large quantity of alcohol and is charged with an offence of specific intent. The evidence raises a genuine issue as to whether, because of the intoxication, the defendant actually formed the specific intent required for that offence.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the relevance of voluntary intoxication?","options":{"A":"Voluntary intoxication automatically acquits a defendant of every offence.","B":"It is never relevant to mens rea.","C":"For a specific-intent offence, voluntary intoxication may be relevant to whether the prosecution proves the required specific intent, although liability for another basic-intent offence may still need consideration where applicable.","D":"Intoxication converts every offence into negligence.","E":"Drinking alcohol creates a complete defence even where the required intent was actually formed."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Voluntary intoxication can be relevant to whether the prosecution establishes the mens rea of a specific-intent offence. It is not a free-standing universal defence, and different principles apply to basic-intent offences.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Voluntary intoxication may prevent proof of specific intent if that intent was not actually formed; it is not a general excuse.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Voluntary intoxication may prevent proof of specific intent if that intent was not actually formed; it is not a general excuse.","C":"Correct. Voluntary intoxication can be relevant to whether the prosecution establishes the mens rea of a specific-intent offence. It is not a free-standing universal defence, and different principles apply to basic-intent offences.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Voluntary intoxication may prevent proof of specific intent if that intent was not actually formed; it is not a general excuse.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Drinking alcohol creates a complete defence even where the required intent was actually formed' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Voluntary intoxication may prevent proof of specific intent if that intent was not actually formed; it is not a general excuse."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law intoxication principles, including DPP v Majewski.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Voluntary intoxication may prevent proof of specific intent if that intent was not actually formed; it is not a general excuse.","common_trap":"Treating intoxication either as always irrelevant or as an automatic complete defence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"64c3678b66f97089"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0067","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Mode of trial — either-way offences","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An adult defendant appears before magistrates charged with an either-way offence and indicates a not-guilty plea. The case proceeds to allocation. The magistrates conclude after considering the relevant matters that their sentencing powers are sufficient and accept jurisdiction.\n\nWhat procedural choice ordinarily remains for the defendant?","options":{"A":"None: acceptance of jurisdiction by magistrates automatically fixes trial in the magistrates’ court.","B":"The defendant may ordinarily elect trial in the Crown Court.","C":"The defendant may require the case to be tried in the Supreme Court.","D":"The defendant may demand that the prosecution discontinue.","E":"The defendant may convert the charge into a civil claim."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"For an adult charged with an either-way offence, if the magistrates accept jurisdiction after allocation, the defendant ordinarily retains the right to elect Crown Court trial.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. In an either-way case, magistrates’ acceptance of jurisdiction does not ordinarily remove the adult defendant’s election for Crown Court trial.","B":"Correct. For an adult charged with an either-way offence, if the magistrates accept jurisdiction after allocation, the defendant ordinarily retains the right to elect Crown Court trial.","C":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. In an either-way case, magistrates’ acceptance of jurisdiction does not ordinarily remove the adult defendant’s election for Crown Court trial.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The defendant may demand that the prosecution discontinue' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. In an either-way case, magistrates’ acceptance of jurisdiction does not ordinarily remove the adult defendant’s election for Crown Court trial.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The defendant may convert the charge into a civil claim' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. In an either-way case, magistrates’ acceptance of jurisdiction does not ordinarily remove the adult defendant’s election for Crown Court trial."},"primary_legal_authority":"Magistrates' Courts Act 1980 and allocation procedure.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"In an either-way case, magistrates’ acceptance of jurisdiction does not ordinarily remove the adult defendant’s election for Crown Court trial.","common_trap":"Assuming allocation by magistrates finally determines venue whenever they accept jurisdiction.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"19bc150fce996f95"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0068","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Bail — general right","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant appears before the magistrates’ court before trial. The prosecution asks for remand in custody. No automatic statutory exclusion applies, so the court must consider the ordinary Bail Act framework.\n\nWhat is the correct starting point?","options":{"A":"Every defendant must be remanded in custody until trial.","B":"There is a general right to bail, subject to the statutory exceptions and conditions in the Bail Act regime.","C":"Bail is available only after conviction.","D":"Only the prosecution can grant bail.","E":"Bail depends entirely on whether the defendant can pay a cash deposit."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Bail Act 1976 establishes a general right to bail for persons within its scope, subject to statutory exceptions. The court considers whether an exception justifies withholding bail and whether conditions can address relevant risks.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The Bail Act starts from a general right to bail, qualified by statutory exceptions.","B":"Correct. The Bail Act 1976 establishes a general right to bail for persons within its scope, subject to statutory exceptions. The court considers whether an exception justifies withholding bail and whether conditions can address relevant risks.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The Bail Act starts from a general right to bail, qualified by statutory exceptions.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The Bail Act starts from a general right to bail, qualified by statutory exceptions.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Bail depends entirely on whether the defendant can pay a cash deposit' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The Bail Act starts from a general right to bail, qualified by statutory exceptions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Bail Act 1976.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The Bail Act starts from a general right to bail, qualified by statutory exceptions.","common_trap":"Treating pre-trial custody as the default.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6610c48793eefde2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0069","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Bad character evidence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"At a defendant’s criminal trial, the prosecution seeks to adduce evidence of a previous conviction as evidence of the defendant’s bad character. The defence argues that previous convictions are always inadmissible.\n\nWhich is the best general statement?","options":{"A":"Previous convictions are always admissible for any purpose.","B":"Previous convictions are always inadmissible.","C":"Evidence of a defendant’s bad character may be admissible through one or more statutory gateways, subject to the applicable safeguards and exclusion provisions.","D":"Bad character evidence is admissible only in civil proceedings.","E":"A previous conviction automatically proves the present charge."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Criminal Justice Act 2003 establishes statutory gateways for admission of defendant bad-character evidence. Admission is not automatic merely because a conviction exists, and the legislation contains safeguards and exclusion powers.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Defendant bad-character evidence is governed by statutory gateways and safeguards, not absolute admissibility or exclusion.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Defendant bad-character evidence is governed by statutory gateways and safeguards, not absolute admissibility or exclusion.","C":"Correct. The Criminal Justice Act 2003 establishes statutory gateways for admission of defendant bad-character evidence. Admission is not automatic merely because a conviction exists, and the legislation contains safeguards and exclusion powers.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Defendant bad-character evidence is governed by statutory gateways and safeguards, not absolute admissibility or exclusion.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Defendant bad-character evidence is governed by statutory gateways and safeguards, not absolute admissibility or exclusion."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Justice Act 2003, Part 11, Chapter 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Defendant bad-character evidence is governed by statutory gateways and safeguards, not absolute admissibility or exclusion.","common_trap":"Using an absolute rule for previous convictions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"113a2d0f8f3ee113"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0070","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Sentencing — guilty plea","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant intends to plead guilty and asks whether the timing of the plea can affect sentence. Assume the offence is one for which a sentence reduction for a guilty plea is relevant.\n\nWhich statement best describes the principle?","options":{"A":"A guilty plea can never affect sentence.","B":"A guilty plea automatically eliminates any sentence.","C":"The court may reduce sentence to reflect a guilty plea, with the level of reduction governed by the applicable statutory framework and sentencing guidance, including the stage at which the plea is indicated.","D":"The reduction depends solely on whether the victim consents.","E":"Only a plea entered after trial begins can receive credit."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The sentencing framework permits credit for a guilty plea, and the amount is affected by timing under the applicable legislation and Sentencing Council guideline. Earlier indication can attract greater credit than a later plea, subject to the detailed rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A guilty plea may reduce sentence, and timing is relevant to the available credit.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A guilty plea may reduce sentence, and timing is relevant to the available credit.","C":"Correct. The sentencing framework permits credit for a guilty plea, and the amount is affected by timing under the applicable legislation and Sentencing Council guideline. Earlier indication can attract greater credit than a later plea, subject to the detailed rules.","D":"Incorrect. This makes consent or agreement determinative when the legal test requires the identified statutory or doctrinal conditions. A guilty plea may reduce sentence, and timing is relevant to the available credit.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A guilty plea may reduce sentence, and timing is relevant to the available credit."},"primary_legal_authority":"Sentencing Act 2020; Sentencing Council guideline on reduction in sentence for a guilty plea.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A guilty plea may reduce sentence, and timing is relevant to the available credit.","common_trap":"Assuming guilty-plea credit is either unavailable or a fixed amount regardless of timing.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cd27f2d5c8c7358d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0071","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"LLP legal personality","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A group of consultants wants to trade together while using an incorporated vehicle. They form a limited liability partnership under the Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A supplier later argues that the LLP cannot own an office lease in its own name because it is merely the collective name of its members.\n\nWhich statement best describes the LLP?","options":{"A":"An LLP has legal personality separate from its members and can hold property and incur obligations in its own name.","B":"An LLP is legally identical to an ordinary partnership in every respect and can never own property.","C":"An LLP has separate personality only if it has more than 20 members.","D":"An LLP can own property only through a corporate trustee.","E":"An LLP loses legal personality whenever a member leaves."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An incorporated LLP is a body corporate with legal personality separate from its members. Changes in membership do not themselves end its existence.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An incorporated LLP is a body corporate with legal personality separate from its members. Changes in membership do not themselves end its existence.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An LLP is a separate legal person from its members.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An LLP is a separate legal person from its members.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An LLP is a separate legal person from its members.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An LLP is a separate legal person from its members."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000, ss 1–2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An LLP is a separate legal person from its members.","common_trap":"Treating an LLP as though it were simply an ordinary partnership without separate personality.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"43b9b96dab390dc1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0072","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Directors — substantial property transactions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company proposes to sell a valuable freehold warehouse to one of its directors. The asset is substantial for Companies Act purposes and no statutory exception applies. The board believes the price is fair and wants to complete immediately without involving the members.\n\nWhat additional corporate approval is ordinarily required?","options":{"A":"Approval only from the company’s auditors.","B":"Approval by the company’s members under the statutory substantial property transaction regime.","C":"Approval from Companies House before exchange.","D":"Unanimous approval of all company creditors.","E":"No further approval because a fair price removes the statutory requirement."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Companies Act regulates arrangements under which a director or connected person acquires a substantial non-cash asset from the company, or vice versa. Member approval is ordinarily required unless an exception applies.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Substantial non-cash asset transactions with directors can require member approval.","B":"Correct. The Companies Act regulates arrangements under which a director or connected person acquires a substantial non-cash asset from the company, or vice versa. Member approval is ordinarily required unless an exception applies.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Approval from Companies House before exchange' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Substantial non-cash asset transactions with directors can require member approval.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Unanimous approval of all company creditors' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Substantial non-cash asset transactions with directors can require member approval.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No further approval because a fair price removes the statutory requirement' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Substantial non-cash asset transactions with directors can require member approval."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 190–196.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Substantial non-cash asset transactions with directors can require member approval.","common_trap":"Assuming an arm’s-length or fair price eliminates the statutory approval requirement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"26b9a6ef5228ded2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0073","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Company borrowing — fixed and floating charges","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company grants a bank security over a changing pool of stock that the company may continue selling and replacing in the ordinary course of business until a specified enforcement event. The security documentation is intended to attach to the class of assets rather than permanently immobilise each item.\n\nHow is this security most naturally characterised?","options":{"A":"A legal mortgage over land.","B":"A fixed charge because every company asset is necessarily fixed security.","C":"A floating charge over the relevant class of circulating assets.","D":"A retention-of-title clause granted by the bank.","E":"A personal guarantee by the shareholders."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A floating charge characteristically hovers over a class of changing assets while allowing the company to deal with them in the ordinary course until crystallisation or another relevant event.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A legal mortgage over land' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A floating charge can cover a changing class of assets that the company may deal with before crystallisation.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A floating charge can cover a changing class of assets that the company may deal with before crystallisation.","C":"Correct. A floating charge characteristically hovers over a class of changing assets while allowing the company to deal with them in the ordinary course until crystallisation or another relevant event.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A retention-of-title clause granted by the bank' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A floating charge can cover a changing class of assets that the company may deal with before crystallisation.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A personal guarantee by the shareholders' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A floating charge can cover a changing class of assets that the company may deal with before crystallisation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986 and Companies Act 2006 principles concerning company charges.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A floating charge can cover a changing class of assets that the company may deal with before crystallisation.","common_trap":"Assuming every proprietary company security interest is a fixed charge.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a563ce656bc76a50"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0074","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — transaction at undervalue","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An insolvent company transfers machinery worth £300,000 to an unconnected purchaser for £20,000. The transfer occurs within the relevant statutory period before liquidation. The liquidator investigates whether the transaction can be challenged. Assume the statutory insolvency conditions are satisfied and no defence applies.\n\nWhich concept is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Wrongful trading solely because the company made a poor bargain.","B":"A preference, which requires every undervalue transaction to favour an existing creditor.","C":"A valid distribution to shareholders.","D":"A transaction at an undervalue, because the company transferred property for consideration significantly less than its value.","E":"A compulsory share buyback."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A company enters a transaction at an undervalue where, among other statutory cases, it receives consideration whose value in money or money’s worth is significantly less than the value it provides. The statutory timing and insolvency requirements must also be satisfied.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Wrongful trading solely because the company made a poor bargain' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A materially inadequate exchange can constitute a transaction at an undervalue if the statutory conditions are met.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A materially inadequate exchange can constitute a transaction at an undervalue if the statutory conditions are met.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A valid distribution to shareholders' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A materially inadequate exchange can constitute a transaction at an undervalue if the statutory conditions are met.","D":"Correct. A company enters a transaction at an undervalue where, among other statutory cases, it receives consideration whose value in money or money’s worth is significantly less than the value it provides. The statutory timing and insolvency requirements must also be satisfied.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A compulsory share buyback' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A materially inadequate exchange can constitute a transaction at an undervalue if the statutory conditions are met."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, s 238.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A materially inadequate exchange can constitute a transaction at an undervalue if the statutory conditions are met.","common_trap":"Confusing undervalue transactions with preferences, which address putting creditors or sureties into a better insolvency position.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"de3c9ece33ab6dad"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0075","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership — liability of incoming partner","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor joins an existing general partnership. Before she joined, the firm incurred a contractual debt to a stationery supplier. There is no agreement between the new partner, the creditor and the existing partners making her liable for that old debt.\n\nWhat is the ordinary Partnership Act position?","options":{"A":"She is automatically personally liable for every partnership debt incurred before she joined.","B":"The old debt is automatically extinguished when a new partner joins.","C":"She is liable only because the debt is contractual rather than tortious.","D":"She becomes liable only if the partnership has fewer than five partners.","E":"She is not, merely by becoming a partner, liable to the firm’s creditors for obligations incurred before she became a partner."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"An incoming partner is not automatically liable to creditors for partnership debts incurred before joining. Liability can be altered through an appropriate agreement or novation, but none exists here.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An incoming partner is not automatically liable for pre-admission partnership debts.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An incoming partner is not automatically liable for pre-admission partnership debts.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An incoming partner is not automatically liable for pre-admission partnership debts.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An incoming partner is not automatically liable for pre-admission partnership debts.","E":"Correct. An incoming partner is not automatically liable to creditors for partnership debts incurred before joining. Liability can be altered through an appropriate agreement or novation, but none exists here."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, s 17.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An incoming partner is not automatically liable for pre-admission partnership debts.","common_trap":"Assuming partnership membership retrospectively attaches all historic liabilities.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b3cc27cc2f1f6882"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0076","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Statements of case — amendment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant discovers after service of the particulars of claim that an additional factual allegation should be pleaded. The statement of case has already been served. The defendant will not consent to the amendment.\n\nWhat procedural route should the claimant ordinarily consider?","options":{"A":"Applying for the court’s permission to amend the statement of case, subject to the CPR and any limitation implications.","B":"Simply altering the served document privately without informing anyone.","C":"Starting criminal proceedings against the defendant.","D":"Waiting until after judgment and then changing the pleading retrospectively.","E":"Treating the amendment as automatically effective because it concerns facts."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Once a statement of case has been served, amendment ordinarily requires the written consent of all other parties or the court’s permission. Additional restrictions can apply where limitation has expired.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Once a statement of case has been served, amendment ordinarily requires the written consent of all other parties or the court’s permission. Additional restrictions can apply where limitation has expired.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Simply altering the served document privately without informing anyone' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. After service, amendment ordinarily requires consent or court permission.","C":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. After service, amendment ordinarily requires consent or court permission.","D":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. After service, amendment ordinarily requires consent or court permission.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. After service, amendment ordinarily requires consent or court permission."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 17.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"After service, amendment ordinarily requires consent or court permission.","common_trap":"Assuming a party may unilaterally rewrite a served pleading.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9e923eeb1de0693c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0077","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Disclosure — standard disclosure","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"In civil proceedings governed by the ordinary disclosure rules applicable to the case, a party identifies a document that seriously damages its own pleaded position. The document is within its control and falls within the applicable disclosure obligation.\n\nCan the party omit it merely because it is adverse to its own case?","options":{"A":"Yes, because disclosure covers only helpful documents.","B":"No. An applicable disclosure obligation can extend to documents adverse to the disclosing party’s own case as well as supportive documents.","C":"Yes, if the client asks the solicitor not to mention it.","D":"No, but only if the opponent already knows the document exists.","E":"Yes, unless the document is more than one page long."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Disclosure is not a process for selecting only favourable material. Where the applicable disclosure rule requires disclosure of adverse documents, a party must comply subject to privilege and other lawful limitations.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Disclosure obligations can require adverse as well as supportive documents.","B":"Correct. Disclosure is not a process for selecting only favourable material. Where the applicable disclosure rule requires disclosure of adverse documents, a party must comply subject to privilege and other lawful limitations.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Yes, if the client asks the solicitor not to mention it' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Disclosure obligations can require adverse as well as supportive documents.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Disclosure obligations can require adverse as well as supportive documents.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Yes, unless the document is more than one page long' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Disclosure obligations can require adverse as well as supportive documents."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 31 where applicable; relevant disclosure regime for the proceedings.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Disclosure obligations can require adverse as well as supportive documents.","common_trap":"Confusing disclosure with advocacy selection of favourable evidence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"116615e3cf19c80b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0078","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Witness statements — statement of truth","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A factual witness signs a witness statement for use in civil proceedings. The solicitor explains that the statement of truth confirms the witness’s honest belief in the truth of the facts stated.\n\nWhat is the principal significance of the statement of truth?","options":{"A":"It converts the witness statement into a deed.","B":"It guarantees that every fact is objectively correct.","C":"It is a formal verification of the truth of the factual case stated, and knowingly making a false statement without honest belief can have serious consequences including contempt proceedings.","D":"It prevents cross-examination of the witness.","E":"It makes disclosure unnecessary."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Statements of truth are formal verifications used under the CPR. A person who knowingly makes a false statement without honest belief may face contempt consequences; the statement does not guarantee objective accuracy or prevent testing of the evidence.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This imposes a formality that the governing rule does not require in this situation. A statement of truth formally verifies specified litigation documents and carries consequences for dishonest falsehoods.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A statement of truth formally verifies specified litigation documents and carries consequences for dishonest falsehoods.","C":"Correct. Statements of truth are formal verifications used under the CPR. A person who knowingly makes a false statement without honest belief may face contempt consequences; the statement does not guarantee objective accuracy or prevent testing of the evidence.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It prevents cross-examination of the witness' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A statement of truth formally verifies specified litigation documents and carries consequences for dishonest falsehoods.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It makes disclosure unnecessary' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A statement of truth formally verifies specified litigation documents and carries consequences for dishonest falsehoods."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 22.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A statement of truth formally verifies specified litigation documents and carries consequences for dishonest falsehoods.","common_trap":"Treating a statement of truth as either meaningless or as conclusive proof.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7f674d2f43141da3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0079","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Undertakings","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor gives another firm a clear professional undertaking to discharge a registered charge from completion monies. After completion, the client instructs the solicitor not to honour the undertaking because the client has fallen out with the lender.\n\nWhat is the best regulatory analysis?","options":{"A":"The solicitor may ignore the undertaking whenever the client changes instructions.","B":"Undertakings are morally persuasive but have no professional significance.","C":"Only undertakings given to courts matter.","D":"The solicitor should perform an undertaking within the agreed timescale or, if none is specified, within a reasonable time; a client instruction does not simply erase the professional obligation.","E":"The solicitor may transfer responsibility for the undertaking to the client without the recipient’s agreement."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The SRA Code requires solicitors to perform undertakings given by them within the agreed timescale or otherwise within a reasonable amount of time. The undertaking is a professional commitment and cannot simply be disregarded because the client later objects.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Solicitors must perform professional undertakings in accordance with their terms and the regulatory Code.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Undertakings are morally persuasive but have no professional significance' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Solicitors must perform professional undertakings in accordance with their terms and the regulatory Code.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Solicitors must perform professional undertakings in accordance with their terms and the regulatory Code.","D":"Correct. The SRA Code requires solicitors to perform undertakings given by them within the agreed timescale or otherwise within a reasonable amount of time. The undertaking is a professional commitment and cannot simply be disregarded because the client later objects.","E":"Incorrect. This makes consent or agreement determinative when the legal test requires the identified statutory or doctrinal conditions. Solicitors must perform professional undertakings in accordance with their terms and the regulatory Code."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 1.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Solicitors must perform professional undertakings in accordance with their terms and the regulatory Code.","common_trap":"Treating an undertaking as merely an expression of the client’s current instructions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"08662c0f9842f2d1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0080","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Client conflicts — substantially common interest","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two commercial clients ask the same solicitor to act for them jointly in negotiating a collaboration agreement. Their objectives are substantially aligned, but there is a significant risk that their interests may diverge over allocation of intellectual-property revenue. The solicitor considers the SRA conflict rules.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"The solicitor may always act for both clients whenever they consent.","B":"A significant risk of conflict is irrelevant until an actual dispute begins.","C":"The solicitor must act because refusing would breach confidentiality.","D":"Commercial clients are outside the conflict rules.","E":"Acting may be possible only if an applicable regulatory exception is satisfied, such as the substantially common interest exception, together with its safeguards; consent alone is not a universal cure."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The Code prohibits acting where there is a conflict or significant risk of conflict between clients unless a specified exception applies. The substantially common interest exception has conditions, including informed consent and reasonable judgment that acting for both is appropriate.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client conflicts require an applicable exception and safeguards; consent by itself is not enough.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A significant risk of conflict is irrelevant until an actual dispute begins' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Client conflicts require an applicable exception and safeguards; consent by itself is not enough.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client conflicts require an applicable exception and safeguards; consent by itself is not enough.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Commercial clients are outside the conflict rules' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Client conflicts require an applicable exception and safeguards; consent by itself is not enough.","E":"Correct. The Code prohibits acting where there is a conflict or significant risk of conflict between clients unless a specified exception applies. The substantially common interest exception has conditions, including informed consent and reasonable judgment that acting for both is appropriate."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, paras 6.2 and related provisions.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client conflicts require an applicable exception and safeguards; consent by itself is not enough.","common_trap":"Treating informed consent as a universal exception to conflicts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bb7b2b5420d2d4f5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0081","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Offer — revocation","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller offers by email to sell equipment to a buyer and promises to keep the offer open for seven days. The buyer gives no consideration for that promise. Before acceptance, the seller clearly communicates withdrawal of the offer to the buyer.\n\nWhat is the ordinary common-law position?","options":{"A":"The seller can ordinarily revoke the offer before acceptance because the unsupported promise to keep it open does not itself create an option contract.","B":"The offer is irrevocable for seven days merely because the seller said so.","C":"Revocation is effective only after the seven days expire.","D":"The buyer owns the equipment immediately when the offer is made.","E":"Every offer made by email is a deed."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Absent consideration or another binding basis for an option, a promise to keep an offer open does not ordinarily prevent revocation before acceptance, provided revocation is effectively communicated.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Absent consideration or another binding basis for an option, a promise to keep an offer open does not ordinarily prevent revocation before acceptance, provided revocation is effectively communicated.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The offer is irrevocable for seven days merely because the seller said so' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An ordinary offer can generally be revoked before acceptance unless an enforceable option or other rule prevents it.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An ordinary offer can generally be revoked before acceptance unless an enforceable option or other rule prevents it.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The buyer owns the equipment immediately when the offer is made' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An ordinary offer can generally be revoked before acceptance unless an enforceable option or other rule prevents it.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An ordinary offer can generally be revoked before acceptance unless an enforceable option or other rule prevents it."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law rules on offer and revocation, including Dickinson v Dodds.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An ordinary offer can generally be revoked before acceptance unless an enforceable option or other rule prevents it.","common_trap":"Confusing a gratuitous promise to keep an offer open with a binding option.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c4efdf73ca72fcc2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0082","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Acceptance — postal rule","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller posts an offer to a buyer. The circumstances make acceptance by post reasonable and the offer does not require actual receipt. The buyer posts an unconditional letter of acceptance correctly addressed and stamped. Before the letter arrives, the seller attempts to revoke the offer.\n\nUnder the traditional postal rule, when is acceptance ordinarily effective?","options":{"A":"Only when the seller reads the letter.","B":"When the acceptance is properly posted, subject to the recognised limits of the postal rule.","C":"When the seller posts the original offer.","D":"Only after the goods are delivered.","E":"Never, because acceptance by post is invalid."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Where the postal rule applies, acceptance is generally effective on proper posting rather than receipt. The rule is subject to limits and can be excluded expressly or by implication.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Where applicable, the postal rule makes acceptance effective on posting.","B":"Correct. Where the postal rule applies, acceptance is generally effective on proper posting rather than receipt. The rule is subject to limits and can be excluded expressly or by implication.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'When the seller posts the original offer' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Where applicable, the postal rule makes acceptance effective on posting.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Where applicable, the postal rule makes acceptance effective on posting.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Where applicable, the postal rule makes acceptance effective on posting."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law postal rule, including Adams v Lindsell and Household Fire v Grant.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Where applicable, the postal rule makes acceptance effective on posting.","common_trap":"Applying the receipt rule automatically to a postal acceptance.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"773698d1cb9084c8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0083","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Economic duress","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A supplier threatens, without lawful commercial justification, to breach an existing critical supply contract unless the customer immediately agrees to pay a much higher price. The customer has no practical alternative because shutdown would cause severe losses, agrees under protest and promptly challenges the variation once replacement supply becomes available.\n\nWhich doctrine is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Frustration.","B":"Mistake as to identity.","C":"Economic duress, potentially rendering the variation voidable if the required elements are established.","D":"Promissory estoppel operating as a cause of action for damages.","E":"Adverse possession."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Illegitimate pressure that causes a party to enter a contract or variation when there is no practical choice can amount to economic duress. The precise analysis considers the nature of the pressure, causation and available alternatives.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Economic duress can render an agreement voidable where illegitimate pressure causes assent without practical choice.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Mistake as to identity' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Economic duress can render an agreement voidable where illegitimate pressure causes assent without practical choice.","C":"Correct. Illegitimate pressure that causes a party to enter a contract or variation when there is no practical choice can amount to economic duress. The precise analysis considers the nature of the pressure, causation and available alternatives.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Promissory estoppel operating as a cause of action for damages' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Economic duress can render an agreement voidable where illegitimate pressure causes assent without practical choice.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Economic duress can render an agreement voidable where illegitimate pressure causes assent without practical choice."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law doctrine of economic duress.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Economic duress can render an agreement voidable where illegitimate pressure causes assent without practical choice.","common_trap":"Assuming all hard commercial bargaining is duress, or conversely that threatened contractual breach can never qualify.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"55469458f2dda4c2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0084","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consumer Rights Act — goods quality","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A consumer buys a new domestic washing machine from a retailer. On normal first use, the machine repeatedly leaks because of an inherent manufacturing defect. No unusual warning or disclosed defect existed when the contract was made.\n\nWhich statutory term is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The consumer guarantees the retailer a profit.","B":"Goods sold to consumers need only be usable for one day.","C":"Consumer contracts contain no implied statutory terms.","D":"The goods must be of satisfactory quality, judged under the Consumer Rights Act 2015 framework.","E":"The manufacturer alone is necessarily the consumer’s contractual counterparty."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Consumer Rights Act implies terms into contracts for goods, including that goods are of satisfactory quality. The assessment takes account of matters such as description, price and other relevant circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The consumer guarantees the retailer a profit' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Consumer goods are subject to the CRA 2015 satisfactory-quality term.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Consumer goods are subject to the CRA 2015 satisfactory-quality term.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Consumer contracts contain no implied statutory terms' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Consumer goods are subject to the CRA 2015 satisfactory-quality term.","D":"Correct. The Consumer Rights Act implies terms into contracts for goods, including that goods are of satisfactory quality. The assessment takes account of matters such as description, price and other relevant circumstances.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The manufacturer alone is necessarily the consumer’s contractual counterparty' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Consumer goods are subject to the CRA 2015 satisfactory-quality term."},"primary_legal_authority":"Consumer Rights Act 2015, ss 9 and related remedies provisions.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Consumer goods are subject to the CRA 2015 satisfactory-quality term.","common_trap":"Assuming the consumer has contractual rights only against the manufacturer.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fe4cf6132198340b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0085","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Occupiers' Liability 1984 — trespassers","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An occupier knows that teenagers frequently trespass onto an abandoned industrial site and climb near an unfenced deep pit. The occupier knows of the danger and has reasonable grounds to believe trespassers may come into its vicinity, but takes no precautions.\n\nWhich statute is most directly relevant to a possible duty owed to an injured trespasser?","options":{"A":"Occupiers’ Liability Act 1957 only, because trespassers are visitors.","B":"Consumer Rights Act 2015.","C":"Companies Act 2006.","D":"Wills Act 1837.","E":"Occupiers’ Liability Act 1984, which can impose a duty to persons other than visitors when its statutory conditions are satisfied."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The 1984 Act addresses duties to persons other than visitors. Its statutory conditions focus on the occupier’s awareness of the danger, grounds for believing the person may come into its vicinity, and whether the risk is one against which protection may reasonably be expected.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Occupiers’ Liability Act 1957 only, because trespassers are visitors' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The OLA 1984 can impose a limited duty toward non-visitors if its conditions are met.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Consumer Rights Act 2015' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The OLA 1984 can impose a limited duty toward non-visitors if its conditions are met.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Companies Act 2006' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The OLA 1984 can impose a limited duty toward non-visitors if its conditions are met.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Wills Act 1837' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The OLA 1984 can impose a limited duty toward non-visitors if its conditions are met.","E":"Correct. The 1984 Act addresses duties to persons other than visitors. Its statutory conditions focus on the occupier’s awareness of the danger, grounds for believing the person may come into its vicinity, and whether the risk is one against which protection may reasonably be expected."},"primary_legal_authority":"Occupiers’ Liability Act 1984, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The OLA 1984 can impose a limited duty toward non-visitors if its conditions are met.","common_trap":"Applying the 1957 visitor regime automatically to trespassers.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fe2ac6d44674fe17"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0086","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Private nuisance","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A factory repeatedly emits substantial noise at night, interfering with a neighbouring landowner’s ordinary use and enjoyment of her home. The interference is not a one-off personal injury event; it is continuing and connected with the factory’s use of its land.\n\nWhich tort is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Private nuisance.","B":"Defamation.","C":"Conversion.","D":"Deceit only.","E":"Passing off."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Private nuisance protects interests in land against unreasonable interference with use or enjoyment of land, assessed contextually. Recurrent noise emanating from neighbouring land is a classic type of alleged interference.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Private nuisance protects interests in land against unreasonable interference with use or enjoyment of land, assessed contextually. Recurrent noise emanating from neighbouring land is a classic type of alleged interference.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Defamation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Private nuisance concerns unreasonable interference with use or enjoyment of land.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Conversion' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Private nuisance concerns unreasonable interference with use or enjoyment of land.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Deceit only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Private nuisance concerns unreasonable interference with use or enjoyment of land.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Passing off' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Private nuisance concerns unreasonable interference with use or enjoyment of land."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law private nuisance principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Private nuisance concerns unreasonable interference with use or enjoyment of land.","common_trap":"Treating nuisance primarily as a personal-injury tort detached from interests in land.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4f3ef8308c012aef"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0087","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Product liability — Consumer Protection Act","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A consumer is injured when a household appliance unexpectedly explodes because it is defective. The consumer considers a statutory claim against a producer under Part I of the Consumer Protection Act 1987 and can establish defect, damage and the necessary causal link.\n\nWhat is distinctive about the statutory liability regime?","options":{"A":"The claimant must always prove that the producer was personally negligent.","B":"Liability for defective products under the statutory regime can arise without proving negligence, subject to the Act’s requirements and defences.","C":"The Act applies only to contracts between businesses.","D":"The Act provides only criminal remedies.","E":"A product can never be defective if it complied with its description."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Part I of the 1987 Act establishes a strict-liability regime for damage caused by defective products against specified persons, subject to statutory requirements and defences. Proof of negligence is not an element of that statutory cause of action.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. CPA product liability does not require proof of producer negligence.","B":"Correct. Part I of the 1987 Act establishes a strict-liability regime for damage caused by defective products against specified persons, subject to statutory requirements and defences. Proof of negligence is not an element of that statutory cause of action.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. CPA product liability does not require proof of producer negligence.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. CPA product liability does not require proof of producer negligence.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. CPA product liability does not require proof of producer negligence."},"primary_legal_authority":"Consumer Protection Act 1987, Part I.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"CPA product liability does not require proof of producer negligence.","common_trap":"Confusing statutory product liability with the separate tort of negligence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fad5d3fcfccecc4d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0088","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Parliamentary sovereignty","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client asks whether an ordinary court can simply refuse to apply a valid Act of the UK Parliament because the judge considers the policy unwise. No special constitutional or statutory mechanism is engaged.\n\nWhich proposition best reflects the orthodox constitutional position?","options":{"A":"Courts may disregard any Act they consider unfair.","B":"Only local authorities decide whether Acts are valid.","C":"Parliamentary sovereignty means courts ordinarily apply valid Acts of Parliament; disagreement with policy is not itself a basis for refusing to apply them.","D":"An Act applies only if every judge agrees with it.","E":"Acts of Parliament are merely persuasive sources."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The orthodox doctrine of parliamentary sovereignty recognises Acts of Parliament as supreme legislation. Courts interpret and apply legislation and may use specific statutory constitutional mechanisms where available, but do not disregard an Act merely because they disagree with its policy.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Courts ordinarily give effect to valid primary legislation under the constitutional doctrine of parliamentary sovereignty.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Courts ordinarily give effect to valid primary legislation under the constitutional doctrine of parliamentary sovereignty.","C":"Correct. The orthodox doctrine of parliamentary sovereignty recognises Acts of Parliament as supreme legislation. Courts interpret and apply legislation and may use specific statutory constitutional mechanisms where available, but do not disregard an Act merely because they disagree with its policy.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Courts ordinarily give effect to valid primary legislation under the constitutional doctrine of parliamentary sovereignty.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Acts of Parliament are merely persuasive sources' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Courts ordinarily give effect to valid primary legislation under the constitutional doctrine of parliamentary sovereignty."},"primary_legal_authority":"Constitutional principles of parliamentary sovereignty.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Courts ordinarily give effect to valid primary legislation under the constitutional doctrine of parliamentary sovereignty.","common_trap":"Confusing judicial interpretation or HRA mechanisms with a general judicial veto over primary legislation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8faf1e3ddeecbea9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0089","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Retained/assimilated EU law — supremacy","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor is considering the relationship between domestic legislation and a rule derived from pre-exit EU law after the end of 2023. The solicitor assumes that the old general principle of supremacy of EU law continues automatically and without statutory modification for all purposes.\n\nWhich is the best general approach?","options":{"A":"EU law automatically overrides every Act of Parliament enacted at any time.","B":"EU-derived law ceased to exist entirely on exit day.","C":"Only the European Commission determines domestic priority.","D":"The assumption is unsafe because the post-Brexit domestic effect of EU-derived law is governed by the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018 as amended, including changes made by the Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023.","E":"The Human Rights Act 1998 recreates the former supremacy principle."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The domestic status of EU-derived law after Brexit is statutory and has been materially altered, including from the end of 2023. A candidate must apply the current Withdrawal Act framework rather than assume the former EU supremacy principle continues unchanged.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The domestic status and priority of assimilated EU law are determined by the post-Brexit statutory framework.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'EU-derived law ceased to exist entirely on exit day' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The domestic status and priority of assimilated EU law are determined by the post-Brexit statutory framework.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The domestic status and priority of assimilated EU law are determined by the post-Brexit statutory framework.","D":"Correct. The domestic status of EU-derived law after Brexit is statutory and has been materially altered, including from the end of 2023. A candidate must apply the current Withdrawal Act framework rather than assume the former EU supremacy principle continues unchanged.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Human Rights Act 1998 recreates the former supremacy principle' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The domestic status and priority of assimilated EU law are determined by the post-Brexit statutory framework."},"primary_legal_authority":"European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018 as amended; Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The domestic status and priority of assimilated EU law are determined by the post-Brexit statutory framework.","common_trap":"Applying pre-Brexit supremacy doctrine unchanged after statutory reforms.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"90431e0abfa0a580"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0090","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — procedural impropriety","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A licensing authority has statutory power to revoke a licence after giving the licensee an opportunity to make representations. Without giving any such opportunity, it revokes the licence based on allegations the licensee has never seen.\n\nWhich ground of judicial review is most obviously raised?","options":{"A":"Frustration of contract.","B":"Contributory negligence.","C":"Adverse possession.","D":"Passing off.","E":"Procedural impropriety/failure to follow required fair procedure."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Failure to follow a statutorily required or procedurally fair process can constitute procedural impropriety. The authority’s substantive power does not remove procedural requirements attached to its exercise.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration of contract' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Public decisions can be reviewed for procedural impropriety, including failure to follow required fair procedures.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Contributory negligence' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Public decisions can be reviewed for procedural impropriety, including failure to follow required fair procedures.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Public decisions can be reviewed for procedural impropriety, including failure to follow required fair procedures.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Passing off' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Public decisions can be reviewed for procedural impropriety, including failure to follow required fair procedures.","E":"Correct. Failure to follow a statutorily required or procedurally fair process can constitute procedural impropriety. The authority’s substantive power does not remove procedural requirements attached to its exercise."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administrative-law principles of procedural fairness and judicial review.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Public decisions can be reviewed for procedural impropriety, including failure to follow required fair procedures.","common_trap":"Focusing only on whether the authority possesses substantive power and ignoring how it exercised that power.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ea1ad7bbef4a8640"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0091","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Personal representatives — authority","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person dies leaving a valid will appointing an executor. Before a grant of probate is issued, the executor asks whether the source of an executor’s authority is identical to that of an administrator appointed on intestacy.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"An executor’s authority derives from the will, although a grant is ordinarily needed to prove title for many dealings; an administrator’s authority derives from the grant.","B":"Both derive authority only from registration of the death.","C":"An administrator has authority from the deceased’s unsigned wishes.","D":"Neither can ever deal with estate assets.","E":"An executor obtains authority only from beneficiaries."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An executor’s title derives from the will and takes effect from death, whereas an administrator’s authority arises from the grant. In practice, probate is often required to establish the executor’s title when dealing with assets.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An executor’s title derives from the will and takes effect from death, whereas an administrator’s authority arises from the grant. In practice, probate is often required to establish the executor’s title when dealing with assets.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Executor and administrator authority arise differently: will versus grant.","C":"Incorrect. This imposes a formality that the governing rule does not require in this situation. Executor and administrator authority arise differently: will versus grant.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Neither can ever deal with estate assets' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Executor and administrator authority arise differently: will versus grant.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Executor and administrator authority arise differently: will versus grant."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925; Non-Contentious Probate Rules and general probate principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Executor and administrator authority arise differently: will versus grant.","common_trap":"Assuming probate creates an executor’s underlying authority in exactly the same way as letters of administration create an administrator’s.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"61ce797c330d2b17"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0092","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Lapse and anti-lapse","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator leaves a gift in a will to her adult son. The son dies before the testator leaving children of his own. The will contains no contrary intention. The issue is whether the gift necessarily lapses.\n\nWhich statutory rule should be considered?","options":{"A":"The rule that every predeceased beneficiary’s gift always passes to the Crown.","B":"The Wills Act provision that can prevent lapse of a gift to the testator’s child or remoter descendant where the statutory conditions are met and issue survive.","C":"The Companies Act share-buyback regime.","D":"The doctrine of frustration.","E":"The Bail Act 1976."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 33 of the Wills Act can operate where a gift is made to a child or remoter descendant of the testator who predeceases the testator leaving issue, unless a contrary intention appears. The statutory conditions must be checked.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The Wills Act anti-lapse rule may substitute surviving issue for certain gifts to descendants.","B":"Correct. Section 33 of the Wills Act can operate where a gift is made to a child or remoter descendant of the testator who predeceases the testator leaving issue, unless a contrary intention appears. The statutory conditions must be checked.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Companies Act share-buyback regime' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The Wills Act anti-lapse rule may substitute surviving issue for certain gifts to descendants.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The doctrine of frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The Wills Act anti-lapse rule may substitute surviving issue for certain gifts to descendants.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Bail Act 1976' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The Wills Act anti-lapse rule may substitute surviving issue for certain gifts to descendants."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 33.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The Wills Act anti-lapse rule may substitute surviving issue for certain gifts to descendants.","common_trap":"Assuming every gift to a beneficiary who dies first necessarily lapses.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ce31666bd4177d8e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0093","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"IHT — spouse exemption","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator leaves a substantial part of the estate outright to a surviving spouse. Assume both spouses fall within the ordinary scope of the spouse exemption and no special limitation applies.\n\nWhat is the principal inheritance-tax treatment of that transfer?","options":{"A":"It is always taxed at the highest inheritance-tax rate.","B":"It is corporation-tax income of the spouse.","C":"It is ordinarily exempt from inheritance tax under the spouse exemption.","D":"It is exempt only if the spouse is also an executor.","E":"It is treated as a lifetime potentially exempt transfer."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Transfers between spouses or civil partners are generally exempt from inheritance tax, subject to statutory qualifications that can apply in particular circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Qualifying transfers to a spouse/civil partner are generally IHT-exempt.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It is corporation-tax income of the spouse' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Qualifying transfers to a spouse/civil partner are generally IHT-exempt.","C":"Correct. Transfers between spouses or civil partners are generally exempt from inheritance tax, subject to statutory qualifications that can apply in particular circumstances.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Qualifying transfers to a spouse/civil partner are generally IHT-exempt.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It is treated as a lifetime potentially exempt transfer' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Qualifying transfers to a spouse/civil partner are generally IHT-exempt."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, s 18.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Qualifying transfers to a spouse/civil partner are generally IHT-exempt.","common_trap":"Treating all testamentary transfers as chargeable regardless of the recipient.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bf7771c3ec778cdb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0094","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Withdrawals from client account","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm holds money for an estate in client account. A fee earner wants to withdraw part of it to pay an unrelated office expense of the firm. No bill has been delivered and there is no other basis under the Accounts Rules for taking that estate money.\n\nWhat is the correct approach?","options":{"A":"The withdrawal is permissible because partners own the client account.","B":"Any withdrawal is permitted below £5,000.","C":"Estate money is never client money.","D":"Client money may be withdrawn only for a purpose permitted by the Accounts Rules; it cannot simply be used for unrelated office expenditure.","E":"The fee earner may borrow it if it is replaced within a week."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Client account is not a source of working capital. Withdrawals must fall within the permitted categories under the Accounts Rules and be appropriately authorised/supervised.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The withdrawal is permissible because partners own the client account' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Client money can be withdrawn only in accordance with the Accounts Rules.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Any withdrawal is permitted below £5,000' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Client money can be withdrawn only in accordance with the Accounts Rules.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client money can be withdrawn only in accordance with the Accounts Rules.","D":"Correct. Client account is not a source of working capital. Withdrawals must fall within the permitted categories under the Accounts Rules and be appropriately authorised/supervised.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The fee earner may borrow it if it is replaced within a week' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Client money can be withdrawn only in accordance with the Accounts Rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, including r 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client money can be withdrawn only in accordance with the Accounts Rules.","common_trap":"Treating client account as temporary financing for the firm.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"230b106a2492763a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0095","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Certainty of intention","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person writes to a friend: “I hope you will use £20,000 of the money I have given you to help my niece if you think she needs it.” There is no other evidence imposing an obligation. The niece argues that the words necessarily create an express trust.\n\nWhich issue is central?","options":{"A":"Whether the niece is over 18.","B":"Whether the money was transferred by bank rather than cheque.","C":"Whether the friend is a solicitor.","D":"Whether the niece lives in England.","E":"Whether the words demonstrate a sufficiently certain intention to impose a binding trust obligation rather than merely a moral wish."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"An express trust requires certainty of intention. Precatory language expressing hope or desire may be insufficient if, construed objectively in context, it does not impose a binding obligation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the niece is over 18' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A trust requires sufficiently certain intention to create binding trust obligations.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the money was transferred by bank rather than cheque' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A trust requires sufficiently certain intention to create binding trust obligations.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the friend is a solicitor' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A trust requires sufficiently certain intention to create binding trust obligations.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the niece lives in England' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A trust requires sufficiently certain intention to create binding trust obligations.","E":"Correct. An express trust requires certainty of intention. Precatory language expressing hope or desire may be insufficient if, construed objectively in context, it does not impose a binding obligation."},"primary_legal_authority":"General equitable principles on the three certainties, including certainty of intention.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A trust requires sufficiently certain intention to create binding trust obligations.","common_trap":"Assuming words of hope or moral expectation necessarily create a trust.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"181b48ecfcdd578a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0096","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Knowing receipt","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee transfers trust investments in breach of trust to an outsider for the outsider’s own benefit. The outsider receives the assets beneficially and, by the time relevant to liability, has knowledge making retention of the benefit unconscionable.\n\nWhich equitable personal claim is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Knowing receipt, subject to proof of the required receipt, beneficial receipt and knowledge/unconscionability elements.","B":"Dishonest assistance only, because receipt is irrelevant.","C":"Ademption.","D":"Promissory estoppel.","E":"Private nuisance."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Knowing receipt is a personal equitable liability associated with beneficial receipt of assets transferred in breach of trust or fiduciary duty and the requisite state of knowledge. It is distinct from dishonest assistance.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Knowing receipt is a personal equitable liability associated with beneficial receipt of assets transferred in breach of trust or fiduciary duty and the requisite state of knowledge. It is distinct from dishonest assistance.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Dishonest assistance only, because receipt is irrelevant' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Knowing receipt focuses on beneficial receipt of misapplied trust property plus the requisite knowledge.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Ademption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Knowing receipt focuses on beneficial receipt of misapplied trust property plus the requisite knowledge.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Promissory estoppel' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Knowing receipt focuses on beneficial receipt of misapplied trust property plus the requisite knowledge.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Private nuisance' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Knowing receipt focuses on beneficial receipt of misapplied trust property plus the requisite knowledge."},"primary_legal_authority":"Equitable principles of knowing receipt, including Ivey/Byers-era authorities as applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Knowing receipt focuses on beneficial receipt of misapplied trust property plus the requisite knowledge.","common_trap":"Confusing receipt-based liability with assistance-based liability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"556da8e56aa7d5f0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0097","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Freehold covenants — burden at law","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A freehold owner gives a positive covenant to maintain a boundary wall. The land is later sold. A neighbour argues that the burden of the positive covenant automatically runs at common law to every successor in title to the covenantor.\n\nWhat is the orthodox rule?","options":{"A":"Every positive covenant automatically binds all successors.","B":"The burden of a positive freehold covenant does not ordinarily run with the land at common law merely because title has passed.","C":"Positive covenants bind successors only if registered as legal easements.","D":"The burden runs only when the covenant concerns paint colour.","E":"Common law treats positive and restrictive covenant burdens identically."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The orthodox rule is that the burden of a positive covenant does not run with freehold land at common law. Other mechanisms may be relevant in practice, but automatic common-law transmission is not the rule.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The burden of a positive freehold covenant does not ordinarily run at common law.","B":"Correct. The orthodox rule is that the burden of a positive covenant does not run with freehold land at common law. Other mechanisms may be relevant in practice, but automatic common-law transmission is not the rule.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The burden of a positive freehold covenant does not ordinarily run at common law.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The burden of a positive freehold covenant does not ordinarily run at common law.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Common law treats positive and restrictive covenant burdens identically' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The burden of a positive freehold covenant does not ordinarily run at common law."},"primary_legal_authority":"Austerberry v Oldham Corporation; Rhone v Stephens.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The burden of a positive freehold covenant does not ordinarily run at common law.","common_trap":"Applying the equitable restrictive-covenant doctrine indiscriminately to positive obligations.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"088f83a423d48f9d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0098","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Adverse possession — registered land","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A squatter has been in adverse possession of registered land for ten years and applies to be registered as proprietor. The registered proprietor receives notice and objects. No special statutory condition favouring the squatter applies.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the modern registered-land regime?","options":{"A":"Ten years’ adverse possession automatically transfers registered title without notice to the proprietor.","B":"Registered land can never be acquired by adverse possession.","C":"After the qualifying period an application can be made, but the registered proprietor is generally given an opportunity to respond and can usually defeat the application unless a statutory condition applies.","D":"The squatter becomes proprietor after six months.","E":"Adverse possession is determined solely by whether council tax was paid."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Schedule 6 to the Land Registration Act 2002 replaced the former automatic limitation model for registered land. After ten years, the squatter may apply, but notice and objection mechanisms protect the registered proprietor subject to specified exceptions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Registered-land adverse possession generally requires an application after ten years and gives the registered proprietor statutory protection.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Registered-land adverse possession generally requires an application after ten years and gives the registered proprietor statutory protection.","C":"Correct. Schedule 6 to the Land Registration Act 2002 replaced the former automatic limitation model for registered land. After ten years, the squatter may apply, but notice and objection mechanisms protect the registered proprietor subject to specified exceptions.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The squatter becomes proprietor after six months' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Registered-land adverse possession generally requires an application after ten years and gives the registered proprietor statutory protection.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession is determined solely by whether council tax was paid' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Registered-land adverse possession generally requires an application after ten years and gives the registered proprietor statutory protection."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, Sch 6.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Registered-land adverse possession generally requires an application after ten years and gives the registered proprietor statutory protection.","common_trap":"Applying the old unregistered-land limitation model directly to registered land.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"302efe6bf092d435"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0099","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Seller's title — official copies","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor acts for the buyer of a registered freehold. The seller’s solicitor supplies current official copies of the register and title plan.\n\nWhat is their principal conveyancing function?","options":{"A":"They replace every search and enquiry that could ever be required.","B":"They guarantee the physical condition of the building.","C":"They constitute the transfer deed itself.","D":"They provide evidence of the registered title and entries that must be investigated as part of deducing and checking title.","E":"They prove that no planning issue can exist."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Official copies are central to investigating registered title, showing the registered proprietor and relevant entries. They do not replace searches, physical survey or the transfer itself.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Official copies evidence the registered title for investigation.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'They guarantee the physical condition of the building' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Official copies evidence the registered title for investigation.","C":"Incorrect. This imposes a formality that the governing rule does not require in this situation. Official copies evidence the registered title for investigation.","D":"Correct. Official copies are central to investigating registered title, showing the registered proprietor and relevant entries. They do not replace searches, physical survey or the transfer itself.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'They prove that no planning issue can exist' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Official copies evidence the registered title for investigation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Rules and standard registered conveyancing practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Official copies evidence the registered title for investigation.","common_trap":"Treating registered-title documents as a complete substitute for all due diligence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"023846a08e458221"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0100","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Contract deposit","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"In a standard residential conveyancing transaction incorporating the Standard Conditions of Sale, the parties have not negotiated a different deposit arrangement. The buyer asks what role the deposit plays on exchange.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"A deposit transfers legal title immediately.","B":"Deposits are prohibited in land transactions.","C":"The deposit is paid to HM Land Registry as SDLT.","D":"The seller must always return the deposit even if the buyer repudiates and the contract validly permits forfeiture.","E":"The contractual deposit is ordinarily payable on exchange in accordance with the contract and forms part-payment of the purchase price, while also providing contractual security for performance."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The deposit is ordinarily paid on exchange under the contract and counts toward the price on completion. It also performs a security function, with consequences on default governed by the contract and law.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A deposit transfers legal title immediately' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A conveyancing deposit is ordinarily part-payment and contractual security, paid in accordance with the sale contract.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Deposits are prohibited in land transactions' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A conveyancing deposit is ordinarily part-payment and contractual security, paid in accordance with the sale contract.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The deposit is paid to HM Land Registry as SDLT' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A conveyancing deposit is ordinarily part-payment and contractual security, paid in accordance with the sale contract.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A conveyancing deposit is ordinarily part-payment and contractual security, paid in accordance with the sale contract.","E":"Correct. The deposit is ordinarily paid on exchange under the contract and counts toward the price on completion. It also performs a security function, with consequences on default governed by the contract and law."},"primary_legal_authority":"Standard Conditions of Sale and general conveyancing principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A conveyancing deposit is ordinarily part-payment and contractual security, paid in accordance with the sale contract.","common_trap":"Confusing the exchange deposit with tax or registration fees.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"59d1a544a7e2c6bb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0101","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Sole trader — personal liability","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A graphic designer starts trading alone without incorporating a company or LLP. She signs a studio lease in her own name for the business. The business later cannot meet the rent and she argues that the landlord can claim only against the assets she uses for work.\n\nWhich statement best describes her liability?","options":{"A":"A sole trader has unlimited personal liability for business obligations incurred by her, subject to ordinary rules governing the particular obligation.","B":"A sole trader is legally separate from the business and is never personally liable.","C":"Only equipment used in the trade can answer the debt.","D":"The landlord can claim only if the trader first incorporates.","E":"A sole trader receives the same limited liability as an LLP member."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A sole trader does not create a separate legal person merely by trading under a business name. Business contractual liabilities are therefore her liabilities.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A sole trader does not create a separate legal person merely by trading under a business name. Business contractual liabilities are therefore her liabilities.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A sole trader and the business are not separate legal persons for liability purposes.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A sole trader and the business are not separate legal persons for liability purposes.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A sole trader and the business are not separate legal persons for liability purposes.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A sole trader receives the same limited liability as an LLP member' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A sole trader and the business are not separate legal persons for liability purposes."},"primary_legal_authority":"General business-organisation principles; SRA FLK1 Business Law and Practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A sole trader and the business are not separate legal persons for liability purposes.","common_trap":"Importing corporate limited liability into a sole-trader business.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1d879d0cfe7b370b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0102","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Directors — duty to promote success","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A director must choose between two lawful commercial strategies. One produces a larger immediate dividend but is likely to damage long-term supplier relationships and the company’s reputation. The director considers only the immediate dividend because he believes section 172 requires maximising short-term shareholder distributions.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"Section 172 requires directors to consider only immediate distributable profit.","B":"A director must act in the way considered, in good faith, most likely to promote the company’s success for members as a whole, having regard to the statutory factors including long-term consequences and relevant relationships.","C":"Section 172 requires directors always to prefer employees over members.","D":"The duty applies only when the company is listed.","E":"A director complies automatically whenever the chosen transaction is lawful."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 172 frames the duty around good-faith promotion of success for members as a whole and identifies factors to which regard must be had, including long-term consequences, employees, business relationships, community/environment and reputation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Directors must apply the statutory promote-success duty and have regard to the listed factors.","B":"Correct. Section 172 frames the duty around good-faith promotion of success for members as a whole and identifies factors to which regard must be had, including long-term consequences, employees, business relationships, community/environment and reputation.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Directors must apply the statutory promote-success duty and have regard to the listed factors.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Directors must apply the statutory promote-success duty and have regard to the listed factors.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Directors must apply the statutory promote-success duty and have regard to the listed factors."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 172.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Directors must apply the statutory promote-success duty and have regard to the listed factors.","common_trap":"Reducing section 172 to short-term profit maximisation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a6e705587ca320f7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0103","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Shareholder decisions — special resolution","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company wants to amend its articles by shareholder resolution. Assume the proposed amendment is otherwise lawful and no special provision changes the statutory voting threshold.\n\nWhat type of resolution is ordinarily required?","options":{"A":"An ordinary resolution passed by more than 50%.","B":"A board resolution only.","C":"A special resolution requiring not less than 75% of the votes cast.","D":"Unanimous consent of all creditors.","E":"A court order in every case."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Companies Act provides that a company may amend its articles by special resolution, whose statutory threshold is not less than 75%.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'An ordinary resolution passed by more than 50%' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Amending articles ordinarily requires a special resolution.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A board resolution only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Amending articles ordinarily requires a special resolution.","C":"Correct. The Companies Act provides that a company may amend its articles by special resolution, whose statutory threshold is not less than 75%.","D":"Incorrect. This makes consent or agreement determinative when the legal test requires the identified statutory or doctrinal conditions. Amending articles ordinarily requires a special resolution.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Amending articles ordinarily requires a special resolution."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 21 and 283.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Amending articles ordinarily requires a special resolution.","common_trap":"Confusing ordinary and special shareholder-resolution thresholds.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1f27ae42e698d552"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0104","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Wrongful trading","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Directors continue trading an insolvent company. At a particular point before liquidation, a director knew or ought to have concluded that there was no reasonable prospect that the company would avoid insolvent liquidation. The director then failed to take every step that ought to have been taken to minimise potential loss to creditors.\n\nWhich insolvency concept is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"A lawful dividend.","B":"A members’ voluntary liquidation.","C":"A share redemption.","D":"Wrongful trading.","E":"A statutory merger."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The wrongful-trading regime addresses directors who, before insolvent liquidation or administration, knew or ought to have concluded there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding the relevant insolvency outcome and failed to take the required loss-minimising steps.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A lawful dividend' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Wrongful trading can expose a director to a contribution order when the statutory knowledge and conduct requirements are met.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A members’ voluntary liquidation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Wrongful trading can expose a director to a contribution order when the statutory knowledge and conduct requirements are met.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A share redemption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Wrongful trading can expose a director to a contribution order when the statutory knowledge and conduct requirements are met.","D":"Correct. The wrongful-trading regime addresses directors who, before insolvent liquidation or administration, knew or ought to have concluded there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding the relevant insolvency outcome and failed to take the required loss-minimising steps.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A statutory merger' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Wrongful trading can expose a director to a contribution order when the statutory knowledge and conduct requirements are met."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, s 214 (and related provisions).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Wrongful trading can expose a director to a contribution order when the statutory knowledge and conduct requirements are met.","common_trap":"Assuming liability requires proof of fraud; that is a different concept from wrongful trading.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"567d486cdb0fa16c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0105","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Default judgment","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant is validly served with a straightforward money claim. The defendant neither files an acknowledgment of service nor a defence within the applicable time and has not satisfied the claim. No rule makes default judgment unavailable.\n\nWhat procedural step may the claimant ordinarily consider?","options":{"A":"Applying for summary judgment only after a full trial.","B":"Automatically enforcing against the defendant without obtaining judgment.","C":"Starting the same claim in a criminal court.","D":"Treating silence as a signed admission of every allegation for all purposes.","E":"Seeking judgment in default under the applicable CPR procedure."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Where the CPR conditions are met, failure to acknowledge service or defend can permit the claimant to obtain default judgment without a trial of the merits.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The CPR permits default judgment when the specified procedural conditions are satisfied.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The CPR permits default judgment when the specified procedural conditions are satisfied.","C":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. The CPR permits default judgment when the specified procedural conditions are satisfied.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The CPR permits default judgment when the specified procedural conditions are satisfied.","E":"Correct. Where the CPR conditions are met, failure to acknowledge service or defend can permit the claimant to obtain default judgment without a trial of the merits."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 12.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The CPR permits default judgment when the specified procedural conditions are satisfied.","common_trap":"Confusing default judgment with summary judgment or direct enforcement without judgment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c1f460cbdd83da49"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0106","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Allocation — overriding objective","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A judge is deciding how to manage a civil case and must choose between procedural directions. One option would be disproportionately expensive given the value and complexity of the dispute.\n\nWhich overarching CPR principle should guide the court?","options":{"A":"The overriding objective of dealing with cases justly and at proportionate cost.","B":"The rule that the most expensive procedure must always be chosen.","C":"The claimant’s procedural preference is conclusive.","D":"The court must maximise the number of hearings.","E":"Proportionality is relevant only after final judgment."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The CPR overriding objective requires cases to be dealt with justly and at proportionate cost, and informs the court’s case-management powers.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The CPR overriding objective requires cases to be dealt with justly and at proportionate cost, and informs the court’s case-management powers.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Civil case management is governed by the overriding objective, including proportionality.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The claimant’s procedural preference is conclusive' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Civil case management is governed by the overriding objective, including proportionality.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Civil case management is governed by the overriding objective, including proportionality.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Civil case management is governed by the overriding objective, including proportionality."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Civil case management is governed by the overriding objective, including proportionality.","common_trap":"Treating procedural complexity as an end in itself rather than applying proportionate case management.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"48956799ffa9a8ad"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0107","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Settlement — Tomlin order","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Parties settle ongoing civil proceedings on detailed confidential commercial terms, some of which go beyond remedies the court would ordinarily order in the proceedings. They want the proceedings stayed while preserving a mechanism for enforcing the agreed terms.\n\nWhich form of order is commonly suitable?","options":{"A":"A default judgment.","B":"A Tomlin order, with agreed terms ordinarily set out in a schedule and proceedings stayed on those terms.","C":"A search order against both parties.","D":"A bankruptcy order against the claimant.","E":"A witness summons."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A Tomlin order is commonly used to embody settlement arrangements: the operative court order stays proceedings on agreed terms, while the scheduled contractual terms can extend beyond what the court could order after trial.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A Tomlin order is a conventional mechanism for staying proceedings on agreed settlement terms.","B":"Correct. A Tomlin order is commonly used to embody settlement arrangements: the operative court order stays proceedings on agreed terms, while the scheduled contractual terms can extend beyond what the court could order after trial.","C":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A Tomlin order is a conventional mechanism for staying proceedings on agreed settlement terms.","D":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A Tomlin order is a conventional mechanism for staying proceedings on agreed settlement terms.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A witness summons' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A Tomlin order is a conventional mechanism for staying proceedings on agreed settlement terms."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules and standard settlement practice concerning Tomlin orders.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A Tomlin order is a conventional mechanism for staying proceedings on agreed settlement terms.","common_trap":"Assuming every settlement must be embodied entirely as substantive orders the court could have made at trial.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fd7a61448e6af609"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0108","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Enforcement — charging order","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A judgment creditor has an unpaid money judgment. The debtor owns a beneficial interest in land. The creditor wants security over that asset rather than immediate seizure of goods.\n\nWhich enforcement mechanism is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A witness statement.","B":"A Part 36 offer.","C":"A charging order over the debtor’s relevant interest.","D":"A freezing injunction automatically transferring ownership.","E":"A defence and counterclaim."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A charging order can secure a judgment debt against specified property, including an appropriate interest in land, subject to the statutory and procedural regime.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A witness statement' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A charging order can secure an unpaid judgment against qualifying property.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A Part 36 offer' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A charging order can secure an unpaid judgment against qualifying property.","C":"Correct. A charging order can secure a judgment debt against specified property, including an appropriate interest in land, subject to the statutory and procedural regime.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A charging order can secure an unpaid judgment against qualifying property.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A defence and counterclaim' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A charging order can secure an unpaid judgment against qualifying property."},"primary_legal_authority":"Charging Orders Act 1979; Civil Procedure Rules, Part 73.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A charging order can secure an unpaid judgment against qualifying property.","common_trap":"Confusing security over property with execution against goods or settlement procedure.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"01cdbc0ae76fd477"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0109","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"SRA Principles — independence","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A major commercial client tells its solicitor that it will move all future work elsewhere unless the solicitor gives legal advice that deliberately omits an important adverse legal risk. The solicitor knows the omission would make the advice misleading.\n\nWhich professional obligation is most directly engaged by the pressure?","options":{"A":"A duty to follow every client instruction regardless of professional obligations.","B":"A rule requiring solicitors to guarantee successful outcomes.","C":"A duty to conceal adverse law from clients.","D":"The obligation to act with independence, together with duties of honesty and integrity.","E":"No professional rule applies to commercial clients."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A solicitor must maintain professional independence and cannot allow commercial pressure from a client to override regulatory obligations, including honesty and integrity.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Professional independence cannot be surrendered to client commercial pressure.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A rule requiring solicitors to guarantee successful outcomes' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Professional independence cannot be surrendered to client commercial pressure.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A duty to conceal adverse law from clients' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Professional independence cannot be surrendered to client commercial pressure.","D":"Correct. A solicitor must maintain professional independence and cannot allow commercial pressure from a client to override regulatory obligations, including honesty and integrity.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No professional rule applies to commercial clients' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Professional independence cannot be surrendered to client commercial pressure."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Principles, including independence, honesty and integrity.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Professional independence cannot be surrendered to client commercial pressure.","common_trap":"Treating client autonomy as requiring compliance with improper instructions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"26072b09dc8f8cf4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0110","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Complaints information","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor’s firm is instructed by an individual client. The client asks at the outset what happens if she is dissatisfied with the service or bill.\n\nWhat should the firm’s regulatory arrangements include?","options":{"A":"No complaints information until litigation begins.","B":"A promise that no complaint will ever be made.","C":"A requirement that all complaints be waived before work starts.","D":"Only the name of the fee earner.","E":"Information about the firm’s complaints procedure and relevant rights to complain, including applicable external complaint routes."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"SRA regulatory requirements include providing clients with appropriate information about how to complain and relevant external complaint rights, alongside handling complaints promptly, fairly and free of charge.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No complaints information until litigation begins' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Clients should receive appropriate complaints information and access to a compliant complaints process.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A promise that no complaint will ever be made' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Clients should receive appropriate complaints information and access to a compliant complaints process.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A requirement that all complaints be waived before work starts' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Clients should receive appropriate complaints information and access to a compliant complaints process.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Clients should receive appropriate complaints information and access to a compliant complaints process.","E":"Correct. SRA regulatory requirements include providing clients with appropriate information about how to complain and relevant external complaint rights, alongside handling complaints promptly, fairly and free of charge."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct and SRA Transparency/complaints requirements as applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Clients should receive appropriate complaints information and access to a compliant complaints process.","common_trap":"Treating complaints information as something disclosed only after a dispute has arisen.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"493c134f1623c6e6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0111","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Mistake — common mistake","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Both parties contract for the sale of a specific cargo that, unknown to either of them and without their fault, had already been destroyed before the contract was made. The existence of that cargo is fundamental to the transaction.\n\nWhich doctrine is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Common mistake as to the existence of the subject matter.","B":"Economic duress.","C":"Promissory estoppel.","D":"Contributory negligence.","E":"Adverse possession."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A sufficiently fundamental common mistake existing at formation, including in appropriate circumstances the non-existence of specific subject matter, can prevent the contract operating as the parties assumed. The precise effect depends on the governing common-law and statutory rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A sufficiently fundamental common mistake existing at formation, including in appropriate circumstances the non-existence of specific subject matter, can prevent the contract operating as the parties assumed. The precise effect depends on the governing common-law and statutory rules.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Economic duress' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A fundamental shared mistake existing at formation may affect contractual validity.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Promissory estoppel' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A fundamental shared mistake existing at formation may affect contractual validity.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Contributory negligence' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A fundamental shared mistake existing at formation may affect contractual validity.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A fundamental shared mistake existing at formation may affect contractual validity."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law mistake principles; Sale of Goods Act 1979, s 6 where applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A fundamental shared mistake existing at formation may affect contractual validity.","common_trap":"Confusing a pre-contract mistake with frustration, which concerns supervening events after formation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4b83e0daec85e2ca"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0112","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Misrepresentation — rescission bars","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer enters a contract after an actionable misrepresentation. After discovering the truth, the buyer expressly tells the seller that she nevertheless intends to keep the contract and continues performing it with full knowledge of the facts.\n\nWhich equitable bar to rescission is most clearly raised?","options":{"A":"Frustration.","B":"Affirmation.","C":"Illegality in every case.","D":"Lapse before contract formation.","E":"Contributory negligence."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A representee who, with knowledge of the misrepresentation and the right to rescind, unequivocally elects to continue the contract may affirm it and lose rescission, although other remedies may remain.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Affirmation can bar rescission after discovery of a misrepresentation.","B":"Correct. A representee who, with knowledge of the misrepresentation and the right to rescind, unequivocally elects to continue the contract may affirm it and lose rescission, although other remedies may remain.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Affirmation can bar rescission after discovery of a misrepresentation.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Lapse before contract formation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Affirmation can bar rescission after discovery of a misrepresentation.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Contributory negligence' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Affirmation can bar rescission after discovery of a misrepresentation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law/equitable misrepresentation principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Affirmation can bar rescission after discovery of a misrepresentation.","common_trap":"Assuming rescission remains available indefinitely regardless of a knowing election to affirm.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"82b18bc416f9566e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0113","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Privity — assignment of contractual rights","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A supplier is owed £50,000 under a contract and wants to transfer the benefit of that debt to a finance company. The contract does not prohibit assignment and the issue concerns transfer of the contractual benefit, not delegation of the supplier’s performance obligations.\n\nWhich concept is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Frustration.","B":"Novation is always the only possible method of transferring a debt receivable.","C":"Assignment of contractual rights.","D":"Adverse possession.","E":"Vicarious liability."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Contractual benefits, including debts, can generally be assigned subject to contractual and legal restrictions. Assignment of rights is distinct from transferring contractual burdens, which ordinarily requires different machinery such as novation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Contractual benefits may generally be assigned, while burdens cannot simply be assigned in the same way.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contractual benefits may generally be assigned, while burdens cannot simply be assigned in the same way.","C":"Correct. Contractual benefits, including debts, can generally be assigned subject to contractual and legal restrictions. Assignment of rights is distinct from transferring contractual burdens, which ordinarily requires different machinery such as novation.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Contractual benefits may generally be assigned, while burdens cannot simply be assigned in the same way.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Vicarious liability' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Contractual benefits may generally be assigned, while burdens cannot simply be assigned in the same way."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 136 for statutory legal assignment; equitable assignment principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contractual benefits may generally be assigned, while burdens cannot simply be assigned in the same way.","common_trap":"Assuming transfer of a contractual benefit always requires substitution of the entire contract by novation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"83140bcea5724529"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0114","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — remoteness","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant negligently causes a minor collision. The claimant seeks damages for a bizarre type of physical damage that no reasonable person could have foreseen as a kind of consequence of the negligence.\n\nWhich principle is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Strict liability for every consequence however extraordinary.","B":"Contractual consideration.","C":"Parliamentary privilege.","D":"Remoteness: the kind of damage must ordinarily be reasonably foreseeable.","E":"Rescission."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"In negligence, recoverable damage is limited by remoteness. The relevant inquiry ordinarily asks whether the kind or type of damage was reasonably foreseeable, not whether its precise extent was foreseeable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Negligence damages are subject to the reasonable-foreseeability remoteness test.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Contractual consideration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Negligence damages are subject to the reasonable-foreseeability remoteness test.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Parliamentary privilege' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Negligence damages are subject to the reasonable-foreseeability remoteness test.","D":"Correct. In negligence, recoverable damage is limited by remoteness. The relevant inquiry ordinarily asks whether the kind or type of damage was reasonably foreseeable, not whether its precise extent was foreseeable.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Rescission' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Negligence damages are subject to the reasonable-foreseeability remoteness test."},"primary_legal_authority":"The Wagon Mound (No 1); general negligence principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Negligence damages are subject to the reasonable-foreseeability remoteness test.","common_trap":"Assuming factual causation makes every consequence recoverable.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0002cfae0b0ad00b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0115","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Employers' liability — personal duty","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An employer provides employees with a dangerous system for moving heavy machinery and fails to train them adequately. An employee is injured while following that system. The employer argues that responsibility for workplace safety was entirely delegated to a supervisor.\n\nWhich principle is most relevant?","options":{"A":"An employer can never owe a direct duty to employees.","B":"Only occupiers’ liability can apply at work.","C":"An employee assumes all workplace risks by accepting employment.","D":"Training is legally irrelevant.","E":"An employer can owe personal duties concerning matters such as competent staff, adequate equipment and a safe system of work; delegation does not necessarily eliminate the employer’s duty."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"At common law employers owe personal duties concerning workplace safety, including a safe system of work. Assigning operational tasks to another employee does not necessarily discharge the employer’s own duty.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Employers can owe non-delegable/personal common-law duties regarding workplace safety.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Employers can owe non-delegable/personal common-law duties regarding workplace safety.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'An employee assumes all workplace risks by accepting employment' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Employers can owe non-delegable/personal common-law duties regarding workplace safety.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Training is legally irrelevant' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Employers can owe non-delegable/personal common-law duties regarding workplace safety.","E":"Correct. At common law employers owe personal duties concerning workplace safety, including a safe system of work. Assigning operational tasks to another employee does not necessarily discharge the employer’s own duty."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law employers’ liability principles, including Wilsons & Clyde Coal Co v English.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Employers can owe non-delegable/personal common-law duties regarding workplace safety.","common_trap":"Assuming internal delegation automatically eliminates the employer’s duty of care.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"494b16a72d761469"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0116","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — serious harm","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company brings a defamation claim over a publication it says damaged its trading reputation. It assumes that showing defamatory meaning alone is sufficient and that it need not address the statutory serious-harm requirement.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"The Defamation Act 2013 requires serious harm; for a body trading for profit, serious harm is not established unless the publication has caused or is likely to cause serious financial loss.","B":"Serious harm applies only to criminal cases.","C":"Every defamatory statement automatically produces liability.","D":"Companies can never sue in defamation.","E":"Financial loss is irrelevant to a trading company’s serious-harm threshold."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 1 imposes a serious-harm threshold. For a body trading for profit, the statutory requirement is tied to serious financial loss caused or likely to be caused by the publication.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 1 imposes a serious-harm threshold. For a body trading for profit, the statutory requirement is tied to serious financial loss caused or likely to be caused by the publication.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Defamation liability includes the statutory serious-harm threshold, with a specific rule for profit-making bodies.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Defamation liability includes the statutory serious-harm threshold, with a specific rule for profit-making bodies.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Defamation liability includes the statutory serious-harm threshold, with a specific rule for profit-making bodies.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Financial loss is irrelevant to a trading company’s serious-harm threshold' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Defamation liability includes the statutory serious-harm threshold, with a specific rule for profit-making bodies."},"primary_legal_authority":"Defamation Act 2013, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Defamation liability includes the statutory serious-harm threshold, with a specific rule for profit-making bodies.","common_trap":"Stopping the analysis once defamatory meaning is identified.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d14bb01f935bc4fc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0117","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — section 3 interpretation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A court interprets primary legislation in proceedings engaging a Convention right. The statutory wording is capable of more than one interpretation.\n\nWhat interpretive obligation under the Human Rights Act should the court consider?","options":{"A":"The court must repeal any legislation it dislikes.","B":"So far as possible, legislation must be read and given effect in a way compatible with Convention rights.","C":"Convention rights can never affect statutory interpretation.","D":"Only ministers may interpret legislation compatibly.","E":"Section 3 applies only to contracts."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 3 requires primary and subordinate legislation to be read and given effect, so far as possible, compatibly with Convention rights. It is distinct from a declaration of incompatibility under section 4.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. HRA section 3 imposes a compatibility-oriented interpretive obligation so far as possible.","B":"Correct. Section 3 requires primary and subordinate legislation to be read and given effect, so far as possible, compatibly with Convention rights. It is distinct from a declaration of incompatibility under section 4.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. HRA section 3 imposes a compatibility-oriented interpretive obligation so far as possible.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. HRA section 3 imposes a compatibility-oriented interpretive obligation so far as possible.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. HRA section 3 imposes a compatibility-oriented interpretive obligation so far as possible."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"HRA section 3 imposes a compatibility-oriented interpretive obligation so far as possible.","common_trap":"Jumping directly to a declaration of incompatibility without first considering the interpretive obligation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"722ffd6ff9742605"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0118","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — illegality","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A public authority uses a statutory power to achieve a purpose wholly outside the purpose for which Parliament conferred that power.\n\nWhich ground of judicial review is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Private nuisance.","B":"Contributory negligence.","C":"Illegality, including use of a statutory power for an improper purpose.","D":"Frustration.","E":"Passing off."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A public body must use statutory powers within the legal limits and purposes for which they were conferred. Use for an improper purpose is a conventional form of illegality.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Private nuisance' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Judicial review for illegality can address use of statutory power for an improper purpose.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Contributory negligence' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Judicial review for illegality can address use of statutory power for an improper purpose.","C":"Correct. A public body must use statutory powers within the legal limits and purposes for which they were conferred. Use for an improper purpose is a conventional form of illegality.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Judicial review for illegality can address use of statutory power for an improper purpose.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Passing off' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Judicial review for illegality can address use of statutory power for an improper purpose."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administrative-law principles of illegality and proper purpose.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Judicial review for illegality can address use of statutory power for an improper purpose.","common_trap":"Assuming possession of a statutory power permits its use for any objective whatsoever.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c122df1b38555ad1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0119","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Rule of law — executive power","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A government department claims that it may impose a coercive legal obligation on citizens even though neither statute nor a recognised prerogative power authorises it.\n\nWhich constitutional principle most directly challenges that claim?","options":{"A":"The executive may create any coercive power simply by announcement.","B":"Only private companies require legal authority.","C":"Courts cannot examine the legal basis of executive action.","D":"The rule of law requires executive action to have lawful authority.","E":"Parliamentary sovereignty means ministers personally possess Parliament’s legislative powers."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The rule of law includes the principle that executive action must have a lawful basis. Government cannot ordinarily impose coercive obligations merely because it considers them desirable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The executive may create any coercive power simply by announcement' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Executive coercive action requires lawful authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Executive coercive action requires lawful authority.","C":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Executive coercive action requires lawful authority.","D":"Correct. The rule of law includes the principle that executive action must have a lawful basis. Government cannot ordinarily impose coercive obligations merely because it considers them desirable.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Parliamentary sovereignty means ministers personally possess Parliament’s legislative powers' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Executive coercive action requires lawful authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Constitutional rule-of-law principles; Entick v Carrington.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Executive coercive action requires lawful authority.","common_trap":"Confusing parliamentary sovereignty with unlimited autonomous ministerial power.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"db8e4e4f5d553552"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0120","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Grant — will annexed","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator leaves a valid will but appoints no executor. A person entitled under the probate rules seeks authority to administer the estate according to the will.\n\nWhich grant is ordinarily relevant?","options":{"A":"Grant of probate to a non-existent executor.","B":"Letters of administration on a wholly intestate estate only.","C":"A lasting power of attorney.","D":"A bankruptcy order.","E":"Grant of letters of administration with the will annexed."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Where there is a valid will but no executor able and willing to prove it, the appropriate grant may be letters of administration with the will annexed, subject to the order of entitlement under the probate rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Grant of probate to a non-existent executor' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A valid will without an acting executor can require letters of administration with will annexed.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Letters of administration on a wholly intestate estate only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A valid will without an acting executor can require letters of administration with will annexed.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A lasting power of attorney' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A valid will without an acting executor can require letters of administration with will annexed.","D":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A valid will without an acting executor can require letters of administration with will annexed.","E":"Correct. Where there is a valid will but no executor able and willing to prove it, the appropriate grant may be letters of administration with the will annexed, subject to the order of entitlement under the probate rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Non-Contentious Probate Rules; Administration of Estates Act 1925.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A valid will without an acting executor can require letters of administration with will annexed.","common_trap":"Assuming every testate estate necessarily produces a grant of probate.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4f639fa3aa445bdd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0121","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"PR protection — unknown creditors","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Personal representatives are ready to distribute an estate but are concerned about unknown creditors. They want to reduce the risk of personal liability if a creditor later emerges, while recognising that a creditor’s underlying claim against beneficiaries may be a separate matter.\n\nWhich protective procedure should they consider?","options":{"A":"Advertising for claims in accordance with the statutory notice procedure before distribution.","B":"Destroying the estate accounts.","C":"Distributing immediately and ignoring all creditors.","D":"Obtaining consent from one beneficiary only.","E":"Closing the deceased’s bank account without inquiry."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Personal representatives can obtain statutory protection against personal liability to certain unknown claimants by giving the prescribed notices and waiting the required period before distribution, while the claimant’s substantive rights against recipients may remain.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Personal representatives can obtain statutory protection against personal liability to certain unknown claimants by giving the prescribed notices and waiting the required period before distribution, while the claimant’s substantive rights against recipients may remain.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Destroying the estate accounts' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Statutory notices can protect PRs personally when distributing against unknown claims if the requirements are followed.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Distributing immediately and ignoring all creditors' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Statutory notices can protect PRs personally when distributing against unknown claims if the requirements are followed.","D":"Incorrect. This makes consent or agreement determinative when the legal test requires the identified statutory or doctrinal conditions. Statutory notices can protect PRs personally when distributing against unknown claims if the requirements are followed.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Closing the deceased’s bank account without inquiry' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Statutory notices can protect PRs personally when distributing against unknown claims if the requirements are followed."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 1925, s 27.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Statutory notices can protect PRs personally when distributing against unknown claims if the requirements are followed.","common_trap":"Assuming a notice extinguishes every creditor’s substantive claim.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bd987aa946191a04"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0122","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"IHT — gift with reservation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A parent gives a house outright to an adult child but continues living in the entire property rent-free for life under an informal understanding made at the time of the gift. The parent retains no legal title.\n\nWhich inheritance-tax concept is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Potentially exempt transfer that can never have any further IHT relevance.","B":"Gift with reservation of benefit.","C":"Business property relief automatically.","D":"Corporation tax.","E":"Ademption."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Where a donor gives property but retains enjoyment or benefit within the statutory rules, the gift-with-reservation regime can cause the property to remain relevant to the donor’s estate for IHT purposes despite the transfer of legal title.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Retained benefit after a lifetime gift can engage the gift-with-reservation rules.","B":"Correct. Where a donor gives property but retains enjoyment or benefit within the statutory rules, the gift-with-reservation regime can cause the property to remain relevant to the donor’s estate for IHT purposes despite the transfer of legal title.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Retained benefit after a lifetime gift can engage the gift-with-reservation rules.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Corporation tax' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Retained benefit after a lifetime gift can engage the gift-with-reservation rules.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Ademption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Retained benefit after a lifetime gift can engage the gift-with-reservation rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Finance Act 1986, ss 102 ff; IHT principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Retained benefit after a lifetime gift can engage the gift-with-reservation rules.","common_trap":"Assuming transfer of legal title alone necessarily removes the property from the donor’s IHT estate.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4e3668d8efd08e94"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0123","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Receipt of mixed money","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm receives one electronic payment containing £9,000 of client money and £1,000 that belongs to the firm for billed costs. The amounts are readily identifiable.\n\nHow should the firm approach the receipt under the Accounts Rules?","options":{"A":"The entire £10,000 permanently becomes client money.","B":"The entire sum must permanently remain in the business account.","C":"It should deal with the mixed receipt promptly so that client money is held in client account and the firm’s money is placed in the business account in accordance with the rules.","D":"Mixed receipts are prohibited and must always be returned to the payer.","E":"The firm may leave the money unidentified indefinitely."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require proper treatment of receipts containing client and non-client money, with allocation/transfers made promptly so the correct money is held in the appropriate account.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The entire £10,000 permanently becomes client money' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Mixed receipts must be promptly allocated so client and firm money are held appropriately.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Mixed receipts must be promptly allocated so client and firm money are held appropriately.","C":"Correct. The Accounts Rules require proper treatment of receipts containing client and non-client money, with allocation/transfers made promptly so the correct money is held in the appropriate account.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Mixed receipts must be promptly allocated so client and firm money are held appropriately.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The firm may leave the money unidentified indefinitely' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Mixed receipts must be promptly allocated so client and firm money are held appropriately."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, including rules governing client money and mixed receipts.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Mixed receipts must be promptly allocated so client and firm money are held appropriately.","common_trap":"Treating the presence of any client money as changing ownership of the whole receipt.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2cacb568c12b01ae"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0124","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Strong v Bird","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A donor intends to make an immediate gift of a debt owed to her by a friend but does not complete the formal steps needed during her lifetime. The intention to give continues unchanged until death. The intended donee later becomes the donor’s personal representative, so legal title to the relevant chose passes to the donee in that representative capacity.\n\nWhich equitable exception should be considered?","options":{"A":"The rule that an imperfect gift can never be perfected by later events.","B":"Proprietary estoppel automatically.","C":"Frustration.","D":"The rule in Strong v Bird, subject to satisfaction of its requirements.","E":"The postal rule."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Strong v Bird is a recognised exception to the general rule against perfecting imperfect gifts. Broadly, a continuing intention to make an immediate gift may be perfected where the intended donee later acquires legal title as executor or administrator, subject to the doctrine’s requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Strong v Bird can perfect certain imperfect lifetime gifts when continuing intention and later acquisition of legal title coincide.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Strong v Bird can perfect certain imperfect lifetime gifts when continuing intention and later acquisition of legal title coincide.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Strong v Bird can perfect certain imperfect lifetime gifts when continuing intention and later acquisition of legal title coincide.","D":"Correct. Strong v Bird is a recognised exception to the general rule against perfecting imperfect gifts. Broadly, a continuing intention to make an immediate gift may be perfected where the intended donee later acquires legal title as executor or administrator, subject to the doctrine’s requirements.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The postal rule' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Strong v Bird can perfect certain imperfect lifetime gifts when continuing intention and later acquisition of legal title coincide."},"primary_legal_authority":"Strong v Bird (1874) LR 18 Eq 315; SRA FLK2 from September 2026.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Strong v Bird can perfect certain imperfect lifetime gifts when continuing intention and later acquisition of legal title coincide.","common_trap":"Applying the general imperfect-gift rule without considering an expressly examinable exception.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7034bf06e205b3c7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0125","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Choithram v Pagarani","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor declares that he gives property to a charitable foundation of which he is himself one of several trustees, but does not formally transfer the property to all the trustees before death. The issue is whether equity necessarily treats the disposition as wholly ineffective.\n\nWhich authority is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Milroy v Lord establishes that every such declaration is automatically effective regardless of facts.","B":"Hadley v Baxendale.","C":"Rylands v Fletcher.","D":"Donoghue v Stevenson.","E":"Choithram v Pagarani, concerning circumstances in which a donor who is also one of the trustees may have constituted the trust despite incomplete transfer to the other trustees."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Choithram is an expressly examinable qualification to the imperfect-gift principle. Where the donor is one of the trustees and the words and circumstances amount to a trust obligation, equity may regard the donor as bound to hold the property with the other trustees.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Choithram can apply where the donor is also a trustee and has sufficiently constituted a trust obligation despite incomplete transfer to co-trustees.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Hadley v Baxendale' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Choithram can apply where the donor is also a trustee and has sufficiently constituted a trust obligation despite incomplete transfer to co-trustees.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Rylands v Fletcher' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Choithram can apply where the donor is also a trustee and has sufficiently constituted a trust obligation despite incomplete transfer to co-trustees.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Donoghue v Stevenson' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Choithram can apply where the donor is also a trustee and has sufficiently constituted a trust obligation despite incomplete transfer to co-trustees.","E":"Correct. Choithram is an expressly examinable qualification to the imperfect-gift principle. Where the donor is one of the trustees and the words and circumstances amount to a trust obligation, equity may regard the donor as bound to hold the property with the other trustees."},"primary_legal_authority":"Choithram International SA v Pagarani [2001] 1 WLR 1; SRA FLK2 from September 2026.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Choithram can apply where the donor is also a trustee and has sufficiently constituted a trust obligation despite incomplete transfer to co-trustees.","common_trap":"Treating either Milroy v Lord or Choithram as an absolute rule without analysing the form of the intended disposition.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b578d3f31337ee97"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0126","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Lease — exclusive possession","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An occupier is granted possession of a self-contained flat for a fixed term at rent. The written agreement is labelled a “licence”, but in substance the occupier has exclusive possession and the arrangement is not within an exceptional category.\n\nWhich principle is most relevant to classification?","options":{"A":"The substance of the arrangement, including exclusive possession, can indicate a lease despite the label chosen by the parties.","B":"Calling the agreement a licence conclusively prevents a tenancy.","C":"Rent can never be relevant to a lease.","D":"Only freehold owners can have exclusive possession.","E":"A lease exists only if the document uses the word tenancy."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Classification depends on the substantive rights created rather than the label alone. Exclusive possession for a term is a central indicator of a tenancy, subject to recognised exceptions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Classification depends on the substantive rights created rather than the label alone. Exclusive possession for a term is a central indicator of a tenancy, subject to recognised exceptions.","B":"Incorrect. This makes consent or agreement determinative when the legal test requires the identified statutory or doctrinal conditions. Substance prevails over labels when distinguishing a lease from a licence.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Substance prevails over labels when distinguishing a lease from a licence.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Substance prevails over labels when distinguishing a lease from a licence.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Substance prevails over labels when distinguishing a lease from a licence."},"primary_legal_authority":"Street v Mountford [1985] AC 809.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Substance prevails over labels when distinguishing a lease from a licence.","common_trap":"Treating the parties’ chosen heading as conclusive.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d7698e39ad668bac"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0127","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Overreaching","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two trustees hold a registered house on trust for themselves and another beneficiary. They sell the property and the purchaser pays the purchase money to both trustees. The beneficiary’s equitable interest is capable of being overreached and no special exception applies.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect of payment to the two trustees?","options":{"A":"The purchaser necessarily takes subject to every beneficial interest forever.","B":"The beneficial interest can be overreached, shifting from the land to the sale proceeds so the purchaser takes free of that equitable interest.","C":"Overreaching transfers the legal title to the beneficiary.","D":"Two trustees can never sell trust land.","E":"The beneficiary automatically becomes the purchaser’s tenant."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Where statutory requirements are met, including payment of capital money to at least two trustees or a trust corporation, overreaching detaches qualifying equitable interests from the land and transfers them to the proceeds.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Overreaching can shift beneficial interests from land to capital money on a qualifying disposition.","B":"Correct. Where statutory requirements are met, including payment of capital money to at least two trustees or a trust corporation, overreaching detaches qualifying equitable interests from the land and transfers them to the proceeds.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Overreaching transfers the legal title to the beneficiary' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Overreaching can shift beneficial interests from land to capital money on a qualifying disposition.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Overreaching can shift beneficial interests from land to capital money on a qualifying disposition.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Overreaching can shift beneficial interests from land to capital money on a qualifying disposition."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, ss 2 and 27.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Overreaching can shift beneficial interests from land to capital money on a qualifying disposition.","common_trap":"Confusing overreaching with priority based solely on registration or actual occupation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b95f2fbccfeae5e3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0128","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Pre-completion search — OS1","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer is about to complete the purchase of the whole of a registered title. The buyer’s solicitor wants to check for adverse entries since the official copies were obtained and secure a priority period for the intended registration.\n\nWhich search is ordinarily appropriate?","options":{"A":"A local authority search only.","B":"A bankruptcy search against the seller instead of any land-registry search.","C":"An official search of the register with priority against the whole title (OS1).","D":"A company incorporation application.","E":"A search of the probate registry."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"An OS1 is the standard Land Registry priority search used before completion of a disposition of the whole of a registered title. It reveals relevant intervening entries and provides a priority period for registration.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A local authority search only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. OS1 is the conventional pre-completion priority search for a disposition of the whole registered title.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A bankruptcy search against the seller instead of any land-registry search' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. OS1 is the conventional pre-completion priority search for a disposition of the whole registered title.","C":"Correct. An OS1 is the standard Land Registry priority search used before completion of a disposition of the whole of a registered title. It reveals relevant intervening entries and provides a priority period for registration.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A company incorporation application' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. OS1 is the conventional pre-completion priority search for a disposition of the whole registered title.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A search of the probate registry' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. OS1 is the conventional pre-completion priority search for a disposition of the whole registered title."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Rules; HM Land Registry practice on official searches with priority.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"OS1 is the conventional pre-completion priority search for a disposition of the whole registered title.","common_trap":"Confusing pre-contract local searches with pre-completion Land Registry priority protection.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7b6b37552acfd7cd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0129","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Theft — appropriation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A shopper takes an item from a store shelf and dishonestly switches its price label intending to pay a much lower price and permanently deprive the retailer of the difference. The issue is whether appropriation requires the shopper to assume every right of the owner.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the Theft Act?","options":{"A":"Appropriation occurs only after the shopper leaves the store.","B":"Every right of the owner must be assumed simultaneously.","C":"Appropriation requires physical destruction of the property.","D":"Appropriation can consist of assuming any of the rights of an owner; assumption of all ownership rights is unnecessary.","E":"Consent always prevents appropriation as a matter of law."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 3 defines appropriation as any assumption of the rights of an owner. It does not require assumption of every proprietary right, and later authorities confirm the breadth of the concept.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Appropriation requires assumption of any right of an owner, not all rights.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Appropriation requires assumption of any right of an owner, not all rights.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Appropriation requires physical destruction of the property' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Appropriation requires assumption of any right of an owner, not all rights.","D":"Correct. Section 3 defines appropriation as any assumption of the rights of an owner. It does not require assumption of every proprietary right, and later authorities confirm the breadth of the concept.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Appropriation requires assumption of any right of an owner, not all rights."},"primary_legal_authority":"Theft Act 1968, s 3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Appropriation requires assumption of any right of an owner, not all rights.","common_trap":"Setting the actus reus threshold too high by demanding complete dominion over the property.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3e9a474512e6b5a8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0130","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Police interview — right to legal advice","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An arrested suspect at a police station asks to speak privately with a solicitor before interview. No lawful basis exists for delaying access under the exceptional provisions.\n\nWhat is the ordinary position?","options":{"A":"Legal advice is available only after charge.","B":"Police may refuse legal advice whenever an interview would be quicker without it.","C":"Only suspects accused of indictable-only offences may obtain legal advice.","D":"The suspect must first admit the offence.","E":"The suspect is entitled to consult a solicitor privately at any time, subject to the limited statutory/PACE exceptions."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"PACE provides an arrested person held at a police station with a right to consult a solicitor privately, subject to tightly controlled exceptions concerning delay in specified cases.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A detained suspect ordinarily has a right to private legal advice at the police station.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A detained suspect ordinarily has a right to private legal advice at the police station.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A detained suspect ordinarily has a right to private legal advice at the police station.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A detained suspect ordinarily has a right to private legal advice at the police station.","E":"Correct. PACE provides an arrested person held at a police station with a right to consult a solicitor privately, subject to tightly controlled exceptions concerning delay in specified cases."},"primary_legal_authority":"Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984, s 58; PACE Code C.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A detained suspect ordinarily has a right to private legal advice at the police station.","common_trap":"Treating access to legal advice as a discretionary police courtesy.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ccc0594a836fcc70"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0131","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership — dissolution by notice","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Three people carry on business in partnership without agreeing a fixed term. Their partnership agreement contains no contrary provision about termination. One partner decides that she no longer wishes the partnership to continue.\n\nWhich statement best describes the ordinary statutory route available to that partner?","options":{"A":"A partnership for an undefined time can ordinarily be dissolved by a partner giving notice to the other partners, subject to any agreement between them.","B":"She can leave only if every creditor consents.","C":"A partnership can be dissolved only by a court order.","D":"She must first convert the firm into an LLP.","E":"Notice is ineffective unless given through Companies House."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where no fixed term has been agreed, the Partnership Act permits dissolution by a partner giving notice to the others, subject to the partnership agreement.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where no fixed term has been agreed, the Partnership Act permits dissolution by a partner giving notice to the others, subject to the partnership agreement.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A partnership at will can ordinarily be dissolved by notice from a partner.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A partnership at will can ordinarily be dissolved by notice from a partner.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A partnership at will can ordinarily be dissolved by notice from a partner.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Notice is ineffective unless given through Companies House' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A partnership at will can ordinarily be dissolved by notice from a partner."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, s 32(c).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A partnership at will can ordinarily be dissolved by notice from a partner.","common_trap":"Assuming dissolution of an ordinary partnership always requires unanimity or a court order.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"93c6f28af185cc6f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0132","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Directors — declaration of interest","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A director of a private company has a personal financial interest in a proposed contract between the company and a supplier. The other directors do not know about that interest. No statutory exception applies.\n\nWhat should the director ordinarily do before the company enters into the transaction?","options":{"A":"Remain silent unless another director asks directly.","B":"Declare the nature and extent of the interest to the other directors in accordance with the Companies Act.","C":"Resign automatically because disclosure can never cure the issue.","D":"Tell Companies House instead of the board.","E":"Wait until after completion and disclose only if the transaction makes a loss."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 177 requires a director who is directly or indirectly interested in a proposed transaction or arrangement with the company to declare the nature and extent of that interest to the other directors, subject to statutory exceptions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Remain silent unless another director asks directly' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A director must disclose a relevant interest in a proposed company transaction as required by statute.","B":"Correct. Section 177 requires a director who is directly or indirectly interested in a proposed transaction or arrangement with the company to declare the nature and extent of that interest to the other directors, subject to statutory exceptions.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A director must disclose a relevant interest in a proposed company transaction as required by statute.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Tell Companies House instead of the board' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A director must disclose a relevant interest in a proposed company transaction as required by statute.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A director must disclose a relevant interest in a proposed company transaction as required by statute."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 177.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A director must disclose a relevant interest in a proposed company transaction as required by statute.","common_trap":"Confusing disclosure duties with an automatic requirement to resign.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0206f061864002dd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0133","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Capital gains tax — disposal","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual shareholder sells shares held as an investment for more than their allowable acquisition cost. The shares are not trading stock and no exemption eliminates the gain. Any rates, annual exempt amount or other monetary figures required by the question are supplied.\n\nWhich tax is principally relevant to the individual’s gain?","options":{"A":"Corporation tax because shares are always corporate assets.","B":"Inheritance tax only.","C":"Capital gains tax, subject to the applicable computation, reliefs and exemptions.","D":"SDLT on the seller’s gain.","E":"VAT on every investment disposal."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"An individual’s chargeable gain on disposal of an investment asset falls within the capital gains tax regime, subject to statutory exemptions and reliefs. The SQE supplies monetary rates or thresholds where their application is required.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Individuals can incur CGT on chargeable gains from disposals of investment assets.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Inheritance tax only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Individuals can incur CGT on chargeable gains from disposals of investment assets.","C":"Correct. An individual’s chargeable gain on disposal of an investment asset falls within the capital gains tax regime, subject to statutory exemptions and reliefs. The SQE supplies monetary rates or thresholds where their application is required.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'SDLT on the seller’s gain' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Individuals can incur CGT on chargeable gains from disposals of investment assets.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Individuals can incur CGT on chargeable gains from disposals of investment assets."},"primary_legal_authority":"Taxation of Chargeable Gains Act 1992; SRA FLK1 taxation specification.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Individuals can incur CGT on chargeable gains from disposals of investment assets.","common_trap":"Confusing the company-level corporation tax regime with taxation of an individual shareholder’s gain.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"abc3b7eeb00708f9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0134","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Bankruptcy — bankruptcy order","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual sole trader is unable to pay substantial personal business debts. A creditor is considering formal personal insolvency proceedings. The creditor mistakenly assumes that corporate liquidation is the appropriate procedure because the debts arose from a business.\n\nWhich insolvency regime is principally relevant to the individual?","options":{"A":"Corporate liquidation under the Companies Act only.","B":"Administration of a company.","C":"A compulsory share redemption.","D":"Personal bankruptcy under the Insolvency Act framework, assuming the statutory requirements for a creditor’s petition are satisfied.","E":"Dissolution of an LLP."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A sole trader is an individual, so personal insolvency procedures apply. Bankruptcy is distinct from liquidation of a company, although the debts may have arisen from trading activity.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Corporate liquidation under the Companies Act only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An insolvent sole trader is subject to personal insolvency rather than corporate liquidation.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Administration of a company' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An insolvent sole trader is subject to personal insolvency rather than corporate liquidation.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A compulsory share redemption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An insolvent sole trader is subject to personal insolvency rather than corporate liquidation.","D":"Correct. A sole trader is an individual, so personal insolvency procedures apply. Bankruptcy is distinct from liquidation of a company, although the debts may have arisen from trading activity.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Dissolution of an LLP' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An insolvent sole trader is subject to personal insolvency rather than corporate liquidation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, personal bankruptcy provisions.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An insolvent sole trader is subject to personal insolvency rather than corporate liquidation.","common_trap":"Treating the business activity of a sole trader as creating a separate company for insolvency purposes.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"15870fcedc0fc0dc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0135","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Pre-action conduct","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant has a commercial dispute for which no specialist pre-action protocol applies. The claimant wants to issue immediately without sending meaningful information about the claim or considering settlement, although there is no urgency or limitation problem.\n\nWhich is the best advice?","options":{"A":"Pre-action conduct is relevant only after judgment.","B":"A claimant must always issue before communicating with the defendant.","C":"ADR is prohibited before proceedings.","D":"Failure to follow pre-action expectations can never affect costs or case management.","E":"The parties should ordinarily follow the Practice Direction on Pre-Action Conduct and Protocols, including exchanging sufficient information and considering settlement/ADR before proceedings where appropriate."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Where no specific protocol applies, the general Practice Direction sets expectations concerning exchange of information, consideration of settlement and proportionate pre-action conduct. Non-compliance can have procedural and costs consequences.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Parties ordinarily should comply with applicable pre-action requirements before issuing proceedings.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Parties ordinarily should comply with applicable pre-action requirements before issuing proceedings.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'ADR is prohibited before proceedings' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Parties ordinarily should comply with applicable pre-action requirements before issuing proceedings.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Parties ordinarily should comply with applicable pre-action requirements before issuing proceedings.","E":"Correct. Where no specific protocol applies, the general Practice Direction sets expectations concerning exchange of information, consideration of settlement and proportionate pre-action conduct. Non-compliance can have procedural and costs consequences."},"primary_legal_authority":"Practice Direction — Pre-Action Conduct and Protocols.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Parties ordinarily should comply with applicable pre-action requirements before issuing proceedings.","common_trap":"Assuming civil procedure begins only when the claim form is issued.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6e76771b2c456340"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0136","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Particulars of claim — service timing","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant serves a claim form within the jurisdiction without serving the particulars of claim at the same time. No special direction applies.\n\nBy when must the particulars ordinarily be served, subject also to the overall claim-form service limit?","options":{"A":"Within 14 days after service of the claim form.","B":"Within six months after trial.","C":"Only after the defence.","D":"Within 48 hours of issue in every case.","E":"There is no time limit."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where particulars are not contained in or served with the claim form, CPR 7.4 ordinarily requires service within 14 days after service of the claim form, while still respecting the claim form’s applicable service period.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where particulars are not contained in or served with the claim form, CPR 7.4 ordinarily requires service within 14 days after service of the claim form, while still respecting the claim form’s applicable service period.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Within six months after trial' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Separate particulars ordinarily must be served within the CPR 7.4 timetable.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Separate particulars ordinarily must be served within the CPR 7.4 timetable.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Separate particulars ordinarily must be served within the CPR 7.4 timetable.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'There is no time limit' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Separate particulars ordinarily must be served within the CPR 7.4 timetable."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 7.4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Separate particulars ordinarily must be served within the CPR 7.4 timetable.","common_trap":"Confusing the particulars timetable with the four-month claim-form service period.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ead456c14a55355c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0137","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Appeals — permission","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party loses a civil case and wants to appeal solely because it dislikes the result. The proposed appeal identifies no real prospect of success and no other compelling reason for it to be heard.\n\nWhat is the ordinary position concerning permission to appeal?","options":{"A":"Every losing party has an unconditional appeal as of right.","B":"Permission is generally required, and the usual test asks whether the appeal has a real prospect of success or there is some other compelling reason for it to be heard.","C":"Permission depends only on the amount of costs paid.","D":"The trial judge must automatically reverse the decision first.","E":"Civil appeals are available only to claimants."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Under CPR Part 52, permission to appeal is ordinarily required and the stated threshold concerns a real prospect of success or another compelling reason.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Civil appeals ordinarily require permission under the CPR test.","B":"Correct. Under CPR Part 52, permission to appeal is ordinarily required and the stated threshold concerns a real prospect of success or another compelling reason.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Civil appeals ordinarily require permission under the CPR test.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Civil appeals ordinarily require permission under the CPR test.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Civil appeals ordinarily require permission under the CPR test."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 52.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Civil appeals ordinarily require permission under the CPR test.","common_trap":"Treating an appeal as an automatic retrial whenever a party is dissatisfied.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8a0f46d2e5aa766f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0138","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Privilege — legal advice privilege","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client confidentially asks its solicitor for legal advice about the meaning of a contractual obligation. The communication is made in the professional legal-adviser relationship for the purpose of obtaining that advice and no exception or waiver applies.\n\nWhich protection is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Without-prejudice privilege only.","B":"Parliamentary privilege.","C":"Legal advice privilege.","D":"Public-interest immunity automatically.","E":"No privilege can attach to communications before litigation is contemplated."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Legal advice privilege protects qualifying confidential communications between lawyer and client made for the purpose of giving or obtaining legal advice. Unlike litigation privilege, pending or contemplated litigation is not an essential condition.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Without-prejudice privilege only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Legal advice privilege can protect confidential lawyer-client communications made for legal advice.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Parliamentary privilege' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Legal advice privilege can protect confidential lawyer-client communications made for legal advice.","C":"Correct. Legal advice privilege protects qualifying confidential communications between lawyer and client made for the purpose of giving or obtaining legal advice. Unlike litigation privilege, pending or contemplated litigation is not an essential condition.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Legal advice privilege can protect confidential lawyer-client communications made for legal advice.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No privilege can attach to communications before litigation is contemplated' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Legal advice privilege can protect confidential lawyer-client communications made for legal advice."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law legal professional privilege principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Legal advice privilege can protect confidential lawyer-client communications made for legal advice.","common_trap":"Assuming privilege exists only once litigation is contemplated.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ec00959f63f1c91c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0139","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Client care — costs information","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor is instructed on a new matter. The likely overall cost cannot yet be stated precisely, but the solicitor can explain the charging basis and provide an informed estimate subject to identified uncertainties.\n\nWhat is the appropriate regulatory approach?","options":{"A":"Provide no costs information until the final bill.","B":"Guarantee a fixed fee even where none has been agreed.","C":"Discuss costs only if the client is legally qualified.","D":"Give clients the best possible information about likely overall cost and how charges will arise, updating it as the matter progresses.","E":"Costs information is unnecessary for commercial work."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The SRA Code requires solicitors to ensure clients receive the best possible information about how their matter will be priced and, both at engagement and when appropriate as it progresses, about likely overall cost.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Provide no costs information until the final bill' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Solicitors must provide meaningful and updated costs information.","B":"Incorrect. This makes consent or agreement determinative when the legal test requires the identified statutory or doctrinal conditions. Solicitors must provide meaningful and updated costs information.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Solicitors must provide meaningful and updated costs information.","D":"Correct. The SRA Code requires solicitors to ensure clients receive the best possible information about how their matter will be priced and, both at engagement and when appropriate as it progresses, about likely overall cost.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Costs information is unnecessary for commercial work' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Solicitors must provide meaningful and updated costs information."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 8.7.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Solicitors must provide meaningful and updated costs information.","common_trap":"Treating uncertainty as a reason to give no costs information at all.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"870045e3773789ef"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0140","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"AML — customer due diligence","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor in the regulated sector is asked to establish a new business relationship for a transaction within the Money Laundering Regulations. The client is a company. No simplified treatment has yet been justified.\n\nWhich compliance step is fundamental at the outset?","options":{"A":"Rely solely on the person introducing the client.","B":"Accept anonymity whenever the transaction is commercially urgent.","C":"Carry out CDD only after the transaction completes.","D":"CDD applies only to cash transactions.","E":"Conduct the required customer due diligence, including identifying and verifying the client and, where applicable, beneficial ownership in accordance with the Regulations."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The regulated-sector framework requires customer due diligence in specified circumstances, including establishment of a business relationship, with identification/verification and beneficial-owner measures as applicable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Rely solely on the person introducing the client' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. CDD is a core AML requirement when establishing relevant business relationships.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. CDD is a core AML requirement when establishing relevant business relationships.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. CDD is a core AML requirement when establishing relevant business relationships.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. CDD is a core AML requirement when establishing relevant business relationships.","E":"Correct. The regulated-sector framework requires customer due diligence in specified circumstances, including establishment of a business relationship, with identification/verification and beneficial-owner measures as applicable."},"primary_legal_authority":"Money Laundering, Terrorist Financing and Transfer of Funds (Information on the Payer) Regulations 2017, as amended.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"CDD is a core AML requirement when establishing relevant business relationships.","common_trap":"Assuming commercial urgency or non-cash payment eliminates CDD.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"215f0069204b381c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0141","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Innominate terms","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contract contains a term that is neither classified by statute nor expressly designated as a condition or warranty. The term is properly treated as innominate. It is breached, but the consequences are minor and do not substantially deprive the innocent party of the benefit expected under the contract.\n\nWhat is the ordinary consequence for termination?","options":{"A":"The right to terminate depends on the seriousness of the consequences; a minor breach of this kind ordinarily supports damages but not termination.","B":"Any breach of an innominate term automatically permits termination.","C":"No remedy at all is available for breach of an innominate term.","D":"The contract is void ab initio.","E":"The innocent party acquires a proprietary interest in the defendant’s assets."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"For an innominate term, termination depends on whether the consequences of breach are sufficiently serious, classically whether they substantially deprive the innocent party of the contract’s benefit.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. For an innominate term, termination depends on whether the consequences of breach are sufficiently serious, classically whether they substantially deprive the innocent party of the contract’s benefit.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Consequences of breach determine whether breach of an innominate term justifies termination.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No remedy at all is available for breach of an innominate term' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Consequences of breach determine whether breach of an innominate term justifies termination.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The contract is void ab initio' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Consequences of breach determine whether breach of an innominate term justifies termination.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The innocent party acquires a proprietary interest in the defendant’s assets' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Consequences of breach determine whether breach of an innominate term justifies termination."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hongkong Fir Shipping Co Ltd v Kawasaki Kisen Kaisha Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Consequences of breach determine whether breach of an innominate term justifies termination.","common_trap":"Treating every contractual term as either a condition or warranty with fixed consequences.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"eb4124225b487d76"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0142","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Damages — mitigation","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A supplier breaches a contract. The customer could reasonably obtain an equivalent replacement immediately at modest additional cost but deliberately does nothing for months, allowing avoidable losses to accumulate.\n\nWhich damages principle is most relevant?","options":{"A":"The claimant must always do nothing after breach.","B":"The claimant must take reasonable steps to mitigate loss and cannot ordinarily recover avoidable loss attributable to an unreasonable failure to do so.","C":"Mitigation requires the claimant to take every imaginable step regardless of cost.","D":"Failure to mitigate eliminates liability for all loss, including unavoidable loss.","E":"Mitigation applies only in tort."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The innocent party must act reasonably to mitigate loss. The rule does not impose an absolute duty, but avoidable losses caused by an unreasonable failure to mitigate are generally irrecoverable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contract damages are subject to reasonable mitigation.","B":"Correct. The innocent party must act reasonably to mitigate loss. The rule does not impose an absolute duty, but avoidable losses caused by an unreasonable failure to mitigate are generally irrecoverable.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contract damages are subject to reasonable mitigation.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Failure to mitigate eliminates liability for all loss, including unavoidable loss' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Contract damages are subject to reasonable mitigation.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contract damages are subject to reasonable mitigation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law mitigation principles in contractual damages.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contract damages are subject to reasonable mitigation.","common_trap":"Treating mitigation either as no constraint at all or as a duty to take unreasonable measures.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"456d3479f2c92b12"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0143","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Liquidated damages and penalties","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial contract requires payment of £5 million for any breach, however trivial. The sum is wholly out of proportion to any legitimate interest the innocent party has in enforcement of the relevant obligation.\n\nWhich doctrine should be considered?","options":{"A":"Frustration.","B":"Promissory estoppel.","C":"The penalty rule, which may render the secondary payment obligation unenforceable if it imposes a detriment out of all proportion to the innocent party’s legitimate interest.","D":"Privity.","E":"Adverse possession."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The modern penalty doctrine focuses on whether a secondary obligation triggered by breach imposes a detriment out of all proportion to the innocent party’s legitimate interest in enforcement.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A disproportionate secondary obligation triggered by breach can engage the penalty rule.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Promissory estoppel' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A disproportionate secondary obligation triggered by breach can engage the penalty rule.","C":"Correct. The modern penalty doctrine focuses on whether a secondary obligation triggered by breach imposes a detriment out of all proportion to the innocent party’s legitimate interest in enforcement.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Privity' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A disproportionate secondary obligation triggered by breach can engage the penalty rule.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A disproportionate secondary obligation triggered by breach can engage the penalty rule."},"primary_legal_authority":"Cavendish Square Holding BV v Makdessi; ParkingEye Ltd v Beavis.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A disproportionate secondary obligation triggered by breach can engage the penalty rule.","common_trap":"Assuming every agreed damages clause is enforceable merely because sophisticated parties signed it.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"354d73ff6d368d9d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0144","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligent misstatement — assumption of responsibility","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An accountant gives a specific financial statement directly to a known client for the express purpose of deciding whether to make a particular investment. The accountant knows the client will rely on it without independent verification. The statement is negligently prepared and causes foreseeable financial loss.\n\nWhich concept is central to whether a duty of care for pure economic loss arises?","options":{"A":"Strict liability for all statements.","B":"Occupiers’ liability.","C":"Private nuisance.","D":"Assumption of responsibility and reasonable reliance in the Hedley Byrne line of authority.","E":"Vicarious liability only."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Negligent misstatement can support recovery for pure economic loss where a sufficiently proximate relationship involving assumption of responsibility and reasonable reliance exists.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Strict liability for all statements' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A special relationship involving assumption of responsibility can found a duty for negligent misstatement.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Occupiers’ liability' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A special relationship involving assumption of responsibility can found a duty for negligent misstatement.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Private nuisance' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A special relationship involving assumption of responsibility can found a duty for negligent misstatement.","D":"Correct. Negligent misstatement can support recovery for pure economic loss where a sufficiently proximate relationship involving assumption of responsibility and reasonable reliance exists.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Vicarious liability only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A special relationship involving assumption of responsibility can found a duty for negligent misstatement."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hedley Byrne & Co Ltd v Heller & Partners Ltd; subsequent authorities.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A special relationship involving assumption of responsibility can found a duty for negligent misstatement.","common_trap":"Applying the ordinary physical-damage negligence analysis without addressing the special rules for pure economic loss.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cb5a30724c21dd1c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0145","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Nuisance — locality","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A homeowner complains of persistent noise from neighbouring premises. The defendant argues that identical noise must always be either reasonable or unreasonable regardless of the character of the neighbourhood.\n\nWhich statement best reflects private nuisance?","options":{"A":"Locality is never relevant to nuisance.","B":"Any audible noise automatically constitutes nuisance.","C":"Nuisance requires physical injury to the claimant.","D":"Planning permission automatically immunises every activity from nuisance.","E":"The character of the locality can be relevant when assessing whether an interference with amenity is unreasonable."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Reasonableness in amenity nuisance is contextual. The character of the locality is a traditional factor, although it is not necessarily decisive and different considerations can apply to physical damage.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Locality can affect whether interference with use and enjoyment is unreasonable.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Locality can affect whether interference with use and enjoyment is unreasonable.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Nuisance requires physical injury to the claimant' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Locality can affect whether interference with use and enjoyment is unreasonable.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Locality can affect whether interference with use and enjoyment is unreasonable.","E":"Correct. Reasonableness in amenity nuisance is contextual. The character of the locality is a traditional factor, although it is not necessarily decisive and different considerations can apply to physical damage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Sturges v Bridgman; Coventry v Lawrence.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Locality can affect whether interference with use and enjoyment is unreasonable.","common_trap":"Treating nuisance as applying an identical noise threshold in every environment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f124c3061b1ab3c9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0146","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Occupiers — independent contractors","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An occupier hires a competent specialist contractor to maintain a complex lift. The work is of a kind reasonably entrusted to a specialist. The occupier takes reasonable steps to select a competent contractor and, where appropriate, checks the work. A visitor is later injured by the contractor’s negligent workmanship.\n\nWhich provision is particularly relevant to the occupier’s potential defence?","options":{"A":"The Occupiers’ Liability Act rule concerning work entrusted to an independent contractor.","B":"The Theft Act 1968.","C":"The Wills Act 1837.","D":"The Companies Act rule on dividends.","E":"The rule against perpetuities."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Under the 1957 Act, an occupier may in appropriate circumstances satisfy the common duty of care in relation to dangers arising from contractor work by acting reasonably in entrusting the work and taking reasonable steps regarding competence and checking where appropriate.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Under the 1957 Act, an occupier may in appropriate circumstances satisfy the common duty of care in relation to dangers arising from contractor work by acting reasonably in entrusting the work and taking reasonable steps regarding competence and checking where appropriate.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Theft Act 1968' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An occupier may rely on the statutory independent-contractor provision if its requirements are met.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Wills Act 1837' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An occupier may rely on the statutory independent-contractor provision if its requirements are met.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Companies Act rule on dividends' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An occupier may rely on the statutory independent-contractor provision if its requirements are met.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The rule against perpetuities' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An occupier may rely on the statutory independent-contractor provision if its requirements are met."},"primary_legal_authority":"Occupiers’ Liability Act 1957, s 2(4)(b).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An occupier may rely on the statutory independent-contractor provision if its requirements are met.","common_trap":"Assuming an occupier is invariably liable for every contractor’s negligence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"76711bfbde4e1dc6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0147","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Separation of powers","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A student says the UK constitution requires the legislative, executive and judicial branches to be completely institutionally isolated with no overlap of personnel, functions or accountability.\n\nWhich statement is more accurate?","options":{"A":"The UK has no concept of separation of powers at all.","B":"The UK recognises separation-of-powers principles, but not an absolute institutional separation; constitutional arrangements include checks, balances and some functional overlap.","C":"Judges make all primary legislation.","D":"Ministers exercise the Supreme Court’s judicial jurisdiction.","E":"Parliament has no role in scrutinising the executive."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The UK constitution reflects separation-of-powers values and judicial independence while also containing overlaps characteristic of a parliamentary system. The principle is not one of complete institutional isolation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The UK has no concept of separation of powers at all' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. UK separation of powers is important but not absolute.","B":"Correct. The UK constitution reflects separation-of-powers values and judicial independence while also containing overlaps characteristic of a parliamentary system. The principle is not one of complete institutional isolation.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Judges make all primary legislation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. UK separation of powers is important but not absolute.","D":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. UK separation of powers is important but not absolute.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Parliament has no role in scrutinising the executive' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. UK separation of powers is important but not absolute."},"primary_legal_authority":"UK constitutional principles; Constitutional Reform Act 2005 as relevant to judicial independence.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"UK separation of powers is important but not absolute.","common_trap":"Importing a rigid model of separation that does not describe the UK constitution.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6f72fdf76b736c5f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0148","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Royal prerogative — statutory displacement","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A minister wants to rely on a prerogative power in an area where Parliament has enacted a statutory scheme that regulates the same subject and leaves no room for the proposed inconsistent use of prerogative.\n\nWhich constitutional principle is most relevant?","options":{"A":"Prerogative always overrides statute.","B":"Ministers can suspend Acts of Parliament by announcement.","C":"A prerogative cannot ordinarily be used to frustrate or displace a statutory scheme enacted by Parliament.","D":"Statutes apply only to private persons.","E":"Courts may never examine the legal limits of prerogative power."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Where Parliament has legislated, prerogative power cannot ordinarily be used inconsistently with the statutory scheme or to frustrate Parliament’s legislation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Statute prevails over inconsistent prerogative action.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Ministers can suspend Acts of Parliament by announcement' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Statute prevails over inconsistent prerogative action.","C":"Correct. Where Parliament has legislated, prerogative power cannot ordinarily be used inconsistently with the statutory scheme or to frustrate Parliament’s legislation.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Statute prevails over inconsistent prerogative action.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Statute prevails over inconsistent prerogative action."},"primary_legal_authority":"Attorney General v De Keyser's Royal Hotel Ltd; R (Miller) v Secretary of State for Exiting the EU.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Statute prevails over inconsistent prerogative action.","common_trap":"Treating prerogative power as hierarchically superior to primary legislation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fab18eeca9af678c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0149","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Delegated legislation — ultra vires","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A minister makes regulations under an enabling Act. The regulations impose a requirement that the enabling Act does not authorise, expressly or by necessary implication.\n\nWhich public-law challenge is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The regulations are valid merely because a minister signed them.","B":"Delegated legislation can never be reviewed by a court.","C":"The regulations automatically amend the enabling Act.","D":"The regulations may be substantively ultra vires the enabling Act.","E":"Only Parliament can ever question delegated legislation."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Delegated legislation must remain within the power conferred by the parent statute. A measure outside that authority may be challenged as ultra vires.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This imposes a formality that the governing rule does not require in this situation. Delegated legislation must stay within the scope of its enabling power.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Delegated legislation must stay within the scope of its enabling power.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Delegated legislation must stay within the scope of its enabling power.","D":"Correct. Delegated legislation must remain within the power conferred by the parent statute. A measure outside that authority may be challenged as ultra vires.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Delegated legislation must stay within the scope of its enabling power."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administrative-law principles governing delegated legislation.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Delegated legislation must stay within the scope of its enabling power.","common_trap":"Assuming delegated legislation has the same unconstrained authority as an Act of Parliament.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f51bacd3bdecf685"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0150","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Revocation by destruction","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator deliberately tears up the original will while stating that he intends to revoke it. He has testamentary capacity and personally performs the act. No later revival occurs.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"Physical destruction is irrelevant to revocation.","B":"A will can be revoked only by marriage.","C":"Tearing the will merely creates a codicil.","D":"Revocation requires consent of every beneficiary.","E":"The will can be revoked by destruction where the statutory physical act is accompanied by the required intention to revoke."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The Wills Act recognises revocation by specified physical acts, including destruction, when done by the testator or in the testator’s presence and direction with the intention of revoking the will.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Physical destruction is irrelevant to revocation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Revocation by destruction requires both an appropriate physical act and intention to revoke.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Revocation by destruction requires both an appropriate physical act and intention to revoke.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Tearing the will merely creates a codicil' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Revocation by destruction requires both an appropriate physical act and intention to revoke.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Revocation by destruction requires both an appropriate physical act and intention to revoke.","E":"Correct. The Wills Act recognises revocation by specified physical acts, including destruction, when done by the testator or in the testator’s presence and direction with the intention of revoking the will."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 20.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Revocation by destruction requires both an appropriate physical act and intention to revoke.","common_trap":"Looking only at the physical damage without the accompanying revocatory intention, or vice versa.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e890198d495fb8a0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0151","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Abatement","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An estate is insufficient to satisfy all testamentary gifts after payment of debts, liabilities and administration expenses. The will does not contain a special provision changing the ordinary order in which gifts bear the shortfall.\n\nWhich doctrine governs reduction of testamentary gifts because the estate is insufficient?","options":{"A":"Abatement.","B":"Ademption only.","C":"Frustration.","D":"Overreaching.","E":"Subrogation."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Abatement concerns the reduction of gifts where the estate is insufficient to meet all liabilities and dispositions. It is distinct from ademption, which concerns absence of specifically gifted property.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Abatement concerns the reduction of gifts where the estate is insufficient to meet all liabilities and dispositions. It is distinct from ademption, which concerns absence of specifically gifted property.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Ademption only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Abatement determines how gifts are reduced when estate assets are insufficient.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Abatement determines how gifts are reduced when estate assets are insufficient.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Overreaching' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Abatement determines how gifts are reduced when estate assets are insufficient.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Subrogation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Abatement determines how gifts are reduced when estate assets are insufficient."},"primary_legal_authority":"General succession and administration principles on abatement.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Abatement determines how gifts are reduced when estate assets are insufficient.","common_trap":"Confusing insufficiency of the estate with disappearance of the subject matter of a specific legacy.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8e53913eb9f885e4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0152","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"IHT — nil-rate band","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An estate contains chargeable property on death. The personal representatives are calculating inheritance tax and are given the applicable nil-rate-band figure in the examination question.\n\nWhat is the basic function of the nil-rate band?","options":{"A":"It is a tax charged in addition to inheritance tax.","B":"It is the portion of the taxable estate to which the nil rate applies before the applicable positive IHT rate is charged, subject to the statutory regime and available transferable amounts or reliefs.","C":"It applies only to companies.","D":"It converts every lifetime gift into income.","E":"It is the SDLT threshold for buying land."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The nil-rate band forms part of the IHT computation. The examination supplies monetary values where candidates are required to apply them; candidates must understand the structure of the calculation and relevant exemptions/reliefs.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It is a tax charged in addition to inheritance tax' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The nil-rate band is a core component of the IHT computation.","B":"Correct. The nil-rate band forms part of the IHT computation. The examination supplies monetary values where candidates are required to apply them; candidates must understand the structure of the calculation and relevant exemptions/reliefs.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The nil-rate band is a core component of the IHT computation.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The nil-rate band is a core component of the IHT computation.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It is the SDLT threshold for buying land' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The nil-rate band is a core component of the IHT computation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984; SRA SQE taxation guidance applicable from September 2026.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The nil-rate band is a core component of the IHT computation.","common_trap":"Confusing a tax threshold/band with a separate tax or exemption of every estate asset.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0a8928321fd7a47e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0153","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Interest on client money","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm holds a substantial amount of client money for a significant period. The client asks whether the firm may simply keep all interest generated by holding that money regardless of its written policy or the circumstances.\n\nWhat is the regulatory starting point?","options":{"A":"The firm automatically owns all interest.","B":"Interest must never be paid to a client.","C":"The firm must account to clients or third parties for a fair sum of interest on client money held on their behalf, subject to the firm’s compliant written policy and the Accounts Rules.","D":"Interest rules apply only to conveyancing.","E":"The client must open the firm’s bank account personally."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require a fair sum of interest to be accounted for on client money held, with firms maintaining a written policy designed to achieve that outcome.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Firms must account fairly for interest on client money under a compliant policy.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Firms must account fairly for interest on client money under a compliant policy.","C":"Correct. The Accounts Rules require a fair sum of interest to be accounted for on client money held, with firms maintaining a written policy designed to achieve that outcome.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Firms must account fairly for interest on client money under a compliant policy.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Firms must account fairly for interest on client money under a compliant policy."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 7.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Firms must account fairly for interest on client money under a compliant policy.","common_trap":"Assuming the firm may retain all economic benefit generated by client money.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5f40a2bb5c067e66"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0154","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Resulting trusts — automatic resulting trust","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor transfers property to trustees on express trusts that dispose of only part of the beneficial interest. The trust instrument contains no valid disposition of the remaining beneficial interest.\n\nWhich doctrine ordinarily explains where the undisposed beneficial interest goes?","options":{"A":"A constructive trust for the trustees personally in every case.","B":"Bona vacantia automatically in every case.","C":"A charitable trust regardless of intention.","D":"An automatic resulting trust in favour of the settlor or the settlor’s estate, subject to the circumstances.","E":"Adverse possession."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Where an express trust fails to dispose of the whole beneficial interest, equity may impose an automatic resulting trust of the undisposed interest back to the settlor or estate.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An undisposed beneficial interest can return under an automatic resulting trust.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An undisposed beneficial interest can return under an automatic resulting trust.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A charitable trust regardless of intention' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An undisposed beneficial interest can return under an automatic resulting trust.","D":"Correct. Where an express trust fails to dispose of the whole beneficial interest, equity may impose an automatic resulting trust of the undisposed interest back to the settlor or estate.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An undisposed beneficial interest can return under an automatic resulting trust."},"primary_legal_authority":"Resulting-trust principles; SRA FLK2 September 2026 terminology.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An undisposed beneficial interest can return under an automatic resulting trust.","common_trap":"Confusing automatic resulting trusts with presumed resulting trusts arising from certain transfers or contributions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4e5edf0fb812ff0a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0155","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Fiduciary no-profit rule","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee uses information obtained through the trusteeship to make an unauthorised personal profit closely connected with the fiduciary position. The trust suffers no provable financial loss and the trustee acted in subjective good faith.\n\nWhich is the best statement?","options":{"A":"No liability can arise unless the trust proves an equivalent financial loss.","B":"Good faith automatically permits fiduciary profits.","C":"Trustees may retain every opportunity discovered through office.","D":"Only criminal law regulates such profits.","E":"The fiduciary no-profit rule can require the trustee to account for the unauthorised profit even without proof of loss or bad faith."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Fiduciary obligations include strict conflict and no-profit principles. An unauthorised profit obtained by reason of the fiduciary position can trigger an account even where the principal cannot show corresponding loss and the fiduciary acted honestly.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No liability can arise unless the trust proves an equivalent financial loss' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A fiduciary may have to account for unauthorised profits derived from the fiduciary position.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A fiduciary may have to account for unauthorised profits derived from the fiduciary position.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A fiduciary may have to account for unauthorised profits derived from the fiduciary position.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A fiduciary may have to account for unauthorised profits derived from the fiduciary position.","E":"Correct. Fiduciary obligations include strict conflict and no-profit principles. An unauthorised profit obtained by reason of the fiduciary position can trigger an account even where the principal cannot show corresponding loss and the fiduciary acted honestly."},"primary_legal_authority":"Keech v Sandford; Boardman v Phipps; fiduciary principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A fiduciary may have to account for unauthorised profits derived from the fiduciary position.","common_trap":"Treating fiduciary profit liability as ordinary compensatory negligence requiring loss and fault.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7a0a892bbe0b7b75"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0156","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Registered land — actual occupation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer makes a registrable disposition of registered land for value. A person with a pre-existing equitable proprietary interest is in obvious actual occupation at the time of the disposition. Assume the statutory exceptions do not apply.\n\nWhich priority concept should the buyer consider?","options":{"A":"The interest may qualify as an overriding interest based on actual occupation and bind the buyer despite not appearing as a registered charge.","B":"Actual occupation can never affect registered land.","C":"Every equitable interest is automatically void on sale.","D":"Only legal leases can override.","E":"Occupation matters only if the occupier owns the freehold."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Schedule 3 to the Land Registration Act recognises specified interests of persons in actual occupation as overriding, subject to statutory qualifications. A buyer must therefore investigate occupation as well as the register.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Schedule 3 to the Land Registration Act recognises specified interests of persons in actual occupation as overriding, subject to statutory qualifications. A buyer must therefore investigate occupation as well as the register.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A qualifying proprietary interest coupled with actual occupation may override a registered disposition.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A qualifying proprietary interest coupled with actual occupation may override a registered disposition.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A qualifying proprietary interest coupled with actual occupation may override a registered disposition.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A qualifying proprietary interest coupled with actual occupation may override a registered disposition."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, Sch 3 para 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A qualifying proprietary interest coupled with actual occupation may override a registered disposition.","common_trap":"Assuming registration makes physical occupation legally irrelevant.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6e5868d39fe3fc11"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0157","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Mortgages — equity of redemption","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A mortgage agreement states that even after the borrower repays the loan and all interest, the lender will retain an option permanently preventing the borrower from recovering the property free of the lender’s proprietary control. The term is part of the mortgage bargain itself.\n\nWhich equitable principle should be considered?","options":{"A":"Ademption.","B":"The equity of redemption and the rule against a clog or fetter on redemption.","C":"The postal rule.","D":"Without-prejudice privilege.","E":"The rule in Strong v Bird."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Equity protects the mortgagor’s right to redeem once the secured obligation is satisfied. A term forming part of the mortgage that improperly prevents or fetters redemption may be invalid as a clog.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Ademption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A mortgage cannot improperly clog the mortgagor’s equitable right to redeem.","B":"Correct. Equity protects the mortgagor’s right to redeem once the secured obligation is satisfied. A term forming part of the mortgage that improperly prevents or fetters redemption may be invalid as a clog.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The postal rule' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A mortgage cannot improperly clog the mortgagor’s equitable right to redeem.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Without-prejudice privilege' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A mortgage cannot improperly clog the mortgagor’s equitable right to redeem.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The rule in Strong v Bird' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A mortgage cannot improperly clog the mortgagor’s equitable right to redeem."},"primary_legal_authority":"Equitable mortgage principles concerning the equity of redemption.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A mortgage cannot improperly clog the mortgagor’s equitable right to redeem.","common_trap":"Assuming freedom of contract always validates a term preventing redemption after the debt is discharged.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fa5d3bcdf34cbc64"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0158","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Lease assignment — landlord consent","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial lease contains a qualified covenant prohibiting assignment without the landlord’s consent. The tenant makes a proper written application for consent to assign. The statutory regime governing qualified alienation covenants applies.\n\nWhich obligation is particularly relevant to the landlord?","options":{"A":"The landlord may ignore the application indefinitely.","B":"Consent is automatically unnecessary whenever the tenant finds an assignee.","C":"The landlord must deal with the application within a reasonable time and cannot unreasonably withhold consent, subject to the statutory framework.","D":"The tenant becomes freeholder if consent is delayed.","E":"The landlord must consent to every proposed assignee regardless of circumstances."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Landlord and Tenant Act 1988 imposes duties concerning applications for consent under qualifying alienation covenants, including dealing with them within a reasonable time and not unreasonably withholding consent where the covenant is qualified.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The landlord may ignore the application indefinitely' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Qualified assignment covenants engage statutory duties concerning consent.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Qualified assignment covenants engage statutory duties concerning consent.","C":"Correct. The Landlord and Tenant Act 1988 imposes duties concerning applications for consent under qualifying alienation covenants, including dealing with them within a reasonable time and not unreasonably withholding consent where the covenant is qualified.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The tenant becomes freeholder if consent is delayed' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Qualified assignment covenants engage statutory duties concerning consent.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Qualified assignment covenants engage statutory duties concerning consent."},"primary_legal_authority":"Landlord and Tenant Act 1988; Landlord and Tenant Act 1927, s 19.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Qualified assignment covenants engage statutory duties concerning consent.","common_trap":"Treating the landlord’s consent power as wholly unfettered or, conversely, as requiring consent in every case.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d0dea090d760d7a8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0159","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Criminal damage — basic offence","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person intentionally smashes a neighbour’s window without lawful excuse. The window belongs to the neighbour. The person knows it is not his property.\n\nWhich offence is most directly established on these facts?","options":{"A":"Theft, because every damaged item is appropriated.","B":"Robbery, because damage is force.","C":"Burglary, even though no building was entered.","D":"Basic criminal damage: destroying or damaging property belonging to another, intentionally or recklessly, without lawful excuse.","E":"Fraud by false representation."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Criminal Damage Act creates the basic offence of intentionally or recklessly destroying or damaging property belonging to another without lawful excuse.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Intentional or reckless damage to another’s property without lawful excuse can constitute criminal damage.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Robbery, because damage is force' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Intentional or reckless damage to another’s property without lawful excuse can constitute criminal damage.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Burglary, even though no building was entered' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Intentional or reckless damage to another’s property without lawful excuse can constitute criminal damage.","D":"Correct. The Criminal Damage Act creates the basic offence of intentionally or recklessly destroying or damaging property belonging to another without lawful excuse.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Fraud by false representation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Intentional or reckless damage to another’s property without lawful excuse can constitute criminal damage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Damage Act 1971, s 1(1).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Intentional or reckless damage to another’s property without lawful excuse can constitute criminal damage.","common_trap":"Forcing the facts into theft or burglary when the statutory elements of criminal damage directly fit.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d929522e9cbe4bed"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0160","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Confessions — oppression","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"During a police interview, officers obtain a confession after conduct that the defence alleges amounted to oppression. The prosecution wants to rely on the confession at trial.\n\nWhich statutory provision specifically addresses exclusion where a confession was obtained by oppression or in consequence of things said or done likely to render it unreliable?","options":{"A":"PACE 1984 section 58 only.","B":"Bail Act 1976.","C":"Theft Act 1968 section 1.","D":"Criminal Attempts Act 1981.","E":"PACE 1984 section 76."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"PACE section 76 contains the confession-specific exclusion rule. Where the statutory issue is properly raised, the prosecution bears the relevant burden concerning admissibility under the provision.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'PACE 1984 section 58 only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. PACE s 76 specifically regulates confession evidence obtained by oppression or in circumstances likely to render it unreliable.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Bail Act 1976' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. PACE s 76 specifically regulates confession evidence obtained by oppression or in circumstances likely to render it unreliable.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Theft Act 1968 section 1' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. PACE s 76 specifically regulates confession evidence obtained by oppression or in circumstances likely to render it unreliable.","D":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. PACE s 76 specifically regulates confession evidence obtained by oppression or in circumstances likely to render it unreliable.","E":"Correct. PACE section 76 contains the confession-specific exclusion rule. Where the statutory issue is properly raised, the prosecution bears the relevant burden concerning admissibility under the provision."},"primary_legal_authority":"Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984, s 76.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"PACE s 76 specifically regulates confession evidence obtained by oppression or in circumstances likely to render it unreliable.","common_trap":"Confusing the confession-specific rule with the broader fairness discretion in PACE s 78.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d51f78fd18fa6449"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0161","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Private company — written resolutions","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company wants its members to pass an ordinary resolution without holding a general meeting. The proposed matter is not one of the matters excluded from the statutory written-resolution procedure.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the Companies Act position?","options":{"A":"A private company may use the statutory written-resolution procedure for eligible resolutions, applying the relevant voting threshold.","B":"Written resolutions are available only to public companies.","C":"Every written resolution requires unanimous member approval.","D":"A private company must hold a physical meeting for every shareholder decision.","E":"A written resolution is valid only if Companies House votes for it."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Private companies may use the Companies Act written-resolution procedure for eligible resolutions. The required majority depends on whether the resolution is ordinary or special; unanimity is not the general rule.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Private companies may use the Companies Act written-resolution procedure for eligible resolutions. The required majority depends on whether the resolution is ordinary or special; unanimity is not the general rule.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Eligible private-company member resolutions can be passed using the statutory written procedure.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Eligible private-company member resolutions can be passed using the statutory written procedure.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Eligible private-company member resolutions can be passed using the statutory written procedure.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Eligible private-company member resolutions can be passed using the statutory written procedure."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, Part 13, Chapter 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Eligible private-company member resolutions can be passed using the statutory written procedure.","common_trap":"Assuming written resolutions either require unanimity or are unavailable to private companies.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a6fd1d2bc531f651"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0162","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Directors — reasonable care, skill and diligence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A finance director with substantial professional accounting experience approves complex accounts without reading material that would have revealed an obvious error. She argues that directors are judged only by the knowledge expected of a person with no specialist expertise.\n\nWhat standard applies?","options":{"A":"Only the director’s subjective personal standard.","B":"The statutory standard combines the general knowledge, skill and experience reasonably expected of a person carrying out the director’s functions with the actual knowledge, skill and experience the director has.","C":"The standard is always that of the least experienced board member.","D":"Directors owe no duty of care to the company.","E":"Professional expertise must be ignored when assessing a director."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 174 uses a dual objective/subjective standard: the minimum expected for the functions performed plus any higher actual knowledge, skill and experience possessed by the particular director.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A director’s section 174 standard incorporates both an objective floor and the director’s actual expertise.","B":"Correct. Section 174 uses a dual objective/subjective standard: the minimum expected for the functions performed plus any higher actual knowledge, skill and experience possessed by the particular director.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A director’s section 174 standard incorporates both an objective floor and the director’s actual expertise.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Directors owe no duty of care to the company' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A director’s section 174 standard incorporates both an objective floor and the director’s actual expertise.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A director’s section 174 standard incorporates both an objective floor and the director’s actual expertise."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 174.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A director’s section 174 standard incorporates both an objective floor and the director’s actual expertise.","common_trap":"Ignoring the subjective uplift where a director has specialist knowledge or experience.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5e852805b513aa31"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0163","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Agency — actual authority","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company’s board expressly authorises its purchasing director to sign a particular supply agreement on the company’s behalf. The director signs exactly that agreement within the authority given.\n\nWhich form of authority most directly binds the company?","options":{"A":"Ostensible authority only, because directors can never have actual authority.","B":"Ratification, because authority can exist only after signing.","C":"Express actual authority.","D":"No authority because companies cannot act through agents.","E":"Authority derived solely from the supplier."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Actual authority arises from the consensual relationship between principal and agent and may be express or implied. Here the board expressly conferred authority for the specific transaction.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Express authority given by the principal is actual authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Express authority given by the principal is actual authority.","C":"Correct. Actual authority arises from the consensual relationship between principal and agent and may be express or implied. Here the board expressly conferred authority for the specific transaction.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No authority because companies cannot act through agents' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Express authority given by the principal is actual authority.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Authority derived solely from the supplier' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Express authority given by the principal is actual authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"General agency principles; Freeman & Lockyer v Buckhurst Park Properties as to authority concepts.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Express authority given by the principal is actual authority.","common_trap":"Looking for apparent authority where express actual authority already resolves the issue.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6efa4856c277f7f0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0164","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Corporation tax — chargeable gains","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A UK company sells an investment property for more than its allowable cost. The property is a capital asset rather than trading stock. No exemption removes the gain.\n\nHow is the company's chargeable gain ordinarily taxed?","options":{"A":"As capital gains tax payable by the company in exactly the same way as an individual.","B":"Only through SDLT payable by the seller.","C":"It is never taxed because companies do not pay tax on capital disposals.","D":"As part of the company's profits chargeable to corporation tax, applying the corporation-tax rules for chargeable gains.","E":"As inheritance tax."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Companies within the corporation-tax regime generally bring chargeable gains into the corporation-tax computation rather than paying individual capital gains tax.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'As capital gains tax payable by the company in exactly the same way as an individual' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A company’s chargeable gains are generally brought into its corporation-tax computation.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A company’s chargeable gains are generally brought into its corporation-tax computation.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A company’s chargeable gains are generally brought into its corporation-tax computation.","D":"Correct. Companies within the corporation-tax regime generally bring chargeable gains into the corporation-tax computation rather than paying individual capital gains tax.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'As inheritance tax' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A company’s chargeable gains are generally brought into its corporation-tax computation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Corporation Tax Act 2009; Taxation of Chargeable Gains Act 1992; SRA FLK1 taxation specification.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A company’s chargeable gains are generally brought into its corporation-tax computation.","common_trap":"Applying the individual CGT label directly to a company.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9823641470f4af99"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0165","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Acknowledgment of service and defence","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant is served within the jurisdiction with a claim form accompanied by particulars of claim. The defendant files an acknowledgment of service in time and intends to defend the whole claim. No special rule applies.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect on the time for filing the defence?","options":{"A":"The defence becomes unnecessary.","B":"The defendant has only 24 hours to defend.","C":"The claim is automatically dismissed.","D":"The acknowledgment itself counts as the full defence.","E":"The acknowledgment ordinarily extends the defence deadline to 28 days after service of the particulars of claim."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Under the ordinary CPR timetable, a timely acknowledgment of service generally extends the period for filing a defence from 14 to 28 days after service of the particulars of claim.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The defence becomes unnecessary' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A timely acknowledgment ordinarily gives the defendant 28 days after service of particulars to file a defence.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A timely acknowledgment ordinarily gives the defendant 28 days after service of particulars to file a defence.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A timely acknowledgment ordinarily gives the defendant 28 days after service of particulars to file a defence.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The acknowledgment itself counts as the full defence' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A timely acknowledgment ordinarily gives the defendant 28 days after service of particulars to file a defence.","E":"Correct. Under the ordinary CPR timetable, a timely acknowledgment of service generally extends the period for filing a defence from 14 to 28 days after service of the particulars of claim."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 10.3 and 15.4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A timely acknowledgment ordinarily gives the defendant 28 days after service of particulars to file a defence.","common_trap":"Confusing acknowledgment with the defence itself.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"075c9bf2fc096ebc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0166","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Interim remedies — freezing injunction","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant has a strong arguable money claim and credible evidence that the defendant is rapidly moving assets abroad with the purpose or effect of frustrating enforcement of a future judgment. The claimant seeks an interim order restraining disposal of assets.\n\nWhich remedy is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A freezing injunction.","B":"A declaration of incompatibility.","C":"A charging order before any judgment as an automatic entitlement.","D":"Specific performance of the defendant’s bank account.","E":"A witness summons."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A freezing injunction is an exceptional interim remedy designed to prevent unjustified dissipation of assets that could frustrate enforcement. The applicant must satisfy the applicable stringent requirements and duties, including full and frank disclosure on a without-notice application.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A freezing injunction is an exceptional interim remedy designed to prevent unjustified dissipation of assets that could frustrate enforcement. The applicant must satisfy the applicable stringent requirements and duties, including full and frank disclosure on a without-notice application.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A declaration of incompatibility' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A freezing injunction can restrain asset dissipation threatening enforcement of a prospective judgment.","C":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A freezing injunction can restrain asset dissipation threatening enforcement of a prospective judgment.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Specific performance of the defendant’s bank account' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A freezing injunction can restrain asset dissipation threatening enforcement of a prospective judgment.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A witness summons' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A freezing injunction can restrain asset dissipation threatening enforcement of a prospective judgment."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 25; freezing-injunction principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A freezing injunction can restrain asset dissipation threatening enforcement of a prospective judgment.","common_trap":"Treating a freezing order as security automatically available in every money claim.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7aa01b2737faaf10"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0167","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Part 36 — claimant offer consequences","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant makes a valid Part 36 offer. The defendant does not accept it. At trial the claimant obtains a judgment that is at least as advantageous to the claimant as the proposal. Assume it would not be unjust to apply the ordinary Part 36 consequences.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"The claimant is penalised for having made the offer.","B":"Part 36 can trigger specified enhanced consequences in the claimant’s favour from the end of the relevant period, subject to the rule and the court’s assessment.","C":"Part 36 has no costs consequences after trial.","D":"The judgment is automatically set aside.","E":"The defendant is entitled to its costs from the date of the offer."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Where a claimant matches or beats its own Part 36 offer, CPR 36.17 provides a package of consequences, potentially including enhanced interest, indemnity costs and an additional amount, subject to the rule and injustice exception.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The claimant is penalised for having made the offer' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Beating a claimant Part 36 offer can trigger enhanced consequences under CPR 36.17.","B":"Correct. Where a claimant matches or beats its own Part 36 offer, CPR 36.17 provides a package of consequences, potentially including enhanced interest, indemnity costs and an additional amount, subject to the rule and injustice exception.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Part 36 has no costs consequences after trial' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Beating a claimant Part 36 offer can trigger enhanced consequences under CPR 36.17.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Beating a claimant Part 36 offer can trigger enhanced consequences under CPR 36.17.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The defendant is entitled to its costs from the date of the offer' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Beating a claimant Part 36 offer can trigger enhanced consequences under CPR 36.17."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 36.17.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Beating a claimant Part 36 offer can trigger enhanced consequences under CPR 36.17.","common_trap":"Confusing the consequences of claimant and defendant Part 36 offers.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9fc2eed3278f8e3f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0168","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"SRA — client instructions and capacity","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor has reliable evidence giving serious reason to doubt whether an adult client has capacity to make a particular litigation decision. The solicitor proposes simply to follow the client’s apparent instruction without considering capacity because the client is over 18.\n\nWhich is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Adulthood makes capacity legally irrelevant.","B":"The solicitor should automatically take instructions from any family member.","C":"The solicitor should consider the client’s capacity in relation to the specific decision and apply the relevant legal and professional framework rather than assuming capacity questions cannot arise for adults.","D":"The solicitor must terminate every retainer where capacity is questioned.","E":"Capacity is determined solely by whether the client agrees with the solicitor."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Capacity is decision-specific. Although adults are presumed to have capacity, evidence raising a genuine issue requires appropriate consideration under the Mental Capacity Act and professional duties; disagreement or age alone is not determinative.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adulthood makes capacity legally irrelevant' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Solicitors must address genuine decision-specific capacity concerns within the applicable legal framework.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Solicitors must address genuine decision-specific capacity concerns within the applicable legal framework.","C":"Correct. Capacity is decision-specific. Although adults are presumed to have capacity, evidence raising a genuine issue requires appropriate consideration under the Mental Capacity Act and professional duties; disagreement or age alone is not determinative.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Solicitors must address genuine decision-specific capacity concerns within the applicable legal framework.","E":"Incorrect. This makes consent or agreement determinative when the legal test requires the identified statutory or doctrinal conditions. Solicitors must address genuine decision-specific capacity concerns within the applicable legal framework."},"primary_legal_authority":"Mental Capacity Act 2005; SRA professional obligations.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Solicitors must address genuine decision-specific capacity concerns within the applicable legal framework.","common_trap":"Treating the presumption of capacity as irrebuttable or substituting family instructions automatically.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d5d410f95f96c092"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0169","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Legal professional privilege — waiver","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client voluntarily sends an opponent the substance of a privileged legal opinion in order to support its position in negotiations. The client later claims that disclosure can have no effect on privilege because privilege can never be waived.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Privilege can never be waived.","B":"Only the solicitor, never the client, owns the privilege.","C":"Any waiver automatically discloses every document the client has ever sent a lawyer.","D":"Legal professional privilege can be waived by the client, including through voluntary disclosure, with the scope and consequences depending on the circumstances.","E":"Privilege belongs to the court."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Privilege belongs to the client and may be waived. The effect and breadth of waiver require careful analysis, including fairness and the subject matter of the disclosure.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A client can waive legal professional privilege.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A client can waive legal professional privilege.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A client can waive legal professional privilege.","D":"Correct. Privilege belongs to the client and may be waived. The effect and breadth of waiver require careful analysis, including fairness and the subject matter of the disclosure.","E":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A client can waive legal professional privilege."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law legal professional privilege and waiver principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A client can waive legal professional privilege.","common_trap":"Treating privilege as an absolute protection incapable of voluntary waiver.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"017ee3d5f090d999"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0170","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Interpretation — objective approach","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A written commercial contract uses wording whose meaning is disputed. One party says the court must adopt that party’s undisclosed subjective intention at the time of signing.\n\nWhat is the ordinary approach?","options":{"A":"A party’s secret subjective intention is conclusive.","B":"The court ignores the words used.","C":"Every ambiguity automatically invalidates the contract.","D":"Interpretation is decided by whichever party drafted fewer clauses.","E":"Contractual interpretation is generally objective, asking what the language would convey to a reasonable person with the relevant background knowledge in context."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Modern contractual interpretation is objective and considers the language used in its documentary, factual and commercial context, while respecting the words chosen by the parties.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A party’s secret subjective intention is conclusive' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Contract interpretation ordinarily applies an objective contextual approach.","B":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Contract interpretation ordinarily applies an objective contextual approach.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contract interpretation ordinarily applies an objective contextual approach.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Interpretation is decided by whichever party drafted fewer clauses' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Contract interpretation ordinarily applies an objective contextual approach.","E":"Correct. Modern contractual interpretation is objective and considers the language used in its documentary, factual and commercial context, while respecting the words chosen by the parties."},"primary_legal_authority":"Arnold v Britton; Wood v Capita Insurance Services Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contract interpretation ordinarily applies an objective contextual approach.","common_trap":"Treating undisclosed subjective intention as controlling contractual meaning.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ad3df339ad80996c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0171","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Specific performance","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller contracts to transfer a unique parcel of land but refuses to complete. Damages would not provide the buyer with an adequate substitute, and no equitable bar applies.\n\nWhich remedy may the buyer seek?","options":{"A":"Specific performance, an equitable remedy compelling contractual performance where appropriate.","B":"Punitive damages automatically.","C":"A criminal conviction of the seller.","D":"Rescission only, because courts can never compel performance.","E":"A freezing order transferring title automatically."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Specific performance is a discretionary equitable remedy available where damages are inadequate and equitable considerations favour relief. Contracts for unique land are a conventional context in which it may be appropriate.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Specific performance is a discretionary equitable remedy available where damages are inadequate and equitable considerations favour relief. Contracts for unique land are a conventional context in which it may be appropriate.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Specific performance may compel performance where damages are inadequate and equitable requirements are met.","C":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Specific performance may compel performance where damages are inadequate and equitable requirements are met.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Specific performance may compel performance where damages are inadequate and equitable requirements are met.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Specific performance may compel performance where damages are inadequate and equitable requirements are met."},"primary_legal_authority":"Equitable principles governing specific performance.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Specific performance may compel performance where damages are inadequate and equitable requirements are met.","common_trap":"Assuming damages are invariably the exclusive remedy for breach of contract.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7d4a9c1beb0ca2fb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0172","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Promissory estoppel","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A creditor clearly promises a debtor that, for a specified period, it will accept reduced contractual payments. The debtor relies on that promise in circumstances making it inequitable for the creditor immediately to insist on its strict rights for that period. The debtor seeks to use the promise defensively, not to create an entirely new cause of action.\n\nWhich doctrine is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Frustration.","B":"Promissory estoppel.","C":"Common mistake.","D":"Privity.","E":"Conversion."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Promissory estoppel may prevent a party from insisting on strict legal rights where a clear promise concerning existing rights has been relied on and it would be inequitable to resile. Traditionally it operates as a shield rather than an independent sword.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Promissory estoppel can restrain enforcement of strict existing rights in appropriate reliance circumstances.","B":"Correct. Promissory estoppel may prevent a party from insisting on strict legal rights where a clear promise concerning existing rights has been relied on and it would be inequitable to resile. Traditionally it operates as a shield rather than an independent sword.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Common mistake' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Promissory estoppel can restrain enforcement of strict existing rights in appropriate reliance circumstances.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Privity' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Promissory estoppel can restrain enforcement of strict existing rights in appropriate reliance circumstances.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Conversion' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Promissory estoppel can restrain enforcement of strict existing rights in appropriate reliance circumstances."},"primary_legal_authority":"Central London Property Trust Ltd v High Trees House Ltd; Combe v Combe.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Promissory estoppel can restrain enforcement of strict existing rights in appropriate reliance circumstances.","common_trap":"Using promissory estoppel as a free-standing cause of action creating rights from nothing.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c1881a3d67c3ffb5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0173","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Res ipsa loquitur","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A patient is injured by an occurrence during a routine procedure. The precise mechanism is unknown to the patient, the relevant instrumentality was under the defendant’s control, and the occurrence is of a kind that ordinarily would not happen without negligence. There is no satisfactory innocent explanation at this stage.\n\nWhich evidential doctrine may assist the claimant?","options":{"A":"Volenti automatically.","B":"Novus actus interveniens.","C":"Res ipsa loquitur.","D":"Promissory estoppel.","E":"Ademption."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Res ipsa loquitur can permit an inference of negligence where the circumstances satisfy its requirements, particularly where the event ordinarily does not occur without negligence and the cause was within the defendant’s sphere of control.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Res ipsa loquitur may support an inference of negligence from the nature and circumstances of an accident.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Novus actus interveniens' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Res ipsa loquitur may support an inference of negligence from the nature and circumstances of an accident.","C":"Correct. Res ipsa loquitur can permit an inference of negligence where the circumstances satisfy its requirements, particularly where the event ordinarily does not occur without negligence and the cause was within the defendant’s sphere of control.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Promissory estoppel' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Res ipsa loquitur may support an inference of negligence from the nature and circumstances of an accident.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Ademption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Res ipsa loquitur may support an inference of negligence from the nature and circumstances of an accident."},"primary_legal_authority":"Scott v London and St Katherine Docks Co; negligence evidential principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Res ipsa loquitur may support an inference of negligence from the nature and circumstances of an accident.","common_trap":"Treating the doctrine as strict liability or an automatic finding of negligence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bd5a7f10c193bb2d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0174","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Volenti non fit injuria","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An adult claimant fully understands a specific physical risk created by the defendant and freely agrees to run that risk. The defendant argues that the claimant consented not merely to the activity but to the relevant risk in the legal sense.\n\nWhich defence is potentially engaged?","options":{"A":"Contributory negligence only; consent can never be a complete defence.","B":"Frustration.","C":"Qualified privilege in defamation.","D":"Volenti non fit injuria, if genuine and voluntary agreement to the relevant risk is established.","E":"Necessity in contract."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Volenti is a complete defence where the claimant had sufficient knowledge of the risk and freely agreed to accept it. Mere knowledge of danger is not necessarily enough.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Volenti requires free and informed acceptance of the relevant risk.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Volenti requires free and informed acceptance of the relevant risk.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Qualified privilege in defamation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Volenti requires free and informed acceptance of the relevant risk.","D":"Correct. Volenti is a complete defence where the claimant had sufficient knowledge of the risk and freely agreed to accept it. Mere knowledge of danger is not necessarily enough.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Necessity in contract' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Volenti requires free and informed acceptance of the relevant risk."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law defence of volenti non fit injuria.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Volenti requires free and informed acceptance of the relevant risk.","common_trap":"Equating mere awareness of danger with legal consent to waive the claim.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c6bddfc8e47080d4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0175","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Trespass to land","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A protester deliberately enters privately owned fenced land without the owner’s permission or other lawful authority. No physical damage is caused.\n\nWhich tort is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Private nuisance only, because entry itself is never actionable.","B":"Negligence only if financial loss is proved.","C":"Conversion of the land.","D":"Defamation.","E":"Trespass to land, which is actionable without proof of physical damage."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Intentional direct entry onto land in another’s possession without lawful justification can constitute trespass. The tort is actionable per se and does not require proof of damage.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Unauthorised direct entry onto land can be trespass even without damage.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Unauthorised direct entry onto land can be trespass even without damage.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Conversion of the land' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Unauthorised direct entry onto land can be trespass even without damage.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Defamation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Unauthorised direct entry onto land can be trespass even without damage.","E":"Correct. Intentional direct entry onto land in another’s possession without lawful justification can constitute trespass. The tort is actionable per se and does not require proof of damage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law trespass to land principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Unauthorised direct entry onto land can be trespass even without damage.","common_trap":"Assuming damage is an essential element of trespass to land.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6bedc5c7344e1e9a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0176","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Statutory interpretation — purposive approach","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statutory provision is capable of two linguistically plausible readings. One reading would frustrate the evident statutory scheme, while the other advances the purpose Parliament sought to achieve.\n\nWhich interpretive approach may support consideration of the statute’s purpose?","options":{"A":"The purposive approach.","B":"The rule that statutory purpose can never be considered.","C":"The postal rule.","D":"The doctrine of privity.","E":"The rule against perpetuities."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Courts may use a purposive approach to statutory interpretation, reading legislation in light of the purpose of the statutory scheme while remaining within legitimate interpretive limits.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Courts may use a purposive approach to statutory interpretation, reading legislation in light of the purpose of the statutory scheme while remaining within legitimate interpretive limits.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Statutory interpretation can take account of legislative purpose.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The postal rule' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Statutory interpretation can take account of legislative purpose.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The doctrine of privity' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Statutory interpretation can take account of legislative purpose.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The rule against perpetuities' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Statutory interpretation can take account of legislative purpose."},"primary_legal_authority":"Principles of statutory interpretation.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Statutory interpretation can take account of legislative purpose.","common_trap":"Assuming interpretation is always confined to isolated dictionary meanings.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3ea641f8116f3aef"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0177","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — irrationality","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A public authority makes a discretionary decision that is alleged to be so unreasonable that no reasonable authority properly directing itself could have reached it. No Convention proportionality issue is raised.\n\nWhich traditional judicial-review ground is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Procedural impropriety only.","B":"Irrationality or Wednesbury unreasonableness.","C":"Private nuisance.","D":"Breach of contract automatically.","E":"Adverse possession."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The traditional irrationality ground addresses decisions meeting the stringent Wednesbury threshold. It is distinct from ordinary disagreement with the merits of a lawful discretionary choice.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Procedural impropriety only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Wednesbury irrationality is a demanding public-law standard of unreasonableness.","B":"Correct. The traditional irrationality ground addresses decisions meeting the stringent Wednesbury threshold. It is distinct from ordinary disagreement with the merits of a lawful discretionary choice.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Private nuisance' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Wednesbury irrationality is a demanding public-law standard of unreasonableness.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Wednesbury irrationality is a demanding public-law standard of unreasonableness.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Wednesbury irrationality is a demanding public-law standard of unreasonableness."},"primary_legal_authority":"Associated Provincial Picture Houses Ltd v Wednesbury Corporation.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Wednesbury irrationality is a demanding public-law standard of unreasonableness.","common_trap":"Treating judicial review as a merits appeal whenever a court would have chosen differently.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b4e3377e1f86c631"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0178","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"EU law — preliminary references","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"In litigation concerning an issue of EU law that remains legally relevant under the applicable post-Brexit framework, a solicitor refers to the former mechanism by which UK courts could make preliminary references to the Court of Justice under Article 267 TFEU. The solicitor assumes that UK courts continue to have the same general reference power after withdrawal.\n\nWhich is the safest analysis?","options":{"A":"UK courts remain Member State courts with an unrestricted Article 267 duty in every case.","B":"The CJEU has become the UK Supreme Court.","C":"The assumption is incorrect: the availability and effect of CJEU involvement after UK withdrawal depend on the Withdrawal Agreement and domestic post-Brexit legislation rather than the former general Member State Article 267 position.","D":"EU law can never be relevant in any UK proceeding after Brexit.","E":"Every domestic court must obtain CJEU permission before interpreting an Act of Parliament."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The UK is no longer an EU Member State, so the ordinary Member State preliminary-reference framework cannot simply be assumed. Specific arrangements, including under the Withdrawal Agreement, must be considered where relevant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Post-Brexit CJEU jurisdiction and references depend on the applicable withdrawal arrangements, not the former general Member State position.","B":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Post-Brexit CJEU jurisdiction and references depend on the applicable withdrawal arrangements, not the former general Member State position.","C":"Correct. The UK is no longer an EU Member State, so the ordinary Member State preliminary-reference framework cannot simply be assumed. Specific arrangements, including under the Withdrawal Agreement, must be considered where relevant.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Post-Brexit CJEU jurisdiction and references depend on the applicable withdrawal arrangements, not the former general Member State position.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Post-Brexit CJEU jurisdiction and references depend on the applicable withdrawal arrangements, not the former general Member State position."},"primary_legal_authority":"European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018; EU-UK Withdrawal Agreement as applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Post-Brexit CJEU jurisdiction and references depend on the applicable withdrawal arrangements, not the former general Member State position.","common_trap":"Applying the pre-withdrawal Article 267 regime unchanged.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6a68bee7ad7d22f1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0179","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Will interpretation — incorporation by reference","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A duly executed will refers to a separate written document that already existed when the will was executed, describing it sufficiently clearly and showing an intention that the document form part of the testamentary dispositions.\n\nWhich doctrine may permit the separate document to be treated as part of the will?","options":{"A":"Ademption.","B":"Overreaching.","C":"Frustration.","D":"Incorporation by reference, if its requirements are satisfied.","E":"Promissory estoppel."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A document not itself executed as a will may in appropriate circumstances be incorporated by reference where it existed at execution and is sufficiently identified by the will, among the doctrine’s requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Ademption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A pre-existing sufficiently identified document can sometimes be incorporated into a will by reference.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Overreaching' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A pre-existing sufficiently identified document can sometimes be incorporated into a will by reference.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A pre-existing sufficiently identified document can sometimes be incorporated into a will by reference.","D":"Correct. A document not itself executed as a will may in appropriate circumstances be incorporated by reference where it existed at execution and is sufficiently identified by the will, among the doctrine’s requirements.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Promissory estoppel' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A pre-existing sufficiently identified document can sometimes be incorporated into a will by reference."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law doctrine of incorporation by reference in wills.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A pre-existing sufficiently identified document can sometimes be incorporated into a will by reference.","common_trap":"Assuming every unattested document is necessarily irrelevant to testamentary interpretation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"752469746fa275cb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0180","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Personal representatives — assent","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After administration of an estate, personal representatives are ready to transfer a specifically devised registered property to the beneficiary entitled under the will.\n\nWhich concept describes the personal representatives’ formal transfer of estate property to the beneficiary in satisfaction of the beneficiary’s entitlement?","options":{"A":"A codicil.","B":"A caveat against the grant.","C":"A renunciation of probate.","D":"A Part 36 offer.","E":"An assent."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"An assent is the mechanism by which personal representatives vest estate property in a beneficiary entitled to it, subject to the applicable formalities and registration requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A codicil' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Personal representatives can transfer estate property to a beneficiary by assent.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A caveat against the grant' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Personal representatives can transfer estate property to a beneficiary by assent.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A renunciation of probate' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Personal representatives can transfer estate property to a beneficiary by assent.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A Part 36 offer' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Personal representatives can transfer estate property to a beneficiary by assent.","E":"Correct. An assent is the mechanism by which personal representatives vest estate property in a beneficiary entitled to it, subject to the applicable formalities and registration requirements."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925; Land Registration requirements where applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Personal representatives can transfer estate property to a beneficiary by assent.","common_trap":"Confusing estate administration transfer machinery with execution or alteration of the will.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"954d4dc8babd0992"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0181","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"IHT — immediately chargeable lifetime transfer","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual transfers substantial property into a discretionary trust during lifetime. No exemption applies. The transfer is not an outright gift to another individual.\n\nHow is the transfer ordinarily classified for inheritance-tax purposes?","options":{"A":"As a chargeable lifetime transfer rather than an ordinary potentially exempt transfer, subject to the IHT rules and thresholds.","B":"As a PET in every case because all lifetime gifts are PETs.","C":"As outside IHT permanently.","D":"As SDLT only.","E":"As corporation-tax income."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Lifetime transfers into relevant-property discretionary trusts are generally chargeable lifetime transfers rather than PETs, subject to exemptions, the nil-rate band and the statutory regime.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Lifetime transfers into relevant-property discretionary trusts are generally chargeable lifetime transfers rather than PETs, subject to exemptions, the nil-rate band and the statutory regime.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Transfers into many discretionary trusts are immediately chargeable lifetime transfers rather than PETs.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'As outside IHT permanently' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Transfers into many discretionary trusts are immediately chargeable lifetime transfers rather than PETs.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'As SDLT only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Transfers into many discretionary trusts are immediately chargeable lifetime transfers rather than PETs.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'As corporation-tax income' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Transfers into many discretionary trusts are immediately chargeable lifetime transfers rather than PETs."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984; SRA FLK2 taxation specification.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Transfers into many discretionary trusts are immediately chargeable lifetime transfers rather than PETs.","common_trap":"Treating every lifetime disposition as a potentially exempt transfer.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dec6fd55e919f3c2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0182","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Client account reconciliation","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm maintains client accounts. Its cashier says there is no need to compare the client cash book, client ledger balances and bank statements because the bank’s online balance is sufficient.\n\nWhich is the correct regulatory approach?","options":{"A":"An online bank balance replaces all accounting records.","B":"The firm must carry out client-account reconciliations at least every five weeks and keep the required records, investigating and correcting differences appropriately.","C":"Reconciliation is required only once when the firm closes.","D":"Only the client can reconcile the firm’s client account.","E":"Reconciliation is optional where no cheque is used."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require regular reconciliation of client accounts and records, ordinarily at least every five weeks, as an important safeguard for client money.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'An online bank balance replaces all accounting records' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Regular client-account reconciliation is a core Accounts Rules control.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules require regular reconciliation of client accounts and records, ordinarily at least every five weeks, as an important safeguard for client money.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Regular client-account reconciliation is a core Accounts Rules control.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Regular client-account reconciliation is a core Accounts Rules control.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Reconciliation is optional where no cheque is used' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Regular client-account reconciliation is a core Accounts Rules control."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 8.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Regular client-account reconciliation is a core Accounts Rules control.","common_trap":"Treating the bank balance alone as proof that individual client ledgers and accounting records are correct.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"86c5145021a8d4c5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0183","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Presumed resulting trust — purchase money","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"One person pays the entire purchase price for an investment asset but legal title is put solely into another adult’s name. There is no evidence of a gift, no applicable presumption of advancement, and no contrary intention.\n\nWhich equitable presumption may arise?","options":{"A":"An automatic charitable trust.","B":"A constructive trust always giving equal shares.","C":"A presumed resulting trust in favour of the person who provided the purchase money.","D":"A secret trust regardless of intention.","E":"No equitable interest can ever arise from purchase-money contribution."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Where one person provides purchase money but title is taken in another’s name, equity may presume a resulting trust in favour of the contributor, subject to evidence of contrary intention and the applicable modern rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'An automatic charitable trust' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Purchase-money contributions can give rise to a presumed resulting trust.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Purchase-money contributions can give rise to a presumed resulting trust.","C":"Correct. Where one person provides purchase money but title is taken in another’s name, equity may presume a resulting trust in favour of the contributor, subject to evidence of contrary intention and the applicable modern rules.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A secret trust regardless of intention' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Purchase-money contributions can give rise to a presumed resulting trust.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No equitable interest can ever arise from purchase-money contribution' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Purchase-money contributions can give rise to a presumed resulting trust."},"primary_legal_authority":"Resulting-trust principles; SRA FLK2 specification.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Purchase-money contributions can give rise to a presumed resulting trust.","common_trap":"Confusing presumed resulting trusts with automatic resulting trusts following incomplete beneficial disposition.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ee89424e91fe7719"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0184","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustee delegation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Trustees want to delegate investment functions to an appropriately qualified agent. They assume that once delegation occurs they have no further responsibilities concerning the agent.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the statutory framework?","options":{"A":"Trustees can never delegate investment functions.","B":"Delegation permanently transfers trusteeship to the agent.","C":"Any agent automatically becomes a beneficiary.","D":"Trustees may delegate permitted functions subject to statutory requirements, but duties concerning selection, terms and review of the agent can continue.","E":"Delegation eliminates every statutory duty of care."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Trustee Act permits delegation of specified functions but imposes safeguards, including requirements concerning appointment and ongoing review in relevant cases. Delegation is not an abdication of trusteeship.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Permitted delegation remains subject to statutory safeguards and continuing trustee responsibilities.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Delegation permanently transfers trusteeship to the agent' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Permitted delegation remains subject to statutory safeguards and continuing trustee responsibilities.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Permitted delegation remains subject to statutory safeguards and continuing trustee responsibilities.","D":"Correct. The Trustee Act permits delegation of specified functions but imposes safeguards, including requirements concerning appointment and ongoing review in relevant cases. Delegation is not an abdication of trusteeship.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Permitted delegation remains subject to statutory safeguards and continuing trustee responsibilities."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, Part IV.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Permitted delegation remains subject to statutory safeguards and continuing trustee responsibilities.","common_trap":"Assuming delegation eliminates all supervisory responsibility.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"12258b94cc4a56df"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0185","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Leasehold covenants — assignment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A tenant under a modern commercial lease assigns the lease to an assignee with the landlord’s consent. The landlord requires an authorised guarantee agreement from the outgoing tenant in circumstances where the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995 permits one.\n\nWhat is the function of the authorised guarantee agreement?","options":{"A":"It makes the outgoing tenant owner of the freehold.","B":"It automatically guarantees every future assignee indefinitely.","C":"It prevents the assignment from taking effect.","D":"It converts rent into a mortgage.","E":"It can require the outgoing tenant to guarantee performance by its immediate assignee, within the statutory limits."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The 1995 Act generally releases an assigning tenant from tenant covenants but permits an authorised guarantee agreement in defined circumstances, allowing a guarantee of the immediate assignee subject to statutory controls.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It makes the outgoing tenant owner of the freehold' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An AGA can preserve a limited guarantee by an outgoing tenant for its immediate assignee.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An AGA can preserve a limited guarantee by an outgoing tenant for its immediate assignee.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It prevents the assignment from taking effect' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An AGA can preserve a limited guarantee by an outgoing tenant for its immediate assignee.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It converts rent into a mortgage' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An AGA can preserve a limited guarantee by an outgoing tenant for its immediate assignee.","E":"Correct. The 1995 Act generally releases an assigning tenant from tenant covenants but permits an authorised guarantee agreement in defined circumstances, allowing a guarantee of the immediate assignee subject to statutory controls."},"primary_legal_authority":"Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An AGA can preserve a limited guarantee by an outgoing tenant for its immediate assignee.","common_trap":"Assuming an outgoing tenant either remains liable forever or can never give a post-assignment guarantee.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3809ad11db875f64"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0186","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Proprietary estoppel","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landowner clearly assures a neighbour that the neighbour will have a long-term right to use part of the land. The neighbour reasonably relies on the assurance and, to the landowner’s knowledge, spends substantial money in reliance. The landowner then seeks to deny any right.\n\nWhich equitable doctrine is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Proprietary estoppel, considering assurance, reliance, detriment and unconscionability in context.","B":"Promissory estoppel can only ever concern land ownership and is automatically identical.","C":"Adverse possession regardless of possession.","D":"Resulting trust automatically.","E":"Frustration."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Proprietary estoppel can arise from an assurance concerning rights in property, reasonable reliance and detriment where it would be unconscionable to permit the representor to resile; remedy is then separately assessed.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Proprietary estoppel can arise from an assurance concerning rights in property, reasonable reliance and detriment where it would be unconscionable to permit the representor to resile; remedy is then separately assessed.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Proprietary estoppel can protect detrimental reliance on sufficiently clear property assurances.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession regardless of possession' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Proprietary estoppel can protect detrimental reliance on sufficiently clear property assurances.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Proprietary estoppel can protect detrimental reliance on sufficiently clear property assurances.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Proprietary estoppel can protect detrimental reliance on sufficiently clear property assurances."},"primary_legal_authority":"Thorner v Major; Guest v Guest; proprietary-estoppel principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Proprietary estoppel can protect detrimental reliance on sufficiently clear property assurances.","common_trap":"Treating proprietary estoppel as automatically transferring the exact property promised without a separate remedial analysis.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ee0c90ec59686933"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0187","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Planning permission and building regulations","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer discovers that a substantial extension was constructed several years ago. The seller says that because planning permission was obtained, there was no need to consider building regulations approval.\n\nWhich is the best advice?","options":{"A":"Planning permission automatically constitutes building regulations approval.","B":"Planning control and building regulations are distinct regimes; obtaining planning permission does not by itself establish compliance with building regulations.","C":"Building regulations apply only to commercial buildings.","D":"Neither regime can affect conveyancing due diligence.","E":"Building regulations are contractual terms between buyer and seller."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Planning permission concerns planning control, while building regulations address matters such as construction standards. Conveyancing investigation may need to consider both separately.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Planning permission and building-regulations compliance are separate legal issues.","B":"Correct. Planning permission concerns planning control, while building regulations address matters such as construction standards. Conveyancing investigation may need to consider both separately.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Planning permission and building-regulations compliance are separate legal issues.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Neither regime can affect conveyancing due diligence' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Planning permission and building-regulations compliance are separate legal issues.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Building regulations are contractual terms between buyer and seller' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Planning permission and building-regulations compliance are separate legal issues."},"primary_legal_authority":"Town and Country Planning Act 1990; Building Act 1984 and Building Regulations framework.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Planning permission and building-regulations compliance are separate legal issues.","common_trap":"Treating approval under one public-law regime as approval under the other.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"646a631b698dfeb9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0188","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Title guarantee","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual seller disposes of registered freehold land with full title guarantee. The buyer asks whether the words have statutory significance rather than being merely descriptive.\n\nWhich is the best response?","options":{"A":"The words have no legal effect.","B":"Full title guarantee warrants the physical condition of every part of the building.","C":"Full title guarantee carries statutory implied covenants concerning title, subject to the Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1994.","D":"It eliminates the need to register the transfer.","E":"It guarantees that planning permission will be granted for future development."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The 1994 Act gives statutory effect to dispositions made with full or limited title guarantee, implying specified covenants concerning the disposer’s title and encumbrances, subject to the Act.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The words have no legal effect' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Title guarantee imports statutory covenants about title, not physical condition or future planning outcomes.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Title guarantee imports statutory covenants about title, not physical condition or future planning outcomes.","C":"Correct. The 1994 Act gives statutory effect to dispositions made with full or limited title guarantee, implying specified covenants concerning the disposer’s title and encumbrances, subject to the Act.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It eliminates the need to register the transfer' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Title guarantee imports statutory covenants about title, not physical condition or future planning outcomes.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It guarantees that planning permission will be granted for future development' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Title guarantee imports statutory covenants about title, not physical condition or future planning outcomes."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1994.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Title guarantee imports statutory covenants about title, not physical condition or future planning outcomes.","common_trap":"Treating title guarantee as a comprehensive warranty about the property itself.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7b5aa6b18349150c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0189","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Burglary — entry as trespasser","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person knowingly enters a closed shop as a trespasser intending at the time of entry to steal cash from the till.\n\nWhich offence is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Robbery only because every burglary involves force against a person.","B":"Handling stolen goods, even though nothing has yet been stolen.","C":"Criminal damage only.","D":"Burglary under Theft Act 1968 section 9(1)(a), assuming the remaining statutory elements are satisfied.","E":"Fraud by abuse of position."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 9(1)(a) burglary includes entering a building or part of a building as a trespasser with intent to steal, inflict grievous bodily harm or do unlawful damage, subject to the statutory formulation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Entry as a trespasser with the specified intent can constitute burglary before the intended theft is completed.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Handling stolen goods, even though nothing has yet been stolen' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Entry as a trespasser with the specified intent can constitute burglary before the intended theft is completed.","C":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Entry as a trespasser with the specified intent can constitute burglary before the intended theft is completed.","D":"Correct. Section 9(1)(a) burglary includes entering a building or part of a building as a trespasser with intent to steal, inflict grievous bodily harm or do unlawful damage, subject to the statutory formulation.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Fraud by abuse of position' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Entry as a trespasser with the specified intent can constitute burglary before the intended theft is completed."},"primary_legal_authority":"Theft Act 1968, s 9(1)(a).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Entry as a trespasser with the specified intent can constitute burglary before the intended theft is completed.","common_trap":"Assuming burglary requires completion of the intended ulterior offence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e5d36833bb7b3afa"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0190","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"PACE — exclusion for fairness","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"The prosecution seeks to rely on evidence obtained during a police investigation. The defence argues that, considering all the circumstances in which the evidence was obtained, admission would have such an adverse effect on the fairness of proceedings that it ought not be admitted. The evidence is not a confession.\n\nWhich statutory provision gives the court the relevant exclusion discretion?","options":{"A":"PACE section 76 only, which is exclusively the general rule for all non-confession evidence.","B":"Theft Act 1968 section 3.","C":"Bail Act 1976.","D":"Criminal Attempts Act 1981.","E":"Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984 section 78."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"PACE section 78 gives the court discretion to refuse prosecution evidence where, having regard to all the circumstances, admission would have such an adverse effect on the fairness of proceedings that it ought not be admitted. Section 76 separately addresses confessions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'PACE section 76 only, which is exclusively the general rule for all non-confession evidence' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. PACE s 78 provides a broad fairness-based discretion concerning prosecution evidence.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Theft Act 1968 section 3' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. PACE s 78 provides a broad fairness-based discretion concerning prosecution evidence.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Bail Act 1976' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. PACE s 78 provides a broad fairness-based discretion concerning prosecution evidence.","D":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. PACE s 78 provides a broad fairness-based discretion concerning prosecution evidence.","E":"Correct. PACE section 78 gives the court discretion to refuse prosecution evidence where, having regard to all the circumstances, admission would have such an adverse effect on the fairness of proceedings that it ought not be admitted. Section 76 separately addresses confessions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984, s 78.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"PACE s 78 provides a broad fairness-based discretion concerning prosecution evidence.","common_trap":"Confusing the general fairness discretion with the confession-specific section 76 rule.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b02f983e6be39a8b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0191","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — separate legal personality","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A shareholder owns all the shares in a private company and is also its sole director. The company enters into a supply contract in its own name and later fails to pay. The supplier argues that the shareholder is automatically the contracting party because she controls the company completely.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the ordinary position?","options":{"A":"The company is a separate legal person and its contractual debt is not automatically the shareholder’s personal debt merely because she owns and controls it.","B":"A sole shareholder is always personally liable for every company debt.","C":"The company has separate personality only if it has at least two shareholders.","D":"Control automatically makes every company contract the director’s contract.","E":"Separate personality applies only to public companies."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Incorporation creates a legal person distinct from its members. Sole ownership and control do not, without more, make the shareholder personally liable for the company’s contractual obligations.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Incorporation creates a legal person distinct from its members. Sole ownership and control do not, without more, make the shareholder personally liable for the company’s contractual obligations.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An incorporated company is ordinarily a legal person separate from its shareholders and directors.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An incorporated company is ordinarily a legal person separate from its shareholders and directors.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An incorporated company is ordinarily a legal person separate from its shareholders and directors.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An incorporated company is ordinarily a legal person separate from its shareholders and directors."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006; Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An incorporated company is ordinarily a legal person separate from its shareholders and directors.","common_trap":"Treating ownership/control as enough by itself to disregard corporate personality.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bab2fff63f3da0aa"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0192","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Agency — apparent authority","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company appoints a manager to a role that ordinarily carries authority to enter routine supply contracts and publicly holds her out in that role. Internally, the board secretly limits her authority. A supplier, unaware of the limitation, reasonably relies on the company’s representation and enters a routine contract with her.\n\nWhich doctrine may bind the company despite the undisclosed internal restriction?","options":{"A":"Ratification is the only possible doctrine.","B":"Apparent or ostensible authority arising from the principal’s representation to the third party.","C":"The manager’s private belief that she has authority.","D":"Separate legal personality automatically validates every act of every employee.","E":"The supplier’s authority to bind the company."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Apparent authority can arise where the principal represents that the agent has authority and a third party reasonably relies on that representation. A secret internal limitation may therefore not protect the principal against the third party.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A principal’s representation can create apparent authority despite an undisclosed internal restriction.","B":"Correct. Apparent authority can arise where the principal represents that the agent has authority and a third party reasonably relies on that representation. A secret internal limitation may therefore not protect the principal against the third party.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The manager’s private belief that she has authority' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A principal’s representation can create apparent authority despite an undisclosed internal restriction.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A principal’s representation can create apparent authority despite an undisclosed internal restriction.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The supplier’s authority to bind the company' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A principal’s representation can create apparent authority despite an undisclosed internal restriction."},"primary_legal_authority":"Freeman & Lockyer v Buckhurst Park Properties; general agency principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A principal’s representation can create apparent authority despite an undisclosed internal restriction.","common_trap":"Basing apparent authority solely on the agent’s own representation rather than one attributable to the principal.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"66e494ae1b680ea1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0193","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Share capital — redemption","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company has issued shares whose terms provide that they are redeemable. The directors want the company to redeem them. They assume that because the articles permit redeemable shares, the company may ignore the statutory rules governing redemption and capital maintenance.\n\nWhich is the best advice?","options":{"A":"Any share can be redeemed at any time without statutory compliance.","B":"Redeemable shares can be redeemed only by another shareholder, never by the company.","C":"The Companies Act statutory requirements governing redemption must still be satisfied, including applicable rules on payment and funding.","D":"Redemption automatically converts the company into an LLP.","E":"A provision in the articles disapplies all Companies Act capital rules."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Companies Act permits redeemable shares but regulates their redemption. The company must comply with the statutory conditions and capital-maintenance framework; the articles do not simply displace the legislation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Any share can be redeemed at any time without statutory compliance' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Redemption of redeemable shares remains subject to the Companies Act regime.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Redemption of redeemable shares remains subject to the Companies Act regime.","C":"Correct. The Companies Act permits redeemable shares but regulates their redemption. The company must comply with the statutory conditions and capital-maintenance framework; the articles do not simply displace the legislation.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Redemption of redeemable shares remains subject to the Companies Act regime.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A provision in the articles disapplies all Companies Act capital rules' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Redemption of redeemable shares remains subject to the Companies Act regime."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, Part 18, Chapter 3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Redemption of redeemable shares remains subject to the Companies Act regime.","common_trap":"Assuming contractual or constitutional permission eliminates statutory capital-maintenance requirements.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d8de7741a26e3dd5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0194","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — administration purpose","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An insolvent company has a viable underlying business but faces immediate creditor enforcement. Its advisers consider administration because a temporary collective procedure may allow restructuring or a better outcome than immediate liquidation.\n\nWhich statement best describes administration?","options":{"A":"Administration exists only to punish directors.","B":"Administration automatically dissolves the company on appointment.","C":"Administration is available only to solvent companies.","D":"Its statutory purposes include rescuing the company as a going concern, achieving a better result for creditors as a whole than winding up, or in the appropriate hierarchy realising property for secured or preferential creditors.","E":"Its sole purpose is to distribute dividends to shareholders."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Administration is a statutory corporate insolvency procedure with ordered objectives. Rescue as a going concern is the primary objective where reasonably practicable, followed by the statutory alternatives.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Administration pursues statutory rescue/creditor objectives in an ordered framework.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Administration pursues statutory rescue/creditor objectives in an ordered framework.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Administration pursues statutory rescue/creditor objectives in an ordered framework.","D":"Correct. Administration is a statutory corporate insolvency procedure with ordered objectives. Rescue as a going concern is the primary objective where reasonably practicable, followed by the statutory alternatives.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Its sole purpose is to distribute dividends to shareholders' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Administration pursues statutory rescue/creditor objectives in an ordered framework."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, Sch B1, para 3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Administration pursues statutory rescue/creditor objectives in an ordered framework.","common_trap":"Treating administration as simply another name for liquidation or dissolution.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"57f4a4015f3ae2a3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0195","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Limitation — negligence personal injury","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An adult claimant suffers personal injury allegedly caused by negligence. There is no disability, fraud or other special extension issue. The claimant knew immediately the material facts needed for the limitation analysis.\n\nWhat is the ordinary primary limitation period for a personal-injury negligence action?","options":{"A":"Six years from every negligent act.","B":"One year from the defendant’s admission.","C":"Twelve years in every tort claim.","D":"There is no limitation period for personal injury.","E":"Three years from accrual of the cause of action or, if later, the claimant’s date of knowledge, subject to the statutory regime and the court’s discretion."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Personal-injury actions are ordinarily subject to the three-year regime in section 11, measured from accrual or later date of knowledge, with section 33 providing a discretionary power in appropriate cases.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Personal-injury negligence claims ordinarily fall within the Limitation Act three-year regime.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'One year from the defendant’s admission' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Personal-injury negligence claims ordinarily fall within the Limitation Act three-year regime.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Personal-injury negligence claims ordinarily fall within the Limitation Act three-year regime.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'There is no limitation period for personal injury' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Personal-injury negligence claims ordinarily fall within the Limitation Act three-year regime.","E":"Correct. Personal-injury actions are ordinarily subject to the three-year regime in section 11, measured from accrual or later date of knowledge, with section 33 providing a discretionary power in appropriate cases."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, ss 11, 14 and 33.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Personal-injury negligence claims ordinarily fall within the Limitation Act three-year regime.","common_trap":"Applying the ordinary six-year tort period without recognising the special personal-injury rules.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"380a53d4ff0e696f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0196","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Expert evidence — permission","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party to civil proceedings wants to rely at trial on expert evidence about a technical issue. The party has already instructed an expert privately and assumes that this alone entitles it to adduce the report.\n\nWhat is the ordinary CPR position?","options":{"A":"The court’s permission is generally required to adduce expert evidence, and expert evidence is controlled by CPR Part 35.","B":"Expert evidence may be relied upon without restriction whenever a party has paid for it.","C":"Only jointly instructed experts can ever give evidence.","D":"Expert evidence is admissible only in criminal proceedings.","E":"The expert decides whether permission is necessary."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR Part 35 controls expert evidence and ordinarily requires the court’s permission to rely on expert evidence. The expert’s overriding duty is to the court rather than to the instructing party.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR Part 35 controls expert evidence and ordinarily requires the court’s permission to rely on expert evidence. The expert’s overriding duty is to the court rather than to the instructing party.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Civil expert evidence is subject to court control and ordinarily requires permission.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Civil expert evidence is subject to court control and ordinarily requires permission.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Civil expert evidence is subject to court control and ordinarily requires permission.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The expert decides whether permission is necessary' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Civil expert evidence is subject to court control and ordinarily requires permission."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 35.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Civil expert evidence is subject to court control and ordinarily requires permission.","common_trap":"Confusing private instruction of an expert with entitlement to rely on the evidence in proceedings.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ce752e1fa37d4b47"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0197","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Expert duty to court","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An expert instructed by a claimant is asked by the claimant’s solicitor to omit an important technical qualification because including it would weaken the claim.\n\nWhat is the expert’s overriding duty?","options":{"A":"To advance the claimant’s case regardless of technical accuracy.","B":"To help the court on matters within the expert’s expertise, overriding any obligation to the person instructing or paying the expert.","C":"To guarantee that the claimant wins.","D":"To negotiate settlement for the claimant.","E":"To follow every instruction of the solicitor even if misleading."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Under CPR Part 35, the expert’s duty is to help the court on matters within expertise, and that duty overrides obligations to the instructing or paying party.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'To advance the claimant’s case regardless of technical accuracy' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An expert’s overriding duty is to the court.","B":"Correct. Under CPR Part 35, the expert’s duty is to help the court on matters within expertise, and that duty overrides obligations to the instructing or paying party.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'To guarantee that the claimant wins' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An expert’s overriding duty is to the court.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'To negotiate settlement for the claimant' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An expert’s overriding duty is to the court.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An expert’s overriding duty is to the court."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 35.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An expert’s overriding duty is to the court.","common_trap":"Treating an expert as an advocate whose function is to maximise the instructing party’s prospects.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1e55a406c3493044"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0198","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conflicts — own interest","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor is advising a client whether to sue a business in which the solicitor personally owns a substantial financial interest. The outcome could materially affect the solicitor’s own wealth.\n\nWhich is the best regulatory analysis?","options":{"A":"The solicitor may act whenever the client consents.","B":"Only conflicts between two clients are regulated.","C":"There is an own-interest conflict or significant risk of one, and the SRA prohibition is not cured merely by obtaining client consent.","D":"The solicitor can act if the personal investment is disclosed after judgment.","E":"Financial interests are irrelevant to professional conflicts."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code prohibits acting where there is an own-interest conflict or significant risk of one. Unlike specified client-conflict exceptions, client consent does not provide a general exception to the own-interest prohibition.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A solicitor must not act where an own-interest conflict or significant risk exists.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A solicitor must not act where an own-interest conflict or significant risk exists.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code prohibits acting where there is an own-interest conflict or significant risk of one. Unlike specified client-conflict exceptions, client consent does not provide a general exception to the own-interest prohibition.","D":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A solicitor must not act where an own-interest conflict or significant risk exists.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Financial interests are irrelevant to professional conflicts' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A solicitor must not act where an own-interest conflict or significant risk exists."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 6.1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A solicitor must not act where an own-interest conflict or significant risk exists.","common_trap":"Assuming disclosure and consent automatically cure a solicitor’s personal financial conflict.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4fb0df5556758f65"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0199","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"SRA — supervision","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor delegates substantial work on a client matter to a junior employee. The solicitor assumes that delegation means no supervision is required because the junior is personally responsible for anything that goes wrong.\n\nWhich is the best regulatory position?","options":{"A":"Delegation removes all supervisory responsibilities.","B":"Only partners may supervise legal work.","C":"Supervision is required only in criminal matters.","D":"Solicitors must ensure that work they supervise is effectively supervised and that those they manage are competent to carry out their roles and keep their professional knowledge and skills up to date.","E":"A junior employee can never perform substantive legal work."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Code imposes responsibilities concerning effective supervision and competence. Delegation is permissible but does not amount to abandonment of supervisory duties.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Delegation removes all supervisory responsibilities' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Delegated legal work must be appropriately supervised and performed by competent people.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Delegated legal work must be appropriately supervised and performed by competent people.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Delegated legal work must be appropriately supervised and performed by competent people.","D":"Correct. The Code imposes responsibilities concerning effective supervision and competence. Delegation is permissible but does not amount to abandonment of supervisory duties.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Delegated legal work must be appropriately supervised and performed by competent people."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, paras 3.5–3.6.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Delegated legal work must be appropriately supervised and performed by competent people.","common_trap":"Equating delegation with complete transfer of professional responsibility.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"313d81b32742605b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0200","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consideration — past consideration","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A homeowner asks a neighbour to help move heavy furniture. The neighbour does so as a favour. Only after the work is finished, the homeowner promises to pay £500 for the completed assistance. There was no earlier request accompanied by an understanding that payment would be made and no other exception applies.\n\nWhich rule is most relevant to enforceability of the later promise?","options":{"A":"Any moral obligation is automatically sufficient consideration.","B":"Consideration is unnecessary for every simple contract.","C":"The completed act automatically becomes future consideration.","D":"The promise is enforceable solely because £500 is a reasonable amount.","E":"Past consideration is generally not good consideration for a later promise."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"An act completed before a promise is ordinarily past consideration and cannot support that later promise, subject to recognised exceptions not present on these facts.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Past consideration is generally not sufficient consideration for a subsequent promise.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Past consideration is generally not sufficient consideration for a subsequent promise.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Past consideration is generally not sufficient consideration for a subsequent promise.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The promise is enforceable solely because £500 is a reasonable amount' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Past consideration is generally not sufficient consideration for a subsequent promise.","E":"Correct. An act completed before a promise is ordinarily past consideration and cannot support that later promise, subject to recognised exceptions not present on these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law consideration principles; Re McArdle.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Past consideration is generally not sufficient consideration for a subsequent promise.","common_trap":"Assuming a later promise to reward an earlier gratuitous act necessarily creates an enforceable simple contract.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"badd98dea52b383b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0201","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consideration — sufficiency not adequacy","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two parties bargain at arm’s length. One promises to transfer a valuable item in exchange for a small but legally recognised consideration. There is no duress, misrepresentation, mistake or other vitiating factor.\n\nWhich principle is most relevant?","options":{"A":"Consideration must be sufficient in law but need not be adequate in economic value.","B":"Consideration must be economically equivalent to the promise.","C":"Courts refuse to recognise nominal consideration in all circumstances.","D":"A bargain is invalid whenever one party obtains a good deal.","E":"Only money can constitute consideration."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Common law requires consideration to have sufficient legal value but ordinarily does not assess the adequacy of the bargain merely because values are unequal.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Common law requires consideration to have sufficient legal value but ordinarily does not assess the adequacy of the bargain merely because values are unequal.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Consideration must be sufficient but need not be adequate.","C":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Consideration must be sufficient but need not be adequate.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Consideration must be sufficient but need not be adequate.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Consideration must be sufficient but need not be adequate."},"primary_legal_authority":"Thomas v Thomas; Chappell & Co Ltd v Nestlé Co Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Consideration must be sufficient but need not be adequate.","common_trap":"Confusing legal sufficiency with economic equivalence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cb6f6999e45ef23d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0202","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Discharge — substantial performance","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contractor completes almost all contractual work but leaves a small defect that can be remedied at modest cost. The contract is not entire in a way that makes perfect completion an express condition precedent to all payment.\n\nWhich doctrine may allow the contractor to recover the contract price subject to a deduction for the defect?","options":{"A":"Frustration.","B":"Substantial performance.","C":"Economic duress.","D":"Privity.","E":"Mistake."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Where performance is substantial rather than exact and the breach is comparatively minor, the performing party may be entitled to the contract price less the appropriate deduction, depending on the contract and seriousness of the defect.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Substantial performance can permit recovery subject to deduction for defects.","B":"Correct. Where performance is substantial rather than exact and the breach is comparatively minor, the performing party may be entitled to the contract price less the appropriate deduction, depending on the contract and seriousness of the defect.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Economic duress' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Substantial performance can permit recovery subject to deduction for defects.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Privity' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Substantial performance can permit recovery subject to deduction for defects.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Mistake' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Substantial performance can permit recovery subject to deduction for defects."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hoenig v Isaacs; substantial-performance principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Substantial performance can permit recovery subject to deduction for defects.","common_trap":"Assuming any minor departure from perfect performance necessarily forfeits the whole contract price.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"84b3d3caf3db026f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0203","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Duty of care — novel situations","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant seeks to impose a negligence duty in a genuinely novel situation not governed by an established category. The relationship involves foreseeable harm, but proximity and whether it is fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty require analysis.\n\nWhich framework is conventionally relevant?","options":{"A":"Strict liability whenever harm is foreseeable.","B":"The postal rule.","C":"The Caparo considerations of foreseeability, proximity and whether imposing a duty is fair, just and reasonable, applied incrementally with existing authority.","D":"The rule in Rylands v Fletcher in every negligence case.","E":"The Companies Act directors’ duties."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"For genuinely novel duty situations, the Caparo considerations remain relevant alongside the incremental approach and analogy with established categories. Established duties should not be unnecessarily re-tested as if every case were novel.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Novel negligence duties are assessed incrementally with reference to foreseeability, proximity and fairness considerations.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The postal rule' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Novel negligence duties are assessed incrementally with reference to foreseeability, proximity and fairness considerations.","C":"Correct. For genuinely novel duty situations, the Caparo considerations remain relevant alongside the incremental approach and analogy with established categories. Established duties should not be unnecessarily re-tested as if every case were novel.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Novel negligence duties are assessed incrementally with reference to foreseeability, proximity and fairness considerations.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Companies Act directors’ duties' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Novel negligence duties are assessed incrementally with reference to foreseeability, proximity and fairness considerations."},"primary_legal_authority":"Caparo Industries plc v Dickman; Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire Police.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Novel negligence duties are assessed incrementally with reference to foreseeability, proximity and fairness considerations.","common_trap":"Applying a mechanical three-stage test to overturn established duty categories or treating foreseeability alone as sufficient.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ee041abc09729d18"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0204","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Pure economic loss — defective property","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A purchaser discovers that a building negligently constructed by a contractor is worth less because the defect requires expensive repair. The defect has not caused personal injury or damage to other property. The purchaser sues the contractor in ordinary negligence solely for the cost of repairing the defective building itself.\n\nWhich issue is central?","options":{"A":"All economic loss is automatically recoverable in negligence.","B":"The repair cost is necessarily personal injury.","C":"The contractor is strictly liable for all defects forever.","D":"The claim concerns pure economic loss, which is not ordinarily recoverable in negligence merely because a defective product or building is worth less or needs repair, absent a recognised basis such as assumption of responsibility.","E":"Remoteness is the only issue because duty rules never restrict economic loss."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Negligence generally does not permit recovery for the cost of repairing a defective thing itself where the loss is purely economic, absent an applicable exception or separate duty basis.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Pure economic loss from the defect in the property itself is ordinarily restricted in negligence.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The repair cost is necessarily personal injury' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Pure economic loss from the defect in the property itself is ordinarily restricted in negligence.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The contractor is strictly liable for all defects forever' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Pure economic loss from the defect in the property itself is ordinarily restricted in negligence.","D":"Correct. Negligence generally does not permit recovery for the cost of repairing a defective thing itself where the loss is purely economic, absent an applicable exception or separate duty basis.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Pure economic loss from the defect in the property itself is ordinarily restricted in negligence."},"primary_legal_authority":"Murphy v Brentwood District Council; pure-economic-loss principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Pure economic loss from the defect in the property itself is ordinarily restricted in negligence.","common_trap":"Treating financial consequences of a defective product as ordinary physical property damage.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1183e7369fad4f3a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0205","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — truth defence","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant publishes a statement that is defamatory of the claimant but can prove that the imputation conveyed by the statement is substantially true.\n\nWhich statutory defence is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Honest opinion only.","B":"Qualified privilege is the only possible defence.","C":"Volenti.","D":"Contributory negligence.","E":"Truth."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 2 of the Defamation Act provides a defence where the defendant shows that the imputation conveyed by the statement complained of is substantially true.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Honest opinion only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Substantial truth of the defamatory imputation is a statutory defence.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Substantial truth of the defamatory imputation is a statutory defence.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Volenti' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Substantial truth of the defamatory imputation is a statutory defence.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Contributory negligence' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Substantial truth of the defamatory imputation is a statutory defence.","E":"Correct. Section 2 of the Defamation Act provides a defence where the defendant shows that the imputation conveyed by the statement complained of is substantially true."},"primary_legal_authority":"Defamation Act 2013, s 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Substantial truth of the defamatory imputation is a statutory defence.","common_trap":"Assuming a defamatory meaning necessarily results in liability regardless of truth.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ff56d9ab05ec87b0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0206","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Court hierarchy — Supreme Court precedent","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"The Supreme Court is considering one of its own earlier decisions. The earlier case is directly relevant, but adherence would cause serious injustice because circumstances and legal understanding have materially developed.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the Supreme Court’s approach to its own precedents?","options":{"A":"It ordinarily treats its previous decisions as binding but may depart from them when it appears right to do so, exercising that power cautiously.","B":"It is absolutely bound by every previous decision and can never depart.","C":"It must obtain Parliament’s permission before departing.","D":"Only the High Court can overrule a Supreme Court case.","E":"Every panel may disregard earlier cases whenever it prefers a different result."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Practice Statement introduced flexibility allowing the highest court to depart from its own previous decisions when it appears right, while recognising the importance of certainty and using the power cautiously.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Practice Statement introduced flexibility allowing the highest court to depart from its own previous decisions when it appears right, while recognising the importance of certainty and using the power cautiously.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The Supreme Court may depart from its own precedent in appropriate cases but does so cautiously.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The Supreme Court may depart from its own precedent in appropriate cases but does so cautiously.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The Supreme Court may depart from its own precedent in appropriate cases but does so cautiously.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The Supreme Court may depart from its own precedent in appropriate cases but does so cautiously."},"primary_legal_authority":"Practice Statement (Judicial Precedent) [1966] 1 WLR 1234; Supreme Court precedent practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The Supreme Court may depart from its own precedent in appropriate cases but does so cautiously.","common_trap":"Treating horizontal precedent at the Supreme Court as either absolutely rigid or wholly optional.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b8d181ac42cce388"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0207","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — declaration of incompatibility","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A higher court concludes that a provision of primary legislation cannot be interpreted compatibly with a Convention right using section 3 of the Human Rights Act. The court has jurisdiction to consider a declaration under section 4.\n\nWhat is the effect of a declaration of incompatibility?","options":{"A":"It invalidates the Act automatically from the beginning.","B":"It does not itself affect the validity, continuing operation or enforcement of the incompatible provision; it signals incompatibility through the statutory mechanism.","C":"It automatically rewrites the legislation.","D":"It is a criminal penalty against Parliament.","E":"It binds Parliament to enact a specified replacement text within 24 hours."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A section 4 declaration does not strike down primary legislation. The incompatible provision remains legally valid and operative unless and until changed through the constitutional processes available.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A declaration of incompatibility does not invalidate primary legislation.","B":"Correct. A section 4 declaration does not strike down primary legislation. The incompatible provision remains legally valid and operative unless and until changed through the constitutional processes available.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A declaration of incompatibility does not invalidate primary legislation.","D":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. A declaration of incompatibility does not invalidate primary legislation.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It binds Parliament to enact a specified replacement text within 24 hours' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A declaration of incompatibility does not invalidate primary legislation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A declaration of incompatibility does not invalidate primary legislation.","common_trap":"Confusing the HRA declaration mechanism with constitutional strike-down powers found in some other systems.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c75fbd71d75505de"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0208","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Codicils — republication","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator validly executes a codicil confirming an earlier will. The issue is the date from which the will is treated as speaking for certain purposes after the codicil.\n\nWhich doctrine is relevant?","options":{"A":"Ademption automatically.","B":"Intestacy regardless of the will.","C":"Republication by codicil, under which a duly executed codicil can republish the will as of the codicil’s date, subject to context and applicable rules.","D":"Revocation by marriage in every case.","E":"Overreaching."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A valid codicil can republish the will, causing the will and codicil to be read together and, for relevant purposes, treating the will as re-executed at the codicil date.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A valid codicil can republish an existing will.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Intestacy regardless of the will' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A valid codicil can republish an existing will.","C":"Correct. A valid codicil can republish the will, causing the will and codicil to be read together and, for relevant purposes, treating the will as re-executed at the codicil date.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A valid codicil can republish an existing will.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Overreaching' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A valid codicil can republish an existing will."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837 and common-law principles of republication by codicil.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A valid codicil can republish an existing will.","common_trap":"Assuming a codicil merely adds text without potentially affecting the operative date/context of the will.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fffa65e5bc109cf2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0209","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"IHT — charitable exemption","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator leaves part of the estate outright to a qualifying UK charity. Assume the recipient and transfer satisfy the statutory conditions for the charitable exemption.\n\nWhat is the ordinary IHT treatment of that transfer?","options":{"A":"It is always taxed at the highest rate.","B":"It is a potentially exempt lifetime transfer.","C":"It is subject only to corporation tax payable by the estate.","D":"It is generally exempt from inheritance tax.","E":"Charitable status is irrelevant to IHT."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Qualifying transfers to charities benefit from the inheritance-tax charitable exemption, subject to the statutory conditions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Qualifying charitable transfers are generally IHT-exempt.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It is a potentially exempt lifetime transfer' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Qualifying charitable transfers are generally IHT-exempt.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Qualifying charitable transfers are generally IHT-exempt.","D":"Correct. Qualifying transfers to charities benefit from the inheritance-tax charitable exemption, subject to the statutory conditions.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Charitable status is irrelevant to IHT' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Qualifying charitable transfers are generally IHT-exempt."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, s 23.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Qualifying charitable transfers are generally IHT-exempt.","common_trap":"Treating every testamentary gift as chargeable regardless of exempt recipient.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"20b2d7cc9d97dc1d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0210","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Administration — appropriation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Personal representatives hold several estate assets and wish, with the necessary statutory safeguards, to satisfy a beneficiary’s entitlement by transferring a particular estate asset to that beneficiary rather than selling it and paying cash.\n\nWhich administration power is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Ademption.","B":"Revocation by destruction.","C":"A caveat.","D":"A Part 36 offer.","E":"Appropriation of an estate asset in or towards satisfaction of a beneficiary’s interest."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Personal representatives have a statutory power of appropriation allowing property to be appropriated in or towards satisfaction of an interested person’s share, subject to the statutory requirements including relevant consents.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Ademption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Appropriation can satisfy a beneficiary’s entitlement with a particular estate asset.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Revocation by destruction' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Appropriation can satisfy a beneficiary’s entitlement with a particular estate asset.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A caveat' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Appropriation can satisfy a beneficiary’s entitlement with a particular estate asset.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A Part 36 offer' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Appropriation can satisfy a beneficiary’s entitlement with a particular estate asset.","E":"Correct. Personal representatives have a statutory power of appropriation allowing property to be appropriated in or towards satisfaction of an interested person’s share, subject to the statutory requirements including relevant consents."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925, s 41.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Appropriation can satisfy a beneficiary’s entitlement with a particular estate asset.","common_trap":"Confusing appropriation during estate administration with a specific testamentary gift or assent alone.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b49d76df70b113db"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0211","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Client money — prompt banking","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm receives client money into its hands for a conveyancing matter. There is no agreed alternative arrangement and no exceptional reason to hold it elsewhere.\n\nWhat is the ordinary Accounts Rules requirement?","options":{"A":"Client money must be paid promptly into a client account, subject to the permitted exceptions in the Rules.","B":"The firm may hold the cash indefinitely in an office safe.","C":"All client money must first be paid into a partner’s personal account.","D":"Client money is paid into business account whenever convenient.","E":"Banking is required only at the end of the matter."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require client money to be paid promptly into client account unless a permitted exception applies.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Accounts Rules require client money to be paid promptly into client account unless a permitted exception applies.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The firm may hold the cash indefinitely in an office safe' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Client money ordinarily must be paid promptly into client account.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client money ordinarily must be paid promptly into client account.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client money ordinarily must be paid promptly into client account.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client money ordinarily must be paid promptly into client account."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 2.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client money ordinarily must be paid promptly into client account.","common_trap":"Treating prompt safeguarding of client money as optional.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ade48b2205d915b9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0212","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Secret trusts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator leaves property by will absolutely to a named person, but during lifetime communicates an obligation that the recipient is to hold the property for another person. The recipient accepts the obligation in circumstances satisfying the relevant doctrine.\n\nWhich equitable doctrine may enforce the obligation despite it not appearing fully on the face of the will?","options":{"A":"Resulting trusts only.","B":"Secret trusts.","C":"Adverse possession.","D":"Promissory estoppel.","E":"Frustration."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Equity recognises fully and half-secret trusts in defined circumstances where the necessary intention, communication and acceptance requirements are met, notwithstanding ordinary testamentary formality concerns.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Resulting trusts only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Secret trusts can enforce communicated and accepted testamentary trust obligations in equity.","B":"Correct. Equity recognises fully and half-secret trusts in defined circumstances where the necessary intention, communication and acceptance requirements are met, notwithstanding ordinary testamentary formality concerns.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Secret trusts can enforce communicated and accepted testamentary trust obligations in equity.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Promissory estoppel' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Secret trusts can enforce communicated and accepted testamentary trust obligations in equity.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Secret trusts can enforce communicated and accepted testamentary trust obligations in equity."},"primary_legal_authority":"Secret trust principles, including Ottaway v Norman and Blackwell v Blackwell.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Secret trusts can enforce communicated and accepted testamentary trust obligations in equity.","common_trap":"Assuming every arrangement outside the wording of a will is necessarily void without considering the secret-trust doctrine.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"48598f85574ef98d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0213","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Charitable trusts — public benefit","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor declares a trust for a purpose that falls within a statutory description of charitable purposes. The trustees assume that falling within a listed description alone conclusively establishes charitable status.\n\nWhat further requirement is fundamental?","options":{"A":"Every listed purpose is automatically charitable regardless of benefit.","B":"The trust must have identifiable individual beneficiaries with fixed shares.","C":"The purpose must also be for the public benefit under the statutory charitable framework.","D":"A charity must distribute all income to the settlor’s family.","E":"Only trusts relieving poverty can be charitable."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Under the Charities Act framework, a purpose must fall within the statutory descriptions and be for the public benefit. Public benefit remains a substantive requirement.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Charitable status requires a charitable purpose for the public benefit.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Charitable status requires a charitable purpose for the public benefit.","C":"Correct. Under the Charities Act framework, a purpose must fall within the statutory descriptions and be for the public benefit. Public benefit remains a substantive requirement.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Charitable status requires a charitable purpose for the public benefit.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Charitable status requires a charitable purpose for the public benefit."},"primary_legal_authority":"Charities Act 2011, ss 2–4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Charitable status requires a charitable purpose for the public benefit.","common_trap":"Treating the statutory list of purposes as eliminating the public-benefit requirement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9f5c05389d11d2d6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0214","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — four unities","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two people want to hold the legal estate jointly. Their solicitor explains that a legal estate cannot be held as a tenancy in common.\n\nWhich statement best reflects co-ownership of the legal title?","options":{"A":"The legal estate can be held only as a tenancy in common.","B":"Legal and beneficial ownership must always use the same form of co-ownership.","C":"A tenancy in common creates separate legal titles to physical halves of the property.","D":"The legal estate is held by co-owners as joint tenants, while the beneficial ownership may be a joint tenancy or tenancy in common.","E":"Co-ownership of legal land is prohibited."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Under the statutory scheme, co-owners hold the legal estate jointly. The equitable beneficial interest may be held jointly or in common depending on the circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Co-owned legal title is held jointly; tenancy in common is an equitable form of co-ownership.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Co-owned legal title is held jointly; tenancy in common is an equitable form of co-ownership.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A tenancy in common creates separate legal titles to physical halves of the property' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Co-owned legal title is held jointly; tenancy in common is an equitable form of co-ownership.","D":"Correct. Under the statutory scheme, co-owners hold the legal estate jointly. The equitable beneficial interest may be held jointly or in common depending on the circumstances.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Co-ownership of legal land is prohibited' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Co-owned legal title is held jointly; tenancy in common is an equitable form of co-ownership."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, ss 1(6), 34 and 36.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Co-owned legal title is held jointly; tenancy in common is an equitable form of co-ownership.","common_trap":"Assuming beneficial and legal modes of co-ownership are identical.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5331dc4b4c28f934"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0215","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Lease — certainty of term","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An agreement grants exclusive possession of premises “until the tenant no longer needs them”, with no fixed maximum duration and no other rule making the term certain.\n\nWhich leasehold requirement is most directly in issue?","options":{"A":"Privity of contract only.","B":"Overreaching.","C":"Adverse possession.","D":"The equity of redemption.","E":"Certainty of term."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A lease requires a term that is sufficiently certain under the applicable leasehold principles. An indeterminate duration may fail that requirement unless a statutory or common-law rule resolves the uncertainty.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Privity of contract only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A lease requires sufficient certainty of duration.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Overreaching' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A lease requires sufficient certainty of duration.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A lease requires sufficient certainty of duration.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The equity of redemption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A lease requires sufficient certainty of duration.","E":"Correct. A lease requires a term that is sufficiently certain under the applicable leasehold principles. An indeterminate duration may fail that requirement unless a statutory or common-law rule resolves the uncertainty."},"primary_legal_authority":"Prudential Assurance Co Ltd v London Residuary Body; leasehold principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A lease requires sufficient certainty of duration.","common_trap":"Focusing only on exclusive possession and ignoring the separate certainty-of-term requirement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"00a3147b0a1b8ae4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0216","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Exchange — formula B","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two solicitors hold their respective clients’ signed contracts and agree to exchange by telephone using the Law Society formula designed for an exchange where each solicitor holds their own client’s signed part and undertakes the agreed exchange steps.\n\nWhich practical point is most important?","options":{"A":"Telephone exchange must follow the agreed Law Society formula precisely so that the parties know when exchange becomes binding and what undertakings each solicitor assumes.","B":"A telephone exchange can never create a binding land contract.","C":"The solicitors may improvise the exchange without recording the agreed terms.","D":"Exchange is legally irrelevant because only completion creates a contract.","E":"The buyer alone decides when exchange occurred."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Law Society exchange formulae provide a controlled mechanism for exchange by telephone and create professional undertakings. Accurate compliance is essential because exchange is the point at which the sale contract ordinarily becomes binding.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Law Society exchange formulae provide a controlled mechanism for exchange by telephone and create professional undertakings. Accurate compliance is essential because exchange is the point at which the sale contract ordinarily becomes binding.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Telephone exchange under a Law Society formula must be carried out accurately, with attention to the undertakings and moment of exchange.","C":"Incorrect. This makes consent or agreement determinative when the legal test requires the identified statutory or doctrinal conditions. Telephone exchange under a Law Society formula must be carried out accurately, with attention to the undertakings and moment of exchange.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Telephone exchange under a Law Society formula must be carried out accurately, with attention to the undertakings and moment of exchange.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The buyer alone decides when exchange occurred' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Telephone exchange under a Law Society formula must be carried out accurately, with attention to the undertakings and moment of exchange."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law Society Formulae for exchanging contracts; standard conveyancing practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Telephone exchange under a Law Society formula must be carried out accurately, with attention to the undertakings and moment of exchange.","common_trap":"Treating exchange formulae as informal administrative conventions without legal/professional consequences.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"68ab09ace0771f76"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0217","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Completion — undertaking to redeem mortgage","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller’s registered title is subject to a mortgage that will be discharged from the sale proceeds. The buyer’s solicitor cannot obtain registration free of the charge unless appropriate redemption arrangements are in place.\n\nWhich conveyancing mechanism is commonly used?","options":{"A":"A promise from the estate agent to remove the Land Registry entry.","B":"A solicitor’s undertaking to redeem the existing charge and procure its discharge following completion.","C":"A Part 36 offer.","D":"A declaration of incompatibility.","E":"The buyer simply ignores the registered charge."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Where completion proceeds before the existing registered charge has been formally discharged, the seller’s solicitor commonly gives a professional undertaking to redeem it and obtain the necessary discharge.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A promise from the estate agent to remove the Land Registry entry' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A professional undertaking commonly protects the buyer where an existing mortgage is redeemed from completion funds.","B":"Correct. Where completion proceeds before the existing registered charge has been formally discharged, the seller’s solicitor commonly gives a professional undertaking to redeem it and obtain the necessary discharge.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A Part 36 offer' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A professional undertaking commonly protects the buyer where an existing mortgage is redeemed from completion funds.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A declaration of incompatibility' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A professional undertaking commonly protects the buyer where an existing mortgage is redeemed from completion funds.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The buyer simply ignores the registered charge' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A professional undertaking commonly protects the buyer where an existing mortgage is redeemed from completion funds."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA undertaking obligations; standard conveyancing completion practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A professional undertaking commonly protects the buyer where an existing mortgage is redeemed from completion funds.","common_trap":"Assuming completion automatically erases the seller’s registered mortgage.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5ad96518728c19d1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0218","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Lease renewal — LTA 1954 contracting out","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landlord and business tenant want a new commercial tenancy to fall outside the security-of-tenure provisions of Part II of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954. They intend to agree this before the tenant becomes bound to take the lease.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"A clause saying “no renewal” is always sufficient regardless of procedure.","B":"Security of tenure can never be excluded.","C":"The parties can contract out only by following the prescribed statutory procedure before the tenant becomes contractually bound.","D":"Contracting out can be done orally after the lease expires.","E":"Only a court can exclude the Act after the tenancy begins."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The 1954 Act permits exclusion of security of tenure for a new tenancy if the prescribed warning notice/declaration procedure is correctly followed before the tenant becomes contractually bound.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contracting out of 1954 Act security requires compliance with the prescribed pre-commitment procedure.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contracting out of 1954 Act security requires compliance with the prescribed pre-commitment procedure.","C":"Correct. The 1954 Act permits exclusion of security of tenure for a new tenancy if the prescribed warning notice/declaration procedure is correctly followed before the tenant becomes contractually bound.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Contracting out can be done orally after the lease expires' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Contracting out of 1954 Act security requires compliance with the prescribed pre-commitment procedure.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contracting out of 1954 Act security requires compliance with the prescribed pre-commitment procedure."},"primary_legal_authority":"Landlord and Tenant Act 1954, s 38A; Regulatory Reform (Business Tenancies) (England and Wales) Order 2003.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contracting out of 1954 Act security requires compliance with the prescribed pre-commitment procedure.","common_trap":"Assuming ordinary contractual wording alone disapplies statutory renewal rights.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"87f14b01dd1f00e4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0219","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Robbery — force and theft","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person dishonestly takes another’s phone intending permanently to deprive the owner and, immediately before taking it, deliberately pushes the owner to the ground in order to steal the phone.\n\nWhich offence is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Theft only because force is irrelevant to robbery.","B":"Burglary because every robbery involves entering a building.","C":"Handling stolen goods only.","D":"Robbery, because force is used immediately before the theft and in order to steal.","E":"Criminal damage."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Robbery requires theft plus use or threat of force on a person immediately before or at the time of stealing and in order to steal. The facts directly raise those elements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Theft accompanied by qualifying force used immediately before or at the time and in order to steal can constitute robbery.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Theft accompanied by qualifying force used immediately before or at the time and in order to steal can constitute robbery.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Handling stolen goods only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Theft accompanied by qualifying force used immediately before or at the time and in order to steal can constitute robbery.","D":"Correct. Robbery requires theft plus use or threat of force on a person immediately before or at the time of stealing and in order to steal. The facts directly raise those elements.","E":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Theft accompanied by qualifying force used immediately before or at the time and in order to steal can constitute robbery."},"primary_legal_authority":"Theft Act 1968, s 8.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Theft accompanied by qualifying force used immediately before or at the time and in order to steal can constitute robbery.","common_trap":"Treating robbery as simply theft of high-value property rather than theft plus the statutory force element.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7c599062ac05ee7e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0220","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Hearsay — statutory framework","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"At a criminal trial, the prosecution seeks to adduce an out-of-court statement as evidence of the truth of a matter stated. The defence says hearsay is absolutely inadmissible in every criminal case.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the modern position?","options":{"A":"All hearsay is automatically admissible.","B":"Hearsay is admissible only if the defendant personally agrees.","C":"The Civil Procedure Rules determine criminal hearsay.","D":"An out-of-court statement can never be evidence of its truth.","E":"Hearsay is governed by the statutory framework and may be admissible through recognised statutory gateways or other permitted bases; it is not subject to an absolute exclusion."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The Criminal Justice Act 2003 establishes a structured regime under which hearsay can be admitted through specified gateways, including statutory provisions and other recognised bases.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Criminal hearsay is regulated by statutory admissibility gateways rather than an absolute ban.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Criminal hearsay is regulated by statutory admissibility gateways rather than an absolute ban.","C":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Criminal hearsay is regulated by statutory admissibility gateways rather than an absolute ban.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Criminal hearsay is regulated by statutory admissibility gateways rather than an absolute ban.","E":"Correct. The Criminal Justice Act 2003 establishes a structured regime under which hearsay can be admitted through specified gateways, including statutory provisions and other recognised bases."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Justice Act 2003, Part 11, Chapter 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Criminal hearsay is regulated by statutory admissibility gateways rather than an absolute ban.","common_trap":"Applying an obsolete absolute-exclusion conception of hearsay.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6f8d4c123347d9a7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0221","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — authority to allot shares","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company with more than one class of shares wants its directors to allot new ordinary shares. The directors assume that their general management powers alone always authorise an allotment.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Directors must have the appropriate statutory or company authority to allot shares; general management powers do not invariably suffice.","B":"Directors can always allot unlimited shares without authority.","C":"Only a court can authorise an allotment.","D":"Existing shareholders must unanimously approve every allotment regardless of the statutory framework.","E":"Companies House itself allots the shares."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Directors require authority to allot shares under the Companies Act unless a specific statutory rule removes that requirement for the company and allotment concerned. The articles and existing resolutions must therefore be checked.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Directors require authority to allot shares under the Companies Act unless a specific statutory rule removes that requirement for the company and allotment concerned. The articles and existing resolutions must therefore be checked.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Share allotment requires the authority prescribed by the Companies Act and the company’s constitutional position.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Share allotment requires the authority prescribed by the Companies Act and the company’s constitutional position.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Share allotment requires the authority prescribed by the Companies Act and the company’s constitutional position.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Companies House itself allots the shares' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Share allotment requires the authority prescribed by the Companies Act and the company’s constitutional position."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 549–551.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Share allotment requires the authority prescribed by the Companies Act and the company’s constitutional position.","common_trap":"Assuming ordinary board management powers automatically include unrestricted statutory allotment authority.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3681de2d62c271ff"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0222","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Pre-emption rights on allotment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company proposes to allot new equity securities for cash to a new investor. Existing ordinary shareholders have not waived or disapplied any applicable statutory rights.\n\nWhich issue should the company consider before allotment?","options":{"A":"Whether every shareholder must sell all existing shares.","B":"Whether statutory pre-emption rights require the securities first to be offered to existing qualifying shareholders in proportion to their holdings.","C":"Whether the new investor automatically becomes a director.","D":"Whether the company must liquidate before issuing shares.","E":"Whether the allotment is treated as a dividend."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Companies Act gives existing shareholders statutory pre-emption rights on certain cash allotments of equity securities, subject to exclusions and lawful disapplication.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Cash allotments of equity securities can engage statutory pre-emption rights.","B":"Correct. The Companies Act gives existing shareholders statutory pre-emption rights on certain cash allotments of equity securities, subject to exclusions and lawful disapplication.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Cash allotments of equity securities can engage statutory pre-emption rights.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Cash allotments of equity securities can engage statutory pre-emption rights.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the allotment is treated as a dividend' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Cash allotments of equity securities can engage statutory pre-emption rights."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 561 ff.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Cash allotments of equity securities can engage statutory pre-emption rights.","common_trap":"Ignoring existing shareholders’ statutory pre-emption rights when bringing in a new cash investor.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a6083761c624648d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0223","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership — duty to account for profits","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A partner secretly uses the partnership’s business connection to earn a personal commission from a transaction connected with the firm’s business. The other partners did not consent.\n\nWhat is the ordinary Partnership Act consequence?","options":{"A":"The commission necessarily belongs to the partner because it was paid personally.","B":"Partners owe no fiduciary obligations to each other.","C":"The partner must account to the firm for the private benefit obtained from the partnership transaction or use of partnership property, name or business connection.","D":"The partnership is automatically dissolved with no accounting remedy.","E":"Only criminal sanctions are available."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Partnership Act requires a partner to account to the firm for benefits derived without consent from transactions concerning the partnership or use of its property, name or business connection.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The commission necessarily belongs to the partner because it was paid personally' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Partners must account for unauthorised benefits derived from partnership opportunities or connections.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Partners owe no fiduciary obligations to each other' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Partners must account for unauthorised benefits derived from partnership opportunities or connections.","C":"Correct. The Partnership Act requires a partner to account to the firm for benefits derived without consent from transactions concerning the partnership or use of its property, name or business connection.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Partners must account for unauthorised benefits derived from partnership opportunities or connections.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Partners must account for unauthorised benefits derived from partnership opportunities or connections."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, s 29.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Partners must account for unauthorised benefits derived from partnership opportunities or connections.","common_trap":"Treating partnership opportunities as freely exploitable for undisclosed personal gain.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4f83ef5c4605a607"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0224","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — fixed versus floating charge priority","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company has granted security over its assets. Its lender argues that calling a charge 'fixed' in the document conclusively makes it fixed even though, in substance, the company remains free to deal with the charged receivables and their proceeds in the ordinary course.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"The word 'fixed' is always conclusive.","B":"All security over receivables is necessarily floating.","C":"A charge cannot exist over receivables.","D":"Characterisation depends on the substantive rights and control created by the security arrangement, not merely the label used by the parties.","E":"The company’s freedom to deal with proceeds is legally irrelevant."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Whether security is fixed or floating turns on its legal substance, including the degree of control over the charged asset and proceeds. Contractual labels are not conclusive.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Fixed/floating characterisation depends on substance and control rather than nomenclature alone.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'All security over receivables is necessarily floating' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Fixed/floating characterisation depends on substance and control rather than nomenclature alone.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A charge cannot exist over receivables' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Fixed/floating characterisation depends on substance and control rather than nomenclature alone.","D":"Correct. Whether security is fixed or floating turns on its legal substance, including the degree of control over the charged asset and proceeds. Contractual labels are not conclusive.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The company’s freedom to deal with proceeds is legally irrelevant' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Fixed/floating characterisation depends on substance and control rather than nomenclature alone."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Spectrum Plus Ltd [2005] UKHL 41; company-security principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Fixed/floating characterisation depends on substance and control rather than nomenclature alone.","common_trap":"Treating the drafting label as determinative despite inconsistent substantive rights.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6b4ab40fbbffbf18"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0225","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Jurisdiction — service out","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant wants to serve an English claim form on a defendant outside the jurisdiction. No special regime removes the need to consider the CPR service-out rules.\n\nWhat should the claimant ordinarily establish where permission to serve out is required?","options":{"A":"Only that the claimant prefers an English court.","B":"That the defendant owns any asset anywhere in the world.","C":"That the claim exceeds £10,000.","D":"Nothing beyond issuing the claim form.","E":"An applicable jurisdictional gateway, a serious issue to be tried on the merits, and that England and Wales is the proper place in which to bring the claim, subject to the applicable CPR test."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Service out with permission is governed by the CPR jurisdictional framework, requiring an applicable gateway and satisfaction of the merits and forum requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Permission for service out requires satisfaction of the applicable gateway and jurisdictional tests.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'That the defendant owns any asset anywhere in the world' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Permission for service out requires satisfaction of the applicable gateway and jurisdictional tests.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'That the claim exceeds £10,000' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Permission for service out requires satisfaction of the applicable gateway and jurisdictional tests.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Nothing beyond issuing the claim form' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Permission for service out requires satisfaction of the applicable gateway and jurisdictional tests.","E":"Correct. Service out with permission is governed by the CPR jurisdictional framework, requiring an applicable gateway and satisfaction of the merits and forum requirements."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 6 and Practice Direction 6B.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Permission for service out requires satisfaction of the applicable gateway and jurisdictional tests.","common_trap":"Assuming claimant preference alone establishes international jurisdiction.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8a8e795d7514d9f5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0226","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Strike out","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant applies to strike out a statement of case because it discloses no reasonable grounds for bringing the claim.\n\nWhich CPR power is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The court’s strike-out power under CPR 3.4.","B":"Default judgment under CPR 12.","C":"Part 36 only.","D":"A charging order.","E":"A witness summons."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR 3.4 permits the court to strike out a statement of case on specified grounds, including that it discloses no reasonable grounds for bringing or defending a claim.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR 3.4 permits the court to strike out a statement of case on specified grounds, including that it discloses no reasonable grounds for bringing or defending a claim.","B":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A pleading disclosing no reasonable grounds can be struck out under CPR 3.4.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Part 36 only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A pleading disclosing no reasonable grounds can be struck out under CPR 3.4.","D":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A pleading disclosing no reasonable grounds can be struck out under CPR 3.4.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A witness summons' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A pleading disclosing no reasonable grounds can be struck out under CPR 3.4."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 3.4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A pleading disclosing no reasonable grounds can be struck out under CPR 3.4.","common_trap":"Confusing strike out with summary judgment, which has a distinct test.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"71bad69700fdcbe9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0227","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Relief from sanctions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party misses a procedural deadline and a sanction takes effect. It applies for relief. The solicitor argues that the court need consider only whether the breach was intentional.\n\nWhich approach should the court apply?","options":{"A":"Intent is the sole relevant factor.","B":"The structured relief-from-sanctions approach, considering seriousness/significance of the breach, why the default occurred, and all the circumstances so the application is dealt with justly, including the need for efficient litigation and compliance.","C":"Relief must always be granted for a first breach.","D":"Relief can never be granted once a sanction applies.","E":"The opponent decides the application."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The modern approach to CPR 3.9 is structured by Denton: assess seriousness/significance, consider the reason, then evaluate all circumstances including efficient conduct and compliance.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Intent is the sole relevant factor' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Relief from sanctions is assessed under CPR 3.9 and the Denton framework.","B":"Correct. The modern approach to CPR 3.9 is structured by Denton: assess seriousness/significance, consider the reason, then evaluate all circumstances including efficient conduct and compliance.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Relief from sanctions is assessed under CPR 3.9 and the Denton framework.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Relief from sanctions is assessed under CPR 3.9 and the Denton framework.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The opponent decides the application' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Relief from sanctions is assessed under CPR 3.9 and the Denton framework."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 3.9; Denton v TH White Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Relief from sanctions is assessed under CPR 3.9 and the Denton framework.","common_trap":"Reducing the test to intention or treating relief as automatic.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b6d01bffb80e91ce"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0228","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Confidentiality versus disclosure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor holds confidential information from a former client that would materially assist a new client in litigation against the former client. The information is not generally known and no consent to disclosure has been given.\n\nWhich is the best regulatory starting point?","options":{"A":"Confidentiality ends automatically when a retainer ends.","B":"The solicitor must disclose all useful former-client information to the new client.","C":"The duty of confidentiality to current and former clients continues, and the solicitor must also consider whether acting creates a conflict involving confidential information under the SRA rules.","D":"Former clients have no regulatory protection.","E":"Confidential information may always be used if litigation has begun."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Confidentiality survives the end of the retainer. Acting against a former client can also engage the Code’s provisions concerning confidential information and effective safeguards.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Former-client confidentiality continues and can constrain subsequent adverse representations.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Former-client confidentiality continues and can constrain subsequent adverse representations.","C":"Correct. Confidentiality survives the end of the retainer. Acting against a former client can also engage the Code’s provisions concerning confidential information and effective safeguards.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Former clients have no regulatory protection' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Former-client confidentiality continues and can constrain subsequent adverse representations.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Former-client confidentiality continues and can constrain subsequent adverse representations."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, paras 6.3–6.5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Former-client confidentiality continues and can constrain subsequent adverse representations.","common_trap":"Assuming termination of the retainer frees the solicitor to use confidential information.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8b68f656d31390d4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0229","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Anti-money laundering — suspicious activity","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor in the regulated sector knows or suspects, or has reasonable grounds to know or suspect, that a person is engaged in money laundering. The information came in the course of business in the regulated sector and no relevant exclusion applies.\n\nWhich compliance issue is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"A duty to warn the suspected person immediately.","B":"Automatic termination of every client matter without considering the statutory framework.","C":"A requirement to publish the suspicion online.","D":"The statutory suspicious-activity reporting regime and the firm’s internal AML reporting procedures.","E":"No issue arises unless the solicitor has proof beyond reasonable doubt."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Proceeds of Crime Act reporting framework can be engaged by knowledge or suspicion, or reasonable grounds, arising in the regulated sector. Solicitors must follow the statutory and internal reporting process while avoiding prohibited disclosures.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A duty to warn the suspected person immediately' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Relevant money-laundering suspicion can trigger statutory/internal reporting obligations.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Relevant money-laundering suspicion can trigger statutory/internal reporting obligations.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A requirement to publish the suspicion online' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Relevant money-laundering suspicion can trigger statutory/internal reporting obligations.","D":"Correct. The Proceeds of Crime Act reporting framework can be engaged by knowledge or suspicion, or reasonable grounds, arising in the regulated sector. Solicitors must follow the statutory and internal reporting process while avoiding prohibited disclosures.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No issue arises unless the solicitor has proof beyond reasonable doubt' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Relevant money-laundering suspicion can trigger statutory/internal reporting obligations."},"primary_legal_authority":"Proceeds of Crime Act 2002, ss 330 ff; Money Laundering Regulations 2017 as amended.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Relevant money-laundering suspicion can trigger statutory/internal reporting obligations.","common_trap":"Assuming proof of the underlying crime is required before AML reporting duties can arise.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b6f85ea128821486"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0230","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Battle of forms","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer sends a purchase order on its standard terms. The seller responds with an acceptance expressly on its own inconsistent standard terms and asks the buyer to sign and return an acknowledgment. The buyer signs it and performance follows.\n\nWhich issue is central to determining the governing terms?","options":{"A":"The seller’s terms automatically govern because sellers always prevail.","B":"The buyer’s first document necessarily governs regardless of later communications.","C":"Both sets apply simultaneously even where irreconcilable.","D":"Standard terms can never form contracts.","E":"Offer and acceptance analysis of the competing forms, including which set of terms constituted the operative offer accepted by conduct or signature."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Battle-of-forms disputes are resolved through ordinary formation principles, examining the sequence of offers, counter-offers and acceptance rather than applying an automatic first- or last-document rule without analysis.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Competing standard terms are resolved by objective offer-and-acceptance analysis.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The buyer’s first document necessarily governs regardless of later communications' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Competing standard terms are resolved by objective offer-and-acceptance analysis.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Both sets apply simultaneously even where irreconcilable' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Competing standard terms are resolved by objective offer-and-acceptance analysis.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Competing standard terms are resolved by objective offer-and-acceptance analysis.","E":"Correct. Battle-of-forms disputes are resolved through ordinary formation principles, examining the sequence of offers, counter-offers and acceptance rather than applying an automatic first- or last-document rule without analysis."},"primary_legal_authority":"Butler Machine Tool Co Ltd v Ex-Cell-O Corp; formation principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Competing standard terms are resolved by objective offer-and-acceptance analysis.","common_trap":"Using a mechanical rule without analysing the contractual sequence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"46ceeca90524099f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0231","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Duress — lawful act pressure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial party threatens to do something it is legally entitled to do unless the counterparty agrees to a payment. The counterparty later alleges lawful-act economic duress.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Lawful-act duress is possible only in narrow circumstances; lawful commercial pressure is not ordinarily enough merely because it is powerful.","B":"Any lawful threat automatically constitutes duress.","C":"A lawful act can never, in any circumstances, amount to duress.","D":"Economic duress requires physical violence.","E":"Duress makes every contract automatically void rather than potentially voidable."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Supreme Court has recognised lawful-act economic duress as a narrow doctrine. Hard bargaining or a lawful threat is not, without the exceptional features required by authority, sufficient.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Supreme Court has recognised lawful-act economic duress as a narrow doctrine. Hard bargaining or a lawful threat is not, without the exceptional features required by authority, sufficient.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Lawful-act economic duress is exceptional and does not convert ordinary commercial pressure into duress.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Lawful-act economic duress is exceptional and does not convert ordinary commercial pressure into duress.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Economic duress requires physical violence' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Lawful-act economic duress is exceptional and does not convert ordinary commercial pressure into duress.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Lawful-act economic duress is exceptional and does not convert ordinary commercial pressure into duress."},"primary_legal_authority":"Pakistan International Airline Corp v Times Travel (UK) Ltd [2021] UKSC 40.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Lawful-act economic duress is exceptional and does not convert ordinary commercial pressure into duress.","common_trap":"Equating strong bargaining pressure with illegitimate pressure.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"19c268d11f4d8f9b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0232","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consumer Rights Act — unfair terms","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trader’s standard consumer contract contains a non-negotiated term creating a significant imbalance in the parties’ rights to the consumer’s detriment, contrary to the requirement of good faith. The term concerns neither the transparent core subject matter nor an exempt price assessment.\n\nWhich statutory regime is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The Companies Act dividend rules.","B":"The Consumer Rights Act 2015 fairness test for consumer terms.","C":"The Theft Act.","D":"The rule against perpetuities.","E":"Occupiers’ liability."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The CRA provides that an unfair term of a consumer contract is not binding on the consumer. The fairness test addresses significant imbalance contrary to good faith, subject to the Act’s provisions and exclusions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Companies Act dividend rules' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Consumer contract terms are subject to the CRA statutory fairness regime.","B":"Correct. The CRA provides that an unfair term of a consumer contract is not binding on the consumer. The fairness test addresses significant imbalance contrary to good faith, subject to the Act’s provisions and exclusions.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Theft Act' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Consumer contract terms are subject to the CRA statutory fairness regime.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The rule against perpetuities' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Consumer contract terms are subject to the CRA statutory fairness regime.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Occupiers’ liability' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Consumer contract terms are subject to the CRA statutory fairness regime."},"primary_legal_authority":"Consumer Rights Act 2015, ss 62 ff.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Consumer contract terms are subject to the CRA statutory fairness regime.","common_trap":"Assuming signature makes every standard consumer term enforceable regardless of fairness.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"443103366f8c70ec"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0233","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Rylands v Fletcher","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landowner brings a large and unusual accumulation of a hazardous substance onto land for a non-ordinary use. The substance escapes and causes the type of damage contemplated by the rule to neighbouring property. No defence applies.\n\nWhich tortious doctrine may be relevant?","options":{"A":"Defamation.","B":"Passing off.","C":"The rule in Rylands v Fletcher, as restricted by modern authority.","D":"Trespass to the person only.","E":"Vicarious liability without any underlying tort."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The modern Rylands doctrine is narrow and concerns escape of a dangerous thing accumulated in the course of a non-ordinary use of land, subject to foreseeability and other requirements and defences.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Defamation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Rylands remains a narrow escape-based land tort under modern authority.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Passing off' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Rylands remains a narrow escape-based land tort under modern authority.","C":"Correct. The modern Rylands doctrine is narrow and concerns escape of a dangerous thing accumulated in the course of a non-ordinary use of land, subject to foreseeability and other requirements and defences.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Trespass to the person only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Rylands remains a narrow escape-based land tort under modern authority.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Vicarious liability without any underlying tort' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Rylands remains a narrow escape-based land tort under modern authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Rylands v Fletcher; Transco plc v Stockport MBC.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Rylands remains a narrow escape-based land tort under modern authority.","common_trap":"Applying Rylands as a general strict-liability rule for every hazardous activity.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"02ff271d4cb6b3c6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0234","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Conversion","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person intentionally takes control of another’s valuable chattel and sells it as if it were their own, seriously interfering with the owner’s right to possess it.\n\nWhich tort is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Private nuisance.","B":"Defamation.","C":"Negligent misstatement.","D":"Conversion.","E":"Passing off."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Conversion protects possessory interests in goods against deliberate dealings inconsistent with the claimant’s rights, including unauthorised sale or disposal.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Private nuisance' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Serious intentional interference with another’s possessory rights in goods can constitute conversion.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Defamation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Serious intentional interference with another’s possessory rights in goods can constitute conversion.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Negligent misstatement' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Serious intentional interference with another’s possessory rights in goods can constitute conversion.","D":"Correct. Conversion protects possessory interests in goods against deliberate dealings inconsistent with the claimant’s rights, including unauthorised sale or disposal.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Passing off' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Serious intentional interference with another’s possessory rights in goods can constitute conversion."},"primary_legal_authority":"Torts (Interference with Goods) Act 1977; common-law conversion.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Serious intentional interference with another’s possessory rights in goods can constitute conversion.","common_trap":"Confusing interference with goods with torts protecting land or reputation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dc5abae834dcd0f0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0235","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Limitation — latent damage","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant suffers latent property damage caused by alleged negligence and does not acquire the requisite knowledge until years after the ordinary cause of action accrued. The claim is not for personal injury.\n\nWhich statutory feature may be relevant in addition to the ordinary limitation period?","options":{"A":"There is never any extension for latent negligence damage.","B":"The personal-injury section 33 discretion is automatically the only rule.","C":"Limitation begins only when the defendant admits liability.","D":"Property damage has no limitation period.","E":"The special latent-damage limitation provisions based on date of knowledge, subject to the statutory longstop."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The Limitation Act contains a special regime for latent negligence damage not involving personal injury, including a knowledge-based period and an overriding longstop.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Latent non-personal-injury negligence damage can engage the statutory knowledge period and longstop.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Latent non-personal-injury negligence damage can engage the statutory knowledge period and longstop.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Latent non-personal-injury negligence damage can engage the statutory knowledge period and longstop.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Property damage has no limitation period' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Latent non-personal-injury negligence damage can engage the statutory knowledge period and longstop.","E":"Correct. The Limitation Act contains a special regime for latent negligence damage not involving personal injury, including a knowledge-based period and an overriding longstop."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, ss 14A and 14B.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Latent non-personal-injury negligence damage can engage the statutory knowledge period and longstop.","common_trap":"Applying only the ordinary six-year period without considering the latent-damage regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a2c282f2892167c7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0236","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial independence","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A minister publicly demands that a judge decide a pending private case in the government’s favour and threatens removal if the judge refuses.\n\nWhich constitutional principle is most directly implicated?","options":{"A":"Judicial independence and the constitutional separation between adjudication and improper executive influence.","B":"Parliamentary privilege of the litigants.","C":"The postal rule.","D":"Privity of contract.","E":"Collective ministerial responsibility only."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Judicial independence protects adjudication from improper external pressure and is a central component of the rule of law and constitutional settlement.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Judicial independence protects adjudication from improper external pressure and is a central component of the rule of law and constitutional settlement.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Parliamentary privilege of the litigants' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Judges must decide cases independently of improper executive pressure.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The postal rule' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Judges must decide cases independently of improper executive pressure.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Privity of contract' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Judges must decide cases independently of improper executive pressure.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Collective ministerial responsibility only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Judges must decide cases independently of improper executive pressure."},"primary_legal_authority":"Constitutional Reform Act 2005, s 3; constitutional principles of judicial independence.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Judges must decide cases independently of improper executive pressure.","common_trap":"Treating judicial decisions as subject to ministerial direction.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d5037cded14a51ac"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0237","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Statutory interpretation — presumptions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statute uses general language that, on one interpretation, would retrospectively remove vested rights arising before the statute commenced. The legislation contains no clear wording indicating retrospective operation.\n\nWhich interpretive principle may be relevant?","options":{"A":"A presumption that every statute is retrospective.","B":"The presumption against retrospective operation, subject to the statutory text and context.","C":"The postal rule.","D":"The doctrine of frustration.","E":"The rule that courts may ignore legislation."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Courts generally presume that legislation is not intended to alter the legal character or consequences of past events retrospectively unless Parliament indicates otherwise with sufficient clarity, subject to context.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. There is a general interpretive presumption against retrospectivity absent sufficiently clear contrary intention.","B":"Correct. Courts generally presume that legislation is not intended to alter the legal character or consequences of past events retrospectively unless Parliament indicates otherwise with sufficient clarity, subject to context.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The postal rule' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. There is a general interpretive presumption against retrospectivity absent sufficiently clear contrary intention.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The doctrine of frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. There is a general interpretive presumption against retrospectivity absent sufficiently clear contrary intention.","E":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. There is a general interpretive presumption against retrospectivity absent sufficiently clear contrary intention."},"primary_legal_authority":"Principles of statutory interpretation; presumption against retrospectivity.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"There is a general interpretive presumption against retrospectivity absent sufficiently clear contrary intention.","common_trap":"Assuming new legislation always governs completed past events.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"21bac2a426290aa3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0238","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — legitimate expectation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A public authority gives a clear, unambiguous and unqualified representation that it will follow a particular procedure before making a decision. An affected person relies on the representation. The authority later proposes to depart from it without considering the expectation.\n\nWhich public-law doctrine should be considered?","options":{"A":"Adverse possession.","B":"Promissory estoppel as the only public-law doctrine.","C":"Legitimate expectation.","D":"Frustration.","E":"Conversion."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A sufficiently clear representation or settled practice can generate a procedural or, in appropriate circumstances, substantive legitimate expectation. Whether departure is lawful depends on the public-law framework and justification.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Clear public-authority representations can create legitimate expectations protected by judicial review.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Clear public-authority representations can create legitimate expectations protected by judicial review.","C":"Correct. A sufficiently clear representation or settled practice can generate a procedural or, in appropriate circumstances, substantive legitimate expectation. Whether departure is lawful depends on the public-law framework and justification.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Clear public-authority representations can create legitimate expectations protected by judicial review.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Conversion' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Clear public-authority representations can create legitimate expectations protected by judicial review."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v North and East Devon Health Authority, ex p Coughlan; legitimate-expectation principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Clear public-authority representations can create legitimate expectations protected by judicial review.","common_trap":"Treating public-law expectations as ordinary private contractual promises or assuming every policy statement is binding.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e7d32a7ab8b512a3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0239","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Will execution — witness beneficiary","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator validly signs a will in the presence of two witnesses. One witness is also given a cash legacy by the will. Assume no statutory saving provision applies to the gift.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect?","options":{"A":"The entire will is automatically void.","B":"The witness’s gift is valid but every other gift fails.","C":"The witness automatically becomes executor.","D":"The will itself can remain valid, but the gift to the attesting witness is ordinarily void under the Wills Act.","E":"Witnesses may never be beneficiaries under any circumstances without invalidating execution."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Wills Act generally voids a beneficial gift to an attesting witness (or the witness’s spouse/civil partner within the statutory rule) without necessarily invalidating the will itself.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A beneficiary’s attestation ordinarily affects the gift rather than the validity of the whole will.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A beneficiary’s attestation ordinarily affects the gift rather than the validity of the whole will.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A beneficiary’s attestation ordinarily affects the gift rather than the validity of the whole will.","D":"Correct. The Wills Act generally voids a beneficial gift to an attesting witness (or the witness’s spouse/civil partner within the statutory rule) without necessarily invalidating the will itself.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A beneficiary’s attestation ordinarily affects the gift rather than the validity of the whole will."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 15.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A beneficiary’s attestation ordinarily affects the gift rather than the validity of the whole will.","common_trap":"Assuming an interested witness necessarily invalidates the entire testamentary instrument.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cc223b1f6c2c1c60"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0240","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Intestacy — statutory trusts for issue","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person dies intestate leaving no spouse or civil partner but leaving children. The estate is available for distribution after liabilities and administration expenses.\n\nWhich class has the primary entitlement under the intestacy rules on these facts?","options":{"A":"The Crown immediately.","B":"The deceased’s friends.","C":"The executor named in an earlier revoked will.","D":"The local authority.","E":"The deceased’s issue under the statutory trusts."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Where there is no surviving spouse/civil partner but there is surviving issue, the statutory intestacy scheme gives the residuary estate to the issue on the statutory trusts.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Crown immediately' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Issue take under the statutory intestacy hierarchy where there is no surviving spouse/civil partner.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The deceased’s friends' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Issue take under the statutory intestacy hierarchy where there is no surviving spouse/civil partner.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The executor named in an earlier revoked will' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Issue take under the statutory intestacy hierarchy where there is no surviving spouse/civil partner.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The local authority' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Issue take under the statutory intestacy hierarchy where there is no surviving spouse/civil partner.","E":"Correct. Where there is no surviving spouse/civil partner but there is surviving issue, the statutory intestacy scheme gives the residuary estate to the issue on the statutory trusts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925, s 46.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Issue take under the statutory intestacy hierarchy where there is no surviving spouse/civil partner.","common_trap":"Skipping surviving issue and moving directly to more remote relatives or bona vacantia.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f25d9c9f2266737d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0241","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"IHT — potentially exempt transfer","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual makes an outright lifetime gift of cash to another individual. The gift is not exempt and is not a transfer into a relevant-property trust.\n\nHow is the transfer ordinarily characterised for inheritance-tax purposes?","options":{"A":"As a potentially exempt transfer, becoming exempt if the donor survives the relevant statutory period, subject to the IHT rules.","B":"As an immediately chargeable transfer in every case.","C":"As corporation-tax income.","D":"As SDLT.","E":"As outside the inheritance-tax regime from the moment it is made regardless of survival."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An outright lifetime transfer between individuals is ordinarily a potentially exempt transfer. Its ultimate IHT treatment depends, among other matters, on the donor’s survival and the statutory regime.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An outright lifetime transfer between individuals is ordinarily a potentially exempt transfer. Its ultimate IHT treatment depends, among other matters, on the donor’s survival and the statutory regime.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Outright lifetime gifts to individuals are generally PETs rather than immediately chargeable lifetime transfers.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'As corporation-tax income' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Outright lifetime gifts to individuals are generally PETs rather than immediately chargeable lifetime transfers.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'As SDLT' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Outright lifetime gifts to individuals are generally PETs rather than immediately chargeable lifetime transfers.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'As outside the inheritance-tax regime from the moment it is made regardless of survival' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Outright lifetime gifts to individuals are generally PETs rather than immediately chargeable lifetime transfers."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, s 3A.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Outright lifetime gifts to individuals are generally PETs rather than immediately chargeable lifetime transfers.","common_trap":"Confusing PET treatment with immediate exemption or with transfers into relevant-property trusts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"82d4123ac131cb07"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0242","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Client money — transfers for costs","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm holds client money for a matter. It has delivered a bill of costs to the client and wants to transfer the billed amount from client account to business account.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the Accounts Rules?","options":{"A":"The firm may transfer any amount it chooses before telling the client.","B":"The firm may withdraw client money for its costs only in accordance with the Rules, including first giving a bill or other written notification of the costs and limiting the withdrawal appropriately.","C":"Delivery of a bill transfers ownership of the entire client balance to the firm.","D":"Costs can never be paid from client account.","E":"A fee earner may take client money personally and reimburse the firm later."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Rules permit withdrawal of client money for the firm’s costs only when the regulatory conditions are satisfied, including written notification of the costs and proper accounting.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The firm may transfer any amount it chooses before telling the client' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Transfers from client to business account for costs must comply with the Accounts Rules.","B":"Correct. The Rules permit withdrawal of client money for the firm’s costs only when the regulatory conditions are satisfied, including written notification of the costs and proper accounting.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Delivery of a bill transfers ownership of the entire client balance to the firm' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Transfers from client to business account for costs must comply with the Accounts Rules.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Transfers from client to business account for costs must comply with the Accounts Rules.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A fee earner may take client money personally and reimburse the firm later' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Transfers from client to business account for costs must comply with the Accounts Rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, rr 4.3 and 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Transfers from client to business account for costs must comply with the Accounts Rules.","common_trap":"Treating client money as firm money merely because fees have accrued internally.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7e699bf48b3db8b0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0243","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Certainty of subject matter","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor declares a trust of 'some of my shares in X plc' but neither identifies how many shares nor provides an objective mechanism for determining which quantum is held on trust.\n\nWhich requirement is most directly in issue?","options":{"A":"Certainty of intention only.","B":"The beneficiary principle only.","C":"Certainty of subject matter.","D":"Perpetuity alone.","E":"Trustee investment duties."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"An express trust must identify the trust property with sufficient certainty. If the quantum or property subject to the trust cannot be objectively ascertained, certainty of subject matter may fail.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Certainty of intention only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Trust property must be sufficiently certain.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The beneficiary principle only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Trust property must be sufficiently certain.","C":"Correct. An express trust must identify the trust property with sufficient certainty. If the quantum or property subject to the trust cannot be objectively ascertained, certainty of subject matter may fail.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Perpetuity alone' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Trust property must be sufficiently certain.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Trustee investment duties' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Trust property must be sufficiently certain."},"primary_legal_authority":"Three-certainties doctrine; Palmer v Simmonds and related subject-matter authorities.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trust property must be sufficiently certain.","common_trap":"Focusing on intention or objects while overlooking uncertainty as to what property is held on trust.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ded9dd38004e93cd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0244","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Saunders v Vautier","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trust has one adult beneficiary who is absolutely entitled to the entire beneficial interest, has full capacity, and wants the trustees to transfer the trust property to her immediately even though the trust instrument contemplates later distribution.\n\nWhich principle is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Trustees can always ignore an absolutely entitled adult beneficiary.","B":"The beneficiary must wait until the date stated in every trust instrument regardless of beneficial ownership.","C":"Only a court can ever terminate a trust.","D":"The rule in Saunders v Vautier, allowing beneficiaries who together are absolutely entitled and of full capacity to terminate the trust and require transfer.","E":"The rule in Strong v Bird."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Where all beneficiaries are adult, have capacity and together are absolutely entitled to the trust property, they can ordinarily require the trustees to transfer it and terminate the trust.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Absolutely entitled adult beneficiaries can invoke Saunders v Vautier to end the trust.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Absolutely entitled adult beneficiaries can invoke Saunders v Vautier to end the trust.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Absolutely entitled adult beneficiaries can invoke Saunders v Vautier to end the trust.","D":"Correct. Where all beneficiaries are adult, have capacity and together are absolutely entitled to the trust property, they can ordinarily require the trustees to transfer it and terminate the trust.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The rule in Strong v Bird' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Absolutely entitled adult beneficiaries can invoke Saunders v Vautier to end the trust."},"primary_legal_authority":"Saunders v Vautier (1841) 4 Beav 115.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Absolutely entitled adult beneficiaries can invoke Saunders v Vautier to end the trust.","common_trap":"Assuming settlor-imposed timing necessarily overrides the rights of all absolutely entitled competent beneficiaries.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6b8108de2225b6b9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0245","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Easements — acquisition by prescription","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landowner has openly used a defined path over neighbouring land as of right, without force, secrecy or permission, for the period required by the applicable doctrine. The owner claims a right of way by long use.\n\nWhich method of acquiring an easement is potentially relevant?","options":{"A":"Overreaching.","B":"Ademption.","C":"Resulting trust.","D":"Assignment of a lease.","E":"Prescription."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Easements may be acquired by prescription where the legal requirements for qualifying long use are satisfied under the applicable common-law, lost-modern-grant or statutory route.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Overreaching' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Qualifying long use as of right can support acquisition of an easement by prescription.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Ademption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Qualifying long use as of right can support acquisition of an easement by prescription.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Resulting trust' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Qualifying long use as of right can support acquisition of an easement by prescription.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Assignment of a lease' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Qualifying long use as of right can support acquisition of an easement by prescription.","E":"Correct. Easements may be acquired by prescription where the legal requirements for qualifying long use are satisfied under the applicable common-law, lost-modern-grant or statutory route."},"primary_legal_authority":"Prescription Act 1832 and common-law/lost modern grant principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Qualifying long use as of right can support acquisition of an easement by prescription.","common_trap":"Confusing acquisition by long use with express grant or proprietary estoppel.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e0e19060c276f2e4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0246","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Restrictive covenants — Tulk v Moxhay","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A purchaser acquires freehold land with notice of a negative covenant restricting building on the land. The covenant benefits identifiable neighbouring land and the equitable requirements for transmission of the burden are otherwise satisfied.\n\nWhich doctrine may allow the covenant’s burden to bind the purchaser in equity?","options":{"A":"The restrictive-covenant doctrine associated with Tulk v Moxhay.","B":"The common-law rule that every positive covenant burden runs automatically.","C":"Adverse possession.","D":"The equity of redemption.","E":"Frustration."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Equity can enforce the burden of a restrictive covenant against successors where the established requirements are satisfied, notwithstanding the common-law rule on covenant burdens.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Equity can enforce the burden of a restrictive covenant against successors where the established requirements are satisfied, notwithstanding the common-law rule on covenant burdens.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The burden of a qualifying restrictive covenant can run in equity.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The burden of a qualifying restrictive covenant can run in equity.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The equity of redemption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The burden of a qualifying restrictive covenant can run in equity.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The burden of a qualifying restrictive covenant can run in equity."},"primary_legal_authority":"Tulk v Moxhay; Law of Property Act 1925, s 79 and land-registration rules as applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The burden of a qualifying restrictive covenant can run in equity.","common_trap":"Applying the rule indiscriminately to positive covenants or ignoring the requirements for benefited land and notice/registration.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"25984142608240e6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0247","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Conveyancing — environmental search","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer is considering a property on land previously used for industrial purposes. The buyer’s solicitor wants information relevant to contaminated-land and environmental risks that will not necessarily appear from physical inspection alone.\n\nWhich pre-contract search is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"An OS1 priority search only.","B":"An environmental search.","C":"A bankruptcy search against the buyer only.","D":"A probate search.","E":"A Companies House incorporation filing."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Environmental searches are commonly used to identify environmental risks such as potential contaminated land, flooding and related matters depending on the search product. They complement rather than replace other searches and enquiries.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'An OS1 priority search only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Environmental searches can reveal environmental and contaminated-land risks relevant to a purchase.","B":"Correct. Environmental searches are commonly used to identify environmental risks such as potential contaminated land, flooding and related matters depending on the search product. They complement rather than replace other searches and enquiries.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A bankruptcy search against the buyer only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Environmental searches can reveal environmental and contaminated-land risks relevant to a purchase.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A probate search' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Environmental searches can reveal environmental and contaminated-land risks relevant to a purchase.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A Companies House incorporation filing' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Environmental searches can reveal environmental and contaminated-land risks relevant to a purchase."},"primary_legal_authority":"Standard conveyancing practice; Environmental Protection Act 1990, Part IIA as relevant.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Environmental searches can reveal environmental and contaminated-land risks relevant to a purchase.","common_trap":"Using a pre-completion Land Registry priority search as a substitute for environmental due diligence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4cdb47925e8aa312"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0248","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"SDLT — buyer responsibility","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer completes a chargeable acquisition of land in England. The buyer assumes that the seller is responsible for filing the SDLT return and paying any SDLT due.\n\nWhich is the ordinary position?","options":{"A":"The seller always pays the buyer’s SDLT.","B":"HM Land Registry pays SDLT for both parties.","C":"The purchaser is ordinarily responsible for the land transaction return and any SDLT due, subject to the statutory rules and applicable reliefs.","D":"SDLT is charged only on sellers’ capital gains.","E":"Solicitors personally bear SDLT on every client purchase."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"SDLT is a transaction tax whose filing and payment obligations ordinarily fall on the purchaser for a chargeable land transaction, with monetary rates and thresholds supplied where required in SQE questions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The purchaser ordinarily bears SDLT filing/payment responsibility for a chargeable acquisition.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'HM Land Registry pays SDLT for both parties' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The purchaser ordinarily bears SDLT filing/payment responsibility for a chargeable acquisition.","C":"Correct. SDLT is a transaction tax whose filing and payment obligations ordinarily fall on the purchaser for a chargeable land transaction, with monetary rates and thresholds supplied where required in SQE questions.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The purchaser ordinarily bears SDLT filing/payment responsibility for a chargeable acquisition.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The purchaser ordinarily bears SDLT filing/payment responsibility for a chargeable acquisition."},"primary_legal_authority":"Finance Act 2003; SRA FLK2 taxation specification.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The purchaser ordinarily bears SDLT filing/payment responsibility for a chargeable acquisition.","common_trap":"Confusing the buyer’s transaction tax with the seller’s possible tax on gains.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fafb5a743a29f5f2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0249","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Fraud by false representation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person dishonestly tells an online buyer that a non-existent laptop is available for sale, intending by the false statement to make a financial gain. The person knows the representation is untrue.\n\nWhich offence is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Theft only, because fraud requires physical taking.","B":"Robbery.","C":"Burglary.","D":"Fraud by false representation.","E":"Criminal damage."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Fraud by false representation requires a dishonest false representation, knowledge that it is or might be untrue or misleading, and intent to make a gain or cause/expose another to loss. Actual gain or loss is not required for completion of the offence.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Theft only, because fraud requires physical taking' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Dishonest false representations made with the statutory gain/loss intent can constitute fraud.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Robbery' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Dishonest false representations made with the statutory gain/loss intent can constitute fraud.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Burglary' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Dishonest false representations made with the statutory gain/loss intent can constitute fraud.","D":"Correct. Fraud by false representation requires a dishonest false representation, knowledge that it is or might be untrue or misleading, and intent to make a gain or cause/expose another to loss. Actual gain or loss is not required for completion of the offence.","E":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Dishonest false representations made with the statutory gain/loss intent can constitute fraud."},"primary_legal_authority":"Fraud Act 2006, s 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Dishonest false representations made with the statutory gain/loss intent can constitute fraud.","common_trap":"Assuming fraud requires successful receipt of money or physical appropriation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"77b28c4e88098fda"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0250","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Identification evidence — Turnbull","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"The prosecution case depends substantially on a disputed visual identification made in difficult conditions. The judge concludes that the quality of the identification evidence is poor and there is no other evidence capable of supporting it.\n\nWhich principle is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The postal rule.","B":"The rule in Saunders v Vautier.","C":"Part 36.","D":"The equity of redemption.","E":"The Turnbull guidance on the judicial approach to disputed identification evidence, including the need for caution and the consequences of poor-quality identification."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Turnbull requires careful judicial treatment of disputed visual identification evidence. Where identification quality is poor and unsupported, the case may need to be withdrawn from the jury; otherwise an appropriate warning is required.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The postal rule' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Disputed visual identification evidence is governed by the Turnbull safeguards.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The rule in Saunders v Vautier' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Disputed visual identification evidence is governed by the Turnbull safeguards.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Part 36' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Disputed visual identification evidence is governed by the Turnbull safeguards.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The equity of redemption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Disputed visual identification evidence is governed by the Turnbull safeguards.","E":"Correct. Turnbull requires careful judicial treatment of disputed visual identification evidence. Where identification quality is poor and unsupported, the case may need to be withdrawn from the jury; otherwise an appropriate warning is required."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Turnbull [1977] QB 224; criminal evidence principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Disputed visual identification evidence is governed by the Turnbull safeguards.","common_trap":"Treating confident identification as inherently reliable regardless of viewing conditions and supporting evidence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3b7821b26d01fe03"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0251","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — special resolutions","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company proposes to amend its articles of association. At a properly convened general meeting, 74% of the votes cast by members entitled to vote support the proposed resolution. No special voting provision alters the statutory threshold.\n\nIs the resolution passed?","options":{"A":"No. A special resolution ordinarily requires at least 75% of the votes cast.","B":"Yes, because more than a simple majority voted in favour.","C":"Yes, because amendment of articles requires only an ordinary resolution.","D":"No, because amendment of articles always requires unanimity.","E":"Yes, provided the directors unanimously approve afterwards."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Amendment of articles ordinarily requires a special resolution. A special resolution requires a majority of not less than 75%, so 74% is insufficient.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Amendment of articles ordinarily requires a special resolution. A special resolution requires a majority of not less than 75%, so 74% is insufficient.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Yes, because more than a simple majority voted in favour' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A special resolution requires at least 75% of votes cast, and articles are ordinarily amended by special resolution.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A special resolution requires at least 75% of votes cast, and articles are ordinarily amended by special resolution.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A special resolution requires at least 75% of votes cast, and articles are ordinarily amended by special resolution.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Yes, provided the directors unanimously approve afterwards' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A special resolution requires at least 75% of votes cast, and articles are ordinarily amended by special resolution."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 21 and 283.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A special resolution requires at least 75% of votes cast, and articles are ordinarily amended by special resolution.","common_trap":"Confusing the simple-majority threshold for an ordinary resolution with the 75% special-resolution threshold.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fe2e2dbc789952ff"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0252","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Directors — duty to promote success","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A director chooses between two lawful commercial strategies. He acts in good faith in what he genuinely considers would promote the success of the company for the benefit of members as a whole, having regard to the statutory factors. The strategy later performs badly.\n\nWhich statement best describes section 172?","options":{"A":"Any loss proves a breach of section 172.","B":"The duty is primarily framed by the director's good-faith judgment as to promoting the company's success, although the statutory factors and surrounding duties remain relevant.","C":"The director must maximise short-term profit regardless of all other factors.","D":"The duty is owed personally to each shareholder rather than to the company.","E":"A court substitutes its own commercial preference whenever a strategy fails."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 172 requires a director to act in the way the director considers, in good faith, would be most likely to promote the company's success for members as a whole, while having regard to listed factors. A poor outcome does not itself establish breach.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Any loss proves a breach of section 172' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Section 172 focuses on good-faith pursuit of company success, not hindsight assessment of commercial outcomes.","B":"Correct. Section 172 requires a director to act in the way the director considers, in good faith, would be most likely to promote the company's success for members as a whole, while having regard to listed factors. A poor outcome does not itself establish breach.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Section 172 focuses on good-faith pursuit of company success, not hindsight assessment of commercial outcomes.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The duty is owed personally to each shareholder rather than to the company' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Section 172 focuses on good-faith pursuit of company success, not hindsight assessment of commercial outcomes.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Section 172 focuses on good-faith pursuit of company success, not hindsight assessment of commercial outcomes."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 172.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Section 172 focuses on good-faith pursuit of company success, not hindsight assessment of commercial outcomes.","common_trap":"Using business failure alone as proof that the director breached the statutory duty.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"abd5e83f8fa67ea9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0253","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"LLP — separate legal personality","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two professionals form and register a limited liability partnership. A client later contracts with the LLP. One member argues that an LLP is merely an ordinary partnership and cannot own property or incur obligations in its own name.\n\nWhich statement is correct?","options":{"A":"An LLP has no legal personality separate from its members.","B":"An LLP is legally identical to a sole trader.","C":"An LLP is a body corporate with legal personality separate from its members.","D":"An LLP can exist only as a public company.","E":"LLP members personally own every LLP asset as tenants in common."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A registered LLP is a body corporate with legal personality separate from its members and can own property and incur obligations in its own name.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'An LLP has no legal personality separate from its members' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An LLP has separate corporate personality despite retaining partnership-style internal features.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'An LLP is legally identical to a sole trader' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An LLP has separate corporate personality despite retaining partnership-style internal features.","C":"Correct. A registered LLP is a body corporate with legal personality separate from its members and can own property and incur obligations in its own name.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An LLP has separate corporate personality despite retaining partnership-style internal features.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An LLP has separate corporate personality despite retaining partnership-style internal features."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An LLP has separate corporate personality despite retaining partnership-style internal features.","common_trap":"Confusing an LLP with a traditional partnership under the Partnership Act 1890.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"86ffa7e448998583"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0254","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — transactions at undervalue","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An insolvent company transfers a valuable asset to an unconnected person for consideration worth substantially less than the asset. The transaction falls within the relevant statutory period and no statutory defence applies.\n\nWhich avoidance provision should an office-holder consider?","options":{"A":"The statutory preference provision only, which requires a desire to prefer.","B":"The Companies Act rule on ordinary resolutions.","C":"The rule against penalties.","D":"The Insolvency Act provisions on transactions at an undervalue.","E":"The Partnership Act dissolution provisions."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A company enters a transaction at an undervalue where, broadly, it makes a gift or receives consideration significantly less than the value it provides. The statutory insolvency conditions and defences must also be considered.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The statutory preference provision only, which requires a desire to prefer' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A materially under-value pre-insolvency transaction can be vulnerable under the Insolvency Act.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Companies Act rule on ordinary resolutions' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A materially under-value pre-insolvency transaction can be vulnerable under the Insolvency Act.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The rule against penalties' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A materially under-value pre-insolvency transaction can be vulnerable under the Insolvency Act.","D":"Correct. A company enters a transaction at an undervalue where, broadly, it makes a gift or receives consideration significantly less than the value it provides. The statutory insolvency conditions and defences must also be considered.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Partnership Act dissolution provisions' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A materially under-value pre-insolvency transaction can be vulnerable under the Insolvency Act."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, s 238.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A materially under-value pre-insolvency transaction can be vulnerable under the Insolvency Act.","common_trap":"Confusing an undervalue transaction with a preference, which has different elements including the relevant preference relationship and desire requirement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"371a9f2a6860d239"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0255","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Allocation — small claims track","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A straightforward defended county court money claim has a value within the ordinary small-claims financial limit and raises no exceptional complexity or other factor pointing elsewhere.\n\nWhich track is ordinarily most likely?","options":{"A":"The multi-track in every defended claim.","B":"The Crown Court.","C":"The Commercial Court regardless of value.","D":"No track because allocation applies only after trial.","E":"The small claims track, subject to the court's allocation discretion and applicable exceptions."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The CPR uses value and other factors when allocating defended claims. Straightforward lower-value claims within the applicable small-claims limit are ordinarily allocated to the small claims track, subject to exceptions and judicial discretion.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Straightforward lower-value claims are ordinarily suitable for the small claims track, subject to CPR allocation criteria.","B":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Straightforward lower-value claims are ordinarily suitable for the small claims track, subject to CPR allocation criteria.","C":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Straightforward lower-value claims are ordinarily suitable for the small claims track, subject to CPR allocation criteria.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Straightforward lower-value claims are ordinarily suitable for the small claims track, subject to CPR allocation criteria.","E":"Correct. The CPR uses value and other factors when allocating defended claims. Straightforward lower-value claims within the applicable small-claims limit are ordinarily allocated to the small claims track, subject to exceptions and judicial discretion."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 26.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Straightforward lower-value claims are ordinarily suitable for the small claims track, subject to CPR allocation criteria.","common_trap":"Assuming every defended claim belongs on a more complex track.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e4172b1eecbd58aa"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0256","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Disclosure — standard disclosure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"In proceedings where the court orders standard disclosure under the applicable regime, a party proposes to disclose only documents that positively support its own case.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the traditional standard-disclosure obligation?","options":{"A":"It extends beyond supportive documents and includes documents on which the party relies, documents adversely affecting its own or another party's case, and documents supporting another party's case within the rule.","B":"A party discloses only favourable documents.","C":"Disclosure applies only to documents intended to be used at trial.","D":"Adverse documents are protected merely because they are adverse.","E":"Only original paper documents can fall within disclosure."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where standard disclosure under CPR 31.6 applies, the obligation includes specified categories beyond documents favourable to the disclosing party. Privilege is a separate issue concerning inspection.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where standard disclosure under CPR 31.6 applies, the obligation includes specified categories beyond documents favourable to the disclosing party. Privilege is a separate issue concerning inspection.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Standard disclosure is not limited to favourable documents.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Standard disclosure is not limited to favourable documents.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse documents are protected merely because they are adverse' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Standard disclosure is not limited to favourable documents.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Standard disclosure is not limited to favourable documents."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 31.6, where applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Standard disclosure is not limited to favourable documents.","common_trap":"Confusing relevance to one's own case with a right to suppress adverse non-privileged documents.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a20382058ad46cb1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0257","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Witness statements — statement of truth","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party serves a witness statement for use at trial. The solicitor suggests omitting the statement of truth because witness statements are merely informal summaries.\n\nWhich is the correct approach?","options":{"A":"Statements of truth are never used for witness evidence.","B":"A witness statement used in proceedings ordinarily must comply with the CPR requirements, including verification by a statement of truth.","C":"Only pleadings, never witness statements, can be verified.","D":"A witness statement becomes privileged merely by omitting verification.","E":"Verification is required only after judgment."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The CPR and practice directions require witness statements to contain the prescribed statement of truth. False statements verified without honest belief can have serious consequences.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Witness statements ordinarily require a compliant statement of truth.","B":"Correct. The CPR and practice directions require witness statements to contain the prescribed statement of truth. False statements verified without honest belief can have serious consequences.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Witness statements ordinarily require a compliant statement of truth.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A witness statement becomes privileged merely by omitting verification' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Witness statements ordinarily require a compliant statement of truth.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Witness statements ordinarily require a compliant statement of truth."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 32 and Practice Direction 32.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Witness statements ordinarily require a compliant statement of truth.","common_trap":"Treating formal trial evidence as an informal document outside procedural verification rules.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2d91d8e5ddd4d2b2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0258","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Undertakings","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor gives an unequivocal professional undertaking to another solicitor to pay a specified sum from completion monies once defined conditions occur. The conditions occur, but compliance is inconvenient for the solicitor's firm.\n\nWhich is the best professional position?","options":{"A":"Professional undertakings are merely non-binding courtesies.","B":"An undertaking may always be ignored if it becomes commercially inconvenient.","C":"A solicitor must perform an undertaking given in the course of practice within the agreed timescale or, if none, within a reasonable time; inconvenience does not by itself release the obligation.","D":"Only undertakings given to courts matter.","E":"A solicitor may unilaterally rewrite the undertaking after the recipient relies on it."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code requires solicitors to perform undertakings they give within the agreed time or otherwise within a reasonable time. Undertakings are treated seriously because others rely on them.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Professional undertakings must be honoured in accordance with their terms.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Professional undertakings must be honoured in accordance with their terms.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code requires solicitors to perform undertakings they give within the agreed time or otherwise within a reasonable time. Undertakings are treated seriously because others rely on them.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Professional undertakings must be honoured in accordance with their terms.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A solicitor may unilaterally rewrite the undertaking after the recipient relies on it' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Professional undertakings must be honoured in accordance with their terms."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 1.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Professional undertakings must be honoured in accordance with their terms.","common_trap":"Treating an undertaking as an informal promise that can be withdrawn unilaterally after reliance.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0c2e995b4d7b539d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0259","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Complaints handling","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client complains about the service received. The firm has no written complaints procedure and tells the client that complaints can be considered only if court proceedings are issued.\n\nWhich is the best regulatory analysis?","options":{"A":"A law firm need not operate a complaints process.","B":"Only courts may consider complaints about legal services.","C":"Complaint information is required only after the retainer ends.","D":"The firm must have an effective complaints procedure and provide information about how and to whom a complaint can be made, including relevant external redress information.","E":"A client loses all complaint rights by paying a bill."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"SRA requirements include effective complaints handling and provision of appropriate information about complaint routes and redress. Court proceedings are not a prerequisite.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A law firm need not operate a complaints process' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Firms must maintain effective complaints arrangements and inform clients about them.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Firms must maintain effective complaints arrangements and inform clients about them.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Firms must maintain effective complaints arrangements and inform clients about them.","D":"Correct. SRA requirements include effective complaints handling and provision of appropriate information about complaint routes and redress. Court proceedings are not a prerequisite.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A client loses all complaint rights by paying a bill' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Firms must maintain effective complaints arrangements and inform clients about them."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Firms, complaints requirements; Legal Ombudsman framework.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Firms must maintain effective complaints arrangements and inform clients about them.","common_trap":"Treating service complaints as matters that can be addressed only through litigation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"03f35a709c5f7277"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0260","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Offer — revocation","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An offeror makes an ordinary offer that is not supported by an option. Before the offeree accepts, the offeror communicates a clear revocation to the offeree.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect?","options":{"A":"The offer remains irrevocable for a reasonable time in every case.","B":"Revocation is effective only after the offeree attempts acceptance.","C":"Every offer creates an option contract automatically.","D":"An offer can be revoked only by a court.","E":"The offer is revoked before acceptance and can no longer be accepted, assuming revocation was effectively communicated."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"An ordinary offer may generally be revoked before acceptance, but revocation must be communicated to the offeree, directly or through a sufficiently reliable source under the authorities.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An unsupported offer can ordinarily be revoked before acceptance if revocation is effectively communicated.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An unsupported offer can ordinarily be revoked before acceptance if revocation is effectively communicated.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An unsupported offer can ordinarily be revoked before acceptance if revocation is effectively communicated.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An unsupported offer can ordinarily be revoked before acceptance if revocation is effectively communicated.","E":"Correct. An ordinary offer may generally be revoked before acceptance, but revocation must be communicated to the offeree, directly or through a sufficiently reliable source under the authorities."},"primary_legal_authority":"Byrne v Van Tienhoven; Dickinson v Dodds.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An unsupported offer can ordinarily be revoked before acceptance if revocation is effectively communicated.","common_trap":"Assuming an offer must remain open merely because no expiry date was stated.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6c0376b1e2cc9b8e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0261","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Acceptance — postal rule","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An offer made by letter contemplates acceptance by post. The offeree properly posts an unconditional acceptance while the offer remains open. The offer does not require actual receipt and the postal rule has not otherwise been excluded.\n\nWhen is acceptance ordinarily effective?","options":{"A":"On proper posting of the acceptance.","B":"Only when the offeror actually reads the letter.","C":"When the postal service delivers it, never before.","D":"When the offeree drafts the letter.","E":"Only after the parties perform the contract."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Under the postal rule, where it applies and has not been excluded, acceptance is generally complete on proper posting rather than receipt.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Under the postal rule, where it applies and has not been excluded, acceptance is generally complete on proper posting rather than receipt.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The postal rule can make acceptance effective when properly posted.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The postal rule can make acceptance effective when properly posted.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'When the offeree drafts the letter' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The postal rule can make acceptance effective when properly posted.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The postal rule can make acceptance effective when properly posted."},"primary_legal_authority":"Adams v Lindsell; Household Fire Insurance v Grant.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The postal rule can make acceptance effective when properly posted.","common_trap":"Applying the receipt rule automatically to a postal acceptance where the postal rule governs.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"22f86fd82fe5999d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0262","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Misrepresentation — rescission bars","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contracting party proves an actionable misrepresentation and initially has a right to rescind. After discovering the truth, however, the party unequivocally elects to affirm the contract with full knowledge of the relevant facts.\n\nWhat is the effect on rescission?","options":{"A":"Affirmation strengthens the right to rescind.","B":"Affirmation can bar rescission, although any separate damages claim must be considered independently.","C":"Rescission can never be lost after misrepresentation.","D":"The contract becomes void ab initio automatically.","E":"Affirmation converts the claim into a criminal offence."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Rescission is an equitable remedy and can be barred, including by affirmation after the representee becomes aware of the relevant facts and elects to continue the contract.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Affirmation strengthens the right to rescind' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Knowing affirmation can bar the equitable remedy of rescission.","B":"Correct. Rescission is an equitable remedy and can be barred, including by affirmation after the representee becomes aware of the relevant facts and elects to continue the contract.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Knowing affirmation can bar the equitable remedy of rescission.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Knowing affirmation can bar the equitable remedy of rescission.","E":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Knowing affirmation can bar the equitable remedy of rescission."},"primary_legal_authority":"Misrepresentation Act 1967 and equitable rescission principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Knowing affirmation can bar the equitable remedy of rescission.","common_trap":"Assuming proof of misrepresentation makes rescission permanently available regardless of later conduct.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6901779c852adc5e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0263","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — remoteness","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant negligently causes minor physical damage of a foreseeable type, but an extraordinary and unforeseeable sequence produces a completely different kind of loss.\n\nWhich principle governs whether that different kind of damage is too remote?","options":{"A":"Every consequence of negligence is recoverable however unforeseeable.","B":"Only factual causation matters; remoteness has no role.","C":"The claimant must ordinarily show that the kind or type of damage was reasonably foreseeable, rather than its precise extent or manner necessarily being foreseeable.","D":"The precise sequence must always have been predicted in every detail.","E":"Remoteness applies only to contract."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Wagon Mound approach asks whether the type of damage was reasonably foreseeable. The precise manner or full extent need not necessarily be foreseeable, subject to related principles such as the thin-skull rule.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Negligence remoteness ordinarily turns on foreseeability of the type of damage.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Negligence remoteness ordinarily turns on foreseeability of the type of damage.","C":"Correct. The Wagon Mound approach asks whether the type of damage was reasonably foreseeable. The precise manner or full extent need not necessarily be foreseeable, subject to related principles such as the thin-skull rule.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Negligence remoteness ordinarily turns on foreseeability of the type of damage.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Negligence remoteness ordinarily turns on foreseeability of the type of damage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Overseas Tankship (UK) Ltd v Morts Dock (The Wagon Mound No 1).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Negligence remoteness ordinarily turns on foreseeability of the type of damage.","common_trap":"Confusing foreseeability of the type of harm with a requirement to predict the exact sequence and magnitude.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3764ff15ed5c80cb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0264","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Employer liability — non-delegable duties","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An employer hires competent contractors to provide some workplace equipment and assumes this entirely removes the employer's own duty to take reasonable care to provide a safe system and appropriate equipment for employees.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Delegation always extinguishes the employer's duties.","B":"Employees assume every workplace risk by accepting employment.","C":"Employer duties exist only where the employer personally manufactures equipment.","D":"An employer owes personal duties concerning employee safety that cannot simply be escaped by delegating performance to an independent contractor.","E":"Workplace safety is exclusively a matter of contract."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"At common law an employer owes personal duties to take reasonable care for employees, including in relation to competent staff, adequate material/equipment and a safe system of work. Delegation of performance does not necessarily delegate away responsibility.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Core employer safety duties are personal and cannot simply be avoided by delegation.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Core employer safety duties are personal and cannot simply be avoided by delegation.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Core employer safety duties are personal and cannot simply be avoided by delegation.","D":"Correct. At common law an employer owes personal duties to take reasonable care for employees, including in relation to competent staff, adequate material/equipment and a safe system of work. Delegation of performance does not necessarily delegate away responsibility.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Workplace safety is exclusively a matter of contract' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Core employer safety duties are personal and cannot simply be avoided by delegation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wilsons & Clyde Coal Co Ltd v English.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Core employer safety duties are personal and cannot simply be avoided by delegation.","common_trap":"Assuming outsourcing automatically eliminates the employer's common-law responsibility.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"eace7149cec604df"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0265","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — serious harm","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company trading for profit sues over a defamatory publication. It proves reputational criticism but produces no evidence that the publication caused or is likely to cause serious financial loss.\n\nWhich statutory requirement is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Companies are exempt from the serious-harm requirement.","B":"Any negative statement automatically establishes serious harm.","C":"Financial loss is irrelevant for a trading body.","D":"A company cannot sue in defamation.","E":"For a body trading for profit, serious harm is not established unless the publication has caused or is likely to cause serious financial loss."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The Defamation Act requires serious harm to reputation, and for a body trading for profit this is not serious harm unless it has caused or is likely to cause serious financial loss.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Companies are exempt from the serious-harm requirement' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A profit-making body must satisfy the statutory serious-financial-loss component of serious harm.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A profit-making body must satisfy the statutory serious-financial-loss component of serious harm.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Financial loss is irrelevant for a trading body' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A profit-making body must satisfy the statutory serious-financial-loss component of serious harm.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A company cannot sue in defamation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A profit-making body must satisfy the statutory serious-financial-loss component of serious harm.","E":"Correct. The Defamation Act requires serious harm to reputation, and for a body trading for profit this is not serious harm unless it has caused or is likely to cause serious financial loss."},"primary_legal_authority":"Defamation Act 2013, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A profit-making body must satisfy the statutory serious-financial-loss component of serious harm.","common_trap":"Assuming corporate claimants satisfy serious harm merely by showing criticism.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"98bf77418575a933"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0266","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Rule of law — legality","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A public official claims that because an action would be administratively convenient, the official may impose a coercive requirement on a citizen without any legal power authorising it.\n\nWhich constitutional principle most directly challenges that claim?","options":{"A":"The rule of law requires public authorities to act within lawful powers rather than on convenience alone.","B":"Executive convenience itself creates legal power.","C":"Public officials have unlimited residual authority over citizens.","D":"Only private persons are subject to law.","E":"Courts cannot examine whether public bodies have legal authority."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A core rule-of-law principle is that government action requires lawful authority and public powers must be exercised within legal limits.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A core rule-of-law principle is that government action requires lawful authority and public powers must be exercised within legal limits.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Executive convenience itself creates legal power' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Public authorities require lawful authority for coercive action.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Public officials have unlimited residual authority over citizens' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Public authorities require lawful authority for coercive action.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Public authorities require lawful authority for coercive action.","E":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Public authorities require lawful authority for coercive action."},"primary_legal_authority":"Rule-of-law and legality principles; Entick v Carrington.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Public authorities require lawful authority for coercive action.","common_trap":"Treating administrative convenience as an independent source of legal power.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9d5f23c104cf0e5d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0267","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — procedural fairness","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statutory decision-maker proposes to determine an individual's licence without allowing the individual any meaningful opportunity to answer decisive adverse allegations, although fairness in the circumstances requires such an opportunity and the statute does not exclude it.\n\nWhich ground of judicial review is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Irrationality is the only possible ground.","B":"Procedural unfairness.","C":"Ultra vires delegated legislation only.","D":"Private nuisance.","E":"Promissory estoppel."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Common-law procedural fairness can require an affected person to have an adequate opportunity to know and respond to the case against them, depending on the statutory context and circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A denial of a fair opportunity to answer material allegations can constitute procedural unfairness.","B":"Correct. Common-law procedural fairness can require an affected person to have an adequate opportunity to know and respond to the case against them, depending on the statutory context and circumstances.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Ultra vires delegated legislation only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A denial of a fair opportunity to answer material allegations can constitute procedural unfairness.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Private nuisance' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A denial of a fair opportunity to answer material allegations can constitute procedural unfairness.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Promissory estoppel' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A denial of a fair opportunity to answer material allegations can constitute procedural unfairness."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law procedural fairness; R v Secretary of State for the Home Department, ex p Doody.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A denial of a fair opportunity to answer material allegations can constitute procedural unfairness.","common_trap":"Treating every procedural defect as irrationality rather than identifying fairness as a distinct public-law concern.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"52bf2d9891ef13a8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0268","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — section 3 interpretation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A court is interpreting legislation enacted before the Human Rights Act. One available interpretation is compatible with Convention rights and is possible within the limits of the interpretive obligation.\n\nWhich statutory provision requires legislation, so far as possible, to be read and given effect compatibly with Convention rights?","options":{"A":"Human Rights Act section 4 only.","B":"Companies Act 2006 section 172.","C":"Human Rights Act 1998 section 3.","D":"Senior Courts Act 1981 section 31.","E":"European Communities Act 1972."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 3 imposes an interpretive obligation in relation to primary and subordinate legislation, so far as it is possible to do so consistently with Convention rights. Section 4 provides a different mechanism where compatible interpretation is not possible in qualifying cases.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Human Rights Act section 4 only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. HRA section 3 contains the Convention-compatible interpretation obligation.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Companies Act 2006 section 172' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. HRA section 3 contains the Convention-compatible interpretation obligation.","C":"Correct. Section 3 imposes an interpretive obligation in relation to primary and subordinate legislation, so far as it is possible to do so consistently with Convention rights. Section 4 provides a different mechanism where compatible interpretation is not possible in qualifying cases.","D":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. HRA section 3 contains the Convention-compatible interpretation obligation.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'European Communities Act 1972' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. HRA section 3 contains the Convention-compatible interpretation obligation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"HRA section 3 contains the Convention-compatible interpretation obligation.","common_trap":"Confusing compatible interpretation under section 3 with a declaration of incompatibility under section 4.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"aaec57e6e5b4199e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0269","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Lapse — beneficiary predeceases testator","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A will gives £20,000 to the testator's adult friend. The friend dies before the testator. The will contains no substitution clause and no statutory anti-lapse provision applies.\n\nWhat is the ordinary result?","options":{"A":"The deceased friend's executors automatically take the legacy.","B":"The legacy doubles for the residuary beneficiary.","C":"The entire will is revoked.","D":"The legacy lapses and falls to be dealt with under the will's residuary provisions or, if relevant, intestacy.","E":"The friend is treated as surviving the testator for 28 days."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A testamentary gift ordinarily lapses if the beneficiary predeceases the testator unless the will or a statutory rule, such as the special provision for certain gifts to descendants, saves it.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A gift to a predeceasing beneficiary ordinarily lapses unless saved.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The legacy doubles for the residuary beneficiary' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A gift to a predeceasing beneficiary ordinarily lapses unless saved.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The entire will is revoked' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A gift to a predeceasing beneficiary ordinarily lapses unless saved.","D":"Correct. A testamentary gift ordinarily lapses if the beneficiary predeceases the testator unless the will or a statutory rule, such as the special provision for certain gifts to descendants, saves it.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The friend is treated as surviving the testator for 28 days' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A gift to a predeceasing beneficiary ordinarily lapses unless saved."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, including s 33 where applicable; lapse principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A gift to a predeceasing beneficiary ordinarily lapses unless saved.","common_trap":"Assuming every beneficiary's estate substitutes automatically for the deceased beneficiary.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dbdd5f5d5638f470"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0270","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Grant — executors and administrators","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A valid will appoints an executor who is willing and able to prove the will. A trainee says the appropriate grant must nevertheless be letters of administration because every estate is 'administered'.\n\nWhich grant is ordinarily appropriate?","options":{"A":"Letters of administration in every estate.","B":"No grant can ever be made where there is a will.","C":"A grant of representation only from HM Land Registry.","D":"A bankruptcy order.","E":"A grant of probate to the proving executor."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Where a valid will appoints an executor who proves it, the ordinary grant is probate. Letters of administration are used in different circumstances, including intestacy and certain cases where no executor proves.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A proving executor named by a valid will ordinarily obtains probate.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No grant can ever be made where there is a will' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A proving executor named by a valid will ordinarily obtains probate.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A proving executor named by a valid will ordinarily obtains probate.","D":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A proving executor named by a valid will ordinarily obtains probate.","E":"Correct. Where a valid will appoints an executor who proves it, the ordinary grant is probate. Letters of administration are used in different circumstances, including intestacy and certain cases where no executor proves."},"primary_legal_authority":"Non-Contentious Probate Rules 1987; Administration of Estates Act 1925.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A proving executor named by a valid will ordinarily obtains probate.","common_trap":"Using 'administration' generically and overlooking the distinction between probate and letters of administration.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"233303cb5cf0ff03"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0271","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"IHT — spouse exemption: non-UK domiciled spouse election","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A UK-domiciled testator dies leaving a substantial legacy to a spouse who is not UK-domiciled. The ordinary unlimited spouse exemption is therefore potentially restricted. The surviving spouse is considering the statutory election that can affect the IHT treatment.\n\nWhat is the most relevant consequence of a valid election by the surviving spouse for inheritance-tax purposes?","options":{"A":"The spouse is treated as UK-domiciled for the relevant IHT spouse-exemption rules, which can permit the unlimited spouse exemption but may also bring the spouse's wider estate within the UK IHT domicile regime.","B":"The election converts the legacy into business property relief.","C":"The election removes inheritance tax from both spouses' estates permanently, regardless of future circumstances.","D":"The election changes the deceased's domicile rather than the survivor's treatment.","E":"The election is relevant only to capital gains tax and has no IHT effect."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The special election regime allows a non-UK domiciled spouse/civil partner to elect into UK-domiciled treatment for IHT purposes, addressing the restriction that can otherwise apply to transfers from a UK-domiciled transferor.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The special election regime allows a non-UK domiciled spouse/civil partner to elect into UK-domiciled treatment for IHT purposes, addressing the restriction that can otherwise apply to transfers from a UK-domiciled transferor.","B":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The spouse exemption is generally unlimited between qualifying spouses, but special rules apply where the transferor is UK-domiciled and the transferee is not; the transferee may have a statutory election into UK-domiciled treatment for IHT.","C":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The spouse exemption is generally unlimited between qualifying spouses, but special rules apply where the transferor is UK-domiciled and the transferee is not; the transferee may have a statutory election into UK-domiciled treatment for IHT.","D":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The spouse exemption is generally unlimited between qualifying spouses, but special rules apply where the transferor is UK-domiciled and the transferee is not; the transferee may have a statutory election into UK-domiciled treatment for IHT.","E":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The spouse exemption is generally unlimited between qualifying spouses, but special rules apply where the transferor is UK-domiciled and the transferee is not; the transferee may have a statutory election into UK-domiciled treatment for IHT."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, ss 18 and 267ZA–267ZB","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The spouse exemption is generally unlimited between qualifying spouses, but special rules apply where the transferor is UK-domiciled and the transferee is not; the transferee may have a statutory election into UK-domiciled treatment for IHT.","common_trap":"Applying the ordinary unlimited spouse exemption without checking the domicile mismatch or overlooking the wider consequences of an election.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"36c8760aaba0f1e8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0272","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Use of client account","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client asks a solicitor to receive and immediately forward money unrelated to any underlying legal transaction or regulated service. The only reason for involving the firm is that the client wants to use the firm's client account as a convenient banking facility.\n\nWhich is the best regulatory response?","options":{"A":"The firm must accept any transfer requested by an existing client.","B":"The firm must not use client account to provide a banking facility where there is no proper connection with an underlying transaction or regulated service.","C":"Client account can be used as a general payment service if no fee is charged.","D":"The rule applies only to cash deposits.","E":"The firm may process unrelated funds whenever the client gives written consent."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules prohibit using client account as a banking facility. Payments into and transfers or withdrawals from client account must have a proper connection with the delivery of regulated services by the firm.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client account cannot be used as a general banking facility.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules prohibit using client account as a banking facility. Payments into and transfers or withdrawals from client account must have a proper connection with the delivery of regulated services by the firm.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Client account can be used as a general payment service if no fee is charged' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Client account cannot be used as a general banking facility.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client account cannot be used as a general banking facility.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client account cannot be used as a general banking facility."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 3.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client account cannot be used as a general banking facility.","common_trap":"Assuming client consent turns an unrelated money-transfer service into a permissible client-account transaction.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"04ec8fc18cc1eca9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0273","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Constitution — Milroy v Lord","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A donor intends to make an outright gift of shares but does not complete the transfer formalities. The donor has also not declared herself trustee. The recipient argues that equity should simply treat the failed gift as a trust because that would achieve the donor's intended result.\n\nWhich principle is most relevant?","options":{"A":"Every failed gift automatically becomes a trust.","B":"Intention alone always transfers legal title.","C":"Equity will not ordinarily perfect an imperfect gift by recharacterising an intended outright transfer as a declaration of trust, subject to recognised exceptions.","D":"A recipient can complete any donor's transfer unilaterally.","E":"The rule in Saunders v Vautier perfects all gifts."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Milroy v Lord expresses the principle that equity will not generally perfect an imperfect gift or treat an ineffective intended transfer as a self-declaration of trust merely to save it. Separate exceptions must be analysed on their own facts.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Equity ordinarily will not rescue an imperfect gift by converting one intended method of transfer into another.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Equity ordinarily will not rescue an imperfect gift by converting one intended method of transfer into another.","C":"Correct. Milroy v Lord expresses the principle that equity will not generally perfect an imperfect gift or treat an ineffective intended transfer as a self-declaration of trust merely to save it. Separate exceptions must be analysed on their own facts.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A recipient can complete any donor's transfer unilaterally' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Equity ordinarily will not rescue an imperfect gift by converting one intended method of transfer into another.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The rule in Saunders v Vautier perfects all gifts' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Equity ordinarily will not rescue an imperfect gift by converting one intended method of transfer into another."},"primary_legal_authority":"Milroy v Lord; constitution-of-trusts principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Equity ordinarily will not rescue an imperfect gift by converting one intended method of transfer into another.","common_trap":"Using trust doctrine simply to cure incomplete gift formalities without satisfying an established exception.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9b20b6a6eb22de7a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0274","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Tracing — mixed bank account","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee wrongfully pays trust money into the trustee's personal bank account, mixing it with personal funds, and then makes withdrawals. The beneficiaries seek a proprietary remedy against identifiable substitute assets or remaining funds.\n\nWhich equitable process is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Ademption.","B":"Prescription.","C":"Frustration.","D":"Tracing, using the equitable rules applicable to mixed funds to identify value representing trust property.","E":"Part 36."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Equitable tracing enables beneficiaries in appropriate circumstances to identify trust value through substitutions and mixed funds. The applicable presumptions and proprietary remedies depend on the transaction sequence and assets remaining.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Ademption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Equitable tracing can identify misapplied trust value through substitutions and mixtures.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Prescription' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Equitable tracing can identify misapplied trust value through substitutions and mixtures.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Equitable tracing can identify misapplied trust value through substitutions and mixtures.","D":"Correct. Equitable tracing enables beneficiaries in appropriate circumstances to identify trust value through substitutions and mixed funds. The applicable presumptions and proprietary remedies depend on the transaction sequence and assets remaining.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Part 36' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Equitable tracing can identify misapplied trust value through substitutions and mixtures."},"primary_legal_authority":"Foskett v McKeown; Re Hallett's Estate; Re Oatway.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Equitable tracing can identify misapplied trust value through substitutions and mixtures.","common_trap":"Treating tracing as itself a personal cause of action or assuming mixing necessarily destroys all proprietary claims.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3a209c9a110547a9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0275","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Adverse possession — registered land: boundary exception","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A registered proprietor discovers that a neighbour has occupied a narrow strip along the boundary for more than ten years. The neighbour reasonably believed throughout the relevant period that the strip belonged to them, the exact boundary has not been determined, and the other statutory boundary conditions are satisfied.\n\nWhy can these facts matter even if the registered proprietor objects to the adverse-possession application?","options":{"A":"They may engage the statutory boundary exception permitting registration despite objection if all Schedule 6 conditions are met.","B":"Objection always defeats an adverse-possession application to registered land.","C":"Ten years' occupation automatically transfers registered title without an application.","D":"Reasonable belief is irrelevant to every registered-land adverse-possession case.","E":"The neighbour acquires only a contractual licence."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Schedule 6 contains limited exceptions under which an applicant may succeed despite the registered proprietor's objection, including the qualifying adjacent-boundary case with reasonable belief and the other statutory conditions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Schedule 6 contains limited exceptions under which an applicant may succeed despite the registered proprietor's objection, including the qualifying adjacent-boundary case with reasonable belief and the other statutory conditions.","B":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. After ten years' adverse possession of registered land an application may be made; notification ordinarily allows the proprietor to defeat it, but Schedule 6 paragraph 5 contains specified exceptions, including a tightly defined boundary case.","C":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. After ten years' adverse possession of registered land an application may be made; notification ordinarily allows the proprietor to defeat it, but Schedule 6 paragraph 5 contains specified exceptions, including a tightly defined boundary case.","D":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. After ten years' adverse possession of registered land an application may be made; notification ordinarily allows the proprietor to defeat it, but Schedule 6 paragraph 5 contains specified exceptions, including a tightly defined boundary case.","E":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. After ten years' adverse possession of registered land an application may be made; notification ordinarily allows the proprietor to defeat it, but Schedule 6 paragraph 5 contains specified exceptions, including a tightly defined boundary case."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, Sch 6, paras 1, 5","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"After ten years' adverse possession of registered land an application may be made; notification ordinarily allows the proprietor to defeat it, but Schedule 6 paragraph 5 contains specified exceptions, including a tightly defined boundary case.","common_trap":"Remembering only the general notification rule and overlooking the Schedule 6 exceptions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d3d0eef2353214ab"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0276","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Freehold covenants — positive burden","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A freeholder covenants to maintain a boundary wall. The land is later sold. The neighbour argues that the burden of this positive covenant automatically runs with the freehold land at common law in the same way as a restrictive covenant may run in equity.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"The burden of a positive freehold covenant does not ordinarily run with the land to bind successors merely because they acquire the land, though other mechanisms may sometimes produce practical liability.","B":"The burden of every positive covenant automatically runs in equity.","C":"Positive and restrictive covenants have identical rules.","D":"A positive covenant automatically becomes an easement.","E":"Registration alone always makes the positive burden run."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The orthodox rule is that the burden of a positive covenant does not run with freehold land at common law, and Tulk v Moxhay does not generally extend to positive obligations. Devices such as chains of indemnity or benefit-and-burden principles require separate analysis.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The orthodox rule is that the burden of a positive covenant does not run with freehold land at common law, and Tulk v Moxhay does not generally extend to positive obligations. Devices such as chains of indemnity or benefit-and-burden principles require separate analysis.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Positive freehold covenant burdens do not ordinarily run automatically to successors.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Positive and restrictive covenants have identical rules' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Positive freehold covenant burdens do not ordinarily run automatically to successors.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Positive freehold covenant burdens do not ordinarily run automatically to successors.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Positive freehold covenant burdens do not ordinarily run automatically to successors."},"primary_legal_authority":"Austerberry v Oldham Corp; Rhone v Stephens.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Positive freehold covenant burdens do not ordinarily run automatically to successors.","common_trap":"Applying the equitable restrictive-covenant rule to positive obligations.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d616188aefc81712"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0277","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Contract — deposit","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"On exchange of a residential sale contract incorporating standard conditions, the buyer is required to pay the contractual deposit. The parties have not agreed a different percentage.\n\nWhat is the conventional standard deposit?","options":{"A":"1% in every case.","B":"10% of the purchase price, subject to the contract and any agreed variation.","C":"50% by statute.","D":"No deposit can be paid on exchange.","E":"The full purchase price must always be paid at exchange."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Under conventional residential conveyancing practice and standard conditions, a 10% deposit is ordinarily payable on exchange unless the parties agree otherwise, though a reduced amount is common in some transactions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The conventional exchange deposit is 10%, subject to the agreed contract.","B":"Correct. Under conventional residential conveyancing practice and standard conditions, a 10% deposit is ordinarily payable on exchange unless the parties agree otherwise, though a reduced amount is common in some transactions.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition '50% by statute' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The conventional exchange deposit is 10%, subject to the agreed contract.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No deposit can be paid on exchange' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The conventional exchange deposit is 10%, subject to the agreed contract.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The conventional exchange deposit is 10%, subject to the agreed contract."},"primary_legal_authority":"Standard Conditions of Sale; conveyancing practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The conventional exchange deposit is 10%, subject to the agreed contract.","common_trap":"Confusing the standard contractual deposit with a statutory requirement that can never be varied.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b028a4f8333b806a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0278","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Registered title — official copies","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor acting for a buyer of registered land wants authoritative current information about the registered proprietor, registered charges and entries affecting title before advising on the transaction.\n\nWhich document is the primary starting point?","options":{"A":"The seller's council tax bill alone.","B":"A Companies House search regardless of the seller.","C":"Official copies of the registered title and title plan obtained from HM Land Registry.","D":"The property's energy bill.","E":"A probate registry search."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Official copies of the register provide the core registered-title information, normally considered alongside the title plan and other conveyancing searches and enquiries.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The seller's council tax bill alone' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. HM Land Registry official copies are the primary registered-title evidence for conveyancing investigation.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A Companies House search regardless of the seller' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. HM Land Registry official copies are the primary registered-title evidence for conveyancing investigation.","C":"Correct. Official copies of the register provide the core registered-title information, normally considered alongside the title plan and other conveyancing searches and enquiries.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The property's energy bill' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. HM Land Registry official copies are the primary registered-title evidence for conveyancing investigation.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A probate registry search' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. HM Land Registry official copies are the primary registered-title evidence for conveyancing investigation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002; HM Land Registry conveyancing practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"HM Land Registry official copies are the primary registered-title evidence for conveyancing investigation.","common_trap":"Relying on non-title documents as substitutes for the Land Register.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0854621a07850249"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0279","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Murder — mens rea","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant unlawfully kills a person while intending to cause that person really serious bodily injury, but the prosecution cannot prove an intention to kill.\n\nAssuming causation and the other requirements are satisfied, is the mens rea for murder capable of being established?","options":{"A":"No. Murder invariably requires a purpose to cause death and nothing less.","B":"No offence can arise without an intention to kill.","C":"Only manslaughter is legally possible whenever death was unintended.","D":"Yes. An intention to cause grievous bodily harm can constitute the mens rea for murder.","E":"Murder is a strict-liability offence."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Under established English law, the mens rea of murder is an intention to kill or to cause grievous bodily harm. An intention to cause really serious injury can therefore suffice.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No. Murder invariably requires a purpose to cause death and nothing less' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Intent to kill or cause grievous bodily harm is sufficient mens rea for murder.","B":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Intent to kill or cause grievous bodily harm is sufficient mens rea for murder.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Intent to kill or cause grievous bodily harm is sufficient mens rea for murder.","D":"Correct. Under established English law, the mens rea of murder is an intention to kill or to cause grievous bodily harm. An intention to cause really serious injury can therefore suffice.","E":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Intent to kill or cause grievous bodily harm is sufficient mens rea for murder."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Vickers; R v Cunningham [1982] AC 566.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Intent to kill or cause grievous bodily harm is sufficient mens rea for murder.","common_trap":"Assuming lack of an intention to kill necessarily excludes murder.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"074fdbee1fa4eb9b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0280","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Mode of trial — summary-only offence","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant is charged only with a summary-only offence. No exceptional statutory provision gives the Crown Court original trial jurisdiction over it.\n\nWhere will the trial ordinarily take place?","options":{"A":"In the Crown Court before a jury.","B":"In the High Court.","C":"In the Court of Appeal.","D":"In the Supreme Court.","E":"In the magistrates' court."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Summary-only offences are ordinarily tried in the magistrates' court. Crown Court trial is associated with indictable-only offences and either-way offences sent or elected for Crown Court trial.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A summary-only offence is ordinarily tried in the magistrates' court.","B":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A summary-only offence is ordinarily tried in the magistrates' court.","C":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A summary-only offence is ordinarily tried in the magistrates' court.","D":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A summary-only offence is ordinarily tried in the magistrates' court.","E":"Correct. Summary-only offences are ordinarily tried in the magistrates' court. Crown Court trial is associated with indictable-only offences and either-way offences sent or elected for Crown Court trial."},"primary_legal_authority":"Magistrates' Courts Act 1980; criminal procedure principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A summary-only offence is ordinarily tried in the magistrates' court.","common_trap":"Confusing offence classification with the defendant's general preference for jury trial.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d046980219af92ee"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0281","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — model articles and quorum","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company has two directors and uses the unmodified model articles for private companies limited by shares. One director is abroad and cannot participate. The remaining director wants to make a board decision alone, and no special provision or prior valid board decision changes the quorum.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"The sole participating director cannot ordinarily make the substantive board decision because the model articles' default quorum for directors' meetings is two.","B":"One director always constitutes a quorum in every private company.","C":"The shareholder with the largest holding automatically replaces the absent director.","D":"Companies House sets the quorum separately for each meeting.","E":"A quorum is required only for public companies."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Under the model articles for private companies limited by shares, the default quorum for directors' meetings is two unless otherwise fixed, subject to provisions dealing with situations such as insufficient directors.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Under the model articles for private companies limited by shares, the default quorum for directors' meetings is two unless otherwise fixed, subject to provisions dealing with situations such as insufficient directors.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The default board quorum under the relevant model articles is two.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The default board quorum under the relevant model articles is two.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Companies House sets the quorum separately for each meeting' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The default board quorum under the relevant model articles is two.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The default board quorum under the relevant model articles is two."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies (Model Articles) Regulations 2008, Sch 1, arts 11–12.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The default board quorum under the relevant model articles is two.","common_trap":"Assuming a private company can always act through a single director regardless of its articles and board composition.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0e4a350d8f2503f7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0282","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — removal of director","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Members of a company want to remove a director before the end of the director's term. The director's service contract may give rise to separate compensation issues.\n\nWhich corporate-law mechanism is ordinarily relevant?","options":{"A":"A special resolution requiring 75% in every case.","B":"An ordinary resolution under the statutory director-removal procedure, subject to special notice and the director's statutory rights.","C":"A unanimous written resolution, which is the mandatory route.","D":"A board resolution passed by the director being removed.","E":"Automatic removal merely because a majority shareholder sends an email."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Companies Act 2006 section 168 permits removal of a director by ordinary resolution at a meeting, notwithstanding contrary provisions in an agreement or articles, while preserving possible compensation and requiring the statutory procedure including special notice.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Members can remove a director by ordinary resolution using the statutory procedure, with contractual consequences considered separately.","B":"Correct. Companies Act 2006 section 168 permits removal of a director by ordinary resolution at a meeting, notwithstanding contrary provisions in an agreement or articles, while preserving possible compensation and requiring the statutory procedure including special notice.","C":"Incorrect. This imposes a formality that the governing rule does not require in this situation. Members can remove a director by ordinary resolution using the statutory procedure, with contractual consequences considered separately.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A board resolution passed by the director being removed' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Members can remove a director by ordinary resolution using the statutory procedure, with contractual consequences considered separately.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Automatic removal merely because a majority shareholder sends an email' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Members can remove a director by ordinary resolution using the statutory procedure, with contractual consequences considered separately."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 168–169 and 312.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Members can remove a director by ordinary resolution using the statutory procedure, with contractual consequences considered separately.","common_trap":"Confusing removal from office with termination consequences under a service contract or assuming a written resolution can be used.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"31e370cc525ecf06"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0283","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership — dissolution on notice","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Three people carry on business in partnership without agreeing a fixed term or any contractual mechanism restricting dissolution. One partner gives the others clear notice that the partnership is to end immediately.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the default statutory position?","options":{"A":"A partnership can be dissolved only by court order.","B":"Notice is ineffective unless every partner consents.","C":"A partnership for an undefined time may ordinarily be dissolved by a partner giving notice to the others, subject to any contrary partnership agreement.","D":"A partnership without a fixed term lasts for 99 years.","E":"Only insolvency can dissolve a partnership."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"In a partnership at will, the Partnership Act provides for dissolution by notice from a partner, subject to the partnership agreement and the statutory rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A partnership at will can ordinarily be dissolved by a partner's notice.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A partnership at will can ordinarily be dissolved by a partner's notice.","C":"Correct. In a partnership at will, the Partnership Act provides for dissolution by notice from a partner, subject to the partnership agreement and the statutory rules.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A partnership without a fixed term lasts for 99 years' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A partnership at will can ordinarily be dissolved by a partner's notice.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A partnership at will can ordinarily be dissolved by a partner's notice."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, ss 26 and 32(c).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A partnership at will can ordinarily be dissolved by a partner's notice.","common_trap":"Assuming unanimity or a court order is always required to end an indefinite partnership.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cd86ddb0d866a5a2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0284","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Tax — trading losses","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A sole trader makes a genuine trading loss in a tax year and asks whether the loss is simply ignored because there is no trading profit for that year.\n\nWhich is the best general answer for SQE purposes?","options":{"A":"A trading loss is always ignored permanently.","B":"Every trading loss is automatically repaid in cash by HMRC in full.","C":"Trading losses can be used only against inheritance tax.","D":"Trading losses may qualify for statutory loss relief, subject to the conditions, choices, restrictions and tax rules applicable to the taxpayer and period.","E":"A sole trader's losses are corporation-tax losses."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Income-tax legislation provides relief mechanisms for qualifying trade losses. The precise relief depends on the facts and statutory conditions; a loss is not simply treated as legally irrelevant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Qualifying trading losses can be relieved under statutory income-tax loss-relief rules.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Qualifying trading losses can be relieved under statutory income-tax loss-relief rules.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Qualifying trading losses can be relieved under statutory income-tax loss-relief rules.","D":"Correct. Income-tax legislation provides relief mechanisms for qualifying trade losses. The precise relief depends on the facts and statutory conditions; a loss is not simply treated as legally irrelevant.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A sole trader's losses are corporation-tax losses' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Qualifying trading losses can be relieved under statutory income-tax loss-relief rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Income Tax Act 2007, Part 4; SRA FLK1 taxation specification.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Qualifying trading losses can be relieved under statutory income-tax loss-relief rules.","common_trap":"Assuming the absence of current-year profit means the tax system gives no recognition to a qualifying trade loss.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2c6ece5b8dbb90ad"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0285","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Default judgment","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant has been properly served with a money claim and particulars of claim but files neither an acknowledgment of service nor a defence within the applicable time. The claim is not within a category where default judgment is unavailable.\n\nWhat procedural step may the claimant ordinarily consider?","options":{"A":"Applying for summary judgment is the only possible response.","B":"The claimant automatically loses the claim.","C":"Starting an unrelated criminal prosecution.","D":"Applying for judicial review of the defendant.","E":"Seeking default judgment under CPR Part 12, provided the applicable conditions are satisfied."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"CPR Part 12 permits default judgment in eligible claims where the defendant has failed to acknowledge service or defend within the applicable procedural requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Failure to acknowledge or defend can permit default judgment in an eligible claim.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Failure to acknowledge or defend can permit default judgment in an eligible claim.","C":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Failure to acknowledge or defend can permit default judgment in an eligible claim.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Applying for judicial review of the defendant' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Failure to acknowledge or defend can permit default judgment in an eligible claim.","E":"Correct. CPR Part 12 permits default judgment in eligible claims where the defendant has failed to acknowledge service or defend within the applicable procedural requirements."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 12.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Failure to acknowledge or defend can permit default judgment in an eligible claim.","common_trap":"Assuming judgment arises automatically without the claimant satisfying the applicable Part 12 process.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6e6ae2b15014c95c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0286","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Offers — Calderbank offers","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party makes a genuine written settlement offer expressed 'without prejudice save as to costs'. It does not comply with Part 36. The case proceeds to judgment.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Although it does not attract the automatic Part 36 regime, the court may consider the offer when exercising its discretion as to costs.","B":"The offer is legally irrelevant to costs because it is not Part 36.","C":"The offer must be shown to the trial judge before liability is decided.","D":"It automatically produces all CPR 36.17 consequences.","E":"It conclusively determines who wins the substantive claim."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A non-Part 36 offer made without prejudice save as to costs can be relevant to the court's broad costs discretion after the substantive issues are determined, although it does not receive Part 36's prescribed consequences.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A non-Part 36 offer made without prejudice save as to costs can be relevant to the court's broad costs discretion after the substantive issues are determined, although it does not receive Part 36's prescribed consequences.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The offer is legally irrelevant to costs because it is not Part 36' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Calderbank-type offers can influence discretionary costs without being Part 36 offers.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Calderbank-type offers can influence discretionary costs without being Part 36 offers.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Calderbank-type offers can influence discretionary costs without being Part 36 offers.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'It conclusively determines who wins the substantive claim' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Calderbank-type offers can influence discretionary costs without being Part 36 offers."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 44.2; without-prejudice-save-as-to-costs principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Calderbank-type offers can influence discretionary costs without being Part 36 offers.","common_trap":"Treating Part 36 as the only settlement-offer mechanism capable of affecting costs.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ba0c8581afc58b83"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0287","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Costs — qualified one-way costs shifting","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant brings an ordinary personal injury claim within the scope of the QOCS regime and loses at trial. The defendant obtains a costs order and wants to enforce it against the claimant.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the starting point?","options":{"A":"QOCS means no costs order can ever be made against a claimant.","B":"Enforcement of the defendant's costs order is restricted by the QOCS rules, subject to the detailed exceptions and limits in CPR Part 44.","C":"The defendant may always enforce all costs against every asset without restriction.","D":"QOCS applies to every civil claim regardless of subject matter.","E":"The claimant automatically receives indemnity costs after losing."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"QOCS limits enforcement of costs orders against claimants in proceedings within its scope. The precise enforcement position and exceptions must be analysed under the current CPR provisions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. QOCS principally restricts enforcement of adverse costs against qualifying claimants rather than eliminating costs orders altogether.","B":"Correct. QOCS limits enforcement of costs orders against claimants in proceedings within its scope. The precise enforcement position and exceptions must be analysed under the current CPR provisions.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. QOCS principally restricts enforcement of adverse costs against qualifying claimants rather than eliminating costs orders altogether.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. QOCS principally restricts enforcement of adverse costs against qualifying claimants rather than eliminating costs orders altogether.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. QOCS principally restricts enforcement of adverse costs against qualifying claimants rather than eliminating costs orders altogether."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 44, Section II.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"QOCS principally restricts enforcement of adverse costs against qualifying claimants rather than eliminating costs orders altogether.","common_trap":"Equating QOCS with absolute immunity from any adverse costs order or enforcement consequence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fa9eff09977ed38b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0288","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Client care — costs information","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor accepts a new matter but decides not to give the client any information about likely costs because the final amount cannot yet be predicted exactly.\n\nWhich is the best professional approach?","options":{"A":"Costs information is prohibited until the final bill is known.","B":"Only corporate clients are entitled to costs information.","C":"The client should receive the best possible information about how the matter will be priced and its likely overall cost, both at engagement and as the matter progresses.","D":"A solicitor may keep charging arrangements secret if precise prediction is difficult.","E":"Costs information is required only in criminal cases."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code requires solicitors to ensure clients receive the best possible information about pricing and likely overall cost, at engagement and when appropriate as the matter progresses.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Costs information is prohibited until the final bill is known' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Uncertainty about the final figure does not remove the duty to provide meaningful costs information.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Uncertainty about the final figure does not remove the duty to provide meaningful costs information.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code requires solicitors to ensure clients receive the best possible information about pricing and likely overall cost, at engagement and when appropriate as the matter progresses.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A solicitor may keep charging arrangements secret if precise prediction is difficult' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Uncertainty about the final figure does not remove the duty to provide meaningful costs information.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Uncertainty about the final figure does not remove the duty to provide meaningful costs information."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 8.7.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Uncertainty about the final figure does not remove the duty to provide meaningful costs information.","common_trap":"Treating inability to give a perfect forecast as a reason to provide no pricing information at all.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d939054d813541b8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0289","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Equality, diversity and inclusion","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor says professional obligations concerning equality and diversity apply only to recruitment and have no relevance to the way clients and colleagues are treated in practice.\n\nWhich is the best response?","options":{"A":"Equality obligations are confined to recruitment advertisements.","B":"Solicitors are exempt from equality law when providing legal services.","C":"The duty applies only to courts.","D":"The SRA framework requires solicitors to act in a way that encourages equality, diversity and inclusion, and related legal duties can apply beyond recruitment.","E":"The duty applies only where a client expressly requests it."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Professional standards include an obligation to act in a way that encourages equality, diversity and inclusion. The Equality Act framework can also apply in employment and service provision contexts.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Equality obligations are confined to recruitment advertisements' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Equality, diversity and inclusion obligations are not confined to recruitment.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Solicitors are exempt from equality law when providing legal services' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Equality, diversity and inclusion obligations are not confined to recruitment.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Equality, diversity and inclusion obligations are not confined to recruitment.","D":"Correct. Professional standards include an obligation to act in a way that encourages equality, diversity and inclusion. The Equality Act framework can also apply in employment and service provision contexts.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Equality, diversity and inclusion obligations are not confined to recruitment."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 1.1; Equality Act 2010.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Equality, diversity and inclusion obligations are not confined to recruitment.","common_trap":"Narrowing professional and statutory equality obligations to a single employment stage.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c7fe620126a5ba7d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0290","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Terms — conditions and warranties","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contract expressly and validly classifies an important obligation as a condition. One party commits a repudiatory breach of that condition.\n\nWhich remedy position ordinarily follows, subject to election and any contractual qualification?","options":{"A":"Breach of a condition can never justify termination.","B":"Only nominal damages are available.","C":"The contract is automatically void from inception.","D":"The innocent party must obtain a criminal conviction before terminating.","E":"The innocent party may ordinarily terminate for breach of condition and claim damages."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A condition is a major contractual term for which breach ordinarily gives the innocent party a right to terminate and claim damages, subject to affirmation, waiver and the contract's proper construction.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Breach of a contractual condition ordinarily carries a termination right plus damages.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Breach of a contractual condition ordinarily carries a termination right plus damages.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Breach of a contractual condition ordinarily carries a termination right plus damages.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Breach of a contractual condition ordinarily carries a termination right plus damages.","E":"Correct. A condition is a major contractual term for which breach ordinarily gives the innocent party a right to terminate and claim damages, subject to affirmation, waiver and the contract's proper construction."},"primary_legal_authority":"Poussard v Spiers; contractual classification principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Breach of a contractual condition ordinarily carries a termination right plus damages.","common_trap":"Confusing conditions with warranties, breach of which ordinarily sounds in damages alone.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c211c7122dc219dd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0291","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Terms — innominate terms: effect of breach","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contract contains an innominate term. A breach causes only a short interruption and does not deprive the innocent party of substantially the whole benefit expected under the contract.\n\nWhat remedy consequence follows most directly from classifying the term as innominate?","options":{"A":"Termination depends on the seriousness and consequences of the breach; on these facts damages may be available without a right to terminate.","B":"Every breach of an innominate term automatically entitles termination.","C":"An innominate term can never support damages.","D":"The label makes the contract void from the outset.","E":"The innocent party must terminate before claiming any damages."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"For an innominate term, the right to terminate turns on whether the consequences of breach are sufficiently serious to deprive the innocent party of substantially the whole contractual benefit.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. For an innominate term, the right to terminate turns on whether the consequences of breach are sufficiently serious to deprive the innocent party of substantially the whole contractual benefit.","B":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. Unlike a condition or warranty, an innominate term is classified by the consequences of its breach when deciding whether termination is available.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition is too categorical and contradicts the recognised rule or exception engaged by the facts. Unlike a condition or warranty, an innominate term is classified by the consequences of its breach when deciding whether termination is available.","D":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Unlike a condition or warranty, an innominate term is classified by the consequences of its breach when deciding whether termination is available.","E":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Unlike a condition or warranty, an innominate term is classified by the consequences of its breach when deciding whether termination is available."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hongkong Fir Shipping Co Ltd v Kawasaki Kisen Kaisha Ltd [1962] 2 QB 26","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Unlike a condition or warranty, an innominate term is classified by the consequences of its breach when deciding whether termination is available.","common_trap":"Treating 'innominate' as another name for a condition.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ab1082828af31a05"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0292","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Damages — mitigation: reasonable mitigation expenditure","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Following a breach, the innocent party reasonably spends money on an attempted measure designed to reduce the resulting loss. The attempt is sensible when undertaken but ultimately does not succeed.\n\nHow is that reasonable mitigation expenditure ordinarily treated when damages are assessed?","options":{"A":"Reasonable expenditure incurred in a genuine attempt to mitigate can be recoverable even if the attempt ultimately fails.","B":"It is irrecoverable whenever the mitigation attempt does not succeed.","C":"The claimant can recover it only if the defendant approved the expenditure in advance.","D":"Mitigation expenditure is recoverable only as punitive damages.","E":"The expenditure automatically prevents recovery of the underlying contractual loss."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A claimant is expected to act reasonably, not infallibly. Reasonable costs incurred in mitigation may form part of recoverable loss even where the mitigation measure proves unsuccessful.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A claimant is expected to act reasonably, not infallibly. Reasonable costs incurred in mitigation may form part of recoverable loss even where the mitigation measure proves unsuccessful.","B":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. The mitigation principle does not penalise a claimant for a reasonable but unsuccessful response to breach.","C":"Incorrect. This incorrectly makes a non-exclusive factor the sole legal test. The mitigation principle does not penalise a claimant for a reasonable but unsuccessful response to breach.","D":"Incorrect. This incorrectly makes a non-exclusive factor the sole legal test. The mitigation principle does not penalise a claimant for a reasonable but unsuccessful response to breach.","E":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. The mitigation principle does not penalise a claimant for a reasonable but unsuccessful response to breach."},"primary_legal_authority":"Banco de Portugal v Waterlow & Sons Ltd [1932] AC 452; mitigation principles","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The mitigation principle does not penalise a claimant for a reasonable but unsuccessful response to breach.","common_trap":"Applying the headline doctrine without identifying the distinct consequence, exception or procedural stage tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"13629ea296ac0439"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0293","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — thin skull rule","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant negligently causes a foreseeable type of personal injury. Because of the claimant's unusual pre-existing physical vulnerability, the injury is far more severe than an ordinary person would have suffered.\n\nWhich principle is most relevant to the extent of liability?","options":{"A":"The claimant's unusual vulnerability always breaks causation.","B":"Damages are capped at what an average person would have suffered.","C":"The defendant generally takes the claimant as found once the relevant type of injury is foreseeable.","D":"The claim fails unless the defendant knew of the vulnerability.","E":"The thin-skull principle applies only in contract."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The thin-skull rule means a tortfeasor takes the victim as found. Once the relevant kind of personal injury is within the scope of liability, unusual susceptibility does not ordinarily reduce damages to an average level.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A defendant takes the claimant as found in relation to unusual susceptibility.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Damages are capped at what an average person would have suffered' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A defendant takes the claimant as found in relation to unusual susceptibility.","C":"Correct. The thin-skull rule means a tortfeasor takes the victim as found. Once the relevant kind of personal injury is within the scope of liability, unusual susceptibility does not ordinarily reduce damages to an average level.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The claim fails unless the defendant knew of the vulnerability' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A defendant takes the claimant as found in relation to unusual susceptibility.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A defendant takes the claimant as found in relation to unusual susceptibility."},"primary_legal_authority":"Smith v Leech Brain & Co Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A defendant takes the claimant as found in relation to unusual susceptibility.","common_trap":"Confusing remoteness of the type of damage with unforeseeability of its exceptional extent in a vulnerable claimant.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d8e260b33fd7aa29"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0294","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — intervening acts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant's negligence causes an initial injury. A later independent event is alleged to break the chain of causation before a further loss occurs.\n\nWhat is the relevant causation issue?","options":{"A":"Whether the claimant signed a contract.","B":"Whether the defendant owed fiduciary duties.","C":"Whether the claimant owns registered land.","D":"Whether the later event constitutes a novus actus interveniens sufficiently independent to break the chain of legal causation.","E":"Whether consideration was adequate."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A sufficiently independent intervening act can break the chain of causation. Whether it does so depends on matters such as foreseeability, voluntariness and the relationship between the original wrong and later event.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This imposes a formality that the governing rule does not require in this situation. A novus actus may break legal causation where the later event is sufficiently independent.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the defendant owed fiduciary duties' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A novus actus may break legal causation where the later event is sufficiently independent.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether the claimant owns registered land' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A novus actus may break legal causation where the later event is sufficiently independent.","D":"Correct. A sufficiently independent intervening act can break the chain of causation. Whether it does so depends on matters such as foreseeability, voluntariness and the relationship between the original wrong and later event.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Whether consideration was adequate' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A novus actus may break legal causation where the later event is sufficiently independent."},"primary_legal_authority":"Causation principles; McKew v Holland & Hannen & Cubitts.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A novus actus may break legal causation where the later event is sufficiently independent.","common_trap":"Assuming every later event automatically breaks causation, or that no later event ever can.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f37ad8e9a096d6ee"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0295","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Occupiers' liability — independent contractors: negligent selection","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An occupier engages an apparently specialist contractor to perform highly technical maintenance. The occupier makes no enquiry at all into competence even though readily available information would have revealed that the contractor lacked the necessary qualifications. A visitor is injured because the work was incompetently performed.\n\nWhich consideration is most relevant to whether the occupier can rely on the independent-contractor protection?","options":{"A":"Whether it was reasonable to entrust the work to the contractor and whether the occupier took reasonable steps to satisfy itself that the contractor was competent.","B":"An occupier is automatically liable for every act of every independent contractor.","C":"An occupier is automatically immune whenever a contractor is described as independent.","D":"The visitor must prove that the occupier personally performed the defective work.","E":"The contractor defence depends solely on whether the contractor carried insurance."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The statutory protection is conditional. The occupier must have acted reasonably in entrusting the work and taken reasonable steps, where appropriate, to satisfy itself as to the contractor's competence and the work.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The statutory protection is conditional. The occupier must have acted reasonably in entrusting the work and taken reasonable steps, where appropriate, to satisfy itself as to the contractor's competence and the work.","B":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. Under OLA 1957 s 2(4)(b), an occupier may avoid liability for contractor work only where it acted reasonably in entrusting the work and took reasonable steps concerning competence and, where appropriate, inspection.","C":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. Under OLA 1957 s 2(4)(b), an occupier may avoid liability for contractor work only where it acted reasonably in entrusting the work and took reasonable steps concerning competence and, where appropriate, inspection.","D":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Under OLA 1957 s 2(4)(b), an occupier may avoid liability for contractor work only where it acted reasonably in entrusting the work and took reasonable steps concerning competence and, where appropriate, inspection.","E":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Under OLA 1957 s 2(4)(b), an occupier may avoid liability for contractor work only where it acted reasonably in entrusting the work and took reasonable steps concerning competence and, where appropriate, inspection."},"primary_legal_authority":"Occupiers' Liability Act 1957, s 2(4)(b)","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Under OLA 1957 s 2(4)(b), an occupier may avoid liability for contractor work only where it acted reasonably in entrusting the work and took reasonable steps concerning competence and, where appropriate, inspection.","common_trap":"Treating engagement of an independent contractor as an automatic complete defence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"95f4a42fd512fc6c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0296","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Parliamentary privilege — proceedings","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A litigant asks a court to impose civil liability solely on an MP for words spoken during a debate in the House of Commons as part of parliamentary proceedings.\n\nWhich constitutional protection is directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Parliamentary privilege protects freedom of speech and debate in parliamentary proceedings from being impeached or questioned in court, subject to its proper scope.","B":"MPs have a general immunity for everything they do anywhere.","C":"Parliamentary privilege applies only to local councillors.","D":"Courts may freely impose liability for the content of parliamentary debate as if it were an ordinary publication.","E":"Privilege means Acts of Parliament cannot be interpreted by courts."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Article 9 of the Bill of Rights protects freedom of speech and debates or proceedings in Parliament from being impeached or questioned in courts. The protection is tied to parliamentary proceedings, not a general personal immunity.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Article 9 of the Bill of Rights protects freedom of speech and debates or proceedings in Parliament from being impeached or questioned in courts. The protection is tied to parliamentary proceedings, not a general personal immunity.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'MPs have a general immunity for everything they do anywhere' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Article 9 protects parliamentary proceedings from judicial questioning within its scope.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Article 9 protects parliamentary proceedings from judicial questioning within its scope.","D":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Article 9 protects parliamentary proceedings from judicial questioning within its scope.","E":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Article 9 protects parliamentary proceedings from judicial questioning within its scope."},"primary_legal_authority":"Bill of Rights 1689, art 9.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Article 9 protects parliamentary proceedings from judicial questioning within its scope.","common_trap":"Turning parliamentary privilege into a blanket immunity for all conduct by parliamentarians.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d8118f0c12d3b20c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0297","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Royal prerogative — statutory displacement","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A minister claims an old prerogative power permits executive action in an area where Parliament has enacted a statute comprehensively regulating the same power and conditions for its exercise.\n\nWhich principle should be considered?","options":{"A":"Prerogative powers always override Acts of Parliament.","B":"Where statute occupies the field and regulates the relevant power, the executive cannot ordinarily use prerogative power to circumvent the statutory scheme.","C":"Statutes cannot affect prerogative powers.","D":"A minister may choose whichever source of power has fewer legal constraints.","E":"Courts have no jurisdiction to identify the legal source of executive power."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Constitutional authority establishes that statutory regulation can displace or place prerogative power in abeyance in the same field; the executive cannot use prerogative to frustrate Parliament's scheme.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Statutory regulation can displace prerogative power in the same field.","B":"Correct. Constitutional authority establishes that statutory regulation can displace or place prerogative power in abeyance in the same field; the executive cannot use prerogative to frustrate Parliament's scheme.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Statutes cannot affect prerogative powers' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Statutory regulation can displace prerogative power in the same field.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A minister may choose whichever source of power has fewer legal constraints' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Statutory regulation can displace prerogative power in the same field.","E":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Statutory regulation can displace prerogative power in the same field."},"primary_legal_authority":"Attorney General v De Keyser's Royal Hotel Ltd; constitutional prerogative principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Statutory regulation can displace prerogative power in the same field.","common_trap":"Assuming executive prerogative can be used to evade statutory limits.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1ec466a5060c72e5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0298","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"EU-derived law — interpretation after 2023 reforms","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor is analysing a domestic provision originating in EU-derived law after the post-2023 reforms. She assumes every pre-Brexit EU interpretive principle and supremacy rule continues automatically and unchanged forever.\n\nWhich is the safest SQE analysis?","options":{"A":"All EU law ceased to exist for every domestic purpose on exit day.","B":"Every EU principle automatically overrides every later Act of Parliament.","C":"The current domestic status and interpretation of assimilated law must be determined under the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018 as amended and any relevant post-Brexit legislation; automatic unchanged continuation should not be assumed.","D":"The UK remains an EU Member State for statutory interpretation.","E":"Only the CJEU may interpret assimilated law."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023 changed terminology and aspects of the domestic treatment of retained/assimilated law. The applicable statutory framework, amendments and relevant authority must be used rather than assuming the pre-Brexit position persists unchanged.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Post-Brexit EU-derived rules are governed by the current domestic statutory framework, including the amended 2018 Act.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Post-Brexit EU-derived rules are governed by the current domestic statutory framework, including the amended 2018 Act.","C":"Correct. The Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023 changed terminology and aspects of the domestic treatment of retained/assimilated law. The applicable statutory framework, amendments and relevant authority must be used rather than assuming the pre-Brexit position persists unchanged.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The UK remains an EU Member State for statutory interpretation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Post-Brexit EU-derived rules are governed by the current domestic statutory framework, including the amended 2018 Act.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Post-Brexit EU-derived rules are governed by the current domestic statutory framework, including the amended 2018 Act."},"primary_legal_authority":"European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018 as amended; Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Post-Brexit EU-derived rules are governed by the current domestic statutory framework, including the amended 2018 Act.","common_trap":"Using a frozen pre-withdrawal model of supremacy and general principles without checking the current statutory regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3e066510ae177a67"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0299","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Will revocation — accidental destruction","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator accidentally spills liquid over the original will and, believing it unreadable, throws it away. The testator does not intend to revoke the will and does not direct anyone to destroy it for that purpose.\n\nWhat is the best statement about revocation by destruction?","options":{"A":"Physical destruction alone is insufficient: revocation by destruction requires the statutory act together with an intention to revoke.","B":"Any damage to the original will automatically revokes it.","C":"A will can be revoked by destruction only by a solicitor.","D":"Accidental destruction converts the will into an intestacy automatically even if its contents and due execution can be proved.","E":"Revocation depends solely on whether the paper can still be read."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Revocation by destruction requires both an act of burning, tearing or otherwise destroying the will and the intention of revoking it. Accidental destruction lacks the required animus revocandi.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Revocation by destruction requires both an act of burning, tearing or otherwise destroying the will and the intention of revoking it. Accidental destruction lacks the required animus revocandi.","B":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. Wills Act 1837 s 20 requires the prescribed destructive act to be accompanied by an intention to revoke.","C":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Wills Act 1837 s 20 requires the prescribed destructive act to be accompanied by an intention to revoke.","D":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. Wills Act 1837 s 20 requires the prescribed destructive act to be accompanied by an intention to revoke.","E":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Wills Act 1837 s 20 requires the prescribed destructive act to be accompanied by an intention to revoke."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 20","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Wills Act 1837 s 20 requires the prescribed destructive act to be accompanied by an intention to revoke.","common_trap":"Focusing on the physical condition of the document and ignoring intention.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7e4bdc25a3a36505"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0300","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Administration — debts and liabilities","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Personal representatives receive estate assets and immediately propose to distribute all residue to beneficiaries, although known funeral expenses, administration expenses and enforceable estate debts remain unpaid.\n\nWhich is the correct priority?","options":{"A":"Beneficiaries always take before creditors.","B":"Funeral and administration expenses are paid only if beneficiaries consent.","C":"All debts lapse automatically on death.","D":"Distribution must occur before the personal representatives identify liabilities.","E":"Personal representatives must administer the estate and meet liabilities in the proper order before distributing the net estate to beneficiaries."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Personal representatives collect and administer estate assets, pay funeral, testamentary and administration expenses and enforceable liabilities according to the applicable priority rules, and distribute the resulting net estate.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Estate liabilities must be dealt with before beneficiaries receive the distributable net estate.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Estate liabilities must be dealt with before beneficiaries receive the distributable net estate.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Estate liabilities must be dealt with before beneficiaries receive the distributable net estate.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Estate liabilities must be dealt with before beneficiaries receive the distributable net estate.","E":"Correct. Personal representatives collect and administer estate assets, pay funeral, testamentary and administration expenses and enforceable liabilities according to the applicable priority rules, and distribute the resulting net estate."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925; estate-administration principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Estate liabilities must be dealt with before beneficiaries receive the distributable net estate.","common_trap":"Treating testamentary beneficiaries as having priority over the deceased's enforceable liabilities.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1dd1330dd521dc12"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0301","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"IHT — annual exemption","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual makes lifetime gifts potentially within the inheritance-tax regime and asks whether legislation provides any annual exemption for qualifying lifetime transfers.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate for SQE purposes?","options":{"A":"An annual exemption exists, subject to statutory conditions; where a question requires application of a monetary amount, the assessment can provide the relevant figure.","B":"There is no annual exemption in inheritance tax.","C":"The annual exemption applies only to companies.","D":"Every lifetime gift is unlimitedly exempt.","E":"The exemption is an SDLT relief."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"IHT legislation provides an annual exemption for qualifying lifetime transfers. Under the updated SQE tax guidance, monetary values of exemptions, reliefs, rates or thresholds are supplied where candidates are required to apply them.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. IHT legislation provides an annual exemption for qualifying lifetime transfers. Under the updated SQE tax guidance, monetary values of exemptions, reliefs, rates or thresholds are supplied where candidates are required to apply them.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'There is no annual exemption in inheritance tax' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. IHT includes a statutory annual exemption; monetary figures are supplied in SQE questions where application is required.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. IHT includes a statutory annual exemption; monetary figures are supplied in SQE questions where application is required.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. IHT includes a statutory annual exemption; monetary figures are supplied in SQE questions where application is required.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The exemption is an SDLT relief' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. IHT includes a statutory annual exemption; monetary figures are supplied in SQE questions where application is required."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, s 19; SRA SQE1 Assessment Specification applicable from 1 September 2026.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"IHT includes a statutory annual exemption; monetary figures are supplied in SQE questions where application is required.","common_trap":"Memorising changing monetary values instead of identifying the legal structure and using supplied figures when required.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9e3d83bfaa83ac3e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0302","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Residual balances","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A conveyancing matter has ended. The firm still holds a small residual client balance and knows the client's current contact details. The cashier suggests retaining the money indefinitely because the amount is modest.\n\nWhich is the best regulatory approach?","options":{"A":"Small balances automatically become the firm's property.","B":"Client money should be returned promptly as soon as there is no proper reason to hold it; residual balances must be dealt with under the Accounts Rules rather than retained indefinitely.","C":"Residual client money may always remain in client account permanently.","D":"The firm may transfer the balance to profit without notification.","E":"The client loses entitlement when the file closes."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require client money to be returned promptly once there is no proper reason to hold it. Specific provisions and SRA guidance address residual balances where the rightful owner cannot be traced; those are not a reason to retain a known client's money.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client money must be returned promptly when there is no proper reason to retain it.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules require client money to be returned promptly once there is no proper reason to hold it. Specific provisions and SRA guidance address residual balances where the rightful owner cannot be traced; those are not a reason to retain a known client's money.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client money must be returned promptly when there is no proper reason to retain it.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The firm may transfer the balance to profit without notification' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Client money must be returned promptly when there is no proper reason to retain it.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The client loses entitlement when the file closes' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Client money must be returned promptly when there is no proper reason to retain it."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 2.5 and residual-balance requirements.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client money must be returned promptly when there is no proper reason to retain it.","common_trap":"Treating a small amount as de minimis property of the firm.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b86a457796ae52dd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0303","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustee investment — standard investment criteria","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Trustees exercise a statutory power of investment. They focus exclusively on the expected headline return and refuse to consider whether the investments are suitable for the trust or whether diversification is appropriate.\n\nWhich statutory duties are particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Trustees must always select the single investment with the highest projected return.","B":"Diversification is prohibited for trustees.","C":"The standard investment criteria require consideration of suitability and the need for diversification, so far as appropriate to the trust.","D":"Suitability matters only to company directors.","E":"Trustees have no statutory investment framework."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Trustee Act requires trustees exercising investment powers to have regard to the standard investment criteria, including suitability and the need for diversification so far as appropriate, and to review investments from time to time.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Trustee investment decisions must address suitability and appropriate diversification.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Diversification is prohibited for trustees' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Trustee investment decisions must address suitability and appropriate diversification.","C":"Correct. The Trustee Act requires trustees exercising investment powers to have regard to the standard investment criteria, including suitability and the need for diversification so far as appropriate, and to review investments from time to time.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Trustee investment decisions must address suitability and appropriate diversification.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Trustees have no statutory investment framework' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Trustee investment decisions must address suitability and appropriate diversification."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, ss 3–5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trustee investment decisions must address suitability and appropriate diversification.","common_trap":"Reducing prudent trust investment to maximisation of headline return.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"be18515906cbfdb9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0304","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Breach of trust — limitation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A beneficiary discovers that a trustee fraudulently converted trust property to the trustee's own use. The trustee argues that the ordinary six-year trust limitation period necessarily bars the beneficiary's recovery claim.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Every breach-of-trust claim is absolutely barred after six years.","B":"Trust claims have no limitation rules of any kind.","C":"Fraud shortens the limitation period to one year.","D":"Statutory exceptions mean that no ordinary limitation period under the relevant provision applies to certain beneficiary actions involving trustee fraud or fraudulent breach of trust, or recovery of trust property or proceeds retained or converted by the trustee.","E":"Limitation depends only on the trust instrument."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 21 of the Limitation Act contains important exceptions to the ordinary limitation regime for specified trust claims, including fraud/fraudulent breach and recovery of trust property or proceeds retained or converted by the trustee.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Certain fraud and trust-property recovery claims fall within statutory exceptions to the ordinary trust limitation period.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Trust claims have no limitation rules of any kind' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Certain fraud and trust-property recovery claims fall within statutory exceptions to the ordinary trust limitation period.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Fraud shortens the limitation period to one year' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Certain fraud and trust-property recovery claims fall within statutory exceptions to the ordinary trust limitation period.","D":"Correct. Section 21 of the Limitation Act contains important exceptions to the ordinary limitation regime for specified trust claims, including fraud/fraudulent breach and recovery of trust property or proceeds retained or converted by the trustee.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Certain fraud and trust-property recovery claims fall within statutory exceptions to the ordinary trust limitation period."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, s 21.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Certain fraud and trust-property recovery claims fall within statutory exceptions to the ordinary trust limitation period.","common_trap":"Applying a blanket six-year period to every beneficiary action against a trustee.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"080c2f6f77fc81af"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0305","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Mortgage — equity of redemption","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A mortgage deed states that the borrower may redeem the property only during a single 24-hour window forty years after a short commercial loan is due, making redemption in substance illusory.\n\nWhich equitable principle is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Parties can always make redemption practically impossible if the deed is signed.","B":"A mortgage permanently transfers all equitable ownership to the lender.","C":"The borrower has no redemption right after executing a legal mortgage.","D":"The rule against perpetuities is the only relevant doctrine.","E":"A mortgage cannot contain a provision that improperly clogs or fetters the mortgagor's equity of redemption."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Equity protects the mortgagor's right to redeem and scrutinises provisions that make redemption illusory or improperly restrict it. The maxim that a mortgage remains redeemable underlies the anti-clog doctrine.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Terms that improperly prevent or fetter redemption can be invalid as clogs on the equity of redemption.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A mortgage permanently transfers all equitable ownership to the lender' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Terms that improperly prevent or fetter redemption can be invalid as clogs on the equity of redemption.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The borrower has no redemption right after executing a legal mortgage' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Terms that improperly prevent or fetter redemption can be invalid as clogs on the equity of redemption.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Terms that improperly prevent or fetter redemption can be invalid as clogs on the equity of redemption.","E":"Correct. Equity protects the mortgagor's right to redeem and scrutinises provisions that make redemption illusory or improperly restrict it. The maxim that a mortgage remains redeemable underlies the anti-clog doctrine."},"primary_legal_authority":"Equitable mortgage principles; Fairclough v Swan Brewery Co Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Terms that improperly prevent or fetter redemption can be invalid as clogs on the equity of redemption.","common_trap":"Treating contractual freedom as permitting the essential redemption right to be destroyed.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9a759b9d8e9e0179"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0306","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — overreaching: payment to sole trustee","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A beneficial interest exists behind a trust of land. A buyer pays the purchase money to the sole registered proprietor, who is the only trustee. No trust corporation is involved.\n\nDoes payment to the sole individual trustee ordinarily overreach the beneficiary's equitable interest?","options":{"A":"No. Overreaching on a sale ordinarily requires payment of the capital money to at least two trustees or a trust corporation.","B":"Yes. Payment to any registered proprietor always overreaches every equitable interest.","C":"Yes, but only because the land is registered.","D":"No, because beneficial interests can never be overreached.","E":"Yes, provided the beneficiary is in actual occupation."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The statutory machinery generally requires capital money to be paid to two trustees or a trust corporation. Payment to one individual trustee does not ordinarily satisfy that requirement.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The statutory machinery generally requires capital money to be paid to two trustees or a trust corporation. Payment to one individual trustee does not ordinarily satisfy that requirement.","B":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. Overreaching detaches qualifying equitable interests from the land when statutory conditions are met, including payment of capital money to two trustees or a trust corporation.","C":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Overreaching detaches qualifying equitable interests from the land when statutory conditions are met, including payment of capital money to two trustees or a trust corporation.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition is too categorical and contradicts the recognised rule or exception engaged by the facts. Overreaching detaches qualifying equitable interests from the land when statutory conditions are met, including payment of capital money to two trustees or a trust corporation.","E":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Overreaching detaches qualifying equitable interests from the land when statutory conditions are met, including payment of capital money to two trustees or a trust corporation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, ss 2 and 27","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Overreaching detaches qualifying equitable interests from the land when statutory conditions are met, including payment of capital money to two trustees or a trust corporation.","common_trap":"Assuming registration or payment to the legal owner alone is enough.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"740c09885f33b05b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0307","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Pre-contract enquiries — TA forms","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"In a routine residential sale, the buyer's solicitor wants standard information from the seller about matters such as boundaries, disputes, notices and practical features of the property.\n\nWhich conveyancing mechanism is commonly used?","options":{"A":"An OS1 search alone.","B":"Standard property information enquiries/forms completed by the seller, supplemented by additional enquiries where appropriate.","C":"A bankruptcy search against the seller alone.","D":"A Crown Court witness statement.","E":"A Part 36 offer."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Residential conveyancing commonly uses standard property information forms to obtain seller-provided information, with the buyer's solicitor raising additional enquiries where the transaction requires them. Search results and inspection remain separate sources of information.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'An OS1 search alone' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Standard seller property-information forms and tailored enquiries form part of pre-contract investigation.","B":"Correct. Residential conveyancing commonly uses standard property information forms to obtain seller-provided information, with the buyer's solicitor raising additional enquiries where the transaction requires them. Search results and inspection remain separate sources of information.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A bankruptcy search against the seller alone' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Standard seller property-information forms and tailored enquiries form part of pre-contract investigation.","D":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. Standard seller property-information forms and tailored enquiries form part of pre-contract investigation.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A Part 36 offer' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Standard seller property-information forms and tailored enquiries form part of pre-contract investigation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law Society conveyancing forms and standard residential conveyancing practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Standard seller property-information forms and tailored enquiries form part of pre-contract investigation.","common_trap":"Treating Land Registry priority searches as substitutes for factual enquiries of the seller.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4f09fa5738f56fbe"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0308","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Completion — late completion","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A residential sale contract incorporates the Standard Conditions of Sale. The contractual completion date passes because the buyer fails to provide completion monies. The seller is ready, willing and able to complete.\n\nWhich contractual mechanism may the seller ordinarily consider to make time of the essence and require completion within the contractual notice period?","options":{"A":"Applying for default judgment under CPR Part 12 as the conveyancing mechanism.","B":"Automatically treating the contract as void without notice in every case.","C":"Serving a notice to complete in accordance with the contract.","D":"Registering the buyer as proprietor.","E":"Serving a section 21 housing notice."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Under the Standard Conditions, time is not ordinarily of the essence for completion until a valid notice to complete is served. The notice then engages the contractual timetable and consequences.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This introduces a court-order requirement that is not part of the rule being applied. A notice to complete can make time of the essence under the standard contractual machinery.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A notice to complete can make time of the essence under the standard contractual machinery.","C":"Correct. Under the Standard Conditions, time is not ordinarily of the essence for completion until a valid notice to complete is served. The notice then engages the contractual timetable and consequences.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Registering the buyer as proprietor' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A notice to complete can make time of the essence under the standard contractual machinery.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Serving a section 21 housing notice' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A notice to complete can make time of the essence under the standard contractual machinery."},"primary_legal_authority":"Standard Conditions of Sale, completion and notice-to-complete provisions.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A notice to complete can make time of the essence under the standard contractual machinery.","common_trap":"Assuming any delay on the completion date automatically ends the contract without use of the contractual mechanism.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3e0196c16a88efc2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0309","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Gross negligence manslaughter","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A professional owes the victim a duty of care, commits a very serious breach causing death, and the circumstances of the breach are alleged to be so exceptionally bad as to justify criminal liability.\n\nWhich offence is potentially engaged?","options":{"A":"Murder automatically, even without the necessary mens rea.","B":"Theft.","C":"Robbery.","D":"Gross negligence manslaughter, if the jury is satisfied that the breach causing death was gross in the criminal sense.","E":"Fraud by false representation."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Gross negligence manslaughter requires, among other elements, a duty of care, breach, causation of death and negligence sufficiently gross to justify criminal punishment. The assessment of grossness is for the jury under the legal directions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A causative breach of duty that is truly gross can amount to gross negligence manslaughter.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Theft' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A causative breach of duty that is truly gross can amount to gross negligence manslaughter.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Robbery' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A causative breach of duty that is truly gross can amount to gross negligence manslaughter.","D":"Correct. Gross negligence manslaughter requires, among other elements, a duty of care, breach, causation of death and negligence sufficiently gross to justify criminal punishment. The assessment of grossness is for the jury under the legal directions.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Fraud by false representation' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A causative breach of duty that is truly gross can amount to gross negligence manslaughter."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Adomako [1995] 1 AC 171.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A causative breach of duty that is truly gross can amount to gross negligence manslaughter.","common_trap":"Treating ordinary civil negligence causing death as automatically sufficient for criminal manslaughter.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1d6014b9278585bc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0310","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Sentencing — totality","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant is being sentenced at the same hearing for several offences. Individual sentences that are each proportionate in isolation would, if simply added together, produce an overall sentence disproportionate to the offending as a whole.\n\nWhich sentencing principle is directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Every sentence must always run consecutively.","B":"Every sentence must always run concurrently.","C":"The court must ignore offences other than the most serious one.","D":"Part 36 determines the overall sentence.","E":"The totality principle, requiring the overall sentence to be just and proportionate to the offending as a whole."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"When sentencing for multiple offences, the court must consider totality so that the aggregate sentence is just and proportionate, deciding concurrent or consecutive structure in accordance with the applicable principles.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Multiple-offence sentencing must produce a just and proportionate overall sentence.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Multiple-offence sentencing must produce a just and proportionate overall sentence.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Multiple-offence sentencing must produce a just and proportionate overall sentence.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Part 36 determines the overall sentence' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Multiple-offence sentencing must produce a just and proportionate overall sentence.","E":"Correct. When sentencing for multiple offences, the court must consider totality so that the aggregate sentence is just and proportionate, deciding concurrent or consecutive structure in accordance with the applicable principles."},"primary_legal_authority":"Sentencing Code; Sentencing Council, Totality guideline.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Multiple-offence sentencing must produce a just and proportionate overall sentence.","common_trap":"Adding individually appropriate sentences mechanically without assessing the aggregate punishment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"23e44c59450809ea"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0311","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — written resolutions","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company proposes an ordinary resolution by the statutory written-resolution procedure. Members holding 55% of the total voting rights of eligible members signify agreement before the resolution lapses. The articles do not require a higher threshold.\n\nIs the ordinary written resolution passed?","options":{"A":"Yes. An ordinary written resolution is passed by a simple majority of the total voting rights of eligible members.","B":"No. Every written resolution requires 75%.","C":"No. Written resolutions require unanimity.","D":"Yes, but only if every director also signs.","E":"No. Private companies cannot use written resolutions."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An ordinary written resolution of a private company is passed when members representing a simple majority of the total voting rights of eligible members signify agreement, subject to the statutory procedure.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An ordinary written resolution of a private company is passed when members representing a simple majority of the total voting rights of eligible members signify agreement, subject to the statutory procedure.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An ordinary written resolution ordinarily requires a simple majority of eligible members' total voting rights.","C":"Incorrect. This imposes a formality that the governing rule does not require in this situation. An ordinary written resolution ordinarily requires a simple majority of eligible members' total voting rights.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. An ordinary written resolution ordinarily requires a simple majority of eligible members' total voting rights.","E":"Incorrect. This imposes a formality that the governing rule does not require in this situation. An ordinary written resolution ordinarily requires a simple majority of eligible members' total voting rights."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 288–296.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An ordinary written resolution ordinarily requires a simple majority of eligible members' total voting rights.","common_trap":"Importing the 75% special-resolution threshold or an unanimity requirement into an ordinary written resolution.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1f884fc4a3cf9b72"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0312","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — directors' duty of care","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A director with extensive professional expertise approves a transaction without reading readily available financial information that a reasonably diligent director would have reviewed. The director argues that personal inattention cannot breach a statutory duty unless dishonesty is proved.\n\nWhich duty is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Only the duty to avoid conflicts of interest.","B":"The duty to exercise reasonable care, skill and diligence, assessed by the statutory objective and subjective standards.","C":"Only the duty to declare interests in existing transactions.","D":"No duty can be breached without dishonesty.","E":"The duty is owed exclusively to individual shareholders."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 174 combines an objective minimum standard with the knowledge, skill and experience actually possessed by the director. Dishonesty is not an element of this duty.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Directors must meet the section 174 objective standard and any higher subjective standard arising from their actual attributes.","B":"Correct. Section 174 combines an objective minimum standard with the knowledge, skill and experience actually possessed by the director. Dishonesty is not an element of this duty.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Directors must meet the section 174 objective standard and any higher subjective standard arising from their actual attributes.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No duty can be breached without dishonesty' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Directors must meet the section 174 objective standard and any higher subjective standard arising from their actual attributes.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The duty is owed exclusively to individual shareholders' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Directors must meet the section 174 objective standard and any higher subjective standard arising from their actual attributes."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 174.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Directors must meet the section 174 objective standard and any higher subjective standard arising from their actual attributes.","common_trap":"Assuming negligence-based directors' duties require dishonesty or fraud.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e4becda110ed8fd1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0313","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — substantial property transactions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company proposes to acquire a substantial non-cash asset from one of its directors. The transaction falls within the statutory substantial-property-transaction regime and no exception applies. The board intends to complete without member approval.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Board approval alone is always sufficient for transactions with directors.","B":"Every transaction with a director is automatically void.","C":"The statutory regime generally requires member approval before the company enters the arrangement, subject to its detailed conditions and exceptions.","D":"Only Companies House can approve the transaction.","E":"Member approval is relevant only to public companies."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Companies Act regulates substantial non-cash asset transactions involving directors and connected persons and ordinarily requires approval by resolution of members where the statutory conditions are met.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Qualifying substantial property transactions with directors generally require member approval.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Qualifying substantial property transactions with directors generally require member approval.","C":"Correct. The Companies Act regulates substantial non-cash asset transactions involving directors and connected persons and ordinarily requires approval by resolution of members where the statutory conditions are met.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Qualifying substantial property transactions with directors generally require member approval.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Qualifying substantial property transactions with directors generally require member approval."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 190–196.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Qualifying substantial property transactions with directors generally require member approval.","common_trap":"Treating ordinary board authority as displacing statutory controls on significant director-related transactions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1de797c76722eb73"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0314","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Tax — corporation tax chargeable gains","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A UK company sells a chargeable capital asset at a gain in the course of its business. Its finance director assumes companies pay capital gains tax in exactly the same way as individuals.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Companies never pay tax on gains from capital assets.","B":"Every company gain is subject only to income tax.","C":"Capital gains are taxed only when distributed as dividends.","D":"Companies generally bring chargeable gains into the corporation-tax regime rather than paying capital gains tax as individuals do.","E":"The gain is automatically exempt because the seller is incorporated."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Companies within the corporation-tax regime can be charged to corporation tax on chargeable gains. The calculation and any reliefs depend on the statutory rules and facts.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Corporate chargeable gains are generally dealt with within corporation tax.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Corporate chargeable gains are generally dealt with within corporation tax.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Corporate chargeable gains are generally dealt with within corporation tax.","D":"Correct. Companies within the corporation-tax regime can be charged to corporation tax on chargeable gains. The calculation and any reliefs depend on the statutory rules and facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Corporate chargeable gains are generally dealt with within corporation tax."},"primary_legal_authority":"Taxation of Chargeable Gains Act 1992; Corporation Tax Act framework; SRA FLK1 taxation specification.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Corporate chargeable gains are generally dealt with within corporation tax.","common_trap":"Applying the individual capital-gains-tax charging structure directly to companies.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5613165ff861f882"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0315","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Statements of case — defence deadline after acknowledgment","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant is served with particulars of claim and files a timely acknowledgment of service indicating an intention to defend the whole claim. No extension has been agreed or ordered.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect of the acknowledgment on the time for filing the defence?","options":{"A":"It ordinarily extends the defence deadline from 14 days after service of the particulars of claim to 28 days after service of the particulars of claim.","B":"It removes any deadline for filing a defence.","C":"It requires the defence to be filed on the same day as the acknowledgment.","D":"It automatically gives the defendant 56 days.","E":"It prevents the claimant from ever seeking default judgment."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A timely acknowledgment of service ordinarily extends the CPR 15.4 defence period to 28 days after service of the particulars of claim.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A timely acknowledgment of service ordinarily extends the CPR 15.4 defence period to 28 days after service of the particulars of claim.","B":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The normal defence period is 14 days after service of particulars, or 28 days where the defendant files an acknowledgment of service under Part 10.","C":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The normal defence period is 14 days after service of particulars, or 28 days where the defendant files an acknowledgment of service under Part 10.","D":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. The normal defence period is 14 days after service of particulars, or 28 days where the defendant files an acknowledgment of service under Part 10.","E":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The normal defence period is 14 days after service of particulars, or 28 days where the defendant files an acknowledgment of service under Part 10."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 10.3 and 15.4","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The normal defence period is 14 days after service of particulars, or 28 days where the defendant files an acknowledgment of service under Part 10.","common_trap":"Confusing the acknowledgment deadline with the resulting defence deadline.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9cd7087e0ea32deb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0316","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Interim remedies — freezing injunction: full and frank disclosure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant seeks a freezing injunction without notice because advance warning may lead to dissipation of assets. The claimant knows of a significant fact that supports the defendant's position.\n\nWhat should the claimant do with that adverse fact at the without-notice hearing?","options":{"A":"Disclose it to the court as part of the duty of full and frank disclosure of material matters.","B":"Suppress it because only facts supporting the application are relevant.","C":"Disclose it only after the injunction has expired.","D":"Tell the defendant privately but not the judge.","E":"Ignore it unless the judge asks the precise question."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A party seeking exceptional relief without notice owes a stringent duty to disclose material facts, including material adverse matters, because the absent respondent cannot present them.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A party seeking exceptional relief without notice owes a stringent duty to disclose material facts, including material adverse matters, because the absent respondent cannot present them.","B":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Without-notice interim injunction applications require full and frank disclosure of all material facts; breach can justify discharge of the order.","C":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Without-notice interim injunction applications require full and frank disclosure of all material facts; breach can justify discharge of the order.","D":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Without-notice interim injunction applications require full and frank disclosure of all material facts; breach can justify discharge of the order.","E":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Without-notice interim injunction applications require full and frank disclosure of all material facts; breach can justify discharge of the order."},"primary_legal_authority":"CPR Part 25 and Practice Direction 25A; Brink's-Mat Ltd v Elcombe [1988] 1 WLR 1350","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Without-notice interim injunction applications require full and frank disclosure of all material facts; breach can justify discharge of the order.","common_trap":"Treating the hearing as ordinary adversarial advocacy where only favourable facts need be advanced.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4cbc1847ada94bf6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0317","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Part 36 — claimant beats own offer: additional amount cap","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant makes a valid Part 36 offer which the defendant does not accept. At trial the claimant obtains a money judgment at least as advantageous as the offer. The court finds it would not be unjust to apply the ordinary Part 36 consequences.\n\nWhich feature can form part of the enhanced consequences in the claimant's favour?","options":{"A":"An additional amount calculated under CPR 36.17, subject to the rule's monetary cap, alongside the other applicable enhanced consequences.","B":"Automatic doubling of the entire damages award with no cap.","C":"Automatic imprisonment of the defendant.","D":"Cancellation of all interest otherwise payable.","E":"A rule that the claimant must pay the defendant's post-offer costs."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where a claimant matches or beats its own Part 36 offer, CPR 36.17 can provide enhanced interest, indemnity costs and an additional amount calculated under the rule, subject to its cap.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where a claimant matches or beats its own Part 36 offer, CPR 36.17 can provide enhanced interest, indemnity costs and an additional amount calculated under the rule, subject to its cap.","B":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The claimant-beats-own-offer consequences are specified by CPR 36.17(4), unless unjust; the additional amount is formula-based and capped.","C":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The claimant-beats-own-offer consequences are specified by CPR 36.17(4), unless unjust; the additional amount is formula-based and capped.","D":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The claimant-beats-own-offer consequences are specified by CPR 36.17(4), unless unjust; the additional amount is formula-based and capped.","E":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The claimant-beats-own-offer consequences are specified by CPR 36.17(4), unless unjust; the additional amount is formula-based and capped."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 36.17","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The claimant-beats-own-offer consequences are specified by CPR 36.17(4), unless unjust; the additional amount is formula-based and capped.","common_trap":"Remembering that Part 36 is punitive in effect but inventing an uncapped doubling rule.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3a162a5fa665e088"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0318","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Client capacity and instructions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An adult client appears unable to understand, retain and weigh information needed for a particular legal decision. The solicitor's assistant says that an eccentric choice alone proves lack of capacity and permits the firm to ignore the client's wishes.\n\nWhich is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Any unusual decision proves incapacity.","B":"Solicitors may assume incapacity whenever relatives disagree with the client.","C":"Capacity is decision-specific and must be assessed under the applicable legal framework; an unwise decision alone does not establish lack of capacity.","D":"Capacity is an all-or-nothing status that never varies by decision or time.","E":"A solicitor may substitute personal preferences without assessment."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Mental Capacity Act starts from a presumption of capacity and makes capacity decision-specific. A person is not to be treated as unable to decide merely because the decision is unwise.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Any unusual decision proves incapacity' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Capacity is presumed, decision-specific, and not disproved merely by an unwise choice.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Capacity is presumed, decision-specific, and not disproved merely by an unwise choice.","C":"Correct. The Mental Capacity Act starts from a presumption of capacity and makes capacity decision-specific. A person is not to be treated as unable to decide merely because the decision is unwise.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Capacity is presumed, decision-specific, and not disproved merely by an unwise choice.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A solicitor may substitute personal preferences without assessment' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Capacity is presumed, decision-specific, and not disproved merely by an unwise choice."},"primary_legal_authority":"Mental Capacity Act 2005, ss 1–3; SRA client-care duties.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Capacity is presumed, decision-specific, and not disproved merely by an unwise choice.","common_trap":"Equating disagreement, eccentricity or an unwise decision with legal incapacity.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e3ea7a9ae4e3679b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0319","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Legal professional privilege — waiver","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client voluntarily discloses privileged legal advice to an opponent in litigation without preserving confidentiality and then seeks to prevent the opponent relying on the disclosed communication solely by asserting that it was originally privileged.\n\nWhich issue is central?","options":{"A":"Privilege can never be waived by a client.","B":"Only solicitors, never clients, can waive privilege.","C":"Once privileged, a document remains confidential against everyone regardless of deliberate disclosure.","D":"Whether privilege has been waived by the client's voluntary disclosure, with the scope of any waiver determined under the applicable principles.","E":"Privilege is identical to without-prejudice protection."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Legal professional privilege belongs to the client and can be waived. Voluntary disclosure inconsistent with maintaining confidentiality can amount to waiver, though the extent of waiver requires careful analysis.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The client can waive legal professional privilege through conduct inconsistent with maintaining it.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. The client can waive legal professional privilege through conduct inconsistent with maintaining it.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Once privileged, a document remains confidential against everyone regardless of deliberate disclosure' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The client can waive legal professional privilege through conduct inconsistent with maintaining it.","D":"Correct. Legal professional privilege belongs to the client and can be waived. Voluntary disclosure inconsistent with maintaining confidentiality can amount to waiver, though the extent of waiver requires careful analysis.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Privilege is identical to without-prejudice protection' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The client can waive legal professional privilege through conduct inconsistent with maintaining it."},"primary_legal_authority":"Legal professional privilege and waiver principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The client can waive legal professional privilege through conduct inconsistent with maintaining it.","common_trap":"Treating privilege as an immutable property of a document rather than a client right capable of waiver.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"474b9b2738e0b4c1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0320","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Interpretation — objective meaning","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A detailed commercial contract contains disputed wording. One party relies on an undisclosed subjective intention that was never communicated during contracting.\n\nHow will the court ordinarily approach interpretation?","options":{"A":"It asks only what one party privately intended.","B":"It automatically rewrites the bargain to produce the fairest result.","C":"Commercial context is always inadmissible.","D":"The court ignores the contract's words whenever one party later regrets them.","E":"It seeks the objective meaning of the language in its documentary, factual and commercial context rather than enforcing an uncommunicated subjective intention."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Modern contractual interpretation is objective. The court considers the language used in the context available to the parties, balancing textual and contextual indicators without substituting an undisclosed private intention.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contract interpretation ordinarily asks what the language objectively means in context.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contract interpretation ordinarily asks what the language objectively means in context.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contract interpretation ordinarily asks what the language objectively means in context.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Contract interpretation ordinarily asks what the language objectively means in context.","E":"Correct. Modern contractual interpretation is objective. The court considers the language used in the context available to the parties, balancing textual and contextual indicators without substituting an undisclosed private intention."},"primary_legal_authority":"Arnold v Britton; Wood v Capita Insurance Services Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contract interpretation ordinarily asks what the language objectively means in context.","common_trap":"Using a party's uncommunicated subjective intention as the governing meaning.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5cf3d428c56edeee"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0321","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Remedies — specific performance: constant supervision","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant seeks specific performance of a contract requiring a defendant to run a complex loss-making business for several years, involving numerous continuing operational decisions and likely repeated court supervision.\n\nWhich factor most strongly weighs against specific performance?","options":{"A":"The order would require prolonged and detailed court supervision of continuing obligations, making equitable enforcement inappropriate.","B":"Specific performance is automatically available for every contractual breach.","C":"Damages must always be nominal before specific performance can be considered.","D":"Specific performance is a criminal remedy and therefore unavailable in civil proceedings.","E":"The claimant's preference for performance conclusively binds the court."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Specific performance is discretionary. Courts are reluctant to order performance of continuing obligations where enforcement would demand constant supervision and damages provide an adequate or more workable remedy.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Specific performance is discretionary. Courts are reluctant to order performance of continuing obligations where enforcement would demand constant supervision and damages provide an adequate or more workable remedy.","B":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. Specific performance is equitable and discretionary; adequacy of damages, supervision, hardship and other equitable considerations affect availability.","C":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. Specific performance is equitable and discretionary; adequacy of damages, supervision, hardship and other equitable considerations affect availability.","D":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Specific performance is equitable and discretionary; adequacy of damages, supervision, hardship and other equitable considerations affect availability.","E":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Specific performance is equitable and discretionary; adequacy of damages, supervision, hardship and other equitable considerations affect availability."},"primary_legal_authority":"Co-operative Insurance Society Ltd v Argyll Stores (Holdings) Ltd [1998] AC 1","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Specific performance is equitable and discretionary; adequacy of damages, supervision, hardship and other equitable considerations affect availability.","common_trap":"Treating specific performance as an entitlement once breach is established.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"86a8e9fa3cc93a74"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0322","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Promissory estoppel","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A creditor clearly promises to accept reduced performance of an existing contractual right for a period. The debtor relies on the promise, and it would be inequitable for the creditor to insist retrospectively on the strict right for that period. The debtor seeks to use the promise defensively, not to create a wholly new cause of action.\n\nWhich doctrine is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Proprietary estoppel only.","B":"Promissory estoppel.","C":"The postal rule.","D":"Frustration.","E":"Res ipsa loquitur."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Promissory estoppel can in appropriate circumstances prevent a party from insisting on strict contractual rights where a clear promise was relied upon and it would be inequitable to resile, while traditionally operating as a shield rather than a sword.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Proprietary estoppel only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Promissory estoppel may suspend or qualify enforcement of existing rights where its equitable requirements are met.","B":"Correct. Promissory estoppel can in appropriate circumstances prevent a party from insisting on strict contractual rights where a clear promise was relied upon and it would be inequitable to resile, while traditionally operating as a shield rather than a sword.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The postal rule' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Promissory estoppel may suspend or qualify enforcement of existing rights where its equitable requirements are met.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Frustration' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Promissory estoppel may suspend or qualify enforcement of existing rights where its equitable requirements are met.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Res ipsa loquitur' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Promissory estoppel may suspend or qualify enforcement of existing rights where its equitable requirements are met."},"primary_legal_authority":"Central London Property Trust Ltd v High Trees House Ltd; Combe v Combe.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Promissory estoppel may suspend or qualify enforcement of existing rights where its equitable requirements are met.","common_trap":"Treating promissory estoppel as a general substitute for consideration creating any new cause of action.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"302a9f6f7245f0d0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0323","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — res ipsa loquitur","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant is injured by an occurrence that would not ordinarily happen without negligence. The thing causing the harm was under the defendant's management, and the claimant cannot identify the precise negligent act.\n\nWhich evidential doctrine may assist the claimant, depending on the facts?","options":{"A":"Volenti non fit injuria.","B":"The rule in Tulk v Moxhay.","C":"Res ipsa loquitur.","D":"Promissory estoppel.","E":"The postal rule."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Res ipsa loquitur can permit an inference of negligence where the circumstances themselves point to negligence and the relevant control and evidential conditions are satisfied. It is an evidential doctrine, not a separate tort.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Volenti non fit injuria' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Res ipsa can support an inference of negligence from the nature and circumstances of an accident.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The rule in Tulk v Moxhay' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Res ipsa can support an inference of negligence from the nature and circumstances of an accident.","C":"Correct. Res ipsa loquitur can permit an inference of negligence where the circumstances themselves point to negligence and the relevant control and evidential conditions are satisfied. It is an evidential doctrine, not a separate tort.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Promissory estoppel' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Res ipsa can support an inference of negligence from the nature and circumstances of an accident.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The postal rule' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Res ipsa can support an inference of negligence from the nature and circumstances of an accident."},"primary_legal_authority":"Scott v London and St Katherine Docks Co; res ipsa loquitur principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Res ipsa can support an inference of negligence from the nature and circumstances of an accident.","common_trap":"Treating inability to identify the precise negligent act as necessarily fatal to a negligence claim.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1b307f241ccebec9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0324","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defences — volenti","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant fully understands the nature and extent of a particular legal risk and freely agrees to run that risk. The defendant argues that this consent defeats the relevant tort claim.\n\nWhich defence is potentially engaged?","options":{"A":"Contributory negligence necessarily bars the entire claim.","B":"Knowledge of danger always proves consent as a matter of law.","C":"Volenti applies only to contract claims.","D":"Volenti non fit injuria, requiring genuine and voluntary agreement to the relevant risk, not merely knowledge that danger exists.","E":"The defence requires the claimant to have caused the defendant's negligence."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Volenti is a complete defence where the claimant freely and voluntarily agreed to accept the relevant risk. Mere awareness of danger is not necessarily equivalent to consent.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Contributory negligence necessarily bars the entire claim' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Volenti requires voluntary acceptance of the relevant risk, not mere knowledge of danger.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Volenti requires voluntary acceptance of the relevant risk, not mere knowledge of danger.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Volenti requires voluntary acceptance of the relevant risk, not mere knowledge of danger.","D":"Correct. Volenti is a complete defence where the claimant freely and voluntarily agreed to accept the relevant risk. Mere awareness of danger is not necessarily equivalent to consent.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The defence requires the claimant to have caused the defendant's negligence' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Volenti requires voluntary acceptance of the relevant risk, not mere knowledge of danger."},"primary_legal_authority":"Morris v Murray; volenti principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Volenti requires voluntary acceptance of the relevant risk, not mere knowledge of danger.","common_trap":"Equating awareness of a risk with legally effective consent to absolve the defendant.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"256a11eb90200953"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0325","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Trespass to land","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person intentionally walks onto privately owned land without the occupier's permission or other lawful justification. No physical damage is caused.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"No tort exists unless the claimant proves financial loss.","B":"Trespass requires permanent occupation for twelve years.","C":"Only negligent entry can constitute trespass.","D":"Trespass to land is exclusively a criminal offence.","E":"Trespass to land can be actionable without proof of physical damage because the unjustified direct entry itself can constitute the tort."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Trespass to land protects possession against unjustified direct interference. It is actionable per se, so proof of consequential damage is not essential.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'No tort exists unless the claimant proves financial loss' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Unauthorised direct entry onto land in another's possession can be actionable per se.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Trespass requires permanent occupation for twelve years' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Unauthorised direct entry onto land in another's possession can be actionable per se.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Unauthorised direct entry onto land in another's possession can be actionable per se.","D":"Incorrect. This confuses the civil/regulatory consequence tested here with a criminal consequence. Unauthorised direct entry onto land in another's possession can be actionable per se.","E":"Correct. Trespass to land protects possession against unjustified direct interference. It is actionable per se, so proof of consequential damage is not essential."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law trespass to land principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Unauthorised direct entry onto land in another's possession can be actionable per se.","common_trap":"Assuming civil trespass requires proof of physical damage or adverse possession.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9ef6cb263b0ca2a1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0326","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Statutory interpretation — literal text and context","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statutory phrase is disputed. One lawyer proposes reading a single word in complete isolation from the rest of the provision, the statute's structure and its legislative context.\n\nWhich statement best reflects modern interpretation?","options":{"A":"Statutory language is interpreted in its context and in light of the statute as a whole; interpretation is not ordinarily a dictionary exercise conducted word by word in isolation.","B":"Courts must always ignore statutory context.","C":"The subjective intention of an individual MP conclusively determines meaning.","D":"Courts may disregard enacted words whenever another policy seems preferable.","E":"Every ambiguity is resolved in favour of the executive."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Modern statutory interpretation starts from the enacted text but reads it in context, including the provision and statutory scheme as a whole and legitimate contextual materials where applicable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Modern statutory interpretation starts from the enacted text but reads it in context, including the provision and statutory scheme as a whole and legitimate contextual materials where applicable.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Statutory text is interpreted contextually as part of the legislative scheme.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The subjective intention of an individual MP conclusively determines meaning' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Statutory text is interpreted contextually as part of the legislative scheme.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Statutory text is interpreted contextually as part of the legislative scheme.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Statutory text is interpreted contextually as part of the legislative scheme."},"primary_legal_authority":"R (Project for the Registration of Children as British Citizens) v Secretary of State for the Home Department [2022] UKSC 3; interpretive principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Statutory text is interpreted contextually as part of the legislative scheme.","common_trap":"Treating statutory interpretation as isolated dictionary analysis detached from enacted context.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"59ad11dd23b42288"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0327","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — irrationality","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A public authority lawfully has discretion to choose among several outcomes. It selects an outcome alleged to be so unreasonable that no reasonable authority acting within the legal framework could have reached it.\n\nWhich judicial-review ground is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Procedural legitimate expectation only.","B":"Irrationality or Wednesbury unreasonableness.","C":"Private nuisance.","D":"Breach of contract automatically.","E":"Adverse possession."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Irrationality review addresses decisions outside the range lawfully open to a reasonable decision-maker, while modern intensity of review can depend on context, including rights and statutory setting.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Procedural legitimate expectation only' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A decision outside the legally permissible range of reasonable responses can be challenged for irrationality.","B":"Correct. Irrationality review addresses decisions outside the range lawfully open to a reasonable decision-maker, while modern intensity of review can depend on context, including rights and statutory setting.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Private nuisance' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A decision outside the legally permissible range of reasonable responses can be challenged for irrationality.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. A decision outside the legally permissible range of reasonable responses can be challenged for irrationality.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Adverse possession' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. A decision outside the legally permissible range of reasonable responses can be challenged for irrationality."},"primary_legal_authority":"Associated Provincial Picture Houses Ltd v Wednesbury Corporation; public-law irrationality principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A decision outside the legally permissible range of reasonable responses can be challenged for irrationality.","common_trap":"Treating judicial review as a general merits appeal allowing the court simply to substitute its preferred outcome.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fe2965e40f1454c6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0328","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — public authorities","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A local authority, plainly a public authority for Human Rights Act purposes, acts in a way incompatible with a Convention right while performing a public function. No primary-legislation defence under the Act applies.\n\nWhich provision is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Section 4 automatically invalidates every incompatible act.","B":"The Companies Act 2006.","C":"Section 6 of the Human Rights Act, which generally makes it unlawful for a public authority to act incompatibly with a Convention right.","D":"The Partnership Act 1890.","E":"The Human Rights Act applies only to central government ministers."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 6 generally makes it unlawful for public authorities to act incompatibly with Convention rights, subject to the Act's qualifications including circumstances compelled by primary legislation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. HRA section 6 directly regulates incompatible acts of public authorities.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Companies Act 2006' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. HRA section 6 directly regulates incompatible acts of public authorities.","C":"Correct. Section 6 generally makes it unlawful for public authorities to act incompatibly with Convention rights, subject to the Act's qualifications including circumstances compelled by primary legislation.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Partnership Act 1890' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. HRA section 6 directly regulates incompatible acts of public authorities.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. HRA section 6 directly regulates incompatible acts of public authorities."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 6.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"HRA section 6 directly regulates incompatible acts of public authorities.","common_trap":"Confusing the duty on public authorities with the separate declaration-of-incompatibility mechanism concerning legislation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"81a2a0ff88fd04dc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0329","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — incorporation by reference","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A duly executed will refers to a separate written list as an existing document and describes it sufficiently to identify it. The list was already in existence when the will was executed. The issue is whether its terms can form part of the testamentary disposition despite the list itself not having been executed as a will.\n\nWhich doctrine is potentially relevant?","options":{"A":"Ademption.","B":"Overreaching.","C":"Promissory estoppel.","D":"Incorporation by reference, if the document and reference satisfy the applicable requirements.","E":"Part 36."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A document not itself executed as a will may in appropriate circumstances be incorporated if it existed when the will was executed and the will refers to it as an existing document with sufficient identification.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Ademption' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An existing sufficiently identified document can potentially be incorporated into a will by reference.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Overreaching' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An existing sufficiently identified document can potentially be incorporated into a will by reference.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Promissory estoppel' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An existing sufficiently identified document can potentially be incorporated into a will by reference.","D":"Correct. A document not itself executed as a will may in appropriate circumstances be incorporated if it existed when the will was executed and the will refers to it as an existing document with sufficient identification.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Part 36' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An existing sufficiently identified document can potentially be incorporated into a will by reference."},"primary_legal_authority":"Incorporation-by-reference principles under wills law.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An existing sufficiently identified document can potentially be incorporated into a will by reference.","common_trap":"Assuming every unattested external document is automatically excluded regardless of the will's reference to it.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e0fccb56d6dc4b19"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0330","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Administration — assent","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Personal representatives have completed the necessary administration steps concerning a registered property specifically devised to a beneficiary and are ready to vest the legal estate in that beneficiary.\n\nWhich estate-administration instrument is commonly used to vest the property in the beneficiary?","options":{"A":"A Part 36 offer.","B":"A codicil executed by the deceased after death.","C":"A bankruptcy petition.","D":"A declaration of incompatibility.","E":"An assent by the personal representatives."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Personal representatives can vest estate property in the person entitled by executing an assent. Registered land then requires the appropriate registration steps.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A Part 36 offer' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An assent is the personal representatives' instrument for vesting estate property in a beneficiary.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A codicil executed by the deceased after death' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An assent is the personal representatives' instrument for vesting estate property in a beneficiary.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A bankruptcy petition' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An assent is the personal representatives' instrument for vesting estate property in a beneficiary.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A declaration of incompatibility' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. An assent is the personal representatives' instrument for vesting estate property in a beneficiary.","E":"Correct. Personal representatives can vest estate property in the person entitled by executing an assent. Registered land then requires the appropriate registration steps."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925, s 36; Land Registration Rules and probate conveyancing practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An assent is the personal representatives' instrument for vesting estate property in a beneficiary.","common_trap":"Confusing distribution by assent with a sale transfer to a purchaser.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b65c3a0e37f1a329"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0331","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"IHT — nil-rate band mechanics","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An estate includes chargeable transfers for inheritance-tax purposes. A trainee says the entire estate is automatically taxed at the death rate from the first pound because IHT has no threshold mechanism.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"The IHT framework includes a nil-rate band and potentially other reliefs or exemptions; where the SQE requires application of monetary values, the relevant figures are supplied.","B":"There is no nil-rate band in inheritance tax.","C":"Every estate pays the same fixed amount of tax.","D":"Only lifetime transfers can use a nil-rate band.","E":"The nil-rate band is an SDLT concept."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"IHT calculations operate within a statutory framework including the nil-rate band and relevant exemptions and reliefs. The updated SQE specification states that monetary values are provided when candidates must apply rates, thresholds, exemptions or reliefs.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. IHT calculations operate within a statutory framework including the nil-rate band and relevant exemptions and reliefs. The updated SQE specification states that monetary values are provided when candidates must apply rates, thresholds, exemptions or reliefs.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'There is no nil-rate band in inheritance tax' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. IHT liability is calculated using the statutory threshold, exemption and relief framework rather than taxing every pound identically.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. IHT liability is calculated using the statutory threshold, exemption and relief framework rather than taxing every pound identically.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. IHT liability is calculated using the statutory threshold, exemption and relief framework rather than taxing every pound identically.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The nil-rate band is an SDLT concept' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. IHT liability is calculated using the statutory threshold, exemption and relief framework rather than taxing every pound identically."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984; SRA SQE1 Assessment Specification applicable from 1 September 2026.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"IHT liability is calculated using the statutory threshold, exemption and relief framework rather than taxing every pound identically.","common_trap":"Assuming all estate value is charged at the headline death rate without applying the statutory framework.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0dff5e45d8f16070"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0332","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Bank reconciliation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm keeps client money. Its finance manager proposes reconciling the client-account bank statements with the client cash account only once a year.\n\nWhich is the best regulatory position?","options":{"A":"Annual reconciliation is always sufficient.","B":"The firm must complete client-account reconciliations at least every five weeks in accordance with the Accounts Rules and promptly investigate and resolve differences.","C":"Reconciliation is optional if the firm's bookkeeping software is reputable.","D":"Only the firm's business account requires reconciliation.","E":"Reconciliation is required only when a client complains."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The SRA Accounts Rules require reconciliation of client-account bank statements against the client cash account and client ledger balances at least every five weeks, with appropriate records and corrective action.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client accounts require regular reconciliation at least every five weeks.","B":"Correct. The SRA Accounts Rules require reconciliation of client-account bank statements against the client cash account and client ledger balances at least every five weeks, with appropriate records and corrective action.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Reconciliation is optional if the firm's bookkeeping software is reputable' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Client accounts require regular reconciliation at least every five weeks.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client accounts require regular reconciliation at least every five weeks.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Client accounts require regular reconciliation at least every five weeks."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 8.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client accounts require regular reconciliation at least every five weeks.","common_trap":"Treating reconciliation as an annual accounting exercise rather than an ongoing safeguard for client money.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cf6fe5ce479b8c4b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0333","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Resulting trusts — failure of express trust","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor transfers property to trustees on an express trust. The declared trust fails completely and the instrument does not validly dispose of the beneficial interest elsewhere.\n\nWhich equitable response may ordinarily arise?","options":{"A":"The trustees necessarily take beneficially.","B":"The property automatically belongs to the Crown in every case.","C":"A resulting trust in favour of the settlor or the settlor's estate, depending on the circumstances.","D":"The failed trust becomes a charitable trust automatically.","E":"Legal title automatically disappears."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Where an express trust fails and the beneficial interest has not otherwise been effectively disposed of, equity may treat the property as resulting back to the settlor or estate.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The trustees necessarily take beneficially' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Failure to dispose of the beneficial interest can generate a resulting trust.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Failure to dispose of the beneficial interest can generate a resulting trust.","C":"Correct. Where an express trust fails and the beneficial interest has not otherwise been effectively disposed of, equity may treat the property as resulting back to the settlor or estate.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Failure to dispose of the beneficial interest can generate a resulting trust.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Failure to dispose of the beneficial interest can generate a resulting trust."},"primary_legal_authority":"Resulting-trust principles; Vandervell v IRC.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Failure to dispose of the beneficial interest can generate a resulting trust.","common_trap":"Assuming trustees acquire beneficial ownership merely because the express trust fails.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"16b648d7ccd28aeb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0334","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — delegation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Trustees want to delegate investment functions to a professional asset manager. They assume that appointment of a professional ends all trustee responsibility for selection, terms and review.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the statutory regime?","options":{"A":"Trustees can never delegate investment functions.","B":"Once an agent is appointed, trustees have no further responsibilities.","C":"Delegation transfers the trusteeship itself to the agent.","D":"Trustees may delegate permitted functions subject to statutory safeguards and retain duties concerning matters such as appointment, terms and review of the agent.","E":"Only beneficiaries may appoint investment managers."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Trustee Act permits delegation of functions, including asset-management functions, subject to requirements concerning the agent, policy statements where applicable, and continuing review duties.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Permitted delegation does not eliminate trustees' statutory responsibilities for proper appointment and supervision/review.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Once an agent is appointed, trustees have no further responsibilities' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Permitted delegation does not eliminate trustees' statutory responsibilities for proper appointment and supervision/review.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Delegation transfers the trusteeship itself to the agent' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Permitted delegation does not eliminate trustees' statutory responsibilities for proper appointment and supervision/review.","D":"Correct. The Trustee Act permits delegation of functions, including asset-management functions, subject to requirements concerning the agent, policy statements where applicable, and continuing review duties.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Permitted delegation does not eliminate trustees' statutory responsibilities for proper appointment and supervision/review."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, Part IV.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Permitted delegation does not eliminate trustees' statutory responsibilities for proper appointment and supervision/review.","common_trap":"Treating delegation as an absolute transfer of fiduciary responsibility.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a6abce919bf79fc7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0335","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Leases — covenants after assignment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A tenant under a modern commercial lease assigns the lease to an assignee. The landlord wants to know whether lease covenants can have effect between landlord and assignee after assignment under the statutory regime for new tenancies.\n\nWhich statutory framework is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"The Wills Act 1837.","B":"The Defamation Act 2013.","C":"The Partnership Act 1890.","D":"The Human Rights Act 1998.","E":"The Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995, under which the benefit and burden of qualifying landlord and tenant covenants generally pass with assignment in accordance with the Act."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"For tenancies within its regime, the 1995 Act provides for landlord and tenant covenants to pass on assignment and generally releases outgoing tenants from future liability, subject to important qualifications such as authorised guarantee agreements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Wills Act 1837' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The 1995 Act governs transmission and release of lease covenants for qualifying new tenancies.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Defamation Act 2013' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The 1995 Act governs transmission and release of lease covenants for qualifying new tenancies.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Partnership Act 1890' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The 1995 Act governs transmission and release of lease covenants for qualifying new tenancies.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The Human Rights Act 1998' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. The 1995 Act governs transmission and release of lease covenants for qualifying new tenancies.","E":"Correct. For tenancies within its regime, the 1995 Act provides for landlord and tenant covenants to pass on assignment and generally releases outgoing tenants from future liability, subject to important qualifications such as authorised guarantee agreements."},"primary_legal_authority":"Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The 1995 Act governs transmission and release of lease covenants for qualifying new tenancies.","common_trap":"Applying only old privity-of-estate rules without identifying the statutory regime for post-1995 tenancies.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bdc1faef468540e6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0336","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Proprietary estoppel","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landowner gives a clear assurance that another person will have a right in land. The other person reasonably relies on the assurance and suffers substantial detriment. It would be unconscionable for the landowner simply to insist on strict legal rights.\n\nWhich doctrine is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Proprietary estoppel.","B":"Promissory estoppel only, which can never concern land.","C":"Adverse possession automatically.","D":"A resulting trust necessarily.","E":"The rule in Rylands v Fletcher."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Proprietary estoppel can arise from assurance, reliance and detriment in circumstances making it unconscionable to withdraw the assurance. The remedy is flexible and seeks to respond proportionately to the equity established.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Proprietary estoppel can arise from assurance, reliance and detriment in circumstances making it unconscionable to withdraw the assurance. The remedy is flexible and seeks to respond proportionately to the equity established.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Assurance, reliance, detriment and unconscionability can found proprietary estoppel concerning rights in land.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Assurance, reliance, detriment and unconscionability can found proprietary estoppel concerning rights in land.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'A resulting trust necessarily' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Assurance, reliance, detriment and unconscionability can found proprietary estoppel concerning rights in land.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The rule in Rylands v Fletcher' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Assurance, reliance, detriment and unconscionability can found proprietary estoppel concerning rights in land."},"primary_legal_authority":"Thorner v Major; Guest v Guest [2022] UKSC 27.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Assurance, reliance, detriment and unconscionability can found proprietary estoppel concerning rights in land.","common_trap":"Assuming the claimant automatically receives the exact property expected without a separate remedial analysis.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b4dc5992359154b4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0337","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Planning — planning permission and building regulations","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer intends to construct a substantial extension. The buyer assumes that obtaining planning permission, if required, automatically means no separate building-regulations approval or compliance can be necessary.\n\nWhich is the best advice?","options":{"A":"Planning permission always replaces building-regulations requirements.","B":"Planning control and building regulations are distinct regimes, so compliance with one does not automatically satisfy the other.","C":"Building regulations apply only to commercial property.","D":"Planning law concerns only ownership of title.","E":"Neither regime can apply to extensions."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Planning permission addresses planning control, while building regulations regulate matters such as technical construction standards. A project may need to satisfy both regimes independently.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Planning permission and building-regulations compliance are separate regulatory questions.","B":"Correct. Planning permission addresses planning control, while building regulations regulate matters such as technical construction standards. A project may need to satisfy both regimes independently.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Planning permission and building-regulations compliance are separate regulatory questions.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Planning permission and building-regulations compliance are separate regulatory questions.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Neither regime can apply to extensions' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Planning permission and building-regulations compliance are separate regulatory questions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Town and Country Planning Act 1990; Building Act 1984 and Building Regulations.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Planning permission and building-regulations compliance are separate regulatory questions.","common_trap":"Treating two distinct property-development control regimes as interchangeable approvals.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"988887132ca780b9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0338","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Sale contract — title guarantee","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller contracts to transfer registered freehold land with full title guarantee. The buyer asks about the significance of that wording.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Title guarantee is merely marketing language with no legal effect.","B":"Full title guarantee means the seller guarantees the property's physical condition.","C":"Full title guarantee carries statutory implied covenants concerning the seller's right to dispose and specified matters affecting title, subject to the statutory framework.","D":"It eliminates every possible third-party right whether known or unknown.","E":"Only unregistered land can be transferred with title guarantee."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1994 implies specified covenants into dispositions made with full or limited title guarantee. These concern title rather than physical condition and operate within statutory limits.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Title guarantee is merely marketing language with no legal effect' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Full title guarantee imports statutory title covenants into the disposition.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Full title guarantee means the seller guarantees the property's physical condition' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Full title guarantee imports statutory title covenants into the disposition.","C":"Correct. The Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1994 implies specified covenants into dispositions made with full or limited title guarantee. These concern title rather than physical condition and operate within statutory limits.","D":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Full title guarantee imports statutory title covenants into the disposition.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Full title guarantee imports statutory title covenants into the disposition."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1994.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Full title guarantee imports statutory title covenants into the disposition.","common_trap":"Confusing title covenants with a guarantee of physical condition or an absolute promise against every defect.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"462eecd4a90a8d34"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0339","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Burglary — section 9(1)(a)","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person enters a building as a trespasser intending at the time of entry to steal property inside. No property is ultimately taken.\n\nWhich statement best reflects burglary liability?","options":{"A":"Burglary requires successful theft in every case.","B":"Burglary is impossible unless force is used to enter.","C":"Only entry into a dwelling can ever be burglary.","D":"Burglary under section 9(1)(a) can be complete on entry as a trespasser with the required ulterior intent, even if the intended theft is not completed.","E":"The defendant must own the building."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 9(1)(a) criminalises entry of a building or part of a building as a trespasser with intent to steal, inflict grievous bodily harm or do unlawful damage. Completion of the intended offence is not required.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Section 9(1)(a) burglary is complete upon trespassory entry with the specified ulterior intent.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition 'Burglary is impossible unless force is used to enter' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. Section 9(1)(a) burglary is complete upon trespassory entry with the specified ulterior intent.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Section 9(1)(a) burglary is complete upon trespassory entry with the specified ulterior intent.","D":"Correct. Section 9(1)(a) criminalises entry of a building or part of a building as a trespasser with intent to steal, inflict grievous bodily harm or do unlawful damage. Completion of the intended offence is not required.","E":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. Section 9(1)(a) burglary is complete upon trespassory entry with the specified ulterior intent."},"primary_legal_authority":"Theft Act 1968, s 9(1)(a).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Section 9(1)(a) burglary is complete upon trespassory entry with the specified ulterior intent.","common_trap":"Confusing section 9(1)(a) with offences requiring the ulterior crime actually to be committed or attempted after entry.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"71a3b79e9a51a9c3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0340","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Evidence — exclusion under PACE section 78","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"The prosecution seeks to rely on evidence obtained in circumstances involving serious breaches of police interview safeguards. The defence argues that admitting the evidence would adversely affect the fairness of proceedings.\n\nWhich statutory discretion is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"All unlawfully obtained evidence is automatically inadmissible.","B":"PACE section 78 applies only to defence evidence.","C":"A breach of police procedure can never affect admissibility.","D":"The jury alone decides admissibility before the judge considers it.","E":"PACE section 78 permits the court to refuse prosecution evidence where, considering all the circumstances, admission would have such an adverse effect on the fairness of proceedings that it ought not be admitted."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 78 gives the court a discretion to exclude prosecution evidence where the circumstances in which it was obtained make admission sufficiently adverse to the fairness of proceedings. It is not a universal automatic exclusionary rule.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. PACE section 78 provides a fairness-based discretion to exclude prosecution evidence.","B":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. PACE section 78 provides a fairness-based discretion to exclude prosecution evidence.","C":"Incorrect. This option states an absolute proposition that is not the governing rule on these facts. PACE section 78 provides a fairness-based discretion to exclude prosecution evidence.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition 'The jury alone decides admissibility before the judge considers it' misstates the legal consequence or test engaged by the scenario. PACE section 78 provides a fairness-based discretion to exclude prosecution evidence.","E":"Correct. Section 78 gives the court a discretion to exclude prosecution evidence where the circumstances in which it was obtained make admission sufficiently adverse to the fairness of proceedings. It is not a universal automatic exclusionary rule."},"primary_legal_authority":"Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984, s 78.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"PACE section 78 provides a fairness-based discretion to exclude prosecution evidence.","common_trap":"Assuming every procedural breach automatically excludes evidence, or that unlawfully obtained evidence is always admissible.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"83bc50c86bdb63ab"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0341","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Directors — conflicts and authorisation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A director of a private company is offered a commercial opportunity because of the director's position. The opportunity could conflict with the company's interests. The company's constitution does not disable the statutory private-company authorisation mechanism, and the interested director does not participate in the authorisation decision.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"The situation engages the duty to avoid conflicts, but a qualifying private-company board authorisation may prevent breach if the statutory requirements are satisfied.","B":"There is no conflict because the company has not yet contracted for the opportunity.","C":"Only shareholders can ever authorise a director's conflict.","D":"The director may take any opportunity offered personally.","E":"Disclosure after taking the opportunity automatically cures every breach."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 175 covers direct and indirect interests that conflict, or possibly may conflict, with the company's interests, including exploitation of opportunities. For a private company, authorisation by the directors may be available if the statutory and constitutional conditions are met.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 175 covers direct and indirect interests that conflict, or possibly may conflict, with the company's interests, including exploitation of opportunities. For a private company, authorisation by the directors may be available if the statutory and constitutional conditions are met.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 175.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A corporate opportunity can engage section 175 even if the company has not yet contracted for it; valid authorisation must satisfy the statutory regime.","common_trap":"Assuming no conflict exists until the company has a legally enforceable right to the opportunity.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3f9dbe93c1351891"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0342","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Shares — buyback out of capital","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company wants to purchase its own shares. It lacks sufficient distributable profits for the proposed purchase and asks whether a payment out of capital can ever be used.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"A company can freely buy back shares out of capital whenever directors agree.","B":"A private company may in prescribed circumstances finance a purchase of its own shares out of capital, but only by complying with the specific statutory safeguards and procedure.","C":"Use of capital for a buyback is absolutely prohibited in all circumstances.","D":"Only a public company can use the statutory capital procedure.","E":"A buyback never requires a contract or member involvement."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Companies Act contains a specific regime allowing certain private-company purchases of own shares out of capital, subject to detailed requirements designed to protect creditors and capital.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Correct. The Companies Act contains a specific regime allowing certain private-company purchases of own shares out of capital, subject to detailed requirements designed to protect creditors and capital.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, Part 18, Chapter 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A private-company buyback out of capital is possible only through the statutory safeguarded procedure.","common_trap":"Treating the capital-maintenance exception as a general power to return capital informally.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8cb981308dfdc2cc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0343","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — wrongful trading","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company's directors conclude, before liquidation, that there is no reasonable prospect of the company avoiding insolvent liquidation. One director nevertheless takes every step that the director ought to take to minimise potential loss to creditors.\n\nWhich fact is most important to potential wrongful-trading liability?","options":{"A":"Liability is automatic whenever a company later enters insolvent liquidation.","B":"Wrongful trading requires proof that the director intended to defraud creditors.","C":"Once the statutory knowledge threshold is reached, taking every step to minimise potential creditor loss is central to the statutory defence.","D":"Only shareholders can bring a wrongful-trading application.","E":"Resignation immediately erases all earlier conduct."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Wrongful trading focuses on the point when the director knew or ought to have concluded that insolvent liquidation could not reasonably be avoided, while the statutory defence concerns taking every step to minimise potential creditor loss.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Correct. Wrongful trading focuses on the point when the director knew or ought to have concluded that insolvent liquidation could not reasonably be avoided, while the statutory defence concerns taking every step to minimise potential creditor loss.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, s 214.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The post-knowledge response of the director is critical: the statutory defence turns on taking every step to minimise potential creditor loss.","common_trap":"Confusing wrongful trading with fraudulent trading, which involves dishonesty.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f781bf3baa7324f1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0344","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership — liability of incoming partner","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A new partner joins an existing partnership. Before admission, the firm incurred a contractual debt. There is no agreement with the creditor making the incoming partner liable for that historic obligation.\n\nIs the incoming partner ordinarily liable for the pre-admission debt merely by joining?","options":{"A":"Yes, every incoming partner automatically assumes all historic debts.","B":"Yes, but only if the debt exceeds the firm's assets.","C":"No partner can ever be liable for partnership debts.","D":"No. A person who becomes a partner is not ordinarily liable to creditors for partnership obligations incurred before becoming a partner.","E":"Liability depends solely on the new partner's capital contribution."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Partnership Act provides that an incoming partner does not merely by joining become liable to creditors for obligations incurred before admission, though liability can arise through a separate agreement or novation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Correct. The Partnership Act provides that an incoming partner does not merely by joining become liable to creditors for obligations incurred before admission, though liability can arise through a separate agreement or novation.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, s 17(1).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Joining a partnership does not itself impose liability for pre-admission firm debts.","common_trap":"Assuming partnership membership retrospectively creates creditor liability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f7d47ccf53871fb2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0345","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Limitation — acknowledgement of debt","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A debtor is liable on a simple contract debt. Before the applicable limitation period expires, the debtor gives the creditor a signed written acknowledgement of the claim.\n\nWhich limitation issue should the creditor consider?","options":{"A":"Acknowledgement can never affect limitation.","B":"Any oral comment automatically creates a new 12-year period.","C":"Limitation is relevant only after judgment.","D":"Acknowledgement extinguishes the underlying debt.","E":"A qualifying signed acknowledgement can cause the statutory limitation period for the debt claim to run afresh from the acknowledgement."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"For specified claims including debts and other liquidated pecuniary claims, the Limitation Act can treat a qualifying written and signed acknowledgement as causing the right of action to accrue afresh.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Correct. For specified claims including debts and other liquidated pecuniary claims, the Limitation Act can treat a qualifying written and signed acknowledgement as causing the right of action to accrue afresh."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, ss 29–30.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A statutory acknowledgement can restart time for qualifying debt claims.","common_trap":"Assuming the original accrual date is immutable despite a qualifying statutory acknowledgement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a11d448f196e53d4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0346","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Settlement — Tomlin order: enforcement of scheduled terms","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Proceedings are stayed by a Tomlin order on confidential scheduled terms. One party later breaches a payment obligation in the schedule.\n\nWhat is the practical significance of the usual 'liberty to apply' machinery?","options":{"A":"It can allow the innocent party to return to the existing proceedings to seek enforcement of the compromise rather than necessarily starting a fresh substantive claim.","B":"It makes the confidential schedule a criminal sentence.","C":"It means the settlement has no contractual effect.","D":"It automatically gives judgment for every sum mentioned in negotiations.","E":"It prevents the court from dealing with any breach of the settlement."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A Tomlin order commonly stays the action except for carrying the settlement terms into effect, enabling an application within the existing proceedings to enforce the compromise.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A Tomlin order commonly stays the action except for carrying the settlement terms into effect, enabling an application within the existing proceedings to enforce the compromise.","B":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The operative order and scheduled compromise must be distinguished: the stay and liberty-to-apply structure facilitates enforcement of the agreed terms.","C":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The operative order and scheduled compromise must be distinguished: the stay and liberty-to-apply structure facilitates enforcement of the agreed terms.","D":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. The operative order and scheduled compromise must be distinguished: the stay and liberty-to-apply structure facilitates enforcement of the agreed terms.","E":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The operative order and scheduled compromise must be distinguished: the stay and liberty-to-apply structure facilitates enforcement of the agreed terms."},"primary_legal_authority":"Tomlin v Standard Telephones and Cables Ltd [1969] 1 WLR 1378; CPR Part 40 practice","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The operative order and scheduled compromise must be distinguished: the stay and liberty-to-apply structure facilitates enforcement of the agreed terms.","common_trap":"Knowing what a Tomlin order is used for but not understanding how its scheduled terms are enforced.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6335cf72a793b459"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0347","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Evidence — expert's overriding duty: instructions affecting opinion","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A jointly instructed expert realises that a factual assumption supplied by the solicitors is incorrect and that correcting it materially changes the expert's opinion.\n\nWhat should guide the expert's response?","options":{"A":"The expert's overriding duty is to help the court on matters within expertise, so the expert should not preserve an opinion known to rest on a materially false assumption merely to support the instructing party.","B":"The expert's only duty is to secure victory for the party paying the fee.","C":"The expert must follow the solicitor's preferred conclusion even if professionally indefensible.","D":"The expert may conceal the changed view until after judgment.","E":"A jointly instructed expert owes no duty to the court."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Part 35 makes the expert's duty to the court overriding. An expert must give independent, objective assistance and address material matters affecting the opinion.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Part 35 makes the expert's duty to the court overriding. An expert must give independent, objective assistance and address material matters affecting the opinion.","B":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. An expert's duty to help the court overrides any obligation to the person instructing or paying the expert.","C":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. An expert's duty to help the court overrides any obligation to the person instructing or paying the expert.","D":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. An expert's duty to help the court overrides any obligation to the person instructing or paying the expert.","E":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. An expert's duty to help the court overrides any obligation to the person instructing or paying the expert."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 35.3 and Practice Direction 35","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An expert's duty to help the court overrides any obligation to the person instructing or paying the expert.","common_trap":"Treating an expert as an advocate for the instructing party.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1944348a9d261596"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0348","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conflicts — substantially common interest","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two sophisticated clients ask one solicitor to act for both in negotiating the same transaction. Their interests presently align on the relevant issue, both give informed consent in writing, and the solicitor reasonably believes the exception's safeguards can be met.\n\nWhich is the best regulatory analysis?","options":{"A":"Client conflicts can never be subject to any exception.","B":"Written consent alone automatically permits every conflict.","C":"The substantially-common-interest exception may permit acting despite a client conflict, but only if all requirements of the SRA exception are satisfied and it remains reasonable to act for both.","D":"The solicitor may act only if the clients waive confidentiality entirely.","E":"The exception applies even where the clients' interests are directly adverse in contested litigation."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code permits limited exceptions to the prohibition on acting where clients have a conflict, including substantially common interest, subject to informed consent and the solicitor being satisfied that it is reasonable and appropriate to act.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code permits limited exceptions to the prohibition on acting where clients have a conflict, including substantially common interest, subject to informed consent and the solicitor being satisfied that it is reasonable and appropriate to act.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 6.2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The common-interest exception is conditional; consent alone does not neutralise every client conflict.","common_trap":"Treating written consent as a universal waiver of the professional conflict rules.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ea5700b2f3d2da05"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0349","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"AML — tipping off","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor in the regulated sector has made a required money-laundering disclosure. The client asks why a transaction has been delayed. The solicitor proposes telling the client expressly that a suspicious activity report has been made and that law enforcement may investigate.\n\nWhich risk must the solicitor consider?","options":{"A":"There is an absolute professional duty to reveal every SAR to the client.","B":"A SAR is automatically public information.","C":"Tipping off exists only if the client is ultimately convicted.","D":"The statutory tipping-off and prejudicing-an-investigation offences, so disclosure to the client must be assessed carefully under the applicable provisions and exceptions.","E":"AML legislation never restricts communications with clients."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"POCA restricts certain disclosures after money-laundering reports where the statutory elements are met. Solicitors must manage client communications without making prohibited disclosures, while considering the precise statutory exceptions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Correct. POCA restricts certain disclosures after money-laundering reports where the statutory elements are met. Solicitors must manage client communications without making prohibited disclosures, while considering the precise statutory exceptions.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Proceeds of Crime Act 2002, ss 333A–333D.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"After a relevant disclosure, client communications can engage POCA's tipping-off restrictions.","common_trap":"Assuming ordinary client-transparency duties require revealing that a SAR has been filed.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"383664d60c7505bb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0350","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Formation — past consideration","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person voluntarily performs a service without any prior request or bargain. Only after the service is complete, the recipient promises to pay £5,000 for what has already been done. No exception applies.\n\nIs the completed service ordinarily good consideration for the later promise?","options":{"A":"Yes, every past act is valid consideration.","B":"Yes, because consideration must always precede a promise.","C":"No contract can ever be supported by services.","D":"Consideration is unnecessary for every simple contract.","E":"No. An act completed before the promise is ordinarily past consideration and is not good consideration for that later promise."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Consideration must ordinarily be given in exchange for the promise. An act wholly completed before a later promise is generally past consideration, subject to recognised exceptions involving prior requests and understood remuneration.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Correct. Consideration must ordinarily be given in exchange for the promise. An act wholly completed before a later promise is generally past consideration, subject to recognised exceptions involving prior requests and understood remuneration."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re McArdle; Lampleigh v Braithwait exception principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Past consideration is ordinarily not good consideration for a later promise.","common_trap":"Assuming moral obligation or benefit previously received automatically supplies contractual consideration.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f80fe19d45821e34"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0351","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Discharge — frustration and self-induced events","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party claims a contract was frustrated because performance became impossible, but the impossibility resulted from that party's own voluntary choice between two available methods of performance.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"A self-induced event will ordinarily prevent the party relying on frustration.","B":"Any increased difficulty automatically frustrates a contract.","C":"Frustration always applies whenever one party chooses not to perform.","D":"Self-induced events are irrelevant to frustration.","E":"Frustration requires a breach by the other party."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Frustration responds to supervening events not due to the fault or election of the party relying on the doctrine. A party cannot ordinarily invoke a frustrating event that it has itself brought about.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Frustration responds to supervening events not due to the fault or election of the party relying on the doctrine. A party cannot ordinarily invoke a frustrating event that it has itself brought about.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Maritime National Fish Ltd v Ocean Trawlers Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Frustration is generally unavailable where the alleged frustrating event is self-induced.","common_trap":"Using frustration to escape the consequences of one's own voluntary performance choice.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e628f8f4ef4a6d72"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0352","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Damages — remoteness and special circumstances","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A carrier is told before contracting that delay will cause an unusual category of business loss because the customer has no replacement equipment. The carrier then breaches, causing the communicated type of loss.\n\nWhich principle is most relevant to remoteness?","options":{"A":"Special losses are never recoverable.","B":"Loss arising from special circumstances can be recoverable where those circumstances were communicated so that the type of loss was within the parties' reasonable contemplation when contracting.","C":"Only losses suffered by all ordinary customers can be recovered.","D":"Communication after breach is sufficient to make every loss recoverable.","E":"Contract damages have no remoteness limitation."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Hadley v Baxendale distinguishes losses arising naturally from breach from losses attributable to special circumstances. Communication of special circumstances can bring the relevant type of loss within reasonable contemplation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Correct. Hadley v Baxendale distinguishes losses arising naturally from breach from losses attributable to special circumstances. Communication of special circumstances can bring the relevant type of loss within reasonable contemplation.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hadley v Baxendale; Victoria Laundry (Windsor) Ltd v Newman Industries Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Communicated special circumstances can make an otherwise unusual type of contractual loss sufficiently foreseeable for recovery.","common_trap":"Ignoring information supplied at formation when assessing contractual remoteness.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"85364c83c3f81383"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0353","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — pure economic loss","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contractor negligently damages a power cable serving a factory. The factory suffers no physical damage to its own property but loses profit solely because production stops during the outage. No assumption of responsibility or other exception applies.\n\nWhich is the best general analysis?","options":{"A":"All foreseeable economic loss is recoverable in negligence.","B":"Economic loss is always treated as physical damage.","C":"The lost profit is relational pure economic loss and is ordinarily irrecoverable in negligence on these facts.","D":"The factory automatically has a proprietary claim to the cable.","E":"The loss is recoverable only because it is financial."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Negligence generally restricts recovery for pure economic loss outside recognised categories such as negligent misstatement/assumption of responsibility. Financial loss resulting from damage to another person's property is a classic problem.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Correct. Negligence generally restricts recovery for pure economic loss outside recognised categories such as negligent misstatement/assumption of responsibility. Financial loss resulting from damage to another person's property is a classic problem.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Spartan Steel & Alloys Ltd v Martin & Co (Contractors) Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Relational pure economic loss caused by damage to another's property is ordinarily not recoverable in negligence absent an exception.","common_trap":"Assuming factual foreseeability alone is enough for recovery of pure economic loss.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f12f6a801da6ee31"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0354","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Vicarious liability — course of employment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An employee commits a tort while carrying out an authorised work task in an improper manner. The employer argues that liability is impossible because it never authorised the tortious way in which the task was performed.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Vicarious liability exists only where the employer expressly orders the tort.","B":"Any employee tort anywhere automatically binds the employer.","C":"Vicarious liability is a form of strict contractual liability owed only to employees.","D":"An employer can be vicariously liable for an employee's tort committed in the course of employment even though the wrongful manner of performing the authorised task was not authorised.","E":"An employer is immune whenever internal rules prohibit the conduct."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Vicarious liability can attach where the employee's tort is sufficiently connected with the employment. Lack of authorisation for the tortious manner does not itself prevent liability.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Correct. Vicarious liability can attach where the employee's tort is sufficiently connected with the employment. Lack of authorisation for the tortious manner does not itself prevent liability.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Lister v Hesley Hall Ltd; Mohamud v WM Morrison Supermarkets plc; Trustees of the Barry Congregation v BXB [2023] UKSC 15.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Unauthorised wrongdoing can still fall within the course or close connection of employment.","common_trap":"Equating lack of express authorisation for the tort with absence of vicarious liability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"30ad5349b8b4261c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0355","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Private nuisance — abnormal sensitivity","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A factory's ordinary and reasonable use of its land causes no interference with normal neighbouring uses, but damages the claimant's exceptionally delicate process, which is unusually sensitive to minor environmental changes.\n\nWhich principle is relevant?","options":{"A":"Abnormal sensitivity automatically makes the defendant strictly liable.","B":"Private nuisance protects every hypersensitive activity regardless of ordinary land use.","C":"Sensitivity is relevant only to trespass.","D":"The claimant always recovers if any physical process is affected.","E":"A claimant cannot ordinarily enlarge nuisance liability merely because its use is abnormally sensitive where ordinary use would not be materially interfered with."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Private nuisance assesses unreasonable interference with ordinary use and enjoyment of land. Abnormal sensitivity can prevent recovery where the interference would not affect ordinary use.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Correct. Private nuisance assesses unreasonable interference with ordinary use and enjoyment of land. Abnormal sensitivity can prevent recovery where the interference would not affect ordinary use."},"primary_legal_authority":"Robinson v Kilvert; private nuisance principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Abnormally sensitive use does not ordinarily turn an otherwise non-actionable interference into nuisance.","common_trap":"Testing nuisance solely by the claimant's unusually delicate activity rather than ordinary land use.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9d164bdadd1eb1b1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0356","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Precedent — distinguishing","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A lower court is faced with an earlier binding appellate authority. The earlier case's ratio would bind if the legally material facts were the same, but the present case contains a material factual difference relevant to the rule.\n\nWhat may the lower court properly do?","options":{"A":"Distinguish the earlier case if the material factual difference means its ratio does not govern the present facts.","B":"Overrule the appellate court.","C":"Ignore any precedent it dislikes.","D":"Declare the earlier judgment void.","E":"Treat every factual difference, however trivial, as sufficient to avoid precedent."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Distinguishing respects precedent while recognising that a ratio applies to materially analogous facts. A lower court cannot overrule a binding higher-court authority but may find its ratio inapplicable because of a legally material distinction.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Distinguishing respects precedent while recognising that a ratio applies to materially analogous facts. A lower court cannot overrule a binding higher-court authority but may find its ratio inapplicable because of a legally material distinction.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Doctrine of precedent and ratio decidendi.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A lower court may distinguish binding authority on a genuinely material factual difference.","common_trap":"Confusing distinguishing with overruling, or relying on immaterial factual differences.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2a5fb5d26aeabcb9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0357","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — standing","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A public-interest organisation seeks judicial review of a decision affecting the statutory scheme in which it has substantial expertise and involvement. The defendant argues that only a person with a private-law cause of action can have standing.\n\nWhich test is applicable?","options":{"A":"Only contractual parties can seek judicial review.","B":"The claimant must have a sufficient interest in the matter to which the application relates; standing is assessed in the public-law context rather than requiring a private-law cause of action.","C":"Standing requires ownership of affected land.","D":"Any person automatically has standing without any interest.","E":"Only the Attorney General can challenge public decisions."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Senior Courts Act uses the sufficient-interest test. Its application is contextual and can permit public-interest claimants where their connection, expertise and the importance of vindicating public law justify standing.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Correct. The Senior Courts Act uses the sufficient-interest test. Its application is contextual and can permit public-interest claimants where their connection, expertise and the importance of vindicating public law justify standing.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Senior Courts Act 1981, s 31(3); R v Inland Revenue Commissioners, ex p National Federation of Self-Employed.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Judicial-review standing turns on sufficient interest, not possession of a private-law cause of action.","common_trap":"Importing private-law title requirements into the public-law standing test.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"78971dbf290a211e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0358","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — declaration of incompatibility","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A senior court concludes that primary legislation cannot be interpreted compatibly with a Convention right using section 3. The legislation itself remains valid and operative.\n\nWhich Human Rights Act mechanism may be available?","options":{"A":"The court must strike down the Act of Parliament.","B":"Section 4 automatically repeals the incompatible provision.","C":"A declaration of incompatibility under section 4, which does not itself invalidate the legislation.","D":"The court must refer every case to the CJEU.","E":"A declaration of incompatibility is available only against private contracts."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Specified higher courts may make a declaration of incompatibility where primary legislation cannot be read compatibly. The declaration does not affect the provision's validity, continuing operation or enforcement.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Correct. Specified higher courts may make a declaration of incompatibility where primary legislation cannot be read compatibly. The declaration does not affect the provision's validity, continuing operation or enforcement.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Section 4 provides a declaration mechanism without judicial invalidation of primary legislation.","common_trap":"Treating the HRA as giving courts a general power to strike down Acts of Parliament.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3bca60a91fe61fb4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0359","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — anti-lapse","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator leaves a gift to her adult son. The son dies before the testator but leaves children who survive the testator. The will contains no contrary intention and the statutory conditions are otherwise met.\n\nWhich rule should be considered before treating the gift as lapsed?","options":{"A":"The gift must always lapse because every predeceasing beneficiary's gift fails.","B":"The son's personal representatives automatically take beneficially.","C":"The gift passes to the Crown.","D":"The statutory anti-lapse provision can substitute the deceased child's issue for the deceased beneficiary, subject to section 33.","E":"The witnesses decide who receives the gift."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 33 can save certain gifts to a testator's child or remoter descendant who predeceases the testator, allowing the beneficiary's issue to take in the statutory manner unless the will shows a contrary intention.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Correct. Section 33 can save certain gifts to a testator's child or remoter descendant who predeceases the testator, allowing the beneficiary's issue to take in the statutory manner unless the will shows a contrary intention.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 33.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Gifts to predeceasing descendants require consideration of the section 33 anti-lapse rule.","common_trap":"Applying the ordinary lapse rule without checking the statutory exception for descendants.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7c18f649327c133a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0360","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Estate administration — section 27 notices","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Personal representatives intend to distribute an estate but are concerned about unknown creditors. They want statutory protection against personal liability for claims of which they have no notice, while preserving claimants' rights against recipients where applicable.\n\nWhich procedure is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Obtaining a Part 36 offer.","B":"Registering a restriction at Companies House.","C":"Executing a codicil after death.","D":"Applying for adverse possession.","E":"Advertising for creditors and claimants in accordance with Trustee Act 1925 section 27 before distribution."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 27 allows trustees and personal representatives to advertise for claims and, after the specified period, distribute with protection against personal liability for claims of which they lacked notice, without extinguishing the claimant's underlying rights against recipients where available.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Correct. Section 27 allows trustees and personal representatives to advertise for claims and, after the specified period, distribute with protection against personal liability for claims of which they lacked notice, without extinguishing the claimant's underlying rights against recipients where available."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 1925, s 27.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Section 27 notices protect personal representatives distributing after proper advertisement against unknown claims in the statutory circumstances.","common_trap":"Assuming the notice extinguishes the creditor's substantive claim altogether.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4d0955ca53636e8b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0361","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"IHT — gifts with reservation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A parent gives a house outright to an adult child but continues living in it rent-free and enjoying substantially the same benefit until death. No relevant exception applies.\n\nWhich inheritance-tax concept is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The gift-with-reservation rules, which can cause the property to be treated as part of the donor's estate for IHT purposes despite the earlier gift.","B":"The property is necessarily outside IHT because legal title was transferred.","C":"The gift is automatically an SDLT exemption that determines IHT.","D":"Only capital gains tax can apply after a lifetime gift.","E":"Continued enjoyment is irrelevant to inheritance tax."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The gift-with-reservation regime counteracts lifetime gifts where the donor retains enjoyment of the gifted property. Subject to the detailed rules, the property can be treated as part of the donor's estate on death.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The gift-with-reservation regime counteracts lifetime gifts where the donor retains enjoyment of the gifted property. Subject to the detailed rules, the property can be treated as part of the donor's estate on death.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Finance Act 1986, ss 102 ff.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Retained enjoyment after a lifetime gift can engage the gift-with-reservation regime.","common_trap":"Assuming transfer of legal title alone conclusively removes property from the donor's death estate for IHT.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9173d1544e06f536"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0362","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Mixed receipts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm receives one electronic payment containing £8,000 of client money and £1,000 belonging to the firm for already billed costs. The payment cannot practicably be split before receipt.\n\nWhich is the best Accounts Rules approach?","options":{"A":"The entire £9,000 must remain client money permanently.","B":"A mixed receipt may be paid into client account, but the firm's part must then be transferred out promptly in accordance with the Rules.","C":"Mixed receipts may never be received by a solicitor.","D":"The entire payment must always go directly to business account.","E":"The firm's £1,000 becomes the client's property."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules permit mixed money to be paid into client account, but money belonging to the firm must be transferred out promptly so that client account does not retain office money unnecessarily.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules permit mixed money to be paid into client account, but money belonging to the firm must be transferred out promptly so that client account does not retain office money unnecessarily.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, rr 4.2 and 4.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Mixed receipts can enter client account, with the firm's portion promptly removed.","common_trap":"Treating the mixed character of a receipt as either prohibiting receipt or converting all funds into client money.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d61894d349a0f429"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0363","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Constitution — Re Rose","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A donor executes the correct share-transfer form and delivers it, together with the share certificate, to the company for registration. Nothing further remains for the donor personally to do, but registration is delayed. The donor then dies.\n\nWhich equitable principle may be relevant to whether the gift was effectively constituted before registration?","options":{"A":"Milroy v Lord means registration delay always defeats the gift regardless of the donor's completed acts.","B":"The rule in Saunders v Vautier.","C":"The Re Rose principle, under which equity may recognise the transfer once the donor has done everything necessary on the donor's part to effect it.","D":"The beneficiary must have paid consideration.","E":"Only Strong v Bird can ever perfect an imperfect gift."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Re Rose qualifies the general imperfect-gift rule where the donor has taken all steps required of the donor and completion depends on action by a third party such as the company registering the transfer.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Correct. Re Rose qualifies the general imperfect-gift rule where the donor has taken all steps required of the donor and completion depends on action by a third party such as the company registering the transfer.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Rose [1952] Ch 499; constitution of trusts and gifts.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Where the donor has done everything necessary on the donor's part, equity may treat the gift as complete despite outstanding third-party registration.","common_trap":"Applying Milroy v Lord mechanically without considering a recognised exception now expressly within the updated FLK.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"83b51c4f67ec8de3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0364","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Fiduciaries — unauthorised profits","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee learns of an investment opportunity solely through the trusteeship, purchases it personally without informed authorisation, and makes a substantial profit. The trust itself could not have afforded the investment.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the fiduciary no-profit rule?","options":{"A":"No liability arises because the trust lacked funds.","B":"A fiduciary may retain any profit made honestly.","C":"Accountability requires proof that the trust suffered matching financial loss.","D":"The trustee may be accountable for the unauthorised profit even if the trust could not itself have taken the opportunity.","E":"Only bribes, never opportunity profits, engage fiduciary duties."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Fiduciary duties impose strict controls on unauthorised profits obtained by reason of the fiduciary position. The fact that the principal could not itself have made the profit does not necessarily allow the fiduciary to retain it.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Correct. Fiduciary duties impose strict controls on unauthorised profits obtained by reason of the fiduciary position. The fact that the principal could not itself have made the profit does not necessarily allow the fiduciary to retain it.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Boardman v Phipps; Keech v Sandford.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A fiduciary can be accountable for unauthorised opportunity profits without proof that the trust could have earned them itself.","common_trap":"Treating absence of corresponding trust loss as a complete answer to the no-profit rule.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1157b7ed769b31e2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0365","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Registered land — actual occupation: failure to disclose on enquiry","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person has a proprietary interest and is actually occupying registered land. Before a disposition, the purchaser makes an enquiry of that person about rights in the property. The person could reasonably have disclosed the interest but fails to do so.\n\nHow can that failure affect overriding status?","options":{"A":"Schedule 3 paragraph 2 can prevent the interest overriding where the statutory enquiry-and-nondisclosure exception is satisfied.","B":"Actual occupation makes every interest override regardless of enquiries.","C":"Failure to answer an enquiry automatically extinguishes the proprietary interest itself.","D":"The purchaser's enquiry is irrelevant under the Land Registration Act 2002.","E":"The interest becomes a registered charge automatically."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Actual occupation is not an absolute priority rule. Schedule 3 paragraph 2 contains an exception where enquiry is made of the interest-holder and the right is not disclosed when it would have been reasonable to disclose it.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Actual occupation is not an absolute priority rule. Schedule 3 paragraph 2 contains an exception where enquiry is made of the interest-holder and the right is not disclosed when it would have been reasonable to disclose it.","B":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. An interest belonging to a person in actual occupation may override, subject to Schedule 3 paragraph 2 exceptions, including qualifying enquiry and nondisclosure.","C":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. An interest belonging to a person in actual occupation may override, subject to Schedule 3 paragraph 2 exceptions, including qualifying enquiry and nondisclosure.","D":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. An interest belonging to a person in actual occupation may override, subject to Schedule 3 paragraph 2 exceptions, including qualifying enquiry and nondisclosure.","E":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. An interest belonging to a person in actual occupation may override, subject to Schedule 3 paragraph 2 exceptions, including qualifying enquiry and nondisclosure."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, Sch 3, para 2","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An interest belonging to a person in actual occupation may override, subject to Schedule 3 paragraph 2 exceptions, including qualifying enquiry and nondisclosure.","common_trap":"Remembering actual occupation but omitting the statutory exceptions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3963768c619c20f9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0366","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — severance","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two beneficial joint tenants own land. One serves on the other a valid written notice demonstrating an immediate intention to sever the beneficial joint tenancy. The notice is properly served during both owners' lifetimes.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect?","options":{"A":"The beneficial joint tenancy is severed so that they thereafter hold their beneficial interests as tenants in common.","B":"The legal estate is severed into separate legal titles.","C":"Severance is impossible without sale of the land.","D":"The notice transfers the entire property to the recipient.","E":"Severance causes the property to become ownerless."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A beneficial joint tenancy may be severed by written notice under section 36(2), converting the beneficial ownership into a tenancy in common. The legal estate remains jointly held because legal co-ownership is necessarily joint.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A beneficial joint tenancy may be severed by written notice under section 36(2), converting the beneficial ownership into a tenancy in common. The legal estate remains jointly held because legal co-ownership is necessarily joint.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 36(2).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Valid written notice can sever the beneficial joint tenancy without severing the legal title.","common_trap":"Confusing severance of equitable ownership with division of the legal estate.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0e7a16ddb8ea953d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0367","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Commercial leases — contracting out: agreement before tenant bound","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landlord and proposed business tenant want the security-of-tenure provisions of Part II of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954 excluded. The landlord serves the prescribed warning notice, and the tenant completes the required declaration before becoming contractually bound to take the tenancy.\n\nWhy is the timing of the warning notice and declaration important?","options":{"A":"The statutory contracting-out procedure must be completed before the tenant becomes contractually bound to enter into the tenancy.","B":"The parties can validly contract out for the first time after the protected tenancy has ended.","C":"A clause in the lease alone is always sufficient without the prescribed procedure.","D":"Contracting out is available only to residential tenancies.","E":"The procedure automatically converts the lease into a licence."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A valid exclusion requires compliance with the prescribed warning/declaration procedure before the tenant becomes bound to enter the tenancy; the declaration form depends on the timing of the notice.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A valid exclusion requires compliance with the prescribed warning/declaration procedure before the tenant becomes bound to enter the tenancy; the declaration form depends on the timing of the notice.","B":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Sections 38A and the 2003 Order permit prospective business tenancies to be contracted out only through the prescribed pre-commitment procedure.","C":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. Sections 38A and the 2003 Order permit prospective business tenancies to be contracted out only through the prescribed pre-commitment procedure.","D":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. Sections 38A and the 2003 Order permit prospective business tenancies to be contracted out only through the prescribed pre-commitment procedure.","E":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. Sections 38A and the 2003 Order permit prospective business tenancies to be contracted out only through the prescribed pre-commitment procedure."},"primary_legal_authority":"Landlord and Tenant Act 1954, s 38A; Regulatory Reform (Business Tenancies) (England and Wales) Order 2003","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Sections 38A and the 2003 Order permit prospective business tenancies to be contracted out only through the prescribed pre-commitment procedure.","common_trap":"Assuming a lease clause excluding the Act is enough by itself.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"676896d5db05a1e0"}];
